/* ==========================================================================
   BA EXAM SIMULATOR · QUESTION BANK
   Original practice questions written against the BABOK® Guide v3 and
   The Business Analysis Standard. Not real IIBA exam questions.
   Checked against the BABOK v3 text (Nov 2026); every question cites its source.

   HOW TO EDIT
   Each question is one object:
     id        unique, letters/digits/-/_ only, e.g. "C-RADD-041"
     exam      "ECBA", "CCBA", "CBAP", or several: "CCBA CBAP"
     area      ECBA domains: UBA, MIND, IMPL, CHANGE, NEED, SOLUTION, STAKEHOLDER, VALUE, CONTEXT
               CCBA/CBAP knowledge areas: BAPM, EC, RLCM, SA, RADD, SE
     level     "easy", "mid" or "hard"
     question  plain text; use \n for a line break
     options   2-6 answer texts (4 is the exam format). Options are shuffled,
               so avoid "all of the above" / "both A and B".
     answer    letter of the correct option as written here: "A", "B", "C"...
     why       why the right answer is right (shown in review and flash cards)
     case      optional: id of a case study in `cases` below. Questions that
               share a case are kept together in full exams.
     ref       optional: source shown with the answer, e.g. "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace
               Requirements". "General practice" marks items BABOK doesn't spell out.

   Mistakes don't break the app: bad questions are skipped and listed under
   Settings › Question bank. Keep this file next to index.html.
   ========================================================================== */
window.BA_BANK = {
  version: "2026.11.10",

  cases: {
    "CASE-INSURER": {
      title: "Harbourline Insurance: claims modernization",
      text: "Harbourline is a regional insurer with 1,200 staff. Settling a standard motor claim takes 14 days on average; the two largest competitors advertise 5 days. Customer satisfaction for claims has fallen for three years in a row, and the claims department has asked for a new claims platform.\n\nThe COO is the sponsor. The Head of Claims, the IT Director, the Compliance Officer, the call center manager and a group of senior claims handlers are involved. The current claims system is 18 years old and is linked to the policy, payments and fraud-scoring systems. Compliance must report every claim to the national regulator within 48 hours of first notice.\n\nInterviews so far show that handlers re-key data from emailed forms, wait an average of four days for repair estimates, and send 30% of claims to manual fraud review. The IT Director believes a packaged claims platform could be live in nine months. The Head of Claims wants the new platform to copy the current screens “so nobody needs retraining.” The board has asked for a recommendation within eight weeks."
    },
    "CASE-PERMITS": {
      title: "City of Larkford: online building permits",
      text: "Six months ago the City of Larkford launched an online portal for building-permit applications. The business case promised that 70% of applications would be submitted online in the first year, that average approval time would drop from 30 to 15 days, and that counter staff would be reduced by two people through attrition.\n\nCurrent figures: 31% of applications arrive online, average approval time is 27 days, and counter staffing is unchanged. Applicants say the portal times out during large drawing uploads. Planning officers print online applications to review them on paper because that is how the review checklist and sign-off policy are written. A union agreement requires a 90-day consultation before any change to officer duties.\n\nThe portal vendor says the upload limit can be raised in a configuration change. The city manager has asked the business analyst to explain the gap between expected and actual results and to recommend what to do next."
    },
    "CASE-LOYALTY": {
      title: "Fennick Retail: loyalty app relaunch",
      text: "Fennick Retail is relaunching its loyalty app. The work is run by one agile team in two-week iterations; the product owner sits in the e-commerce department and a business analyst supports the team.\n\nMarketing wants personalized offers in the next release to support a campaign that starts in ten weeks. Finance wants the points-liability calculation fixed first, because the external auditor flagged it as a material weakness. The data protection officer has said that personalized offers need an explicit consent flow before they can go live. The store operations manager has not attended the last three backlog refinement sessions, although store staff will have to explain the new points rules to customers.\n\nThe backlog has 140 items. Most of them have no acceptance criteria, and several were added directly by stakeholders without the product owner’s knowledge."
    },
    "CASE-LOGISTICS": {
      title: "Northway Logistics: one warehouse system after a merger",
      text: "Northway Logistics has acquired Kestrel Freight. Together they run eleven warehouses on two different warehouse management systems (WMS). The CFO, who sponsors the integration, wants a single WMS within 12 months to save about €2.4 million a year in license and support costs.\n\nThe two operations directors run picking very differently: Northway picks order by order, Kestrel picks in waves across many orders. Each believes its own method is faster. The warehouse staff are unionized, and changes to working practices must be discussed with the union. October to December is peak season and accounts for 40% of annual volume.\n\nIT has only three integration specialists, who also support the transport and billing systems. The two largest customers have service-level agreements with financial penalties for any shipment that leaves later than the same day. An operations manager has suggested using the smallest Kestrel warehouse as a pilot site."
    },
    "CASE-CLINIC": {
      title: "Brightwater Clinics: cutting missed appointments",
      text: "Brightwater Clinics runs six family-medicine clinics. Almost all appointments are booked by phone, and about 30% of booked appointments are missed without notice, which leaves doctors idle and lengthens waiting lists.\n\nReceptionists answer phones, handle walk-ins and rework the day whenever a doctor’s schedule changes. They say the booking process “just works”, but nobody has written it down. The practice manager sponsors the initiative and favours an online booking app that sends reminder messages.\n\nThe clinics’ data protection lead must approve anything that uses patient contact details. Many patients are over 70, and several have poor eyesight."
    },
    "CASE-BANK": {
      title: "Quillmoor Bank: faster small-business loans",
      text: "Quillmoor Bank takes an average of 21 days to approve a small-business loan. The COO, who sponsors the initiative, wants this down to 5 days and has asked about buying a new loan origination system.\n\nAn application passes through four hand-offs between relationship managers, credit analysts and the credit committee, which meets once a week. Credit analysts keep the credit policy rules in their own spreadsheets, and two analysts have been found applying the same rule differently. Data is re-keyed across three systems.\n\nThe credit risk department must approve any change to credit rules. The banking regulator can ask the bank at any time to show how its lending rules meet capital requirements."
    },
    "CASE-COLLEGE": {
      title: "Lakeside College: online enrolment",
      text: "Lakeside College enrols about 9,000 students a year, most of them in a three-week window each August. Students fill in paper forms, which registry staff then re-key into the student records system. About one record in eight has an error, and queues at peak can last two hours.\n\nThe registrar sponsors the initiative and wants online enrolment live before next August, ten months away. The student records system is 12 years old. Its vendor offers an online enrolment add-on, and the college’s IT team has four people.\n\nEach faculty head sets the prerequisites for their own courses. These are published in faculty handbooks, which don’t always match what the student records system enforces. The student union has asked to be involved, and most students are expected to enrol from their phones."
    },
    "CASE-ENERGY": {
      title: "Varrowby Energy: replacing the billing platform",
      text: "Varrowby Energy supplies electricity and gas to 1.2 million homes. Its billing system is 20 years old, and the vendor will end support for it in 18 months. Two options are on the table: buy a packaged cloud billing product, or extend the current system.\n\nBilling errors cause about 15,000 complaints a year. The energy regulator publishes complaint rankings and can fine suppliers. Varrowby offers 300 tariffs, many of them legacy tariffs with only a handful of customers, and its customer records contain many duplicates.\n\nThe CFO sponsors the initiative. The current plan is to finish migrating customers in December. The head of customer operations is worried about call volumes, because winter is the busiest season for both bills and calls."
    }
  },

  questions: [

/* ============================== ECBA ============================== */
/* --- Understanding Business Analysis (UBA) --- */
{ id: "E-UBA-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.2 What is Business Analysis?",
  question: "Which statement best describes the purpose of business analysis?",
  options: ["Producing complete, approved requirements documents that the IT department can build from", "Checking that teams follow the organization’s policies, procedures and methodology on every project", "Controlling the scope, schedule and budget of change initiatives so they deliver as planned", "Enabling change by working out needs and recommending solutions that give stakeholders value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Business analysis exists to enable change: it works out what is needed and recommends solutions that give stakeholders value. Documentation is one possible output, controlling scope, schedule and budget is project management, and policy compliance is an audit function." },
{ id: "E-UBA-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "Which six concepts make up the Business Analysis Core Concept Model (BACCM)?",
  options: ["Strategy, Initiative, Delivery, Value, Risk, Context", "Requirement, Design, Scope, Risk, Value, Change", "Need, Solution, Process, Data, People, Technology", "Change, Need, Solution, Stakeholder, Value, Context"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The model rests on six ideas: need, change, solution, value, stakeholder and context. Each is understood through how it links to the others, which makes the set a useful thinking checklist." },
{ id: "E-UBA-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "A business analyst uses the BACCM to review a proposed initiative. What is the main benefit of doing this?",
  options: ["It sets the order in which business analysis tasks should be performed, starting with need", "It replaces the need for a separate stakeholder analysis", "It defines which deliverables the business analyst must produce and in which order they are approved", "It checks that all six concepts and their relationships are considered, since they affect each other"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The BACCM is a conceptual framework, not a process or a deliverable list. Its value is that the six concepts are interdependent: looking at all of them reveals gaps, such as a solution with no clear need or a change that ignores its context." },
{ id: "E-UBA-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "In the BACCM, how is value best described?",
  options: ["The financial return an initiative delivers compared with its cost", "The benefit an organization gains, measured in money or time", "Benefits minus costs, expressed in money over the life of a solution", "How much something is worth, matters or is useful to a stakeholder in a given context"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Value describes how worthwhile or useful something is to a particular stakeholder in their situation. It may be tangible or intangible, and two stakeholders can see different value in the same change. ROI and price are just two ways of expressing it." },
{ id: "E-UBA-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "A business analyst helps the leadership team set the organization’s direction for the next three years and decide which initiatives to fund. Which horizon is this work in?",
  options: ["Delivery horizon", "Strategy horizon", "Initiative horizon", "Operational horizon"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The Standard refers to the Agile Extension’s three horizons. The strategy horizon covers organizational direction and strategic outcomes; the initiative horizon covers a specific change; the delivery horizon covers building and releasing solution increments." },
{ id: "E-UBA-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.4 Importance of Business Analysis",
  question: "A new executive asks why the company should invest in business analysis. Which answer is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["It keeps projects on schedule and budget by controlling scope", "It reduces how much stakeholders need to be involved, because the analyst represents their views", "It helps the organization understand its needs and make better decisions about change", "It makes sure every requirement is documented before work begins"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The importance of business analysis lies in better decisions about change and a focus on value. It cannot guarantee schedules, it increases rather than reduces stakeholder involvement, and documenting everything up front is not its goal." },
{ id: "E-UBA-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "A team delivered every feature on time, but six months later customers barely use them. Which aspect of business analysis was MOST likely neglected?",
  options: ["Focusing on outcomes and value, not only on outputs", "Writing detailed technical specifications for each feature", "Verifying the requirements", "Testing that each feature works as specified before release"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Delivering outputs (features) is not the same as achieving outcomes. Value is created when the change produces benefits for stakeholders, so business analysis keeps attention on desired outcomes and whether they are reached." },
{ id: "E-UBA-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "hard", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "A program has three related initiatives. After the first one goes live, the measured benefit is small and the sponsor wants to cancel the other two. What should the business analyst point out FIRST?",
  options: ["The first initiative failed and should be rolled back", "The sponsor has the authority to cancel, so the analyst should help close the program cleanly", "Wait until all three initiatives are complete before measuring any of the benefits from the program", "Review the expected value of the whole program, since initiatives often deliver value together"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Desired outcomes are often reached through a series of future states, and value frequently comes from initiatives working together. The analyst should help the sponsor look at program-level value before deciding, not assume failure or simply defer to authority." },
{ id: "E-UBA-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "Which activity is a typical way business analysis contributes to value?",
  options: ["Approving the project budget and the vendor contract", "Translating business needs into requirements and designs", "Negotiating vendor contracts on behalf of procurement", "Writing and testing the production code for the new solution"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Clarifying problems and opportunities, communicating desired outcomes and translating needs into requirements and designs are core business analysis contributions. Budget approval, contract negotiation and coding belong to other roles." },
{ id: "E-UBA-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "While reviewing a change, a business analyst asks: “Who will be affected, and how will they see this change?” Which core concept is the analyst examining?",
  options: ["Context", "Need", "Stakeholder", "Solution"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Stakeholders are people or groups linked to a change, need or solution. Asking who is affected and how they’ll see the change is stakeholder analysis." },
{ id: "E-UBA-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "A new regulation comes into force halfway through an initiative. Using the BACCM, which statement is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["The regulation only affects the solution, so only the design needs to be updated to comply", "Context is fixed at the start of an initiative and should not be revisited", "Regulations are a compliance matter and fall outside business analysis", "A change in context can create new needs and affect the solution, stakeholders and value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The six concepts are interdependent. A change in context (a new regulation) can create new needs, add stakeholders such as the regulator, change the value of the planned solution and require changes to it, so all related concepts should be reassessed." },
{ id: "E-UBA-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "In the BACCM, what is a change?",
  options: ["Transforming something in response to a need", "A request to alter an approved requirement", "A new software release or system upgrade for users", "A difference between two versions of a document"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "In the BACCM, change means transforming something because of a need. Change requests and software releases are narrower ideas that may form part of a change." },

/* --- Mindset for Effective Business Analysis (MIND) --- */
{ id: "E-MIND-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "Which of the following is one of the business analysis principles described in The Business Analysis Standard?",
  options: ["Document everything before starting", "See the whole", "Follow the plan", "Trace requirements back to business objectives"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The Standard lists seven principles taken from the Agile Extension: see the whole, think as a customer, analyze to determine what is valuable, get real using examples, understand what is doable, stimulate collaboration and continuous improvement, and avoid waste." },
{ id: "E-MIND-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "Stakeholders keep interpreting a rule differently. The business analyst walks them through three specific customer situations and agrees the expected result for each. Which principle is the analyst applying?",
  options: ["Avoid waste", "Understand what is doable", "Get real using examples", "See the whole"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Get real using examples means using concrete examples to build shared understanding and to guide acceptance criteria, design and testing. Agreeing the outcome of specific situations removes ambiguity that abstract rules leave open." },
{ id: "E-MIND-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "A business analyst finds that the team produces four weekly reports that nobody reads and suggests stopping them. Which principle does this reflect?",
  options: ["Avoid waste", "Think as a customer", "Stimulate collaboration and continuous improvement", "Analyze to determine what is valuable"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Avoid waste means telling apart activities that add value from those that don’t, and removing the latter. Reports nobody uses consume effort without delivering value." },
{ id: "E-MIND-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst tells the sponsor, with evidence, that the planned launch date is unrealistic, knowing the message will not be welcome. Which shared value is the analyst showing?",
  options: ["Respect", "Customer focus", "Curiosity", "Courage"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Courage is the willingness to raise difficult issues and take considered risks. Raising an unwelcome but evidence-based concern with the sponsor is a clear example." },
{ id: "E-MIND-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 3.4 Foundational Competencies",
  question: "Which of these is one of the foundational competencies in The Business Analysis Standard?",
  options: ["Software development", "Budget management", "Systems thinking", "Contract law"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The Standard names five foundational competencies: adaptability, facilitation, leadership and influencing, problem solving, and systems thinking." },
{ id: "E-MIND-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.4 Foundational Competencies · BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "A business analyst moves from a team that works with detailed specifications to one that works in short iterations with daily conversations. She changes how she works to fit the new team. Which competency is she using?",
  options: ["Adaptability", "Facilitation", "Systems thinking", "Leadership and influencing"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Adaptability is changing your working style to become more effective in a different situation, such as a new team, approach or culture." },
{ id: "E-MIND-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.4 Foundational Competencies · BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "Two departments disagree about a new process. The business analyst plans a structured session with an agenda and ground rules, keeps the discussion on track and makes sure both sides are heard. Which competency is this MOST directly?",
  options: ["Problem solving", "Facilitation", "Systems thinking", "Adaptability"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Facilitation covers running workshops, guiding discussions, negotiating and easing conflicts so a group can reach its objectives. Planning and running a structured session is facilitation." },
{ id: "E-MIND-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "hard", ref: "BA Standard 3.4 Foundational Competencies · BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "Customer complaints have risen. A manager wants a new complaints form. Before agreeing, the business analyst checks whether the form would address why complaints are rising or only how they are recorded. Which foundational competency is MOST evident?",
  options: ["Facilitation", "Adaptability", "Leadership and influencing", "Problem solving"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Problem solving in the Standard is about making sure the value you create addresses the root cause of a problem rather than a symptom. A new form changes how complaints are recorded, not why they happen." },
{ id: "E-MIND-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "hard", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "A team uses the same 60-page requirements template on every initiative, including a two-week tweak to an internal report. The business analyst suggests choosing techniques and documentation that suit each initiative. Which part of an effective mindset does this show?",
  options: ["Making fit-for-purpose choices of techniques, processes and tools", "Value maximization, by producing extra documentation that can be reused later", "Following an organizational standard consistently so all initiatives are comparable", "Customer focus"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "An effective mindset includes making fit-for-purpose choices: selecting techniques, processes and tools that suit the situation. One heavy template for everything adds effort without adding value." },
{ id: "E-MIND-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "Before discussing features, a business analyst maps how customers currently experience the end-to-end process, including their frustrations. Which principle is this?",
  options: ["Understand what is doable", "Avoid waste", "Think as a customer", "Get real using examples"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Think as a customer means understanding the customer’s experience and needs first, then refining them into detail as the solution evolves." },

/* --- Implementing Business Analysis (IMPL) --- */
{ id: "E-IMPL-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.3 Who is a Business Analyst?",
  question: "Who performs business analysis?",
  options: ["People whose job title is “business analyst”", "Only certified professionals such as ECBA, CCBA or CBAP holders", "Anyone who performs business analysis tasks", "Members of the IT department or the business analysis team"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Business analysis is defined by the work, not the title. Product owners, project managers, designers, consultants and many others perform business analysis tasks." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "Requirements for a new customer app are expected to change often, and stakeholders want to see working pieces early and give feedback. Which approach to business analysis work fits BEST?",
  options: ["Predictive: complete and approve all requirements before any development starts", "Adaptive: elaborate requirements iteratively, with frequent feedback", "Hybrid: fixed scope, then build in sprints", "Predictive, with a change freeze to stop requirements changing"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Adaptive approaches suit situations with high uncertainty and changing needs: requirements are refined in small increments and validated through frequent feedback. Predictive approaches suit stable, well-understood needs." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "A business analyst produces a wireframe showing where fields and buttons appear on a screen. How should this be classified?",
  options: ["A business requirement", "A stakeholder requirement", "A transition requirement", "A design"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A requirement represents a need in a usable form, while a design represents a solution. A wireframe shows how the solution could look, so it is a design." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "“Customer data must be migrated from the old system and call center staff trained before go-live.” These needs disappear once the new system is running. What type of requirement are they?",
  options: ["Transition requirements", "Business requirements", "Non-functional requirements", "Stakeholder requirements"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Transition requirements cover what is needed only while moving from today’s state to the new one, such as data migration and training. They are temporary and fall away once the move is done." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.3 Foundational Techniques",
  question: "Which of the following is one of the foundational techniques described in The Business Analysis Standard?",
  options: ["Critical path method", "Earned value analysis", "Customer journey mapping", "Six Sigma DMAIC"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The Standard’s foundational techniques are backlog management, customer journey mapping, data storytelling, process analysis, and workshops/interviews. The others are project management or quality methods." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.3 Foundational Techniques",
  question: "A business analyst presents dashboard data to executives, adding a short narrative about what changed, why it matters and what decision it supports. Which foundational technique is this?",
  options: ["Process analysis", "Data storytelling", "Backlog management", "Customer journey mapping"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Data storytelling uses narrative to add business context to data and highlight the insights that support better decisions." },

/* --- Change --- */
{ id: "E-CHG-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "What does a change strategy describe?",
  options: ["The detailed design of the new solution and how it will be built", "How the enterprise will get from where it is today to the desired future state", "The list of stakeholders affected by the change", "The organization’s process for requesting, assessing and approving changes"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A change strategy is the plan for moving from where the enterprise is now to where it wants to be, often in intermediate steps. Handling individual change requests is a governance topic instead." },
{ id: "E-CHG-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A new system will go live region by region over a year. The business analyst defines what the business will look like after each regional release. What are these intermediate states called?",
  options: ["Future states", "Current states", "Transition states", "Solution scopes"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Transition states are the intermediate states between current and future state. Defining them is part of the change strategy and release planning." },
{ id: "E-CHG-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Before recommending how to roll out a major change, a business analyst checks whether the organization has the capacity, culture and resources to absorb it. What is this activity?",
  options: ["Gap analysis of capabilities", "Stakeholder mapping and analysis", "Root cause analysis", "Enterprise readiness assessment"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "An enterprise readiness assessment looks at whether the organization is ready and able to make and sustain the change: capacity, culture, resources and operational impact." },
{ id: "E-CHG-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance · BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "Midway through an initiative, a manager asks for a new feature. What should the business analyst do FIRST?",
  options: ["Assess it through the agreed change control process, including impact", "Add the feature to scope, since the manager is a key stakeholder and it is small", "Reject it, because scope is already approved", "Ask the developers to estimate it and start building it if it fits the plan"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Changes should go through the agreed change control process: capture the request, analyze its impact on value, cost, schedule and other requirements, and route it to whoever has authority to decide." },
{ id: "E-CHG-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A business analyst compares what the organization can do today with what the future state requires, to work out exactly what has to change. Which technique or activity is this?",
  options: ["SWOT analysis", "Gap analysis", "Benchmarking", "Lessons learned"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Gap analysis compares current-state capabilities with future-state needs to identify the gaps the change must close. SWOT and benchmarking support current-state analysis but do not by themselves identify the gaps." },
{ id: "E-CHG-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy · BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "Warehouse staff are resisting a new picking process because they fear it will lead to job cuts. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Ask the sponsor to make the process mandatory and explain that no jobs will be cut", "Note the resistance but carry on, because the process has already been approved by the sponsor", "Understand their concerns and reflect them in the change strategy, e.g. communication and training", "Pause the change until every member of staff agrees with the new process, so nobody feels forced into it"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Resistance is information about stakeholders and context. Understanding concerns and addressing them through engagement, communication and transition activities makes the change more likely to deliver value. Forcing it or waiting for unanimous agreement are both poor responses." },
{ id: "E-CHG-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "Which statement about change is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["Change involves new technology in some form, even when the main change is to a process or a policy", "Change happens within formal projects that have a defined scope and budget", "A change is complete once the solution has been delivered and accepted", "Change is driven by a need and can involve process, people, policy and technology"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Change is a transformation driven by a need. It can combine processes, organization, policies and technology, can happen outside projects, and is only worthwhile if it creates value." },

/* --- Need --- */
{ id: "E-NEED-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "In the BACCM, a need is:",
  options: ["A problem to solve or an opportunity to take", "A requirement approved by the sponsor", "A feature requested by a user", "A measurable target set out in the business case"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A need is a problem to solve or an opportunity to take. Requirements and features describe ways of meeting a need; they are not the need itself." },
{ id: "E-NEED-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A department head asks for “a new weekly sales report.” What should the business analyst do FIRST?",
  options: ["Draft the report layout with the department head to confirm the content", "Ask the data team whether the data needed for the report is available", "Understand the underlying need, such as the decisions the report supports", "Check whether an existing reporting tool can already produce the report, and build it if not"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A request is often a proposed solution. Understanding the underlying need first, such as the decisions to be made and the problem behind them, may show that a different or simpler solution delivers more value." },
{ id: "E-NEED-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis",
  question: "Deliveries are often late. The business analyst repeatedly asks “why?” and finds that orders are released late because credit checks are done by hand once a day. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Brainstorming possible causes with the team", "The five whys", "Process benchmarking against other companies", "Mind mapping the delivery process"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Asking “why” repeatedly to move from a symptom (late deliveries) to an underlying cause (daily manual credit checks) is the five whys, a root cause analysis technique." },
{ id: "E-NEED-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "“Reduce customer onboarding time by 30% within 12 months.” What kind of requirement is this?",
  options: ["Functional requirement", "Stakeholder requirement", "Transition requirement", "Business requirement"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Business requirements state the goals, objectives and outcomes that explain why a change is being made, from the enterprise’s point of view. This is a measurable business objective." },
{ id: "E-NEED-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Which statement BEST expresses a business need?",
  options: ["Online order abandonment rose from 8% to 19% after the checkout changes, reducing monthly revenue", "Our web servers are overloaded at peak times and need upgrading before the next season", "We need a new checkout system, because the current one is outdated and hard to use", "Customers complain that checkout is slow, so the checkout system’s performance must be improved quickly"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A good statement of need describes the problem or opportunity and its impact, with evidence, without prescribing a solution. The other options are proposed solutions or vague symptoms." },
{ id: "E-NEED-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model · BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A competitor leaves the local market, and the business analyst points out that the company could pick up its customers. How does this relate to the concept of need?",
  options: ["It is not a need, because nothing is broken", "It is a need: needs can be opportunities as well as problems", "It is a risk to manage, because competitor moves are external threats", "It is a strategy question for the executives, not a business need"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Needs are problems or opportunities. A market opportunity is a legitimate need that can drive change even when nothing is currently going wrong." },
{ id: "E-NEED-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Sales says the need is “a faster CRM.” Customer service says it is “fewer missed callbacks.” Data shows 40% of callbacks are missed because requests are logged in two places. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Recommend a faster CRM, since it would help both teams log and find callback requests quicker", "Treat these as two separate needs and start a small initiative for each department", "Ask the sponsor to decide which department’s need takes priority", "Use the evidence to frame one shared need and confirm it with both groups"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholders often describe needs through their own solution or symptom. The analyst should use evidence to frame the underlying need and confirm that shared understanding with both groups before looking at solutions." },

/* --- Solution --- */
{ id: "E-SOL-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "In the BACCM, what is a solution?",
  options: ["A new or changed software system that meets the approved requirements", "A particular way of meeting one or more needs in a given context", "The product delivered by the development team at the end", "The set of approved requirements and designs for a change"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A solution is a particular way of meeting one or more needs in a given setting. It may include software, but also processes, policies, organizational changes or a mix of these." },
{ id: "E-SOL-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "When defining design options, a business analyst considers building a custom system, buying a packaged product, or configuring a package and building a few custom parts. What are these?",
  options: ["Risk responses for each option", "Transition states between current and future state", "Solution approaches: create, purchase or a combination", "Prioritization categories such as Must, Should and Could"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Solution approaches describe whether components will be created, purchased or a combination of both. Each design option uses one of these approaches." },
{ id: "E-SOL-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A new expense tool has been live for three months. The business analyst checks whether it is delivering the expected reduction in processing time. Which activity is this?",
  options: ["Measuring solution performance", "Defining the future state", "Eliciting requirements", "Planning stakeholder engagement"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Solution evaluation includes defining and collecting measures to see whether a solution is delivering the value expected of it." },
{ id: "E-SOL-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "A new scheduling system works as designed, but nurses keep using paper rotas because managers reward the old way of working. What kind of limitation is this?",
  options: ["A solution limitation", "A design defect", "A requirements traceability gap", "An enterprise limitation"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The solution works; the problem lies in the organization around it (incentives and culture). That is an enterprise limitation, not a solution limitation." },
{ id: "E-SOL-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "An old system now costs more to run and maintain than the value it delivers, and a newer platform already covers most of its functions. What is a reasonable recommendation?",
  options: ["Keep it running, because a large amount was originally invested in building it", "Retire the solution and move the remaining functions to the newer platform", "Add new features to it so that it delivers more value for its running cost", "Do nothing, since it still works"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Retiring a solution is a valid recommendation when ongoing cost exceeds value, especially if a replacement exists. The original investment is a sunk cost and should not drive the decision." },
{ id: "E-SOL-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.36 Prototyping",
  question: "A business analyst wants users to react to a proposed screen flow before anything is built. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Prototyping", "Financial analysis", "Data mining", "Benchmarking"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Prototyping gives stakeholders an early, concrete model of the solution so they can give feedback before build effort is spent." },
{ id: "E-SOL-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "A business analyst recommends a revised approval policy plus staff training, with no new software. A stakeholder says this “isn’t a real solution.” Which response is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["The stakeholder is right; a solution needs a technology component to count", "It is a workaround, useful until software that automates the approval can be bought and put in place", "It is a valid solution: a solution can be any way of satisfying a need, including policy and process", "It is a set of transition requirements, since training and policy changes support a solution"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Solutions are not limited to technology. Policy, process, organizational and training changes can satisfy a need, often at lower cost and risk." },

/* --- Stakeholder --- */
{ id: "E-STK-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model · BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Which is the BEST definition of a stakeholder?",
  options: ["Anyone who funds or sponsors the initiative and benefits from it", "A member of the project or delivery team who is working directly on the change", "A manager who approves requirements or decisions about the change", "Anyone with a link to a change, a need or a solution"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholders are people or groups linked to a change, need or solution. That includes those affected by it, not only those who fund or approve the work." },
{ id: "E-STK-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Stakeholder analysis shows that the head of finance has high influence and is sceptical about the initiative. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Send her the final requirements for information, since she is not a direct user", "Avoid involving her until the requirements are approved, to limit early objections", "Escalate her objections to the sponsor for a quick decision", "Engage her early to understand her concerns and involve her appropriately"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Influential stakeholders with negative attitudes can block a change. Engaging them early, understanding their concerns and planning their involvement reduces risk and often improves the solution." },
{ id: "E-STK-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "On a stakeholder matrix, a stakeholder has high influence over the change, but the change has little impact on them. How should the business analyst engage them?",
  options: ["Work closely with them as a key player", "Make sure they remain satisfied", "Keep them informed, since they may feel a lack of control", "Monitor them in case their influence or interest changes"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK’s stakeholder matrix maps influence against impact. High influence, low impact: make sure they remain satisfied. High influence, high impact: work closely with them as key players. Low influence, high impact: keep them informed. Low on both: monitor." },
{ id: "E-STK-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "In a RACI matrix, what does the “A” stand for?",
  options: ["Assigned: the person who does the work on the task", "Advised: someone consulted before a decision", "Accountable: the one person who owns the outcome", "Aware: someone informed after the decision"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "RACI stands for Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed. Accountable is the single person who ultimately owns the decision or outcome." },
{ id: "E-STK-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "A team needs a shared picture of typical users, such as “Maria, a first-time buyer who shops on her phone,” to guide design decisions. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Organizational modelling", "Roles and permissions matrix", "Stakeholder map", "Personas"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Personas are fictional but realistic characters that represent groups of users and their goals, behaviours and pain points. They help teams design with real users in mind." },
{ id: "E-STK-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "A key subject matter expert keeps sending a junior colleague to workshops, and that colleague cannot answer most questions. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Carry on with the junior colleague and record the unanswered points as assumptions to check later", "Talk to the expert, and their manager if needed, about the impact and agree the time needed", "Cancel the workshops until the expert attends", "Use document analysis instead and leave the expert out"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Managing stakeholder collaboration includes agreeing commitments and acting when engagement falls short. Explaining the impact and renegotiating the commitment, escalating if necessary, deals with the cause instead of working around it." },
{ id: "E-STK-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement · BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "Executives want a one-page summary; developers want detailed acceptance criteria. What does this tell the business analyst?",
  options: ["Stakeholders have different communication needs, so tailor the information to each", "One standard format should be used for everyone, so that nobody gets inconsistent information", "Developers should work from the executive summary and ask questions when they need detail", "Executives should review the detailed criteria so they understand what they are approving"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Stakeholders differ in what they need, how much detail they want and which format works for them. Planning and tailoring communication to each audience is part of stakeholder engagement." },

/* --- Value --- */
{ id: "E-VAL-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model · BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Which example describes intangible value?",
  options: ["Cutting printing and postage costs by €20,000 a year", "Processing 15% more orders per day", "Improved employee morale after a process change", "Reducing invoice errors from 4% to 1% within six months"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Intangible value is hard to measure directly, such as morale, reputation or goodwill. The other options are tangible and measurable." },
{ id: "E-VAL-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "General practice, not spelled out in BABOK; closest: BABOK v3 10.33 Prioritization",
  question: "When using MoSCoW prioritization, what does a “Must” requirement mean?",
  options: ["It would be nice to have, and is included if time and budget allow", "It has been agreed for a future release", "It is important, but a workaround exists if it is not delivered", "The solution would fail or not be acceptable without it"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Must means the requirement is essential: the solution would not be viable or acceptable without it. Should is important but has a workaround, Could is desirable, and Won’t is agreed as out of scope this time. Note: MoSCoW is widely used, but BABOK only mentions it in the agile perspective; its own grouping technique uses categories such as high, medium and low." },
{ id: "E-VAL-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State · BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Why should a business analyst help define how value will be measured before a solution is built?",
  options: ["So there is a baseline and agreed measures to judge later whether value was delivered", "Because measures are required before any requirement can be written", "So the project manager can track progress and report on schedule performance", "To make sure all stakeholders agree on the design before any development work begins on it"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Measures and a baseline agreed early make it possible to evaluate the solution later. Without them, nobody can show whether value was actually delivered." },
{ id: "E-VAL-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model · BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "A change will cut operating costs by 12%, but customer service expects more complaints because a support channel is being removed. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Report the cost saving as the main benefit, since it is measurable and the complaints are only expected", "Assess the value for each affected stakeholder group and make the trade-off visible", "Keep the support channel, since customer value should outweigh cost", "Ask customer service to plan for the extra complaints, since the cost saving is agreed"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Value depends on the stakeholder: a change can add value for some and reduce it for others. The analyst should make the gains and losses visible so decision makers can weigh them." },
{ id: "E-VAL-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases",
  question: "What is the main purpose of a business case?",
  options: ["To describe the solution design in enough detail for the budget to be set", "To identify the stakeholders, their roles and their decision rights for the initiative", "To justify a course of action by weighing benefits against costs and risks", "To record that the requirements have been approved for delivery"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A business case gives the justification for a change: the need, the options, and the expected value against costs and risks, so a decision can be made." },
{ id: "E-VAL-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "While defining the future state, a business analyst estimates the benefits the change could bring if it succeeds. What is this estimate called, and why does it matter?",
  options: ["Actual value; it proves the solution is working as intended", "Sunk cost; it shows what has been spent", "Residual risk; it shows how much risk remains after mitigation is applied", "Potential value; it helps decide whether the change is worth pursuing"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Potential value is the value that could be realised by reaching the future state. Comparing it with expected costs and risks helps decide whether, and how, to proceed." },
{ id: "E-VAL-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "A team delivers the highest-value items first and keeps re-checking what is most valuable as it learns. Which principle does this reflect?",
  options: ["Analyze to determine what is valuable", "See the whole", "Avoid waste", "Understand what is doable"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Analyze to determine what is valuable means continuously assessing and prioritizing work so the most value is delivered as understanding grows." },

/* --- Context --- */
{ id: "E-CTX-01", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "In the BACCM, what is context?",
  options: ["The scope statement that defines what is in and out of the change", "The schedule, budget and resources within which the change happens", "The technical environment in which the solution will run", "The circumstances that shape, are shaped by, and help explain the change"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Context is the surrounding set of circumstances that shapes the change, is shaped by it, and helps make sense of it: culture, regulation, technology, markets, history and so on." },
{ id: "E-CTX-02", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "During current-state analysis, a business analyst records that a new consumer-protection law takes effect next year. What is this?",
  options: ["An internal asset", "An external influencer", "A business rule", "A stakeholder requirement"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "External influencers are factors outside the organization that affect it, such as regulation, competitors, customers, suppliers, technology and the economy. A new law is a political/regulatory external influencer." },
{ id: "E-CTX-03", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "In a SWOT analysis, a strong, well-known brand is a(n):",
  options: ["Opportunity", "Threat", "Strength", "Weakness"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats are external. A strong brand is an internal asset, so it is a strength." },
{ id: "E-CTX-04", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State · BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "An organization is highly risk-averse and has a history of failed big-bang rollouts. How should this context MOST likely shape the change?",
  options: ["Favour smaller, incremental releases with early evidence of value", "Plan a single cutover backed by a detailed rollback plan", "Tighten change control so the scope cannot grow during the rollout", "Postpone all change until the culture changes"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Culture and history are part of context. A risk-averse organization with past failures is more likely to accept incremental change that builds confidence through early results." },
{ id: "E-CTX-05", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "“The new solution must use the company’s existing CRM license.” How should this be classified?",
  options: ["An assumption", "A risk", "A business objective", "A constraint"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A constraint limits the options for the solution. An assumption is something believed true but not confirmed, such as “all users have smartphones.”" },
{ id: "E-CTX-06", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "A process that worked well at head office fails when copied to a regional office with different local rules and working practices. What lesson about context does this show?",
  options: ["Regional staff need more training, because the process is proven and the problem must be how it is used", "The same solution can deliver different value in a different context, so analyze it before reusing", "The process was poorly designed from the start, and the head-office results were a lucky exception", "Head office should mandate the process, since standard processes across sites reduce cost and risk"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Value and suitability depend on context. Before reusing a solution elsewhere, analyze the new context (rules, culture, practices) and adapt the solution if needed." },
{ id: "E-CTX-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Which of these is an external influencer a business analyst might consider when analyzing the current state?",
  options: ["The organization’s internal reporting structure and spans of control", "The skills of the existing IT team", "Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates and inflation", "The company’s internal policies"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Macroeconomic factors are external influencers. Organizational structure, staff skills and internal policies are internal elements of the current state." },

/* ========================= CCBA & CBAP ========================= */
/* --- Business Analysis Planning and Monitoring (BAPM) --- */
{ id: "C-BAPM-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "A sponsor wants to launch a new digital service quickly. The market is changing, stakeholders expect to learn as they go, and the team will release every two weeks. How should the business analyst plan the business analysis approach?",
  options: ["Use a predictive approach so all requirements are approved before development starts", "Use an adaptive approach, planning analysis iteratively and elaborating requirements just in time", "Delay detailed planning until the requirements stabilize, then plan the full analysis", "Use the approach that worked on the last project, since the team already knows it"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "When to use each approach depends on uncertainty and how often change is expected. With high uncertainty and frequent releases, an adaptive approach lets analysis happen in small, iterative pieces, close to when the work is delivered." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-02", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "A bank is replacing its anti-money-laundering monitoring system. Regulators will audit how requirements were defined and approved. Which consideration should MOST influence the business analysis approach?",
  options: ["The team’s preference for informal documentation, so analysis can move faster", "The need for formal, well-documented deliverables and approvals", "Reducing the number of stakeholders involved, so approvals are quicker to obtain", "The availability of subject matter experts"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Formality and level of detail of deliverables are part of planning the business analysis approach. Regulatory scrutiny calls for more formality, documented decisions and traceable approvals." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "While planning stakeholder engagement, a business analyst needs to know who can approve requirements and who can only recommend. Which part of stakeholder analysis covers this?",
  options: ["Attitudes", "Decision-making authority", "Communication preferences", "Power and influence"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Stakeholder analysis looks at roles, attitudes, decision-making authority, and level of power or influence. Decision-making authority identifies who can approve or veto, which also feeds governance planning." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "The VP of Operations has a lot of influence and openly doubts that the initiative is needed. The sponsor asks the business analyst how to handle her. What is the BEST approach?",
  options: ["Limit her involvement to the final review, so her doubts don’t slow down elicitation", "Ask the CEO to confirm the initiative’s priority to her, so she knows she is expected to cooperate", "Plan frequent, tailored engagement, understand her concerns early, and involve her in decisions", "Send her regular status reports and the approved requirements, so she stays informed"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Influential stakeholders with negative attitudes need closer, tailored collaboration. Understanding their concerns early surfaces risks and requirements and builds support; excluding them usually makes resistance worse." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-05", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Halfway through an initiative, nobody knows who can authorize a change to approved requirements, and changes are being made informally. Which planning output should have covered this?",
  options: ["Governance approach", "Stakeholder engagement approach", "Information management approach", "Elicitation activity plan"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The governance approach covers decision making, the change control process (including who authorizes changes), the prioritization approach and the approval plan." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Team members can’t find the latest version of the requirements, and some stakeholders are editing files they should only be reading. Which part of business analysis planning was MOST likely skipped?",
  options: ["Planning the elicitation logistics, such as tools and shared workspaces", "Planning business analysis governance, including who may change requirements", "Planning storage and access, as part of information management", "Identifying business analysis performance improvements"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Planning business analysis information management covers how information is organized, the level of abstraction, traceability, reuse, storage and access, and which attributes to keep." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "A medical-device software project must show that every safety requirement is covered by design and tests. Where should the business analyst decide how much traceability to maintain?",
  options: ["When verifying the safety requirements before sign-off", "When planning business analysis information management", "When conducting elicitation", "When planning the business analysis governance approach"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The traceability approach is planned as part of business analysis information management. It is based on complexity, domain, regulation and risk; a regulated safety domain needs more rigorous traceability." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Over three iterations, much of the business analyst’s work has been reworked because stakeholders found gaps late in reviews. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Ask for more analysis time in future iterations so that the requirements are complete before review", "Baseline the requirements after each review and route later gaps to change control", "Raise the late feedback in the retrospective so stakeholders change their behaviour", "Assess the BA work’s performance, find the causes of rework, and recommend corrective or preventive actions"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Identifying business analysis performance improvements involves analyzing performance against measures, finding root causes and recommending actions such as preventive, corrective or improvement actions." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "What is the main output of planning stakeholder engagement?",
  options: ["Stakeholder engagement approach", "Elicitation results", "Governance approach", "Requirements architecture"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Plan Stakeholder Engagement produces the stakeholder engagement approach: the stakeholder list, how each will collaborate, and their communication needs." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "A business analyst decides that each requirement will record its source, priority, status, owner and stability. When is this decision typically made?",
  options: ["When validating the requirements with stakeholders before approval", "When defining the requirements architecture and views", "When planning business analysis information management", "When the first requirements are formally approved"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Requirements attributes (such as source, priority, complexity, ownership, stability, status and urgency) are chosen when planning business analysis information management, so information is captured consistently from the start." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-11", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Marketing and sales keep arguing about which requirements matter most, and there is no agreed way to settle it. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Let the project manager decide each case, since they balance scope against schedule", "Prioritize using the analyst’s own judgement, since the analyst is neutral", "Have each team rank its own requirements, then merge the two lists into one backlog", "Agree a prioritization approach (criteria, process, who decides) in the governance approach"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Planning the prioritization approach (criteria, process, participants and decision rights) is part of planning business analysis governance. An agreed approach turns repeated arguments into a repeatable decision process." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Key stakeholders are spread across Singapore, Frankfurt and Toronto, and some prefer written to spoken communication in English. What should the business analyst do when planning stakeholder engagement?",
  options: ["Use the standard communication plan, since good practice is the same for any team", "Hold all meetings in the sponsor’s time zone so decisions can be made quickly", "Rely on written email updates, since some stakeholders prefer written English", "Account for geography, time zones, language and culture in collaboration and communication plans"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholder communication needs include geography, culture, language, timing and preferred formats. Planning for them up front makes collaboration across locations more effective." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-13", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Executives need a high-level view of the requirements, while developers need detailed specifications. What should the business analyst plan for?",
  options: ["Producing one detailed specification that both groups can use", "Different levels of abstraction for different audiences", "Validating the detailed specifications with the executives", "A traceability matrix for each audience"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The level of abstraction is planned as part of information management: information is presented at the level of detail that suits each stakeholder group." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-14", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "A new initiative involves 40 stakeholder groups, 12 system interfaces and a fixed regulatory deadline. How should this affect the business analysis approach?",
  options: ["More complexity and risk call for more planning, more formality and more analysis effort", "Complexity affects delivery planning but not how business analysis is approached", "The approach should be as light as possible to save time", "The project manager handles complexity, so the business analysis approach can stay standard"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Complexity and risk are explicit inputs to the business analysis approach. Many stakeholders, interfaces and a fixed deadline increase risk, so the approach needs more careful planning, coordination and often more formality." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.19 Estimation",
  question: "A business analyst estimates the effort to analyze a module: optimistic 4 days, most likely 6 days, pessimistic 14 days. Using a three-point (PERT) estimate, what is the expected effort?",
  options: ["6 days", "7 days", "8 days", "9 days"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "PERT expected value = (O + 4M + P) / 6 = (4 + 24 + 14) / 6 = 42 / 6 = 7 days." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-16", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "In an organization where every decision needs written sign-off from department heads, which part of governance planning should the business analyst pay MOST attention to?",
  options: ["Requirements reuse across the department’s initiatives", "Elicitation logistics", "The plan for approvals: who approves, when and how", "Business analysis performance measures for the sign-off process"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Planning for approvals covers the type of requirements to be approved, the timing, the process and who approves. It should reflect the organization’s culture and formality, such as mandatory written sign-off." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.27 Lessons Learned · BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Which technique is MOST useful for collecting feedback on how well business analysis work went at the end of a release?",
  options: ["Lessons learned", "Data modelling", "Vendor assessment", "Functional decomposition"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Lessons learned sessions gather what went well and what didn’t, and they feed directly into identifying business analysis performance improvements." },
{ id: "C-BAPM-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "The same privacy regulations apply to five product lines that will each be changed over the next two years. How should the business analyst plan for this?",
  options: ["Write separate privacy requirements for each product line, since each will be changed at a different time", "Let each product team raise privacy needs when its own work starts, to avoid analysis that may go stale", "Ask the legal department to write and own the privacy requirements for each product line, since they interpret the regulations", "Plan to write the privacy requirements once, at a reusable level, and store them where all teams can find them"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Planning for requirements reuse identifies requirements that can be used across initiatives, such as regulatory ones, and decides how they will be written and stored so others can find and reuse them." },

/* --- Elicitation and Collaboration (EC) --- */
{ id: "C-EC-01", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "What is the output of preparing for elicitation?",
  options: ["Elicitation results (unconfirmed)", "Elicitation activity plan", "Stakeholder engagement approach", "Requirements (verified)"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Prepare for Elicitation produces the elicitation activity plan: activities, techniques, logistics, participants and supporting material. Unconfirmed results come from Conduct Elicitation." },
{ id: "C-EC-02", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.31 Observation",
  question: "Claims handlers struggle to describe how they process a complex claim; they say they “just know what to do.” Which elicitation technique is MOST appropriate?",
  options: ["Survey", "Document analysis", "Observation", "Focus group"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Observation suits work that people find hard to put into words, including tacit knowledge, workarounds and exceptions. Watching handlers work shows what interviews and surveys would miss." },
{ id: "C-EC-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.45 Survey or Questionnaire",
  question: "A retailer wants feedback from 8,000 store employees in 14 countries about a proposed scheduling tool, and needs results it can compare across regions. Which technique is BEST?",
  options: ["Survey or questionnaire", "One-to-one interviews", "Workshops in each store", "Observation"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Surveys collect information from large, dispersed groups efficiently and give structured, comparable data. Interviews, workshops or observation would not scale to 8,000 people." },
{ id: "C-EC-04", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops",
  question: "Five department heads describe the order-approval process differently, and a decision is needed within a week. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Interview each head separately and go with the most senior head’s view", "Send a survey to all five", "Analyze the current procedure documents and adopt the version they describe", "Run a facilitated workshop with all five to reconcile their views"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Workshops bring key stakeholders together to collaborate, resolve differences and reach decisions in a short time. Separate interviews would keep the conflict going." },
{ id: "C-EC-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.18 Document Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst needs to understand the business rules in a legacy payroll system that has detailed user manuals but whose original designers have left. Which technique should the analyst start with?",
  options: ["Brainstorming", "Document analysis", "Focus groups", "Collaborative games"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Document analysis reviews existing materials to elicit information. It is particularly useful when the people who knew the system are gone but documentation remains." },
{ id: "C-EC-06", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "A week before a requirements workshop, the business analyst sends participants the agenda, objectives, the current process model and questions to think about. Which part of elicitation work is this?",
  options: ["Preparing stakeholders, as part of Prepare for Elicitation", "Capturing elicitation outcomes before the workshop takes place", "Communicating business analysis information to the participants", "Confirming elicitation results with participants in advance"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Preparing for elicitation includes understanding scope, selecting techniques, setting up logistics, securing supporting material and preparing stakeholders, so they know what is expected and can contribute well." },
{ id: "C-EC-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation · BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "After a series of interviews, a business analyst has detailed notes. What should happen to this information before it is used as a basis for requirements?",
  options: ["Have the sponsor approve it, so it becomes the agreed baseline for the requirements", "Trace it to test cases so every point can be checked during testing", "Confirm it by checking it against source information and other elicitation results", "Prioritize it with stakeholders so the most important points are analyzed first"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Conduct Elicitation produces unconfirmed results. Confirm Elicitation Results checks them for accuracy and consistency against the source and other results before they are treated as reliable." },
{ id: "C-EC-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "In separate interviews, the finance manager says invoices over €10,000 need two approvals, but the procurement lead says the threshold is €5,000. What should the business analyst do NEXT?",
  options: ["Use €5,000, because the stricter control is the safer assumption until someone objects", "Record both values as requirements and let the team configure the threshold later", "Use €10,000, because finance owns invoice approval and has the authority to decide", "Treat it as an inconsistency: check the approval policy and follow up with both stakeholders"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Confirming elicitation results means comparing them with source information and with other results. Inconsistencies should be resolved, for example by checking the policy and following up, not settled by guesswork." },
{ id: "C-EC-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation",
  question: "Nobody knows whether customers would use a proposed self-service feature. The team builds a simple version and runs it with a small customer group to see what happens. Which type of elicitation is this?",
  options: ["Collaborative", "Research", "Experiments", "Interviews"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Elicitation can be collaborative, research-based or experimental. Experiments (proofs of concept, prototypes, A/B tests, observational studies) produce information that cannot be found any other way." },
{ id: "C-EC-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation",
  question: "Which pair of techniques is MOST associated with research-based elicitation?",
  options: ["Workshops and focus groups", "Document analysis and data mining", "Brainstorming and collaborative games", "Interviews and observation"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Research-based elicitation systematically discovers and studies information from materials or sources not directly provided by stakeholders in a session, for example documents and data." },
{ id: "C-EC-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "A business analyst must tell the steering committee about a scope trade-off that needs a decision at Friday’s meeting. Which format is MOST appropriate?",
  options: ["A short presentation that focuses on the decision, the options and their impacts", "The full 80-page requirements specification, so the committee can see the whole picture", "An informal conversation with the committee chair before the meeting", "The workshop notes, so the committee sees the stakeholders’ own words"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "When communicating business analysis information, the analyst picks the objective and format that suit the audience. A decision-focused presentation fits a steering committee that has to decide." },
{ id: "C-EC-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "A key stakeholder has stopped replying to review requests and has missed two sign-off meetings. Delivery depends on her input. What should the business analyst do FIRST?",
  options: ["Escalate to the sponsor right away, since delivery depends on her input", "Treat her silence as approval, since she had time to respond", "Speak with her to understand why and agree how she can take part", "Ask her manager to nominate someone else who can review on her behalf"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Managing stakeholder collaboration includes monitoring engagement and acting when it drops. The first step is to understand why and agree a way forward with the stakeholder; escalation comes later if that fails. Silence is never approval." },
{ id: "C-EC-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "Before elicitation starts, a business analyst agrees with line managers that each subject matter expert will spend four hours a week on the initiative for six weeks. What is the analyst doing?",
  options: ["Preparing supporting material, by booking the experts’ time before sessions are scheduled", "Defining the stakeholder communication needs for the elicitation phase", "Planning approvals, by confirming which managers sign off the experts’ involvement", "Gaining agreement on commitments, as part of managing stakeholder collaboration"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Gaining agreement on commitments means getting stakeholders (and their managers) to agree on the time and resources needed for business analysis activities, so participation is planned rather than hoped for." },
{ id: "C-EC-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.25 Interviews",
  question: "A business analyst is exploring a new business area and does not yet know which questions matter. Which kind of interview is MOST suitable?",
  options: ["Closed-question interview", "Structured interview with a fixed list of questions", "Survey", "Unstructured interview"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Unstructured interviews let the conversation follow what emerges and suit early exploration. Structured interviews use predefined questions and suit situations where the information needed is already understood." },
{ id: "C-EC-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.21 Focus Groups",
  question: "A company wants to learn how customers feel about a new subscription concept before investing in it. It wants a discussion where participants react to one another’s views. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Data mining", "Focus group", "Document analysis", "Interface analysis"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A focus group is a moderated discussion with pre-qualified participants. It draws out attitudes, perceptions and reactions, and participants build on each other’s comments." },
{ id: "C-EC-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.5 Brainstorming",
  question: "During a brainstorming session, a senior manager keeps criticising ideas as soon as they are suggested and participants have gone quiet. What should the business analyst as facilitator do?",
  options: ["Restate the session’s objective and time limit, then carry on as before", "End the session and collect ideas anonymously with a survey instead", "Remind the group that ideas are judged later, not while they are being collected", "Ask the manager to explain each objection so the group can refine the idea"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Brainstorming works by producing many ideas without immediate judgement; evaluation is a separate step. The facilitator should enforce that rule so everyone feels able to contribute." },
{ id: "C-EC-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.10 Collaborative Games",
  question: "Stakeholders find it hard to agree on what matters most to customers. The business analyst asks small teams to design the box a product would be sold in, listing its top selling points. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Collaborative games", "Mind mapping", "Survey", "Benchmarking"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Collaborative games, such as product box, use game-like exercises to encourage shared understanding and creative thinking about a problem or solution." },
{ id: "C-EC-18", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.36 Prototyping",
  question: "In an early workshop, a business analyst sketches screens on paper with users to explore what information they need. The sketches will not be reused in development. What kind of prototype is this?",
  options: ["Evolutionary prototype", "Functional prototype", "Proof of principle", "Throw-away prototype"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A throw-away prototype is a quick, low-cost model used to clarify needs and then discarded. An evolutionary prototype is refined over time until it becomes part of the final solution." },
{ id: "C-EC-19", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.31 Observation",
  question: "A business analyst sits with a loan officer for a morning, watching without interrupting, and keeps her questions until the end. What type of observation is this?",
  options: ["Active (visible) observation", "Passive (invisible) observation", "Participatory observation", "Simulation"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "In passive observation the analyst watches without interrupting and asks questions afterwards. In active observation the analyst asks questions or takes part while the work is being done." },
{ id: "C-EC-20", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.14 Data Mining",
  question: "A business analyst wants to find out which customer behaviours come before a subscription is cancelled, using two years of transaction data. Which technique is MOST appropriate?",
  options: ["Data mining", "Interviews", "Focus groups", "Process modelling"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Data mining analyzes large amounts of data to find patterns and relationships, such as behaviours that predict cancellation, which stakeholders might not know about." },
{ id: "C-EC-21", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops",
  question: "In a workshop, two vocal participants dominate while four others say almost nothing. What should the facilitating business analyst do?",
  options: ["Ask the sponsor to join the next session to encourage wider input", "Close the workshop and interview the quiet participants later", "Ask the two vocal participants, in front of the group, to let others speak first", "Use structured techniques such as round-robin or silent writing so everyone contributes"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Facilitation means making sure all relevant views are heard. Structured techniques balance participation without singling anyone out and give better, more representative results." },
{ id: "C-EC-22", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Which is part of setting up logistics when preparing for elicitation?",
  options: ["Getting the requirements approved by the sponsor before the sessions", "Assessing the organization’s risk tolerance for the elicitation approach", "Arranging the participants, location, time, communication channels and tools", "Defining the solution scope so participants know exactly what is in and out"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Setting up logistics covers the practical arrangements: who takes part, where and when, the communication channels, materials and tools." },
{ id: "C-EC-23", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "A business analyst is asked to elicit requirements for a new returns process. Before selecting techniques, what should the analyst establish?",
  options: ["The scope of elicitation: the business domain, sources and stakeholders involved", "The proposed solution design, so the questions can focus on gaps in it", "The traceability approach for the returns requirements", "The acceptance and evaluation criteria for the returns solution"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Understanding the scope of elicitation (domain, stakeholders, sources and the information needed) comes first in preparing for elicitation, because it drives which techniques are appropriate." },
{ id: "C-EC-24", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops",
  question: "In a workshop, which role is mainly responsible for recording decisions, open issues and action items?",
  options: ["Sponsor", "Scribe", "Timekeeper", "Facilitator"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The scribe records the decisions, issues and actions so the facilitator can concentrate on guiding the session." },
{ id: "C-EC-25", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "A business analyst sends the same detailed process model to operations staff and to the CFO. The CFO says it is unreadable. What did the analyst MOST likely miss?",
  options: ["Getting the model approved by operations before sharing it with the CFO", "Using a professional modelling tool and notation instead of a simple drawing tool", "Determining the objectives and format of communication for each audience", "Tracing the model to the financial requirements the CFO cares about"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Communicating business analysis information starts with deciding what each audience needs and in what format. A CFO usually needs a summary of impacts and decisions, not the full process model." },
{ id: "C-EC-26", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation · BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "After confirming elicitation results, the business analyst finds that nobody has described how refunds work for international orders. What should happen?",
  options: ["Assume refunds work the same way as for domestic orders and note it in the specification", "Plan further elicitation to fill the gap, because elicitation is iterative", "Raise a change request to add international refunds", "Let testers find the gap during testing, when real international orders can be used"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Elicitation and collaboration is iterative. Gaps found when confirming results lead to more elicitation; undocumented assumptions or silent scope changes create risk." },

/* --- Requirements Life Cycle Management (RLCM) --- */
{ id: "C-RLCM-01", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "A business analyst links each requirement to the test cases that will show it has been met. Which traceability relationship is this?",
  options: ["Derive", "Depends", "Validate", "Satisfy"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The four relationships are derive, depends, satisfy and validate. Validate links a requirement to a test or other element that can determine whether the solution fulfils it." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Requirement R2 (send shipping notifications by SMS) only makes sense if requirement R1 (capture customers’ mobile numbers) is also implemented. What kind of relationship is this?",
  options: ["Depends: effort", "Depends: necessity", "Derive", "Satisfy"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A necessity link means one requirement is pointless without the other. An effort link means one requirement is simpler to build when the other is built too." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "A business requirement to “reduce manual data entry by 50%” is broken down into several solution requirements for automated document capture. Which relationship links them?",
  options: ["Validate", "Depends", "Satisfy", "Derive"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Derive is used when a requirement is developed from another, such as solution requirements derived from a business requirement." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Which relationship connects a solution component, such as a payment module, to the requirement it fulfils?",
  options: ["Satisfy", "Derive", "Validate", "Depends"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Satisfy is the relationship between an implementation element, such as a component or design, and the requirement it fulfils." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "A regulator changes a reporting rule. Thanks to maintained traceability, the business analyst lists the affected requirements, designs and tests within an hour. Which benefit of traceability does this show?",
  options: ["It removes the need for a separate change control process", "It makes impact analysis faster and more complete", "It confirms that the changed rule meets the regulator’s intent", "It replaces requirements verification"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Traceability supports impact analysis, scope and coverage checks and release decisions. It doesn’t remove change control or verification; it makes them faster and more reliable." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-06", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "Some requirements in the repository still show an old priority and an outdated status, even though decisions changed weeks ago. Which task addresses this?",
  options: ["Approve Requirements", "Trace Requirements", "Maintain Requirements", "Specify and Model Requirements"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Maintain Requirements keeps requirements and their attributes accurate and current over time, and makes them available for reuse." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-07", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A requirement has low business benefit, but the solution cannot launch legally without it. How should it be prioritized?",
  options: ["Low, because benefit is the main criterion", "Medium, balancing its low benefit against its legal importance", "Let the legal team set its priority, since compliance is outside business analysis", "High, because regulatory compliance can override other criteria"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Prioritization can be based on benefit, penalty, cost, risk, dependencies, time sensitivity, stability and regulatory or policy compliance. Mandatory compliance often makes a requirement non-negotiable." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-08", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements · BABOK v3 10.33 Prioritization",
  question: "Asked to prioritize 60 requirements, every stakeholder marks every item “high.” What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Accept the ratings, then plan delivery by effort so the quickest items are finished first", "Use a technique that forces trade-offs, such as a points budget, and facilitate the discussion", "Rank them by the seniority of the stakeholder who raised each one, to reflect authority", "Ask the project manager to set the order, since they own the schedule, the resources and the delivery plan"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Stakeholders resisting trade-offs is a common prioritization challenge. Techniques that force distinctions, such as a 100-point budget, timeboxing or capped MoSCoW categories, combined with facilitation produce a usable order." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-09", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A promotional feature is only valuable if it is live before the holiday season starts in eight weeks. Which prioritization factor applies MOST directly?",
  options: ["Time sensitivity", "Business benefit", "Penalty", "Cost"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Time sensitivity covers requirements whose value declines or disappears if they are not delivered by a certain time." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Stakeholders still disagree on how a requirement should work and expect it to keep changing. How might this affect its priority?",
  options: ["It should be implemented first, so that the debate is settled by working software", "Instability has no effect on priority, which depends only on business value", "Its low stability may justify deferring it until it is better understood", "Mark it medium until stakeholders agree"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Stability is a prioritization factor: requirements likely to change may be given lower priority to avoid rework, while work focuses on stable, well-understood items." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Requirement X has high value but cannot be delivered until the data platform upgrade (requirement Y) is complete. What should the business analyst consider when prioritizing?",
  options: ["Prioritize X first, because its value is higher, and schedule Y afterwards", "Treat the dependency as a scheduling matter for the project manager, not a prioritization factor", "Leave Y out, because it has no direct business value", "Account for the dependency: Y may need to come first so X can deliver value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Dependencies are a basis for prioritization. A low-value enabler may need a higher priority because higher-value requirements depend on it." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "A stakeholder asks to add multi-currency support after requirements were approved. When assessing the change, which set of factors should the business analyst consider?",
  options: ["Benefit, cost, impact, schedule and urgency", "The cost of the change and the requester’s seniority", "Whether the developers have time in the current sprint", "Only whether it was in the original scope"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Impact analysis for a requirements change looks at benefit, cost, impact (on the solution, stakeholders and other requirements), schedule and urgency, so decision makers understand the trade-off." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "An agile team gets a change to a story that has not started yet. How formal should the change assessment usually be?",
  options: ["Fully formal, with a written impact analysis and a change board review, to protect the iteration goal", "Proportionate to the approach and governance; in adaptive work this is often just re-prioritizing the backlog", "None, because in agile a change to a story that hasn’t started is accepted as it is", "The same as in a predictive project, so that all changes are treated consistently and are auditable"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The formality of change assessment depends on the business analysis approach, governance, the stage of work and the risk. In adaptive approaches, changes to items not yet started are often handled by backlog refinement." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes · BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "A business analyst has completed the impact analysis for a change request. Who decides whether the change is approved?",
  options: ["The business analyst, who knows the requirements best", "The project manager, who owns the plan", "Whoever has that authority under the governance approach", "The stakeholder who requested it, since they understand its value"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The business analyst analyzes and recommends; decisions are made by whoever the governance approach gives authority to, such as the sponsor, a change control board or the product owner." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-15", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "A change to the discount rules is approved after the related feature is half built. What should the business analyst make sure happens next?",
  options: ["The developers are told at the next stand-up so they can adjust the work in progress", "The original requirement is replaced in the repository so only the current version remains", "Nothing further, because approval through change control completes the process", "The decision is recorded and communicated, and affected requirements, designs and traces are updated"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Impact resolution includes documenting and communicating the decision and updating affected requirements, designs and traceability so everyone works from the same current information." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Which activity is part of Approve Requirements?",
  options: ["Gaining consensus among stakeholders and tracking and communicating approval decisions", "Writing acceptance criteria that approvers will use to judge each requirement", "Verifying that requirements are complete and consistent before sign-off", "Prioritizing the requirements so that approvers can focus on the critical ones"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Approve Requirements covers understanding stakeholder roles, managing conflicts and issues, gaining consensus, and tracking and communicating approval decisions." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-17", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "The sponsor wants to approve a set of requirements, but the head of operations, a key SME without approval authority, disagrees strongly with one of them. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Get the sponsor’s approval, since the head of operations has no approval authority", "Facilitate a discussion of the concern, make sure the sponsor hears it, and record the decision", "Remove the disputed requirement for now and revisit it after the others are approved", "Ask the head of operations to raise the issue with the CEO, who has authority over both"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Approval involves understanding who has authority, managing conflicts and issues, and seeking consensus. The analyst should bring the concern into the open so the authorized decision maker decides with full information, and record the outcome." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "General practice, not spelled out in BABOK; closest: BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Scoring requirements on value and cost gives: R1 (value 8, cost 4), R2 (value 9, cost 6), R3 (value 5, cost 2), R4 (value 6, cost 5). Using the value-to-cost ratio, which should come first?",
  options: ["R1", "R2", "R3", "R4"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Value/cost ratios: R1 = 2.0, R2 = 1.5, R3 = 2.5, R4 = 1.2. R3 has the highest ratio, so it comes first on this basis." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-19", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "In an adaptive approach, how is prioritization usually handled?",
  options: ["It is done once, at the start of the project, then frozen", "The sponsor prioritizes the backlog once, at the start of each release", "The delivery team ranks the backlog by technical dependencies", "It is ongoing: the backlog is re-prioritized as the team learns"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Prioritization is ongoing. In adaptive approaches the backlog is refined and re-prioritized regularly as new information, feedback and changes come in." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-20", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "The team wants to reuse a set of customer identity verification requirements from a previous project. What makes requirements MOST suitable for reuse?",
  options: ["They are at the right level of abstraction, current, and stored where others can find them", "They were approved by a senior executive and passed a formal quality review", "They are copied word for word into the new specification, so the original approval still applies", "They include the previous project’s design details, so the solution can be rebuilt quickly"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Maintaining requirements for reuse means keeping them accurate, at a level of abstraction that applies beyond one solution, and organized so they can be found. Embedded design details make reuse harder." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-21", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A requirement is tagged with a high “penalty” score. What does that mean?",
  options: ["It will be costly to implement, so delivering it carries a high financial penalty", "Not implementing it has negative consequences, such as fines or lost customers", "It is unstable and likely to change, so building it early risks rework", "It depends on other requirements, so delaying it holds up related work"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Penalty describes the consequences of not implementing a requirement, such as regulatory fines, lost customers or reputational harm. It is different from benefit, cost and stability." },
{ id: "C-RLCM-22", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements · BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "A team only traces business requirements to the releases that deliver them, and skips the detailed links between solution requirements and tests. When is this level of traceability appropriate?",
  options: ["It is never acceptable; requirements are traced to every artifact on every initiative", "Once the requirements are approved and baselined", "When the traceability approach agreed it and it fits the initiative’s risk and regulation", "In adaptive approaches, where detailed traceability is replaced by the backlog"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The level of formality in traceability is planned and should match the context. Low-risk, unregulated work may need only coarse traceability; safety-critical or regulated work needs more detail. Over-tracing adds cost without value." },

/* --- Strategy Analysis (SA) --- */
{ id: "C-SA-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "The CEO says: “Customer retention is falling. Fix it.” What should the business analyst do FIRST?",
  options: ["Recommend a CRM upgrade, since better customer data is the usual way to improve retention", "Define the future state and retention targets so the team knows what success looks like", "Analyze the current state to understand what is driving falling retention", "Build a business case for a retention program so funding is secured early"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Analyze Current State comes first: understand why change is needed, what is driving the problem and the current capabilities and context. Recommending a CRM jumps to a solution without understanding the need." },
{ id: "C-SA-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "While analyzing the current state, a business analyst studies the organization’s structure, culture, processes, technology, policies and outside influences. Which output does this analysis mainly produce?",
  options: ["Current state description and business requirements", "Design options and a recommended solution approach", "Solution performance measures and analysis", "A future state description and business objectives"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Analyze Current State produces the current state description and business requirements, the problem or opportunity statement that explains why change is needed." },
{ id: "C-SA-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "Which business objective is BEST written?",
  options: ["Improve customer service significantly across all channels and regions over the coming year or so", "Become the highest-rated insurer in the region for customer satisfaction", "Implement a new call center system with improved routing by the end of Q3", "Increase first-contact resolution in the call center from 62% to 75% by the end of next year"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Business objectives should be SMART: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound. The first two are vague goals, and the third describes a solution, not an outcome." },
{ id: "C-SA-04", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "When defining the future state, the business analyst learns that any solution must run on the existing cloud platform and must be live before a contract expires in 11 months. How should these be handled?",
  options: ["Record them as constraints, because they limit the solution space", "Record them as assumptions to confirm later", "Defer them to design, when the technical options are known", "Treat them as business objectives, since they set deadlines"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Constraints limit the options available to meet the need. Identifying them when defining the future state shapes the solution scope and the design options considered." },
{ id: "C-SA-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "The future state assumes that 60% of customers will switch to self-service within a year, but nobody has tested this. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Keep the figure as stated, because the sponsor provided it and owns the business case", "Record it as an assumption, treat it as a risk, and test it early if possible", "Replace it with a more conservative 40%, so the business case is safer", "Remove it from the future state, since unverified figures don’t belong in a business case"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Assumptions made when defining the future state carry uncertainty. They should be stated explicitly and assessed as risks, and testing them early, for example with a pilot, reduces the risk to value." },
{ id: "C-SA-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "When defining the future state, why does the business analyst estimate potential value?",
  options: ["To set the project budget, which should not exceed the potential value", "To evaluate vendor proposals against the value each one promises", "To judge whether the future state is worth its cost and risk", "To provide the baseline against which solution performance is measured"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Potential value is part of the future state definition. It lets stakeholders judge whether the change is worth pursuing before investing in a solution." },
{ id: "C-SA-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "A hospital board is strongly risk-averse. Two options exist: X has higher expected value but much more uncertainty; Y has lower but more predictable value. Which is the board MOST likely to prefer?",
  options: ["Option X, because higher expected value is the rational choice for any board", "Neither option until the uncertainty in option X has been fully removed", "Both options in parallel, so that the risk is spread evenly across them", "Option Y, because a risk-averse board accepts lower value for more certainty"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Risk tolerance (risk-averse, neutral or seeking) affects which options are acceptable. Risk-averse organizations favour predictable outcomes even at a lower expected value." },
{ id: "C-SA-08", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.38 Risk Analysis and Management",
  question: "To deal with the risk of a vendor failing to deliver on time, the company adds penalty clauses and a performance bond to the contract. Which risk response is this?",
  options: ["Avoid", "Transfer", "Accept", "Mitigate"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Transfer moves some or all of the risk’s consequences to another party, here the vendor through contract terms. Mitigate reduces probability or impact, avoid removes the source, and accept takes no action beyond monitoring." },
{ id: "C-SA-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A business analyst compares three ways to reach the future state (a big-bang rollout, a phased rollout by region, and a pilot followed by a phased rollout), looking at timing, risk and readiness, and recommends one. Which task is this?",
  options: ["Define Change Strategy", "Define Future State", "Define Design Options", "Plan Business Analysis Approach"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Defining the change strategy means developing and comparing different routes to the future state, including interim states and release planning, and recommending the best one." },
{ id: "C-SA-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Finance is going through an ERP upgrade, an office move and a restructuring this year. A new budgeting process is planned for the same department. What should the business analyst’s change strategy take into account?",
  options: ["Nothing extra, because each change has its own sponsor, plan and change strategy", "Mainly the technical complexity of the new budgeting process and its systems", "Enterprise readiness: the department’s capacity to absorb another change", "Mainly the sponsor’s preferred date, aligned with the start of the budgeting cycle"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An enterprise readiness assessment looks at capacity, culture and competing changes. Several concurrent changes reduce readiness and may call for a different timing or phasing." },
{ id: "C-SA-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Which output of Define Change Strategy defines the boundaries of the solution that will meet the need?",
  options: ["Business objectives", "Solution scope", "Current state description", "Risk analysis results"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Define Change Strategy produces the change strategy and the solution scope. The solution scope sets out the capabilities the solution will include." },
{ id: "C-SA-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.6 Business Capability Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst wants to show executives which business capabilities underperform and are strategically important, to focus investment. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Data dictionary", "Sequence diagrams of key processes", "State modelling", "Business capability analysis"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Business capability analysis describes what the organization does and assesses each capability’s performance and importance, which highlights where to focus change." },
{ id: "C-SA-13", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.4 Benchmarking and Market Analysis",
  question: "Leadership wants to know how its loan approval times compare with those of other banks and what practices the best performers use. Which technique is MOST appropriate?",
  options: ["Root cause analysis of approval delays", "Mind mapping of best practices", "Process modelling of approvals", "Benchmarking and market analysis"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Benchmarking compares the organization’s performance and practices with peers or industry best practice; market analysis looks at customers, competitors and trends." },
{ id: "C-SA-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis",
  question: "A team uses a fishbone diagram to group possible causes of shipping errors into categories such as people, process, equipment and materials. What should the business analyst remember about the result?",
  options: ["The category with the most causes listed is the most likely root cause", "Fishbone diagrams suit manufacturing better, so a five whys would be more reliable here", "It shows possible causes, which still need checking against data before acting", "Once the team agrees on the diagram, the main causes can be addressed directly"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Root cause analysis techniques such as the fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram structure the search for possible causes, but those causes have to be tested against evidence before the root cause is accepted." },
{ id: "C-SA-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.8 Business Model Canvas",
  question: "Executives want a one-page view of how a proposed new service would create, deliver and capture value, covering customer segments, channels, key partners, cost structure and revenue streams. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Business model canvas", "Balanced scorecard", "Use case diagram", "Decision table"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The business model canvas describes how an organization creates, delivers and captures value across nine building blocks, including customer segments, channels, partners, costs and revenues." },
{ id: "C-SA-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "A proposal costs €300,000 upfront and is expected to deliver €100,000 in net benefits each year for five years. Ignoring discounting, what are the payback period and the simple ROI over five years?",
  options: ["3 years; 66.7%", "3 years; 166.7%", "5 years; 66.7%", "2.5 years; 100%"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Payback = €300,000 / €100,000 per year = 3 years. Total benefit over five years is €500,000, so simple ROI = (500,000 − 300,000) / 300,000 = 66.7%." },
{ id: "C-SA-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "General practice, not spelled out in BABOK; closest: BABOK v3 10.16 Decision Analysis",
  question: "Option X has a 60% chance of a €200,000 gain and a 40% chance of a €50,000 loss. Option Y is a guaranteed €90,000 gain. Based only on expected monetary value, which is better?",
  options: ["Option Y, €90,000 versus €80,000", "Option X, €100,000 versus €90,000", "They are equal, at €90,000 each in value", "Option X, €120,000 versus €90,000"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "EMV(X) = 0.6 × 200,000 + 0.4 × (−50,000) = 120,000 − 20,000 = €100,000. That beats Y’s €90,000, although a risk-averse organization might still prefer Y." },
{ id: "C-SA-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "In a SWOT analysis for a regional bank, a new fintech competitor entering the market is a(n):",
  options: ["Strength", "Weakness", "Opportunity", "Threat"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Opportunities and threats are external; strengths and weaknesses are internal. A new competitor is an external factor that could hurt the bank, so it is a threat." },

/* --- Requirements Analysis and Design Definition (RADD) --- */
{ id: "C-RADD-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "A data flow diagram belongs to which category of requirements models?",
  options: ["People and roles", "Rationale", "Activity flow", "Data and information"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The model categories are people and roles, rationale, activity flow, capability, and data and information. Data flow diagrams, data models, data dictionaries, glossaries and state models are data and information models." },
{ id: "C-RADD-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.17 Decision Modelling",
  question: "An insurance pricing rule depends on six conditions, such as age band, region, vehicle type and claims history, with many combinations. Which representation is MOST effective?",
  options: ["A decision table", "A process flow with gateways", "A sequence diagram", "An organizational chart"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Decision modelling with decision tables or trees is well suited to complex rules with many combinations. It makes missing or conflicting combinations easy to spot." },
{ id: "C-RADD-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.44 State Modelling",
  question: "A business analyst needs to show how an order moves through statuses (created, paid, shipped, returned, cancelled) and which events trigger each transition. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Process modelling", "State modelling", "Functional decomposition", "Data dictionary"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "State modelling shows the states an entity can be in, the transitions between them and the events that cause each transition." },
{ id: "C-RADD-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.42 Sequence Diagrams",
  question: "A team needs to understand the order in which messages pass between the mobile app, the payment gateway and the bank when a payment is authorized. Which model is MOST appropriate?",
  options: ["Context diagram", "Business model canvas", "Sequence diagram", "Organizational model"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Sequence diagrams show how objects or systems interact over time and the order in which messages are exchanged." },
{ id: "C-RADD-05", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.47 Use Cases and Scenarios",
  question: "In a use case for “Withdraw cash,” the business analyst describes what happens if the card is rejected or the account balance is too low. What are these?",
  options: ["Alternative or exception flows", "Preconditions of the use case", "Postconditions of the use case", "Business rules for the use case"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Use cases describe a main success flow plus alternative and exception flows for variations and errors. Preconditions must be true before the use case starts; postconditions describe the state afterwards." },
{ id: "C-RADD-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.48 User Stories",
  question: "A team writes: “As a returning customer, I want to save my card details so that I can check out faster.” What is MOST needed to make this story ready for development?",
  options: ["A data flow diagram showing where card details are stored", "Sign-off from the payments product manager", "A sequence diagram for the checkout flow", "Acceptance criteria that define when the story is done"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "User stories are usually paired with acceptance criteria, which set out the conditions that must be met for the story to be accepted and make it testable." },
{ id: "C-RADD-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "“The system shall be fast and user-friendly.” Which quality characteristics does this requirement MOST clearly fail?",
  options: ["Atomic and prioritized", "Unambiguous and testable", "Feasible and concise", "Consistent and complete"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "“Fast” and “user-friendly” mean different things to different people and cannot be measured, so the requirement is ambiguous and untestable. A better version gives measurable targets." },
{ id: "C-RADD-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "“The system shall allow users to register, reset passwords, and export their data in CSV and PDF.” Which quality characteristic does this requirement fail?",
  options: ["Feasible", "Prioritized", "Atomic", "Understandable"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An atomic requirement is self-contained and covers one thing. This one bundles several capabilities, so they can’t be prioritized, traced or tested independently." },
{ id: "C-RADD-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "A business analyst reviews requirements with a checklist to make sure they are consistent, unambiguous, correctly structured and free of conflicts. Which task is this?",
  options: ["Verify Requirements", "Validate Requirements", "Approve Requirements", "Confirm Elicitation Results"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Verification checks that requirements and designs are well formed and of sufficient quality. Validation checks that they align with business goals and deliver value." },
{ id: "C-RADD-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.3 Validate Requirements",
  question: "A well-written requirement asks for a detailed internal analytics dashboard, but it does not support any business objective in the current solution scope. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Keep it, because it passed verification and is well written, clear and testable", "Mark it as approved, since requirements that meet the quality criteria can proceed", "Raise it as a validation issue: it may be out of scope, so stakeholders should decide", "Pass it to the developers as a quick win, since it is small and users have asked for it"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Validate Requirements checks that each requirement supports the business need and fits the solution scope. A requirement can be well written (verified) and still not deliver value (not valid)." },
{ id: "C-RADD-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.3 Validate Requirements",
  question: "When validating requirements, a business analyst defines how the team will know, after implementation, whether a requirement delivered its intended benefit. What is this element?",
  options: ["Defining measurable evaluation criteria", "Defining the solution scope for the requirement", "Creating the traceability matrix for the requirement", "Planning approvals"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Validate Requirements includes identifying assumptions, defining measurable evaluation criteria and evaluating alignment with the solution scope. The criteria make it possible to evaluate value later." },
{ id: "C-RADD-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "A business analyst organizes requirements into views (process, data, rules, interfaces) so each stakeholder group can review the parts relevant to them, and to check that the set as a whole is complete. Which task is this?",
  options: ["Specify and Model Requirements", "Maintain Requirements for reuse", "Define Design Options", "Define Requirements Architecture"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Define Requirements Architecture structures requirements into viewpoints and views, relates them to one another and checks that the whole set is complete and consistent." },
{ id: "C-RADD-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "When checking relationships in a requirements architecture, the business analyst makes sure each relationship is necessary, correct and unambiguous. What is the fourth quality criterion?",
  options: ["Approved", "Consistent", "Prioritized", "Bidirectionally traced"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK lists four quality criteria for requirements relationships: necessary, correct, unambiguous and consistent, meaning relationships are described the same way, using the standard descriptions defined in the viewpoints." },
{ id: "C-RADD-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "A team is choosing between building a custom booking engine, buying a SaaS product, or configuring a SaaS product with some custom extensions. In BABOK terms, what are these?",
  options: ["Transition states between the current and the future state", "Requirements viewpoints for different stakeholders", "Risk responses: avoid, transfer or accept", "Solution approaches: create, purchase or a combination"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Define Design Options includes defining solution approaches: whether components will be created, purchased or a combination of both." },
{ id: "C-RADD-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "While defining design options, a business analyst notices that automating a manual reconciliation step would also let staff spend more time on customer queries. What is the analyst identifying?",
  options: ["An improvement opportunity, such as increased efficiency", "A transition requirement for moving staff onto customer queries", "A business rule about how reconciliation is done", "A constraint on how the solution can be designed"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Identifying improvement opportunities, such as increasing efficiency, improving access to information or finding additional capabilities, is part of defining design options." },
{ id: "C-RADD-16", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "A business analyst decides which requirements will be met by the new software, which by a revised manual procedure, and which will be delivered in release 2. What is this called?",
  options: ["Requirements validation", "Requirements allocation", "Requirements approval", "Requirements elicitation"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Requirements allocation assigns requirements to solution components and releases so that value is delivered as intended." },
{ id: "C-RADD-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "After analyzing three design options, the business analyst finds that none of them delivers enough value to justify its cost and risk. What should the analyst recommend?",
  options: ["The cheapest option, because it limits the loss if the expected value does not materialize", "The option the sponsor prefers, since the sponsor owns the business case and the risk", "A combination of the three options, taking the strongest features of each to raise the overall value", "Not to pursue any of them for now, and explain why"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution may lead to recommending that no option be pursued if none delivers enough value. The recommendation is based on value, not on preference or price alone." },
{ id: "C-RADD-18", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.30 Non-Functional Requirements Analysis",
  question: "“The portal shall be available 99.9% of the time during business hours.” What kind of requirement is this?",
  options: ["Functional requirement", "Business requirement", "Non-functional requirement", "Transition requirement"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Non-functional requirements describe qualities or conditions the solution must meet, such as availability, performance, security and usability. Availability is a classic example." },
{ id: "C-RADD-19", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.9 Business Rules Analysis",
  question: "“A customer is classed as Gold when their spending in the last 12 months exceeds €5,000.” What type of business rule is this?",
  options: ["Definitional rule", "Behavioural rule", "Functional requirement", "Process step"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Definitional rules define or classify something, here what makes a customer Gold. Behavioural rules govern actions, for example “an order must not ship before payment is confirmed.”" },
{ id: "C-RADD-20", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.9 Business Rules Analysis",
  question: "Discount rules change every quarter, but they are currently embedded in the process model and the code. What should the business analyst recommend?",
  options: ["Add the discount rules to the glossary so every team uses the same definitions", "Document the rules in more detail within the process model so changes are easier to find", "Move ownership of the rules to the development team, since they are implemented in code", "Manage the rules separately from the process and code so they can change independently"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Business rules analysis recommends stating and managing rules separately from the processes and systems that enforce them, so that frequent rule changes don’t force process or system redesign." },
{ id: "C-RADD-21", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.12 Data Dictionary",
  question: "Two systems define “customer ID” differently: one as an 8-digit number and the other as alphanumeric. Which technique helps create one agreed definition, with format and allowed values?",
  options: ["Mind mapping", "Data dictionary", "Process modelling", "SWOT analysis"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A data dictionary holds standard definitions of data elements, their meanings, formats and allowed values, so everyone uses them consistently." },
{ id: "C-RADD-22", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.23 Glossary",
  question: "Stakeholders use “client,” “customer” and “account holder” to mean slightly different things. Which technique MOST directly addresses this?",
  options: ["Glossary", "Sequence diagram", "Prototyping", "Estimation"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A glossary defines key terms so stakeholders share an understanding and terminology is consistent." },
{ id: "C-RADD-23", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.11 Concept Modelling",
  question: "Business stakeholders want to agree the key business concepts (Policy, Claim, Insured Party, Coverage) and how they relate, without discussing database design. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Physical data model", "Interface analysis", "Concept modelling", "State modelling"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Concept modelling organizes business vocabulary and the relationships between concepts, independent of any data or system design. It suits business-facing discussions." },
{ id: "C-RADD-24", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.15 Data Modelling",
  question: "In a logical data model, one customer can place many orders and each order belongs to exactly one customer. What does this describe?",
  options: ["An attribute of the Customer entity that records how many orders exist", "A behavioural business rule that should be modelled outside the data model", "A state transition between the Customer and Order entities", "The cardinality of the relationship between Customer and Order"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Cardinality describes how many instances of one entity can relate to instances of another, here one-to-many between Customer and Order." },
{ id: "C-RADD-25", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.22 Functional Decomposition",
  question: "A business analyst breaks a large capability, “manage customer accounts,” into smaller functions, and those into sub-functions, to make the scope easier to understand and estimate. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Functional decomposition", "Root cause analysis", "Benchmarking", "Data mining"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Functional decomposition breaks complex processes, functions or deliverables into smaller parts that are easier to analyze, estimate and allocate." },
{ id: "C-RADD-26", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.35 Process Modelling",
  question: "A business analyst draws a process model with swimlanes for customer, sales, credit control and warehouse, and notices that an order changes hands six times. What is this MOST useful for?",
  options: ["Defining the data formats passed between the four departments", "Identifying hand-offs, delays and bottlenecks that could be improved", "Assessing workload and satisfaction in each department", "Defining which roles may approve each step of the order"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Process models with swimlanes show who does what and in what order, which exposes hand-offs, delays, duplicated work and other improvement opportunities." },
{ id: "C-RADD-27", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.24 Interface Analysis",
  question: "A new CRM must exchange data with billing, the website and a partner portal. Integration surprises have derailed past projects. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Leave integration to the developers, since interfaces are a technical design matter", "Write the interface requirements during testing, once the actual data is known", "Rely on the CRM vendor’s standard connectors, which usually cover these systems", "Perform interface analysis early to identify each interface, what is exchanged, how and when"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Interface analysis identifies interfaces between solutions and components and defines what is exchanged. Doing it early reduces integration risk, delays and rework." },
{ id: "C-RADD-28", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.39 Roles and Permissions Matrix",
  question: "Managers can approve expenses up to €2,000, directors up to €20,000, and only the CFO can approve more. Which technique documents this clearly?",
  options: ["State model", "Roles and permissions matrix", "Data flow diagram", "Business model canvas"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A roles and permissions matrix shows which roles are allowed to do which actions, such as approval limits." },
{ id: "C-RADD-29", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "A business analyst is writing requirements for executives, process owners and developers. What should the analyst do about the level of abstraction?",
  options: ["Write everything at the most detailed level, so nothing is lost between audiences", "Write everything at a high level and leave the detail to developers", "Match the level of abstraction to each audience while keeping views consistent", "Produce models rather than text, since models suit every audience"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Implementing appropriate levels of abstraction means presenting requirements at a level of detail that suits the audience, from business requirements down to detailed solution requirements, while keeping them consistent." },
{ id: "C-RADD-30", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.1 Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria",
  question: "What is the difference between acceptance criteria and evaluation criteria?",
  options: ["Acceptance criteria are minimum conditions to accept; evaluation criteria compare options or assess value", "They are the same thing, named differently in predictive and adaptive approaches", "Acceptance criteria apply only to software; evaluation criteria apply only to business processes", "Evaluation criteria are pass/fail conditions; acceptance criteria are weighted scores used to compare options"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Acceptance criteria define the minimum requirements for acceptance (pass/fail). Evaluation criteria assess and compare solutions or options against a set of measures, often weighted." },
{ id: "C-RADD-31", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.49 Vendor Assessment",
  question: "Two SaaS vendors meet all functional requirements. What else should a vendor assessment MOST likely consider?",
  options: ["The total license and implementation price over five years, since functional fit is already equal", "The number of additional features each offers beyond the requirements, for future flexibility", "Which vendor the end users preferred in the demonstrations, because adoption drives value", "Non-functional factors such as financial stability, support, licensing terms, security and fit with the organization"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Vendor assessment looks at the vendor as well as the product: financial stability, reputation, support model, licensing, flexibility and organizational fit all affect long-term value and risk." },
{ id: "C-RADD-32", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.16 Decision Analysis",
  question: "Options are scored out of 10 on cost (weight 40%) and functionality (weight 60%). Vendor X: cost 6, functionality 9. Vendor Y: cost 9, functionality 6. Vendor Z: cost 8, functionality 8. Which scores highest?",
  options: ["Vendor X, 7.8", "Vendor Y, 7.2", "Vendor Z, 8.0", "Vendors X and Z tie"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "X = 0.4 × 6 + 0.6 × 9 = 2.4 + 5.4 = 7.8. Y = 0.4 × 9 + 0.6 × 6 = 3.6 + 3.6 = 7.2. Z = 0.4 × 8 + 0.6 × 8 = 3.2 + 4.8 = 8.0. Vendor Z scores highest." },
{ id: "C-RADD-33", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.37 Reviews",
  question: "A formal review with defined roles (moderator, author, reviewers), a checklist and a goal of finding defects in a requirements package is called a(n):",
  options: ["Walkthrough", "Desk check", "Inspection", "Pass-around"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An inspection is the most formal type of review, with defined roles, checklists and defect logging. Walkthroughs are author-led and less formal; desk checks and pass-arounds are individual and informal." },
{ id: "C-RADD-34", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.41 Scope Modelling · BABOK v3 10.13 Data Flow Diagrams",
  question: "A business analyst draws a diagram with the new system in the middle and the external entities around it (customers, payment provider, tax authority) with the main information flows. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Decision modelling of the information flows", "State modelling of the system", "Organizational modelling of the parties", "Scope modelling"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Scope models show the boundary of a change: what is inside and outside, and how it relates to external elements. In BABOK the context diagram itself appears as the top-level data flow diagram, which shows the whole system with its externals." },
{ id: "C-RADD-35", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.16 Decision Analysis · BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Security wants users to re-authenticate on every transaction; the business says this will drive customers away. The two requirements conflict. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Implement the security requirement, because security risks outweigh usability and protect the organization", "Implement the business requirement, because losing customers would destroy the value of the solution", "Analyze the conflict, set out options and impacts, such as risk-based authentication, and help the decision makers choose", "Record both as requirements and let the design team balance them in the solution"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "When requirements conflict, the analyst analyzes the trade-offs, proposes options and their impacts, and helps the stakeholders with decision authority resolve the conflict rather than picking a winner." },
{ id: "C-RADD-36", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "“The country field shall be a drop-down list sorted alphabetically.” How is this best classified?",
  options: ["Business requirement", "A design", "Stakeholder requirement", "Transition requirement"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "It describes how the solution looks or behaves at the interface level, a usable representation of a solution, so it is a design. The underlying requirement might be “users can specify their country of residence.”" },
{ id: "C-RADD-37", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "Why might a business analyst use a template architecture, a predefined set of viewpoints, when defining the requirements architecture?",
  options: ["To reduce modelling effort by reusing models from earlier initiatives", "To replace stakeholder reviews with a standard completeness checklist", "To make approval easier, since stakeholders already know the format", "To help ensure all relevant viewpoints are covered, so the set is complete"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Template architectures provide a standard set of viewpoints. Using one helps the analyst check that nothing important has been left out and that the architecture is complete." },
{ id: "C-RADD-38", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.36 Prototyping",
  question: "Users struggle to judge a new claims-entry workflow from written requirements. The business analyst builds a clickable mock-up and walks it through with them. What is the main benefit?",
  options: ["Users react to something concrete, which surfaces gaps and validates the design early", "It replaces written requirements, since the prototype becomes the specification", "It fixes the final design early, so the system will look exactly like the mock-up", "It reduces the testing needed later, since users have already approved the screens and the workflow"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Prototyping gives stakeholders a concrete model to react to, which brings out missing or misunderstood requirements and validates the design before build effort is committed." },

/* --- Solution Evaluation (SE) --- */
{ id: "C-SE-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A new returns process went live two months ago, but nobody agreed how its success would be measured. What should the business analyst do FIRST?",
  options: ["Assess the enterprise limitations that may block its value", "Work with stakeholders to define performance measures linked to the business objectives", "Re-validate the requirements against the original business need", "Start collecting every metric the system can produce, then decide which matter"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Measure Solution Performance starts with defining performance measures. If none exist, the analyst works with stakeholders to define measures tied to the expected value and business objectives." },
{ id: "C-SE-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "The IT team proposes measuring a new HR portal by page views. Before using this measure, what should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Use it, because it is easy to collect consistently and higher usage generally means higher value", "Replace it with system uptime and response times, which are more objective technical measures", "Validate it with stakeholders to confirm it reflects the value the portal was meant to deliver", "Ask the vendor for an industry benchmark of page views so the result can be compared with peers"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Performance measures should be validated with stakeholders to make sure they are aligned with business objectives. Page views may not show whether HR processes became faster or cheaper." },
{ id: "C-SE-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A business analyst plans to judge a new fraud model on a single week of data, collected during a public holiday. Which aspects of collecting performance measures are MOST at risk?",
  options: ["Volume or sample size, and frequency and timing", "Stakeholder attitudes and influence over the fraud model", "Traceability and reuse of the model’s performance measures", "Qualitative versus quantitative mix"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Collecting performance measures involves volume or sample size, frequency and timing, and currency. One unusual week is too small and not representative to support conclusions." },
{ id: "C-SE-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "Six months after launch, a business analyst compares the actual reduction in processing cost with the reduction promised in the business case and looks for trends over time. Which task is this?",
  options: ["Assess Solution Limitations", "Define Future State", "Validate Requirements", "Analyze Performance Measures"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Analyze Performance Measures compares actual performance with the desired value, looking at trends, accuracy, variances and the risks they indicate." },
{ id: "C-SE-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "A self-service kiosk regularly freezes when printing receipts because of a driver conflict, and customers queue at the counter instead. How should this be classified?",
  options: ["An enterprise limitation", "A solution limitation", "A stakeholder attitude problem", "A requirements traceability gap"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The problem lies inside the solution (a component defect or conflict), so it is a solution limitation. Enterprise limitations come from the organization around the solution, such as culture, structure or operations." },
{ id: "C-SE-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "A new performance-review tool works as designed, but managers keep using spreadsheets because their bonus targets reward speed over completeness. Which assessment should the business analyst carry out?",
  options: ["Assess enterprise limitations, including culture and incentive structures", "Assess solution limitations, because users are choosing spreadsheets over the tool’s features", "Re-verify the requirements, since users avoiding the tool suggests they were poorly specified", "Re-plan elicitation to find out which spreadsheet features the tool is missing"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Assess Enterprise Limitations looks at how culture, structure, incentives and operations stop a solution from delivering value. The tool works; the incentives undermine it." },
{ id: "C-SE-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Fixing a minor defect in a reporting tool would cost more than the value it would recover, and the tool is scheduled for replacement in six months. What is a reasonable recommendation?",
  options: ["Fix it immediately, because every defect must be fixed", "Replace the tool now", "Do nothing, because the cost of changing outweighs the benefit", "Reduce the complexity of its interfaces"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "“Do nothing” is a legitimate recommendation when the value of a change is lower than its cost or when other factors (here, an imminent replacement) make change unwise." },
{ id: "C-SE-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Two departments enter the same customer data into two different systems after a merger. Which recommendation to increase solution value fits BEST?",
  options: ["Add data-entry staff in both departments so the duplicate work no longer causes delays", "Retire both systems and buy a new platform that replaces them in one step", "Do nothing for now, because the duplication is a temporary side effect of the merger", "Eliminate the redundancy, for example by consolidating data entry into one system of record"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Eliminating redundancy is one of the recommended ways to increase solution value. Duplicate data entry wastes effort and causes inconsistencies." },
{ id: "C-SE-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Management hesitates to retire a legacy system “because we invested €4 million in it.” What should the business analyst point out?",
  options: ["The €4 million is a sunk cost; the decision should rest on future costs and benefits", "Retirement should wait until a replacement has been selected and fully funded", "Retirement is a decision for IT, so it isn’t part of the business analysis recommendation", "The system should be kept until its costs are fully recovered"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "When considering retirement, look at ongoing costs versus initial investment, opportunity cost, necessity and the cost of replacement. Money already spent cannot be recovered and should not drive the decision." },
{ id: "C-SE-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "When can solution evaluation take place?",
  options: ["Once the full solution is in production and has stable usage data", "Only at the end of a project, as part of benefits realization", "On any solution component that exists, including prototypes, pilots and releases", "When performance problems are reported by users or operations"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Solution evaluation can be done on any solution or solution component that exists in some form, including prototypes, proofs of concept, pilots, betas and released increments." },
{ id: "C-SE-11", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "Which statement about metrics and KPIs is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["Every metric collected for a solution is treated as a KPI", "A KPI is a metric chosen to track progress toward a strategic goal", "KPIs should be defined after go-live, once real usage data is available", "Metrics are qualitative; KPIs are quantitative"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Metrics quantify a level of performance. KPIs are the selected metrics that show progress towards strategic goals or objectives. Not every metric is important enough to be a KPI." },
{ id: "C-SE-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.3 Balanced Scorecard",
  question: "Leadership wants to evaluate a new service from more than a financial point of view. Which framework covers financial, customer, internal process, and learning and growth perspectives?",
  options: ["Business model canvas", "SWOT analysis", "PESTLE analysis", "Balanced scorecard"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The balanced scorecard measures performance from four perspectives: financial, customer, internal business process, and learning and growth." },
{ id: "C-SE-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "After a new call-routing solution goes live, average handling time falls by 20%, but repeat calls rise by 35%. What should the business analyst conclude?",
  options: ["The solution is a clear success, because the main target of handling time was met", "Analyze the measures together and investigate the cause before drawing a conclusion", "Recommend more agent training, since repeat calls usually mean wrong answers", "Roll back the solution, because a 35% rise in repeat calls outweighs the gain"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Analyzing performance measures means interpreting them together and checking their accuracy and the risks they show. A gain on one metric that causes a loss on another may mean value is not being delivered." },
{ id: "C-SE-14", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "Users report that a new order system sometimes loses discount codes. The business analyst wants to find the underlying reason before recommending changes. Which activity is this?",
  options: ["Investigating solution problems, for example with root cause analysis", "Enterprise culture assessment, to see whether staff are bypassing the codes", "Stakeholder impact analysis, to see which customers are affected", "Defining the future state for discount handling"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Assess Solution Limitations includes investigating solution problems, typically with root cause analysis, to find why the solution isn’t performing as expected." },

/* --- Case study: Harbourline Insurance --- */
{ id: "K-INS-1", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State", case: "CASE-INSURER",
  question: "The IT Director wants to start vendor demonstrations next week. What should the business analyst do FIRST?",
  options: ["Arrange the demonstrations, so the board gets a recommendation within the eight weeks", "Agree measurable business objectives and the future state, so options can be judged against them", "Write a request for proposal that lists the current system’s features and interfaces", "Ask the vendors which features competitors use to achieve five-day settlement"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Design options should be judged against agreed business objectives and the future state. Without them, demonstrations turn into feature comparisons instead of an assessment of value." },
{ id: "K-INS-2", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State · BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema", case: "CASE-INSURER",
  question: "The Head of Claims wants the new platform to copy the current screens so nobody needs retraining. What is the BEST response?",
  options: ["Agree, because user acceptance is the biggest risk and familiar screens are the quickest way to secure adoption", "Decline, and have the vendor design new screens from its best-practice templates so the claims team is not distracted", "Escalate to the COO as sponsor, since the disagreement affects scope, timelines and the board recommendation, and the sponsor is the one who can settle it", "Explain that copying the screens would keep inefficiencies such as re-keying; base requirements on needs, and cover retraining as a transition requirement"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Replicating the current state locks in known waste. The analyst should keep requirements focused on needs, treat the training concern as a legitimate transition requirement, and stay collaborative rather than refusing or escalating." },
{ id: "K-INS-3", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State · BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis", case: "CASE-INSURER",
  question: "The IT Director believes a new platform will close the gap with competitors. Based on the interview findings, what should the business analyst point out?",
  options: ["Waiting for repair estimates and manual fraud reviews may not be fixed by a new platform, so each cause’s share of the 14 days should be analyzed first", "A packaged platform will remove most of the delay, because an 18-year-old system is the main cause of slow, manual and error-prone claims handling", "Manual fraud review should be removed, so that the new platform can settle standard claims automatically within five days of the claim", "The interview findings are anecdotal, so the analysis should wait for industry data on typical settlement times and for the vendor’s benchmarks"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Current-state analysis and root cause thinking show that the cycle time has several contributors. Some are process, partner or policy issues that new technology alone won’t fix. Quantifying them prevents an expensive solution that misses the need." },
{ id: "K-INS-4", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement · BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance", case: "CASE-INSURER",
  question: "Whose approval should the business analyst make sure is planned for requirements related to the 48-hour regulatory reporting obligation?",
  options: ["The call center manager", "The senior claims handlers", "The Compliance Officer", "The IT Director"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Planning stakeholder engagement and approvals means identifying who holds authority over each area. Regulatory reporting requirements need the Compliance Officer, who represents the regulator’s obligations, to review and approve them." },
{ id: "K-INS-5", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance", case: "CASE-INSURER",
  question: "Which measure would BEST show, after go-live, whether the change delivers the value the board expects?",
  options: ["The number of platform features delivered against the requirements baseline, as evidence of scope delivered", "Whether the go-live date and budget were met, since the board asked for delivery within nine months", "The share of claims handlers trained on the new platform and using it daily by the end of the first month after go-live", "Average settlement time for standard motor claims compared with the 14-day baseline, alongside claims satisfaction"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Performance measures should reflect the business need: settlement time against the baseline and competitors, plus satisfaction. Features, screens and dates are project outputs, not evidence of value." },

/* --- Case study: City of Larkford permits --- */
{ id: "K-PER-1", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures", case: "CASE-PERMITS",
  question: "The business analyst compares 31% online submissions with the 70% target, and 27-day approvals with the 15-day target. Which task is the analyst performing?",
  options: ["Analyze Performance Measures", "Define Future State", "Verify Requirements", "Plan Business Analysis Governance"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Comparing actual performance with the expected value and looking at the size and causes of the variance is Analyze Performance Measures." },
{ id: "K-PER-2", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations", case: "CASE-PERMITS",
  question: "Planning officers print online applications to review them on paper because the review checklist and sign-off policy assume paper. How should the business analyst classify this?",
  options: ["A solution limitation, because the portal does not provide the review and sign-off features officers need", "A requirements traceability gap, because the checklist was never linked to the portal requirements", "An enterprise limitation: policy and working practice prevent the solution from delivering value", "A defect for the vendor to fix, since the portal should present applications in a printable review format"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The portal works; the organization’s policies and practices around it don’t fit digital review. That is an enterprise limitation, assessed through operational and culture assessment." },
{ id: "K-PER-3", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations", case: "CASE-PERMITS",
  question: "How should the upload time-outs for large drawings be classified?",
  options: ["An enterprise limitation", "A stakeholder engagement issue", "A business requirement", "A solution limitation"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The time-outs come from the portal’s own configuration, so they are a solution limitation. The vendor says it can be addressed with a configuration change." },
{ id: "K-PER-4", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value", case: "CASE-PERMITS",
  question: "What should the business analyst recommend to the city manager?",
  options: ["Replace the portal with another vendor’s product, because missing every target shows the solution itself cannot deliver the value", "Raise the upload limit, adapt the checklist and sign-off policy to digital review, start the consultation on officer duties, then measure again", "Make online submission mandatory from next month so that the 70% target is met and counter staff can be reduced", "Wait until the end of the first year before acting, because adoption of new public services usually takes time to build"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The recommendation should deal with both the solution limitation (upload limit) and the enterprise limitations (paper-based policy, staffing change), respect the union consultation constraint, and include re-measuring to confirm the effect." },
{ id: "K-PER-5", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes", case: "CASE-PERMITS",
  question: "Before the vendor raises the upload limit, what should the business analyst make sure happens?",
  options: ["It is assessed through the agreed change process (storage, performance, security) and approved by the right authority", "The vendor applies it straight away, because it is a configuration change that needs no development", "The change is postponed to the next major release, so it can be tested with other changes", "Ask applicants to compress their drawings before uploading, which avoids changing the portal configuration or the vendor contract at all"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Even a configuration change should go through the agreed process: assess its impacts and get it approved by the right authority. Small changes can have side effects on cost, performance or security." },

/* --- Case study: Fennick Retail loyalty app --- */
{ id: "K-LOY-1", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements", case: "CASE-LOYALTY",
  question: "Which prioritization is BEST supported by the case?",
  options: ["Personalized offers first, because the campaign date is fixed and marketing value is lost if it slips", "Points-liability fix and consent flow before personalized offers: compliance, penalty, and offers need consent", "All three in parallel, splitting the team so that each stakeholder sees progress this iteration", "The points-liability fix only, dropping personalized offers until the auditor closes the finding"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Regulatory and penalty factors (the audit finding) and dependencies (offers need consent) both argue for doing the liability fix and consent flow first. Time sensitivity matters, but offers without consent cannot launch anyway." },
{ id: "K-LOY-2", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements", case: "CASE-LOYALTY",
  question: "What traceability relationship exists between “personalized offers” and “explicit consent flow”?",
  options: ["Derive", "Depends: effort", "Depends: necessity", "Satisfy"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Personalized offers only make sense (and are only allowed) if the consent flow is implemented too. That is a necessity dependency." },
{ id: "K-LOY-3", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration", case: "CASE-LOYALTY",
  question: "What should the business analyst do about the store operations manager’s absence from refinement sessions?",
  options: ["Carry on without store operations, because the app is digital and stores are not directly in scope", "Ask the product owner to remove or defer store-related items until store operations takes part", "Treat the manager’s absence as agreement and send the decisions to store operations afterwards", "Contact the manager to find out why, explain the impact on store staff, and agree how they will give input"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Store staff are affected stakeholders. Managing stakeholder collaboration means acting when engagement drops: understand the reason, explain the impact and agree a workable way to collaborate." },
{ id: "K-LOY-4", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.1 Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria · BABOK v3 10.48 User Stories", case: "CASE-LOYALTY",
  question: "Most of the 140 backlog items have no acceptance criteria. What is the MOST effective way to deal with this?",
  options: ["Define acceptance criteria with the product owner for the top items as they are refined", "Write acceptance criteria for all 140 items before the next iteration", "Let the developers define done for each item, since they will build and test it", "Rewrite the items as use cases with scenarios"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "In an adaptive approach, items are elaborated just in time. Defining acceptance criteria for the items about to be worked on gives value quickly without wasting effort on items that may change or be dropped." },
{ id: "K-LOY-5", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance", case: "CASE-LOYALTY",
  question: "Stakeholders have been adding items to the backlog directly, without the product owner knowing. What is the underlying issue the business analyst should help fix?",
  options: ["Too many people have edit rights in the backlog tool, so access should be restricted to the team", "Stakeholders need training on writing user stories so their items meet the team’s standard", "Unclear governance: decision rights and the intake and prioritization process need agreeing", "The team should stop accepting new requests until the existing 140 items are cleared"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Who can add and prioritize work, and how requests are handled, are governance matters. Clarifying decision rights (for example, the product owner owns backlog priority) and the intake process fixes the cause, not the symptom." },
/* ===================== ECBA · batch 1 (Nov 2026) ===================== */
{ id: "E-UBA-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "A team releases a small improvement to its customer portal every two weeks and refines the next items with users before each release. Which horizon is this work mainly in?",
  options: ["Strategy horizon", "Initiative horizon", "Delivery horizon", "Portfolio horizon"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The delivery horizon covers building, releasing and refining solution increments. The strategy horizon is about organizational direction, and the initiative horizon about shaping a specific change." },
{ id: "E-UBA-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "A business analyst helps define the scope, objectives and expected outcomes of one specific change: replacing the company’s expense process. Which horizon is this?",
  options: ["Initiative horizon", "Delivery horizon", "Strategy horizon", "Operational horizon"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The initiative horizon is about a particular change: what it should achieve, its scope and how it contributes to strategy. Delivery is about building and releasing the solution in increments." },
{ id: "E-UBA-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "A team has designed a new booking app in detail, but when asked what problem it solves, nobody can give a clear answer. Using the BACCM, which concept is MOST clearly missing?",
  options: ["Solution", "Need", "Change", "Stakeholder"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The team has a solution but no clear need: the problem or opportunity it addresses. Without a need, there is no way to judge whether the solution delivers value." },
{ id: "E-UBA-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "A business analyst helps a team improve its daily routine for checking supplier invoices. At which level is this work mainly taking place?",
  options: ["Strategic", "Tactical", "Enterprise", "Operational"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Business analysis happens at strategic, tactical and operational levels. Improving a day-to-day routine is operational; deciding the organization’s direction is strategic." },
{ id: "E-UBA-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "Which statement about when value is created is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["Value appears once the whole initiative is finished and handed over", "Value can be created throughout an initiative, whenever benefits are delivered effectively", "Value is created only by strategic decisions, not by delivery work", "Value is created when the business case is approved"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Value is found throughout an initiative as benefits are delivered. Strategic decisions position the organization, and tactical delivery and ongoing improvement create value along the way." },
{ id: "E-UBA-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "A manager says business analysis is only needed at the start of a project, to collect requirements. Which response is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["BA supports work at every level, before, during and after delivery", "That is correct for predictive projects, but not for agile ones", "That is correct; after sign-off, the project manager takes over", "Business analysis is mainly needed after go-live, to measure benefits"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business analysis combines techniques and competencies to support work at every level and across the life of a change, from shaping strategy to evaluating results." },

{ id: "E-MIND-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst keeps asking why a long-standing approval step exists, even though everyone says “it has always been done this way.” Which shared value does this show MOST directly?",
  options: ["Respect", "Curiosity", "Value maximization", "Collaboration"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Curiosity means asking questions and seeking to understand. Questioning a step that “has always been done” is how hidden waste or outdated rules are found." },
{ id: "E-MIND-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis · BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "The project manager asks the business analyst to leave poor pilot results out of the steering committee pack “until we fix them.” The analyst includes them, with context. Which shared value is the analyst upholding?",
  options: ["Customer focus", "Improvement", "Ethics", "Adaptability"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Ethics means acting with integrity and honesty. Decision makers need accurate information, even when it is uncomfortable. Adaptability is a competency, not a shared value." },
{ id: "E-MIND-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "Before recommending a solution, a business analyst checks that it can be delivered within the budget, the deadline and the rules of the operating environment. Which principle is this?",
  options: ["Avoid waste", "See the whole", "Think as a customer", "Understand what is doable"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Understand what is doable means working out how to deliver within constraints, including budget, time, regulation and the operating environment." },
{ id: "E-MIND-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "After each release, the business analyst holds short feedback sessions with users and the team, and uses what they learn to adjust the next release. Which principle is this?",
  options: ["Get real using examples", "Analyze to determine what is valuable", "Understand what is doable", "Stimulate collaboration and continuous improvement"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "This principle is about involving stakeholders on an ongoing basis and using feedback to improve both the solution and the way the team works." },
{ id: "E-MIND-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.4 Foundational Competencies · BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "Without any formal authority, a business analyst gets four department heads to agree on a shared goal and guides them toward the outcome the initiative needs. Which foundational competency is this?",
  options: ["Facilitation", "Leadership and influencing", "Adaptability", "Systems thinking"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Leadership and influencing covers building agreement, proposing solutions and guiding stakeholders toward the intended value, often without positional authority." },
{ id: "E-MIND-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "hard", ref: "BA Standard 3.4 Foundational Competencies · BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "A retailer plans to extend its returns window from 14 to 60 days. The business analyst looks at how this would affect the warehouse, finance, fraud checks and customer service, not just the returns desk. Which competency is MOST evident?",
  options: ["Problem solving", "Facilitation", "Systems thinking", "Adaptability"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Systems thinking means understanding the whole enterprise and its context, including how a change in one place ripples into others, so the change delivers value overall." },

{ id: "E-IMPL-07", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "Which statement BEST describes the difference between a requirement and a design?",
  options: ["Requirements are written by the business; designs are written by IT", "Requirements describe the current state; designs describe the future", "A requirement represents a need; a design represents a solution", "Requirements are detailed specifications; designs are high-level sketches"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A requirement represents a need; a design represents a solution. Either can be high level or detailed, and anyone can write them." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "A tax authority is replacing a calculation engine. The rules are fixed by law, needs are well understood, and every requirement must be formally approved before build. Which approach fits BEST?",
  options: ["Predictive", "Adaptive", "Iterative prototyping", "Adaptive, with requirements written after release"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Predictive approaches suit stable, well-understood needs and situations that call for formal, up-front definition and approval." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 4.3 Foundational Techniques · BABOK v3 10.2 Backlog Management",
  question: "A team keeps an ordered list of remaining work items and re-orders it before each iteration based on value. Which foundational technique is this?",
  options: ["Process analysis", "Customer journey mapping", "Data storytelling", "Backlog management"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Backlog management is the planned approach to recording, tracking and prioritizing the remaining work items." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 4.3 Foundational Techniques · BABOK v3 10.34 Process Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst examines the loan approval process step by step to find delays, rework and hand-offs that could be removed. Which foundational technique is this?",
  options: ["Backlog management", "Process analysis", "Data storytelling", "Workshops/interviews"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Process analysis examines processes for efficiency and effectiveness, to find opportunities to create more value." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "“Branch managers need to see each day’s loan applications grouped by status.” How is this requirement BEST classified?",
  options: ["Business requirement", "Transition requirement", "Stakeholder requirement", "Non-functional requirement"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Stakeholder requirements describe the needs of a particular stakeholder group. A business requirement would state an organizational goal, such as reducing loan approval time." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema · BABOK v3 10.30 Non-Functional Requirements Analysis",
  question: "“The monthly sales report must load in under three seconds.” What kind of requirement is this?",
  options: ["Non-functional requirement", "Functional requirement", "Business requirement", "Transition requirement"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Non-functional requirements describe qualities the solution must have, such as performance, availability or security, rather than what it does." },

{ id: "E-CHG-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "When assessing a proposed change to approved requirements, which set of factors should a business analyst consider?",
  options: ["Who requested it and how senior they are", "Benefit, cost, impact, schedule and urgency", "Only the effort for the development team", "Whether the change was in the original scope"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Assessing a change looks at its benefit, cost, impact on other requirements and stakeholders, effect on the schedule, and urgency, so decision makers can weigh it." },
{ id: "E-CHG-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A bank will introduce a new account-opening process in 40 branches. A failure would disrupt customers badly, and branch staff have little experience with new systems. Which rollout is MOST appropriate?",
  options: ["All 40 branches on the same day, to keep the process consistent", "The largest branches first and permanently, since they serve the most customers", "Postpone it until staff gain system experience", "A pilot in a few branches, then a phased rollout using what was learned"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A change strategy weighs risk and readiness. A pilot followed by phased transition states limits disruption and lets the bank learn before scaling up." },
{ id: "E-CHG-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "“The old and new billing systems must run side by side for one month after go-live.” What type of requirement is this?",
  options: ["Transition requirement", "Business requirement", "Functional requirement", "Stakeholder requirement"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Running both systems in parallel is only needed to move safely from the old state to the new one, which makes it a transition requirement." },
{ id: "E-CHG-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations · BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Three months after a new scheduling process went live, many staff are quietly back to their old spreadsheets. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Declare the change complete, because the process went live as planned", "Ask IT to block access to spreadsheets so staff must use the new process", "Find out why staff revert and recommend actions to sustain the change", "Restart the project with a different solution that staff may prefer"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A change only delivers value if it sticks. Understanding why people revert (solution or enterprise limitations) leads to targeted actions instead of force or a costly restart." },
{ id: "E-CHG-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A business analyst decides which capabilities go into the first, second and third release, based on value, dependencies and how ready each department is. What is this part of?",
  options: ["Requirements verification", "Release planning within the change strategy", "Stakeholder analysis", "Solution performance measurement"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Release planning decides which requirements go into which release and is part of defining the change strategy and its transition states." },
{ id: "E-CHG-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance · BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "Who should decide whether a proposed change to approved requirements goes ahead?",
  options: ["The business analyst who analyzed the change and its impact", "The developer who will implement it", "The person who asked for the change", "Whoever has that authority under the agreed governance"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The business analyst analyzes the change and recommends; decisions are made by whoever the governance approach gives that authority, such as the sponsor or a change board." },

{ id: "E-NEED-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State · BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases",
  question: "A draft problem statement says: “Our onboarding is bad.” What is it MOST clearly missing?",
  options: ["The name of the system that will replace the current one", "The budget, the timeline and the name of the team who will fix it", "A proposed solution to the onboarding problem", "Who is affected, the impact, and what success looks like"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A useful problem statement says what the problem is, who it affects, what its impact is, and what a successful solution would achieve, without prescribing the solution." },
{ id: "E-NEED-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A new regulation forces a company to change how it stores customer data. Where does this business need come from?",
  options: ["An external driver", "A bottom-up process problem", "A strategic goal set by the board", "A middle-management initiative"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business needs can come from the top (strategic goals), the bottom (process problems), middle management, or external drivers such as regulation, competitors and customer demand." },
{ id: "E-NEED-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A director tells the business analyst: “We need SAP.” How should the analyst treat this statement?",
  options: ["As the business need, ready to document", "As a proposed solution, and explore the need behind it", "As a constraint the solution must meet", "As a business objective"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "“We need SAP” names a solution. The analyst should explore the problem or opportunity behind it before assuming that SAP is the best way to address it." },
{ id: "E-NEED-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Stakeholders say returns are “a big problem.” What should the business analyst do to understand the size of the need?",
  options: ["Accept the stakeholders’ view, since they know the business best", "Ask the sponsor to confirm that it is a problem", "Use evidence such as return rates, costs and complaints", "Wait until a solution is chosen, then measure the impact"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Evidence makes the need concrete and measurable, helps prioritize it against other needs, and gives a baseline for judging the solution later." },
{ id: "E-NEED-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Two needs compete for the same budget: slow invoice processing and a rising number of customer complaints. How should the business analyst help decide which to address first?",
  options: ["Compare their impact on value and how well each aligns with business goals", "Address the one raised by the more senior manager, since they own the budget", "Address whichever is cheaper and quicker to fix, to show early results", "Ask stakeholders to vote on which to fund first"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Needs are prioritized by their impact on value and alignment with goals. Cost and seniority matter, but they don’t replace an assessment of value." },
{ id: "E-NEED-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis · BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Sales reports lost orders, finance reports late payments, and support reports angry customers. The business analyst finds that all three trace back to wrong customer addresses captured at sign-up. What is the BEST next step?",
  options: ["Start three separate initiatives, one per department", "Fix the support team’s scripts first, since customers are angry", "Ask each department to improve its own process", "Treat the address capture problem as the root cause and frame one need around it"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "When several symptoms share a root cause, addressing that cause delivers more value than treating each symptom separately." },

{ id: "E-SOL-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options · BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Why does a business analyst usually define several design options before recommending one?",
  options: ["To compare how well each delivers value against cost and risk", "Because each stakeholder group must get its own option", "To give the developers a choice of how to build it", "Because sponsors expect a choice, even when one option is clearly best"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Comparing options helps identify the one that delivers the most value within the constraints, rather than the first idea anyone had." },
{ id: "E-SOL-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A document states which capabilities the new solution will include and which are explicitly out of scope. What is this?",
  options: ["The business case", "The stakeholder register", "The solution scope", "The requirements architecture"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The solution scope sets the boundaries of the solution: what it will and won’t provide to meet the need." },
{ id: "E-SOL-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "Orders entered in a new sales app sometimes don’t reach the warehouse system because of an integration error. What type of limitation is this?",
  options: ["An enterprise limitation", "A solution limitation", "A stakeholder limitation", "A business rule"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The problem lies within the solution’s own components and interfaces, so it is a solution limitation." },
{ id: "E-SOL-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "After a merger, two teams use two different tools to do the same job and enter the same data twice. Which recommendation would MOST directly increase solution value?",
  options: ["Do nothing until the next budget cycle", "Train both teams on both tools", "Eliminate the redundancy by moving to one tool", "Simplify both tools’ interfaces"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Eliminating redundancy is a common way to increase solution value: it removes duplicate cost, effort and data inconsistencies." },
{ id: "E-SOL-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.49 Vendor Assessment",
  question: "A business analyst is helping choose between three software vendors. Besides features, what should the evaluation consider?",
  options: ["Mainly the price of each product, since features are equal", "The vendors’ stability, support, licensing and organizational fit", "Mainly which vendor the users liked best in the product demonstrations", "Mainly how quickly each vendor can implement its product"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Vendor assessment looks at the supplier as well as the product, including financial stability, support, licensing and organizational fit, which affect long-term value and risk." },
{ id: "E-SOL-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.1 Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria",
  question: "Before go-live, stakeholders agree that the new claims tool must process a standard claim in under ten minutes and pass all security tests, or it will not be accepted. What are these?",
  options: ["Evaluation criteria", "Business objectives", "Assumptions", "Acceptance criteria"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Acceptance criteria are the minimum conditions a solution must meet to be accepted. Evaluation criteria are used to compare options or assess value, often with weights." },

{ id: "E-STK-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement · BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "When identifying stakeholders for a new invoicing system, which group is MOST often overlooked?",
  options: ["The sponsor who funds the system", "The finance clerks who will use the new system every day", "The project team building it", "Indirectly affected people, such as the support team"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Indirectly affected groups, such as operational support, downstream teams or regulators, are easy to miss. Missing them can lead to late surprises and unmet needs." },
{ id: "E-STK-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "In a RACI matrix, what is the difference between Consulted and Informed?",
  options: ["Consulted people give input before the decision; Informed people are told afterwards", "Both give input beforehand", "Consulted people approve the decision; Informed people do the work", "Informed people give input; Consulted people are told afterwards"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Consulted stakeholders provide input (two-way communication). Informed stakeholders are kept up to date (one-way communication)." },
{ id: "E-STK-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Which stakeholder role authorizes the work and is responsible for securing its funding?",
  options: ["Domain subject matter expert", "Sponsor", "End user", "Project manager"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The sponsor initiates the effort, defines the business need, and authorizes the work and its funding." },
{ id: "E-STK-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "A developer with deep knowledge of how the current payment platform is built joins requirements sessions. What role are they playing?",
  options: ["Domain subject matter expert", "End user", "Sponsor", "Implementation subject matter expert"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "An implementation SME has specialist knowledge of how a solution is or will be built. A domain SME has expertise in the business area itself." },
{ id: "E-STK-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "A business analyst draws stakeholders in rings: the delivery team at the center, then the business unit that uses the solution, then the wider organization, then external parties. What is this diagram called?",
  options: ["An onion diagram", "A RACI matrix", "A power/interest grid", "An organizational chart"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "An onion diagram shows stakeholders by how closely they are involved with the solution, from the delivery team outward to external stakeholders." },
{ id: "E-STK-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "Sales wants discounts approved within minutes; finance insists every discount over 10% is checked by a controller. Both are firm. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Side with finance, because financial controls protect the company", "Side with sales, because slow approvals cost revenue and frustrate customers", "Leave it to the developers to design a compromise in the system", "Bring both sides together to explore the interests behind their positions"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Negotiation and conflict resolution focus on underlying interests (speed and control here) rather than fixed positions, which often reveals options such as tiered or automated approvals." },

{ id: "E-VAL-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "Moving customer support to a chatbot saves the company money but frustrates some older customers. What does this show about value?",
  options: ["Value matters to the company that pays for the change, so the saving decides it", "The change has no value, because some customers are unhappy", "Value is relative: it can rise for some stakeholders and fall for others", "Value is measured in money, so the saving is what decides it"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Value depends on who is looking and in what situation, so it can rise for some stakeholders and fall for others. A good analysis makes both the gains and the losses visible." },
{ id: "E-VAL-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A new online onboarding process aims to make it easier for customers to open accounts. Which measure would BEST show whether it delivers that value?",
  options: ["The number of screens in the new process", "The completion rate and the average time to open an account", "The share of test cases passed before launch", "Whether the project finished on budget"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Measures of value should reflect the intended outcome, here easier and faster account opening, not project outputs or effort." },
{ id: "E-VAL-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements · BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "Why might a team release the highest-value features of a new app first, instead of waiting to launch everything at once?",
  options: ["So value starts sooner and feedback shapes later releases", "To spread the build cost evenly across several budget periods", "Because low-value features can then be dropped from the scope for good", "To shorten the total development time"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Delivering high-value items early lets benefits start sooner and gives feedback that improves later releases." },
{ id: "E-VAL-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A seasonal promotion feature is worth a lot if it is live before December, but almost nothing if it arrives in January. Which factor should drive its priority?",
  options: ["Stability", "Complexity", "Time sensitivity", "Ownership"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Time sensitivity applies when the value of a requirement depends on when it is delivered, and drops sharply after a certain date." },
{ id: "E-VAL-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "A new tool costs €40,000 and is expected to save €10,000 a year. Ignoring discounting, what is the payback period?",
  options: ["2.5 years", "4 years", "5 years", "10 years"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Payback period = investment ÷ annual net benefit = €40,000 ÷ €10,000 = 4 years." },
{ id: "E-VAL-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A compliance initiative brings in no new revenue and saves no costs, but it stops the company from facing possible fines of several million euros. Does it deliver value?",
  options: ["No, because value requires new revenue or new customers", "No, because value means new revenue or lower costs", "Not really: compliance is a constraint, not a source of value", "Yes: avoiding losses and reducing risk is value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Value includes avoiding losses and reducing risk. Penalties avoided are a real benefit, even when no revenue or saving appears." },

{ id: "E-CTX-08", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "“All warehouse staff will have a company smartphone by go-live.” Nobody has confirmed this. How should it be classified?",
  options: ["A constraint", "An assumption", "A business rule", "A requirement"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "An assumption is something believed to be true that hasn’t been confirmed. It should be written down and checked, because the solution may fail if it turns out to be wrong." },
{ id: "E-CTX-09", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A competitor launches a cheaper subscription that is drawing customers away. In current-state analysis, what is this?",
  options: ["An internal weakness", "An internal asset", "A business rule", "An external influencer"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Competitors are external influencers on the organization, along with customers, suppliers, regulation, technology and the economy." },
{ id: "E-CTX-10", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State · BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "In this company, even small decisions are escalated to the managing director. How should this context affect the business analyst’s work?",
  options: ["Plan approvals around the managing director’s role and availability", "Add detail to the requirements so fewer decisions need escalating", "Avoid involving the managing director, to save time", "Treat it as an enterprise limitation to fix first"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Organizational culture and decision-making habits are part of context. They shape governance, approvals and how long decisions take." },
{ id: "E-CTX-11", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Which of these is part of analyzing the current state?",
  options: ["Choosing the vendor and the technology for the new system", "Writing acceptance criteria for the new system", "Describing today’s capabilities, processes and policies", "Measuring the new solution’s performance after launch"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Analyzing the current state covers the organization’s structure and culture, what it can do and how, its technology, policies, internal assets and outside influences." },
{ id: "E-CTX-12", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A company owns a well-known brand, a large customer database and several patents. In current-state analysis, what are these?",
  options: ["External influencers", "Constraints", "Assumptions", "Internal assets"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Internal assets are tangible and intangible resources the organization owns, such as brand, data, patents and skilled people, which a change can build on." },
{ id: "E-CTX-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "A new customer portal can only go live after another team finishes moving customer data to a new database, which is already running late. How should the business analyst handle this?",
  options: ["Record it as a dependency and risk, and plan for it with the other team", "Record it as a constraint on the portal’s solution scope", "Raise a change request to move the go-live date", "Assume the data will be ready on time"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Dependencies on work outside the initiative are part of context and a common source of risk. They should be recorded, monitored and coordinated, not assumed away." },

/* ===================== CCBA · batch 1 (Nov 2026) ===================== */
/* BAPM */
{ id: "CC-BAPM-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "When planning the business analysis approach, what does the “timing of business analysis work” address?",
  options: ["How long each stakeholder interview should last", "When BA activities happen relative to delivery", "When the project budget will be approved", "How quickly stakeholders must approve requirements"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Timing covers when business analysis activities are performed: mostly up front in predictive approaches, or just before delivery in adaptive ones." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "A business analyst has drafted the business analysis approach for a new initiative. What should happen before it is used?",
  options: ["Nothing further; the approach is the business analyst’s own working plan", "It should be sent to developers for estimation", "It should be reviewed with and agreed by key stakeholders", "It should be baselined under change control"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Gaining stakeholder acceptance of the approach is part of planning it. Agreement up front avoids conflict later about deliverables, timing and involvement." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Halfway through an initiative, the sponsor leaves and is replaced by someone from another department. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Revisit the stakeholder analysis and engagement approach for the new sponsor", "Continue with the existing plan, since the initiative is already approved", "Wait for the new sponsor to contact the team, then brief them on progress", "Ask the project manager to brief the new sponsor"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The stakeholder engagement approach is revisited when stakeholders change. A new sponsor may have different priorities, authority and communication needs." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "A business analyst decides to organize requirements by business process (quote, order, invoice) so they can be found and reused across releases. What is the analyst planning?",
  options: ["The governance approach for the requirements", "The elicitation activity plan for each process", "The change strategy for the quote, order and invoice processes", "How business analysis information is organized"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "How business analysis information is organized and structured is part of planning business analysis information management." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Two department heads disagree about a requirement, and the team doesn’t know who has the final say. What should have been defined in the governance approach?",
  options: ["How requirements are stored and accessed", "Who attends each elicitation session", "Which requirements attributes to track", "Who makes decisions and how disagreements are escalated"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Governance planning defines decision making: who decides, how decisions are made, and the escalation path when stakeholders can’t agree." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Which measure would BEST indicate how well business analysis work is performing on an initiative?",
  options: ["How often the standard templates were used", "The number of stakeholders interviewed", "The amount of rework caused by unclear or missing requirements", "How many requirements were written per week"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Business analysis performance measures look at outcomes such as rework, timeliness, stakeholder satisfaction and the quality of deliverables, not volume of output." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-07", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "A business analyst needs to identify everyone affected by a new HR system and record their roles and interests. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Stakeholder list, map or personas", "Data modelling of HR records", "Sequence diagrams of HR processes", "Financial analysis of the HR budget"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Stakeholder lists, maps and personas help identify stakeholders and analyze their roles, interests, influence and attitudes." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.19 Estimation",
  question: "A business analyst asks three experienced colleagues for independent estimates, shares the results with them anonymously, and repeats the round until the estimates converge. Which estimation method is this?",
  options: ["Bottom-up", "Parametric", "Rolling wave", "Delphi"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Delphi combines expert judgement over several rounds, sharing results between rounds until the estimates converge. Bottom-up adds up estimates for individual activities; rolling wave refines estimates as work proceeds." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach · BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "How does a predictive approach usually affect business analysis governance compared with an adaptive one?",
  options: ["It removes the need for change control", "Changes after approval are usually handled through more formal change control", "Approvals happen informally at each iteration review", "Prioritization is done continuously by the delivery team"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "In predictive approaches, requirements are baselined, so later changes typically go through formal change control. Adaptive approaches handle change through ongoing backlog prioritization." },

/* EC */
{ id: "CC-EC-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.25 Interviews",
  question: "In an interview, the business analyst needs exact figures: how many invoices are processed per day. Which type of question fits BEST?",
  options: ["An open-ended question", "A closed question", "A leading question", "A hypothetical question"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Closed questions get specific, short answers such as numbers or yes/no. Open questions explore views and reasons." },
{ id: "CC-EC-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.29 Mind Mapping",
  question: "During a workshop, the business analyst captures ideas around a central topic, with branches for related themes, to help the group organize its thinking. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Data flow diagram", "Decision table", "Mind mapping", "State modelling"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Mind mapping organizes ideas around a central topic with branches, which helps groups generate and structure thoughts." },
{ id: "CC-EC-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.45 Survey or Questionnaire",
  question: "Before sending a questionnaire to 3,000 customers, what should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Add a question for every requirement so nothing is missed", "Pilot it with a small group to check the questions work", "Make all the questions open-ended, so customers can explain their answers in detail", "Ask the sponsor to answer it first and approve it"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Piloting a survey with a small group finds unclear or leading questions before the full send, when they can no longer be fixed." },
{ id: "CC-EC-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Before a process workshop, the business analyst gathers the current procedure manual, sample forms and last month’s error reports. Which part of preparing for elicitation is this?",
  options: ["Securing supporting material", "Setting up logistics", "Preparing stakeholders", "Confirming elicitation results"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Securing supporting material means gathering the sources and information needed to run the elicitation activity well." },
{ id: "CC-EC-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation",
  question: "During a workshop, what should the business analyst make sure is captured?",
  options: ["The final agreed requirements, written up in the approved template", "A word-for-word transcript of the session", "The attendance list and the agenda items covered", "Decisions, open questions, assumptions and action items"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Capturing elicitation outcomes means recording the information in a usable form, including decisions, open issues and assumptions, so it can be confirmed and followed up." },
{ id: "CC-EC-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "An agile team works from a shared wiki and a backlog tool. How should the business analyst communicate most business analysis information to them?",
  options: ["Formal documents with sign-off pages, for traceability", "Monthly presentations to the whole team and stakeholders", "Informal documentation in the tools the team already uses", "Detailed email threads that everyone can search later"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The format of communication should suit the audience and approach. Agile teams usually work best with informal, collaborative documentation in their own tools." },
{ id: "CC-EC-07", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "In planning sessions, two team leads have started making sarcastic comments about each other’s departments, and others have gone quiet. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Address it early, e.g. talk to each lead and agree ways of working", "Ignore it, because interpersonal issues are not business analysis work", "Ask the sponsor to remind both leads of the initiative’s goals", "Switch to separate interviews with each department"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Managing stakeholder collaboration includes noticing and addressing conflict before it damages engagement and the quality of the information gathered." },
{ id: "CC-EC-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Which item belongs in an elicitation activity plan?",
  options: ["The approved solution design and its components", "The test results from the previous release and open defects", "Techniques, participants, logistics and materials", "The performance measures for the new solution"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The elicitation activity plan describes the activities, techniques, participants, logistics and supporting materials for elicitation." },
{ id: "CC-EC-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "After a workshop, the business analyst sends a summary to participants and asks them to correct anything they recorded differently. What is the analyst doing?",
  options: ["Approving requirements", "Confirming elicitation results", "Prioritizing requirements", "Verifying requirements"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Confirming elicitation results checks that the information captured is accurate and consistent with what stakeholders meant." },

/* RLCM */
{ id: "CC-RLCM-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Requirement R2 (export to PDF) is much easier to build if requirement R1 (a print layout engine) is built first, although R2 could be done without it. What type of relationship is this?",
  options: ["Depends: necessity", "Derive", "Depends: effort", "Validate"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An effort dependency means one requirement is simpler to build when a related one is built too. A necessity dependency means it would be pointless without the other." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Which tool is MOST commonly used to record which requirements relate to which designs, components and tests?",
  options: ["A traceability matrix or repository", "A RACI matrix for the requirements", "A stakeholder map of requirement owners", "A business model canvas of the solution"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Traceability is usually kept in a matrix or a requirements management tool, which records the relationships between requirements and other elements." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.33 Prioritization",
  question: "The team has four weeks before a trade show. It selects requirements in priority order until the available capacity is used up. Which prioritization technique is this?",
  options: ["Grouping", "Ranking", "Negotiation", "Timeboxing/budgeting"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Timeboxing or budgeting fixes the time or money available and selects the highest-priority requirements that fit within it." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.33 Prioritization",
  question: "Stakeholders put each requirement into High, Medium or Low priority. Which prioritization technique is this?",
  options: ["Grouping", "Ranking", "Timeboxing", "Decision analysis"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Grouping places requirements into categories such as High/Medium/Low or MoSCoW. Ranking orders them from most to least important." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.33 Prioritization",
  question: "Two product managers each want their own features first. The business analyst helps them trade off and agree an order both can accept. Which prioritization technique is this?",
  options: ["Ranking", "Negotiation", "Grouping", "Timeboxing"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Negotiation helps stakeholders reach consensus on priorities through discussion and trade-offs." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "In a predictive project, a stakeholder asks to change a requirement that was approved and baselined last month. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Update the requirement directly, since it is still early in the project", "Reject the request, because the baseline is fixed", "Raise it through change control with an impact assessment", "Add it to the next project instead"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Once requirements are baselined, changes go through the agreed change control process with impact analysis, so the right people can make an informed decision." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-07", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "An auditor asks who approved a set of requirements and when. What should the business analyst be able to provide?",
  options: ["A record of who approved what, when, and which version", "The trace from each requirement to its source", "The governance approach naming the approvers", "The sponsor’s verbal confirmation at a meeting"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Tracking and communicating approval is part of Approve Requirements. A clear record of who approved what and when supports audits and avoids disputes." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "A requirement is no longer needed because the related product has been withdrawn. What should the business analyst do with it?",
  options: ["Delete it from the repository so the list stays clean and current", "Leave it unchanged in case the product comes back", "Remove its trace links but keep the requirement text", "Mark it as retired and keep its history and traces"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Maintaining requirements includes keeping their status accurate. Marking them retired, rather than deleting them, preserves traceability and the reasoning behind past decisions." },

/* SA */
{ id: "CC-SA-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "What is the difference between a business goal and a business objective?",
  options: ["They mean the same thing, and BABOK uses the two terms interchangeably", "Goals belong to the current state; objectives to the future state", "A goal is a broader, longer-term aim; an objective is specific and measurable", "Objectives are long-term aims; goals are the short-term steps toward them"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Goals are broad, longer-term statements of what the organization wants. Objectives are specific, measurable steps toward a goal, often written in SMART form." },
{ id: "CC-SA-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.38 Risk Analysis and Management",
  question: "To reduce the chance that a new pricing engine gives wrong prices at launch, the team runs it in parallel with the old one for a month. Which risk response is this?",
  options: ["Avoid", "Accept", "Transfer", "Mitigate"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Mitigating a risk reduces its probability or impact. Running in parallel lowers the chance and impact of wrong prices reaching customers." },
{ id: "CC-SA-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.38 Risk Analysis and Management",
  question: "A risk has a low probability and minor impact, and dealing with it would cost more than it could lose. What is the MOST appropriate response?",
  options: ["Accept it and deal with it if it happens", "Transfer it to an insurer", "Mitigate it with controls", "Avoid it by changing the scope"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Accepting a risk means deciding not to act on it; if it occurs, a workaround is developed at that time. It suits risks whose impact is smaller than the cost of responding." },
{ id: "CC-SA-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.38 Risk Analysis and Management",
  question: "The team drops a planned integration with an unstable third-party system and uses a manual file upload instead, so the risk of integration failure no longer exists. Which risk response is this?",
  options: ["Mitigate", "Avoid", "Accept", "Transfer"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Avoiding a risk removes its source, here by changing the plan so the risky integration is no longer needed." },
{ id: "CC-SA-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "While analyzing the current state, the business analyst finds an internal policy that requires paper signatures for every contract. The future state is fully digital. How should this be treated?",
  options: ["Ignore it, because internal policies are outside business analysis", "Assume the policy will be changed by the time the solution goes live", "Keep the policy and design a paper step into the new process", "Record it in the current state; the policy may need to change too"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Policies are part of the current state. A policy that conflicts with the future state is something the change must address, not ignore or assume away." },
{ id: "CC-SA-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases · BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "When comparing options for a change strategy, why is it useful to include “do nothing” as an option?",
  options: ["It is the baseline for comparing the other options", "It is usually the cheapest, so it should normally be chosen", "It is required by every governance approach", "It sets the future state the other options must reach"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "“Do nothing” shows what happens if no change is made. The value of each option is judged against that baseline." },
{ id: "CC-SA-07", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "The future state for a new service desk assumes first-line staff will resolve 70% of issues themselves. Today they escalate everything to specialists by policy. What must the future-state description also address?",
  options: ["The new ticketing software and its configuration", "The changes needed in roles, skills, authority and culture for first-line staff", "Only the training schedule", "No further changes, since the software will enforce the new process"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Defining the future state covers organizational structure, culture, roles and capabilities as well as technology. Without those changes, the 70% target is unlikely to be met." },
{ id: "CC-SA-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.38 Risk Analysis and Management",
  question: "Risks are scored as probability × impact (impact on a 1–10 scale). Risk 1: 0.2 × 9. Risk 2: 0.6 × 4. Risk 3: 0.5 × 3. Risk 4: 0.1 × 10. Which risk ranks highest?",
  options: ["Risk 1", "Risk 2", "Risk 3", "Risk 4"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Risk 1 = 1.8, risk 2 = 2.4, risk 3 = 1.5, risk 4 = 1.0. Risk 2 has the highest score, even though risks 1 and 4 have bigger impacts." },

/* RADD */
{ id: "CC-RADD-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.48 User Stories",
  question: "“As a user, I want a better system.” What is the main problem with this user story?",
  options: ["It is written from the user’s view, not the system’s", "It has no story-point estimate, so the team can’t plan it", "It is too long to fit on a single story card or sticky note", "It lacks a specific role, goal and benefit, so it can’t be tested"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A useful user story names a specific role, a specific capability and the benefit it brings. Without these, it can’t guide design or testing." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.47 Use Cases and Scenarios",
  question: "In a use case for “Pay invoice online,” the external payment gateway that the system sends requests to is best modelled as:",
  options: ["An actor", "A precondition", "An alternative flow", "A business rule"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Actors are people or systems outside the solution that interact with it. An external payment gateway is a system actor." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.35 Process Modelling",
  question: "In a process model, where the flow splits depending on whether an order is above €10,000, which element is used?",
  options: ["A swimlane for the approval team", "An intermediate event", "A decision point (gateway)", "A data store for order values"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Decision points (gateways) show where the process takes different paths based on a condition." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "One requirement says refunds are paid within 14 days; another says within 30 days. Which quality characteristic does the set fail?",
  options: ["Atomic", "Feasible", "Concise", "Consistent"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Consistent means requirements don’t conflict with each other. Two different refund periods for the same case are a contradiction that must be resolved." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "A requirement says the app must work fully offline and sync instantly with all banks in real time, using technology the company doesn’t have and can’t afford. Which quality characteristic is MOST in question?",
  options: ["Feasible", "Unambiguous", "Prioritized", "Atomic"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A feasible requirement can be met within the known constraints, such as budget, technology and time." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "Requirements for online payment describe what happens when payment succeeds, but say nothing about failed or cancelled payments. Which quality characteristic is missing?",
  options: ["Concise", "Testable", "Complete", "Understandable"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Complete requirements cover all the information needed, including exceptions and alternative cases such as failed payments." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-07", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.23 Glossary · BABOK v3 10.12 Data Dictionary",
  question: "What is the main difference between a glossary and a data dictionary?",
  options: ["A glossary defines business terms; a data dictionary defines data elements", "They are the same thing, kept in different tools", "A glossary is written for developers; a data dictionary is written for business users", "A glossary is built during elicitation; a data dictionary once the design is approved"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A glossary gives shared definitions of business terms. A data dictionary records standard definitions of data elements, including formats, sizes and allowed values." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.36 Prototyping",
  question: "A prototype shows every screen of a new app, with navigation between them, but none of the screens actually does anything yet. What kind of prototype is this?",
  options: ["Vertical prototype", "Horizontal prototype", "Evolutionary prototype", "Proof of concept"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A horizontal prototype shows a broad, shallow view across the interface. A vertical prototype goes deep into one area, including its functionality." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.9 Business Rules Analysis",
  question: "“A discount may not exceed 20% of the order value.” What type of business rule is this?",
  options: ["Definitional rule", "Process step", "Behavioural rule", "Non-functional requirement"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Behavioural rules place obligations or prohibitions on what people or systems do. Definitional rules define or classify concepts." },

/* SE */
{ id: "CC-SE-01", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Can customer satisfaction survey results be used as a solution performance measure?",
  options: ["No; performance is measured in financial terms", "No, only system-generated data is objective enough", "Yes, but for BA performance, not solution performance", "Yes, qualitative measures like satisfaction count"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Performance measures can be quantitative or qualitative. Customer and user perceptions are valid indicators of value." },
{ id: "CC-SE-02", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "A new returns process was expected to cut handling time by 40% but has cut it by 15%. What should the business analyst do next?",
  options: ["Analyze the variance and its causes before recommending anything", "Report the project as a failure to the steering committee", "Assess enterprise limitations, since staff resistance is the most common cause", "Ask the vendor to fix the process, since the target wasn’t met"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Analyzing performance measures includes looking at variances between expected and actual results and finding their causes, which then drive recommendations." },
{ id: "CC-SE-03", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "As part of assessing enterprise limitations, the business analyst looks at which groups are affected by a new solution, how their work changes, and what concerns they have. What is this?",
  options: ["Solution performance analysis", "Stakeholder impact analysis", "Interface analysis", "Root cause analysis"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Stakeholder impact analysis looks at how a solution affects stakeholder groups, including their functions, locations and concerns." },
{ id: "CC-SE-04", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "A new approval workflow requires team leads to approve purchases, but team leads have no budget authority under the current structure. Which enterprise limitation does this show?",
  options: ["A solution defect in the approval workflow", "A data quality issue in the purchase records", "A need to change the organizational structure", "A traceability gap between policy and workflow"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Assessing enterprise limitations includes checking whether the organizational structure and reporting lines support the solution. Here, authority has to change for the workflow to work." },
{ id: "CC-SE-05", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "A new 24/7 chat service is live, but the support team only works 9 to 5 and nobody has been assigned to handle it out of hours. Which assessment would find this?",
  options: ["Requirements verification", "Interface analysis", "Business model canvas", "Operational assessment"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "An operational assessment checks whether the organization has the processes, people and resources to operate and support the solution." },
{ id: "CC-SE-06", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Staff copy data by hand between three systems to complete one task, which causes errors and delays. Which recommendation would MOST directly address this?",
  options: ["Hire more staff so the copying is done faster and checked twice", "Reduce interface complexity, e.g. automate the data transfer", "Retire the three systems and replace them with a single new platform", "Do nothing, since the errors are caught later by the finance team"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Reducing the complexity of interfaces, including manual hand-offs between systems, is one of the recommended actions to increase solution value." },
{ id: "CC-SE-07", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A CRM bought for sales also has a customer service module the company already pays for but doesn’t use. Service staff still log issues in spreadsheets. Which recommendation fits BEST?",
  options: ["Build a new service tool designed for the service team", "Reduce interface complexity by linking the spreadsheets to the CRM", "Do nothing, because the module wasn’t in the original scope", "Assess whether enabling the unused module would add value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Identifying additional capabilities means spotting ways an existing solution could deliver more value, here a module that is already paid for." },
{ id: "CC-SE-08", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A new solution would work well, but the current bonus scheme rewards staff for using the old process. What type of recommendation addresses this?",
  options: ["Retire the solution, since staff are not using it as intended", "Reduce the complexity of the solution’s interfaces", "Organizational change, such as adjusting the bonus scheme", "Eliminate redundancy between the old and new processes"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Some limitations are in the enterprise, not the solution. Recommending organizational change (policies, incentives, roles) helps the solution deliver its value." },

/* ===================== CBAP · batch 1 (Nov 2026) ===================== */
/* BAPM */
{ id: "CP-BAPM-01", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "A bank’s program has two parts. The core ledger changes are fixed by regulation, must be fully specified and are audited. The new customer app is exploratory and will change with user feedback. How should the business analyst plan the business analysis approach?",
  options: ["Use one predictive approach for everything, since part of the work is regulated", "Use one adaptive approach for everything, since mixed approaches confuse governance", "Tailor it: predictive for the ledger, adaptive for the app, with shared governance", "Plan the ledger first, and start the app once the ledger is fully baselined"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The approach should fit the work. Different parts of an initiative can use different approaches, as long as governance and integration points between them are planned." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-02", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "In a multi-vendor program, the vendor and the business often disagree about what a requirement means, and each claims the other must pay for the rework. What should the business analyst help put in place?",
  options: ["A rule that the vendor’s interpretation applies, since the vendor builds the solution", "Governance that defines who decides disputes, how they escalate and how they’re recorded", "A contract clause stating that the party that wrote the requirement pays for any rework caused by it", "More detailed requirements, so disputes about meaning can’t happen again"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Governance planning defines decision making, escalation and change control. Clear decision rights and records resolve disputes; more detail alone never removes all ambiguity." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Analysis shows that the business analyst’s time per user story keeps rising, because acceptance criteria are reworked after developers question them during the iteration. What is the BEST improvement action?",
  options: ["Review acceptance criteria with developers and testers during refinement", "Write longer, more detailed acceptance criteria for every story", "Ask developers to save their questions for the sprint review", "Accept the trend, since rework is normal in agile delivery"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Identifying performance improvements means finding the cause and recommending actions. Involving developers and testers earlier addresses the cause of the rework, not the symptom." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-04", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "Stakeholders cannot yet describe what they need from a new AI-based service, and the market is shifting quickly. How should this uncertainty affect the business analysis plan?",
  options: ["Plan all analysis up front, to reduce uncertainty before building", "Postpone analysis until the market stabilizes and needs are clear", "Plan short cycles of analysis, experiments and re-planning", "Leave planning to the delivery team, since they will learn fastest"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "High uncertainty favours an adaptive, iterative approach where analysis, learning and re-planning happen in short cycles." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-05", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "In an adaptive initiative, what does approval of business analysis work typically look like?",
  options: ["A formal sign-off of the full requirements before any build", "No approval of any kind, since the team self-manages", "A formal sign-off of each item by the sponsor and the steering committee", "The product owner accepting items against acceptance criteria"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Plans for approval should fit the approach. In adaptive work, approval is usually ongoing and lightweight, tied to backlog items and iteration reviews." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-06", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.19 Estimation",
  question: "A business analyst estimates two pieces of work with PERT. Component X: optimistic 2, most likely 4, pessimistic 12 days. Component Y: optimistic 3, most likely 5, pessimistic 7 days. What is the total expected estimate?",
  options: ["9 days", "10 days", "11 days", "12 days"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK’s PERT value for each component is (optimistic + pessimistic + 4 × most likely) / 6. X = (2 + 12 + 16) / 6 = 5. Y = (3 + 7 + 20) / 6 = 5. Total = 10 days." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "A national regulator is a key stakeholder for a payments initiative but will not attend workshops. How should the business analyst plan engagement with the regulator?",
  options: ["Leave the regulator out of engagement planning, since they won’t attend sessions", "Name the regulator as accountable for the payment requirements in the RACI matrix", "Plan formal, well-timed communication, usually via the compliance team", "Wait until go-live and then send the regulator the final documentation"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Stakeholder engagement is tailored to each stakeholder’s role and constraints. Regulators often need formal, planned communication through defined channels rather than workshop participation." },

/* EC */
{ id: "CP-EC-01", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.25 Interviews · BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "A business analyst needs to understand how employee misconduct cases are handled today. Participants include HR officers, managers and union representatives. Which elicitation technique is MOST appropriate to start with?",
  options: ["A large workshop with all groups, so every view is heard together", "Individual interviews, to protect confidentiality", "An online survey of all employees about how misconduct is handled", "Brainstorming with the whole department on how to improve it"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Sensitive topics are best explored in individual interviews, where confidentiality can be protected. Group sessions may silence people or expose private information." },
{ id: "CP-EC-02", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Two business units have openly competing goals for a shared pricing system, and earlier joint meetings ended in arguments. How should the business analyst plan elicitation?",
  options: ["Separate sessions first, then a facilitated joint session on the differences", "Joint sessions from the start, so that nobody can claim their view wasn’t heard by the others", "Let the more senior unit define the requirements and inform the other", "Avoid elicitation sessions and write requirements from existing documents"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Preparing for elicitation includes choosing techniques and sequencing that fit the stakeholders. Separate sessions surface needs safely; a facilitated joint session then deals with the conflicts." },
{ id: "CP-EC-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "Interview notes describe the order process in five steps. The existing process model has seven steps, including a credit check nobody mentioned. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Use the interview version, because it reflects how people work today", "Use the model, because documented processes are more reliable", "Combine both versions into one model and move on", "Follow up with stakeholders to resolve the difference"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Confirming elicitation results means comparing them with source information and other results. Differences should be investigated, not resolved by preference." },
{ id: "CP-EC-04", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "Requirements for a medical-device interface must be submitted to a regulator as part of an approval. What form of communication is MOST appropriate?",
  options: ["Formal, version-controlled documentation that meets the regulator’s expectations", "A set of user stories with acceptance criteria from the backlog", "A short presentation to the regulator covering the main requirements", "A prototype walkthrough, so the regulator can see how the interface behaves"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The format of business analysis communication depends on the audience and purpose. Regulatory submissions need formal, controlled documentation." },
{ id: "CP-EC-05", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "A senior stakeholder keeps reopening decisions that were agreed weeks ago, which keeps causing rework. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Refuse to discuss decisions once they are made, to protect the schedule", "Re-do the work each time, since keeping senior stakeholders satisfied is critical", "Hold a lessons learned session on why decisions keep being reopened", "Use the decision record, check for new information, and route real changes through change control"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Managing stakeholder collaboration includes keeping agreements visible. New information may justify a change, but through the agreed process, not repeated informal reopening." },
{ id: "CP-EC-06", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation",
  question: "The team can’t agree which of two checkout designs will convert better. It shows each design to a random half of website visitors for two weeks and compares the results. What type of elicitation is this?",
  options: ["Research", "Collaborative", "Experiment", "Observation"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Experiments, such as A/B tests, proofs of concept and prototypes, produce information that can’t be found by asking people or studying documents." },
{ id: "CP-EC-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "Claims handlers say a claim takes about two days to process. System logs show a median of six days. What is the BEST way to proceed?",
  options: ["Trust the handlers’ estimate, because they do the work every day", "Trust the system logs, because recorded data is more reliable than opinions", "Investigate the gap, e.g. by observation and follow-up questions", "Use the average of the two figures as the baseline for the new process"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Combining sources (triangulation) strengthens elicitation. The difference may come from waiting time the handlers don’t see; that insight only appears if the gap is explored." },

/* RLCM */
{ id: "CP-RLCM-01", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements · BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "The sponsor wants to drop a requirement to save time. Traceability shows that four other requirements and two reports depend on it. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Drop it, because the sponsor has authority over scope", "Raise a validation issue, since the sponsor no longer sees its value", "Drop it together with all the dependent requirements", "Show the sponsor what depends on it and the impact of removing it"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Traceability supports impact analysis. Showing what depends on a requirement lets decision makers understand the real consequences before agreeing to remove it." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-02", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "General practice, not spelled out in BABOK; closest: BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Requirements are scored from 1 to 5 on benefit (weight 50%), penalty (30%) and time sensitivity (20%). R1: 4, 2, 5. R2: 3, 5, 2. R3: 5, 1, 3. R4: 2, 4, 5. Which ranks highest?",
  options: ["R1", "R2", "R3", "R4"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "R1 = 2.0 + 0.6 + 1.0 = 3.6. R2 = 1.5 + 1.5 + 0.4 = 3.4. R3 = 2.5 + 0.3 + 0.6 = 3.4. R4 = 1.0 + 1.2 + 1.0 = 3.2. R1 has the highest weighted score." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "Two weeks before a release, a regulator announces a rule change that takes effect on the release date. What should the business analyst expect the governance approach to provide?",
  options: ["A rule that no changes are allowed within a month of release", "Delaying the release until the next planning cycle", "A new business case for the change before it is considered", "An expedited path for urgent changes, with impact assessment and decision"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Urgency is a factor in assessing changes. A good governance approach covers how urgent changes are assessed and decided quickly, without skipping impact analysis or authority." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-04", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements · BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "The only stakeholder authorized to approve a set of requirements is on extended leave, and the deadline is next week. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Use the delegation or alternate approver defined in the governance approach, or escalate to agree one", "Replace approval with a peer verification review", "Ask the most senior person available to approve them, whatever their role", "Start development and seek approval later"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Approval should come from someone with the authority. Governance should define delegation; if it doesn’t, the analyst escalates to agree one rather than assuming approval." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-05", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "After release, the business analyst finds that the delivered discount logic differs slightly from the approved requirement. The business has accepted the delivered behaviour. What should the analyst do?",
  options: ["Raise a defect so the solution matches the original requirement", "Leave the requirement and traces as they are, since the business is happy with the result", "Delete the requirement, since it was not delivered as written", "Update the requirement and traces to match, and record the decision"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Maintaining requirements means keeping them accurate. If the business accepted the delivered behaviour, the requirement should be updated so it can be relied on and reused." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-06", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements · BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Marketing and operations keep arguing over priorities, even though prioritization criteria were agreed at the start. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Choose a winner each time, based on the analyst’s judgement of value", "Apply the agreed criteria openly and escalate through governance", "Prioritize marketing’s items, since marketing brings in revenue", "Let developers choose based on effort, to keep things moving"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Agreed prioritization criteria make decisions transparent and repeatable. When stakeholders still disagree, the governance approach defines who decides." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements · BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "The company wants to build a library of requirements that several product teams can reuse. What matters MOST for this to work?",
  options: ["Requirements written to apply across products, clearly named, current and easy to find", "Each requirement copied into every team’s backlog, so they are visible", "Requirements that include each product’s design details for completeness", "Requirements approved again by each team that wants to use them"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Reusable requirements need the right level of abstraction, clear names and definitions, up-to-date maintenance and a shared repository." },

/* SA */
{ id: "CP-SA-01", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.6 Business Capability Analysis",
  question: "The business analyst colours a business capability map to show which capabilities perform poorly and which matter most to the strategy. What is this view MOST useful for?",
  options: ["Identifying the root cause of poor performance", "Mapping which departments own each capability", "Writing user stories for each capability", "Showing where change investment should focus"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Business capability analysis, often shown as a heat map, highlights the capabilities that are both important and underperforming, which helps target investment." },
{ id: "CP-SA-02", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A new online claims service depends on a document-scanning platform that another team will only deliver in nine months. The sponsor wants value sooner. What should the change strategy include?",
  options: ["A single release once the scanning platform is ready, to avoid rework", "Transition states that deliver value first, adding scanning when it arrives", "Cancel the online service until the scanning platform is available", "Build a separate scanning platform within the claims initiative to remove the dependency"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Transition states let an organization deliver value in steps while working around dependencies, instead of waiting for everything to be ready at once." },
{ id: "CP-SA-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "An investment costs €100,000 today and returns €60,000 at the end of each of the next two years. At a 10% discount rate, what is the net present value (NPV), approximately?",
  options: ["€20,000", "−€4,100", "€9,100", "€4,100"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "PV = 60,000/1.1 + 60,000/1.21 = 54,545 + 49,587 = 104,132. NPV = 104,132 − 100,000 ≈ €4,100. Positive but small, because of discounting." },
{ id: "CP-SA-04", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "After all planned mitigations, the remaining risk of a new trading platform is still well above what the board says it will tolerate. What should the business analyst recommend?",
  options: ["Proceed, since all planned mitigations have now been applied", "Hand the residual risk to the project manager to manage during delivery", "Proceed and review the risk again once the platform is live", "Make the residual risk clear and recommend not proceeding as planned, or an alternative"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Assess Risks compares risk with the organization’s tolerance. If residual risk exceeds tolerance, the recommendation should reflect that, possibly a different approach or not proceeding." },
{ id: "CP-SA-05", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A new end-to-end returns process cuts across three departments, but nobody has been named to own the process after launch. What does this say about enterprise readiness?",
  options: ["A readiness gap: ownership must be settled for the change to last", "Not much yet; process ownership is usually decided once the change is live", "The process should be split into three departmental processes", "IT should own the process, since it runs on a system"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Enterprise readiness includes whether the organization can operate and sustain the change. A process without an owner is a common reason changes fade after launch." },
{ id: "CP-SA-06", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution · BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "When comparing change strategy options, the business analyst notes that choosing option A ties up the same team needed for another high-value initiative next year. What cost is the analyst considering?",
  options: ["Sunk cost", "Fixed cost", "Opportunity cost", "Operating cost"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Opportunity cost is the value of the alternatives given up by choosing an option. It belongs in the comparison even though it doesn’t appear in a budget." },
{ id: "CP-SA-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.38 Risk Analysis and Management",
  question: "A competitor’s product recall creates a short-term chance to win its customers. The company decides to bring its own launch forward and take on extra delivery risk to capture this. Which risk response is this?",
  options: ["Increase", "Avoid", "Transfer", "Accept"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "For positive risks (opportunities), increasing means taking on more risk to pursue the opportunity. Avoid, transfer and mitigate are mainly responses to negative risks." },

/* RADD */
{ id: "CP-RADD-01", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "The process view says an insurance policy can be cancelled by a broker, but the roles and permissions view gives brokers no cancel permission. What does this show about the requirements architecture?",
  options: ["Nothing; different views are allowed to say different things", "The two views are at different levels of abstraction", "The views are inconsistent and must be reconciled", "Brokers should be removed from the process"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Defining the requirements architecture includes checking that views are consistent with each other. Conflicts between viewpoints show gaps or errors that must be resolved." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-02", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "When allocating requirements to releases, the business analyst puts a low-value data migration in release 1, because three high-value release-2 features depend on it. What principle is the analyst applying?",
  options: ["Allocation should follow value only", "Allocation considers dependencies as well as value, so value can be realized", "The cheapest work goes first, so early releases cost less", "Allocation should follow the stability of each requirement"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Requirements allocation weighs value, dependencies, constraints and the capacity of each component or release, so the expected value can actually be delivered." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "While defining design options for a new sales system, the business analyst suggests giving field staff live stock levels on their tablets, which they currently get by phoning the warehouse. What kind of improvement opportunity is this?",
  options: ["Improving access to information", "Eliminating redundancy", "Identifying additional capabilities for later use", "Reducing interface complexity"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Improving access to information is one of the improvement opportunities considered when defining design options, alongside increasing efficiency and identifying additional capabilities." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-04", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "When analyzing the potential value of design options, which costs are MOST often underestimated?",
  options: ["The license price, since vendors often add charges late", "The cost of the business analysis work itself", "The steering committee’s time and the cost of business analysis", "Ongoing operating and support costs, and opportunity cost"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Expected costs include acquisition and implementation, but also ongoing operation, support and opportunity costs, which are easily missed and change which option is best." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-05", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.30 Non-Functional Requirements Analysis · BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Stakeholders ask for 99.99% availability for an internal reporting tool. Moving from 99.9% to 99.99% would triple the infrastructure cost. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Write the requirement as asked, since stakeholders know how critical the tool is", "Lower it to 99% to save money, since the tool is only internal", "Show the cost and benefit of each level so those with authority can decide", "Let the infrastructure team decide, since availability is a technical matter"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Non-functional requirements have costs. Making the trade-off explicit, with the impact of each option, lets decision makers choose a level that matches the actual need." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-06", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.15 Data Modelling",
  question: "A policy can exist before any claim is made against it, and every claim belongs to exactly one policy. In the data model, what is the minimum number of claims a policy can be related to?",
  options: ["Zero", "One", "Many", "It depends on the claim type"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Cardinality is the minimum and maximum number of occurrences an entity can be related to, typically zero, one or many. A policy may have no claims (minimum zero) or many (maximum many), while each claim relates to exactly one policy." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.44 State Modelling",
  question: "A state model for orders allows the transition Shipped → Draft. Business stakeholders say a shipped order can never be edited. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Correct the invalid transition and confirm the allowed ones with stakeholders", "Keep the transition, in case it is needed for corrections later", "Document it as a known issue for the next release", "Ask developers whether the transition is technically possible"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "State models make allowed and disallowed transitions explicit. An invalid transition is a modelling error to correct and confirm, not a technical question." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-08", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "General practice, not spelled out in BABOK; closest: BABOK v3 10.17 Decision Modelling",
  question: "A decision table has three conditions: customer type (2 values), order channel (2 values) and region (3 values). How many rules does a complete table need, before any simplification?",
  options: ["7", "6", "9", "12"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A complete decision table covers every combination: 2 × 2 × 3 = 12 rules. Checking the count is a quick way to find missing combinations." },

/* SE */
{ id: "CP-SE-01", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A wellness program aims to improve employee health, which the company cannot measure directly for privacy reasons. What can the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Drop the measurement, since health can’t be measured directly", "Use participation numbers only, since they are easy to collect and objective", "Track the program’s spend against budget as the main value measure", "Agree proxy measures, such as sick-day trends, and note their limits"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "When value can’t be measured directly, suitable proxy measures can be defined and validated with stakeholders, as long as their limits are understood." },
{ id: "CP-SE-02", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "A new online-ordering feature launched in October. Orders were up 30% in December compared with October. What should the business analyst check before giving the feature the credit?",
  options: ["Seasonal effects, e.g. compare with last December", "Nothing further; the increase comes from the new feature", "Only the number of website visitors over the same period", "Whether the feature passed user acceptance testing"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Analyzing performance measures includes looking at trends and accuracy. Seasonal patterns can easily be mistaken for solution impact." },
{ id: "CP-SE-03", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "A self-service portal shows wrong delivery dates whenever the separate stock service is slow to respond. What is the business analyst examining?",
  options: ["An enterprise culture issue in the support team", "A dependency between internal solution components", "The impact of the delivery dates on customer satisfaction", "An external influencer on the portal"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Assessing solution limitations includes identifying internal component dependencies, where one part of the solution limits the value another can deliver." },
{ id: "CP-SE-04", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "Staff avoid a new digital approval tool because they believe “a signature on paper is safer if something goes wrong.” Which assessment addresses this?",
  options: ["Interface analysis", "Solution performance measurement", "Enterprise culture assessment", "Data modelling"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An enterprise culture assessment looks at beliefs, values and norms that affect whether a solution is accepted and used." },
{ id: "CP-SE-05", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A contact center measures success by calls handled per hour. Agents now rush calls, and customer problems are left unresolved. What should the business analyst recommend?",
  options: ["Run root cause analysis on each unresolved customer problem", "Keep the measure, since it is easy to collect and agents understand it", "Keep the current measure and add coaching, so agents learn to handle calls faster", "Adjust the measures to reflect value, e.g. add first-contact resolution"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Adjusting solution performance measures is a valid recommendation when existing measures drive the wrong behaviour or don’t reflect value." },
{ id: "CP-SE-06", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A legacy reporting system is to be retired. Before recommending a date, what should the business analyst check?",
  options: ["How many people logged in last month, since a small user base means the retirement has low impact", "Only the remaining license period, to avoid paying for unused time", "Whether the vendor still supports it, since unsupported systems have to go", "Which systems, processes and users still depend on it, and how they’ll be served"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Retiring a solution means identifying everything that depends on it, including data, interfaces, processes and people, and planning how those needs will be met." },
{ id: "CP-SE-07", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "General practice, not spelled out in BABOK; closest: BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "After go-live, the project team is disbanded. Who should usually be accountable for making sure the expected benefits are realized?",
  options: ["The vendor, since it delivered the solution and knows it best", "The business owner or sponsor, supported by the business analyst", "The project manager, who stays accountable after the project closes", "The business analyst, who defined the expected benefits"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Benefits are realized in operations, so the business owner is accountable. The business analyst helps define, measure and analyze performance and recommend actions." },

/* --- Case study: Northway Logistics --- */
{ id: "K-LOG-1", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy", case: "CASE-LOGISTICS",
  question: "Which change strategy is BEST supported by the case?",
  options: ["Switch all eleven warehouses to one system in October, to start saving on licenses before year end", "A pilot in one warehouse after the peak season, then a phased rollout, with SLA risks monitored at each step", "Keep both systems permanently and integrate them", "Phase the rollout, largest warehouse first"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The case has high peak-season risk, SLA penalties, limited integration staff and differing processes. A post-peak pilot and phased rollout manage those risks while still working toward the savings." },
{ id: "K-LOG-2", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.31 Observation · BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops", case: "CASE-LOGISTICS",
  question: "The two operations directors describe “picking” in very different ways. Which elicitation approach is MOST effective?",
  options: ["Ask each director to write a description and use the clearer one", "Use the Northway process manual, since Northway is the acquirer", "Observe picking at both companies, then a joint workshop to agree one process", "Survey all pickers about which method they prefer"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Observation shows how the work is really done in each company; a facilitated workshop then reconciles the two into an agreed process. Taking one side’s description would build in bias." },
{ id: "K-LOG-3", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement", case: "CASE-LOGISTICS",
  question: "How should the business analyst plan engagement with the union?",
  options: ["Engage after the system is chosen, to explain the changes to staff", "Leave union matters to HR, since they are not a business analysis concern", "Engage only if the union raises concerns, to avoid slowing things down", "Treat the union as a key stakeholder and plan early, regular engagement"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholder analysis should identify parties with influence over the change. Early engagement with the union reduces the risk of late objections and helps shape the new process." },
{ id: "K-LOG-4", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements", case: "CASE-LOGISTICS",
  question: "Requirements that protect same-day dispatch for the two largest customers are ranked low by the CFO, because they add cost. How should the business analyst argue for their priority?",
  options: ["Point to the penalty: missed SLAs mean contractual penalties and lost customers", "Agree with the CFO, since the CFO sponsors the integration", "Rank them high, because the operations directors support them", "Defer them to a later phase, after the single WMS is live"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Penalty is a basis for prioritization: the consequences of not meeting a requirement, here SLA penalties and customer loss, can outweigh the cost of meeting it." },
{ id: "K-LOG-5", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution", case: "CASE-LOGISTICS",
  question: "The CFO wants to keep whichever of the two current warehouse systems is cheaper to license. What should the business analyst’s recommendation be based on?",
  options: ["License cost only, because license savings are the sponsor’s stated objective", "Which system the IT team prefers, since they will have to support it", "The full expected value of each option: fit, transition and integration cost, SLA risk and savings", "Whichever system Northway already uses, since Northway is the acquiring company"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Recommending a solution means comparing the expected benefits and full costs of each option. License savings matter, but transition cost, fit and risk can change which option delivers the most value." },
/* ===================== ECBA · batch 2 (BABOK-checked) ===================== */
{ id: "E-UBA-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "Which of these is an example of “business analysis information”?",
  options: ["Elicitation results, requirements and design options", "Only approved requirements documents", "Information produced by IT systems, such as logs and reports", "The project schedule and budget kept by the project manager"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business analysis information is any information, at any level of detail, that business analysis work uses or produces, such as elicitation results, requirements, designs and solution options." },
{ id: "E-UBA-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "In BABOK terms, what is an enterprise?",
  options: ["Any company with more than 250 employees spread across several sites", "One or more organizations, plus the solutions they rely on, with shared goals", "The IT department together with all of the systems that it manages", "A single business unit operating inside a larger group of companies"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "An enterprise is a set of one or more organizations plus the solutions (processes, tools, information) they use together toward common goals. Size and IT don’t define it." },
{ id: "E-UBA-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "How does the BABOK Guide define risk?",
  options: ["Any problem that has already happened on a project", "The chance that a project finishes late or over budget", "How uncertainty affects the value of a change, solution or enterprise", "A threat that the sponsor has formally recorded in the risk register"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK frames risk as the effect uncertainty has on value, whether that value belongs to a change, a solution or the wider enterprise. Problems that have already happened are issues, not risks, and risk is wider than schedule or budget." },
{ id: "E-UBA-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "Which of these is a valid way to use the BACCM while working on an initiative?",
  options: ["Calculating the return on investment of a change", "Assigning tasks to team members on a project plan", "Choosing the software vendor for a solution", "Evaluating the concepts and their relationships at any point"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The BACCM can describe the profession, give people shared terms, and help check the concepts and how they relate at any point in the work. It isn’t a planning or financial tool." },
{ id: "E-UBA-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.2 What is Business Analysis?",
  question: "A colleague says business analysis only happens inside formal projects. Which statement is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["It can happen within projects or as ongoing enterprise improvement", "Correct; outside projects the work is called operations management", "That’s right; business analysis needs a project charter and a project manager", "Business analysis only happens in strategy teams, outside projects"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business analysis can take place inside a project, or as part of the enterprise’s ongoing evolution and continuous improvement." },
{ id: "E-UBA-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "In the BABOK Guide, what do the perspectives (such as Agile or Business Intelligence) describe?",
  options: ["Extra tasks that only certified analysts perform, on top of the knowledge area tasks", "How business analysis is practised in specific contexts or types of initiative", "The personal opinions of different stakeholder groups", "Alternative definitions of business analysis that replace the core model"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Perspectives focus on business analysis in a particular context, such as agile, business intelligence, IT, business architecture or business process management. They complement the knowledge areas rather than replace them." },
{ id: "E-UBA-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "In the BABOK Guide, what are underlying competencies?",
  options: ["The tasks business analysts must complete before go-live", "The certifications a business analyst should hold", "Skills, knowledge and characteristics that support BA work", "The minimum years of experience for each certification level"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Underlying competencies are the skills, knowledge and characteristics, such as analytical thinking, communication and interaction skills, that support the effective performance of business analysis." },
{ id: "E-UBA-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "How do requirements and designs usually relate as work progresses?",
  options: ["Requirements must be fully approved before any design is drawn", "Designs replace requirements once development starts", "Designs come first; requirements are then written to fit", "They evolve together: designs can reveal new requirements"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Requirements and designs form a cycle. Requirements inform designs, and reviewing designs often uncovers more requirements, so both are refined over time." },
{ id: "E-UBA-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which BABOK knowledge area covers defining the future state and the strategy to get there?",
  options: ["Strategy Analysis", "Elicitation and Collaboration", "Requirements Life Cycle Management", "Business Analysis Planning and Monitoring"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Strategy Analysis covers analyzing the current state, defining the future state, assessing risks and defining the change strategy." },
{ id: "E-UBA-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which knowledge area covers assessing a solution that is already in use and recommending ways to increase its value?",
  options: ["Requirements Analysis and Design Definition", "Strategy Analysis", "Solution Evaluation", "Elicitation and Collaboration"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Solution Evaluation covers measuring and analyzing the performance of a solution in use, finding its limitations and recommending actions to increase its value." },
{ id: "E-UBA-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Must the BABOK tasks be performed in a fixed sequence, starting with planning?",
  options: ["Yes, each knowledge area is a phase that must finish before the next starts", "Yes, in predictive approaches; in adaptive ones the order is free", "No; tasks can be done in any order, provided the inputs they need are available", "No; tasks are optional and can be skipped whenever time is short"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Knowledge areas aren’t phases. Tasks can happen in any order once the inputs they need are available, and work often begins with analyzing the current state or measuring solution performance." },
{ id: "E-UBA-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "A team claims a project is “done” because the solution was delivered on time. Which BACCM concept shows why that may not be enough?",
  options: ["Change: the transformation has been completed on schedule", "Value: whether stakeholders actually benefit in their context", "Solution: a specific way to meet the need was delivered", "Context: the environment stayed stable during delivery"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A delivered solution only matters if it creates value for stakeholders in their context. Delivery on time says nothing about whether value was realized." },

{ id: "E-MIND-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "Stakeholders are stuck choosing between the same two options. The business analyst runs a session to generate completely new alternatives before deciding. Which underlying competency is this?",
  options: ["Creative thinking", "Decision making", "Conceptual thinking", "Teamwork"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Creative thinking is about generating new ideas, approaches and alternatives, and helping others think creatively about problems and opportunities." },
{ id: "E-MIND-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "A business analyst receives dozens of interview notes, reports and emails and works out how the pieces fit together into a bigger picture. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Visual thinking", "Learning", "Conceptual thinking", "Organization and time management"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Conceptual thinking helps business analysts understand how large amounts of disparate information fit into a larger picture, which details matter, and how they connect." },
{ id: "E-MIND-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "A business analyst turns a complicated set of business rules into a simple diagram so stakeholders can understand it quickly. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Visual thinking", "Creative thinking", "Personal accountability", "Negotiation and conflict resolution"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Visual thinking is the ability to turn complex concepts into understandable visual representations, which helps engage stakeholders." },
{ id: "E-MIND-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "A business analyst chases down every open question after a workshop and plans the work to make sure what is delivered matches the business need. Which behavioural characteristic is this?",
  options: ["Adaptability", "Personal accountability", "Trustworthiness", "Ethics"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Personal accountability includes planning business analysis work to meet goals, following up on all leads and loose ends, and making sure the value delivered is aligned with business needs." },
{ id: "E-MIND-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "Staff only share the real reasons for errors with the business analyst once they are confident the information won’t be used against them. Which characteristic is MOST important here?",
  options: ["Organization and time management", "Creative thinking", "Teaching", "Trustworthiness"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Earning stakeholders’ trust helps business analysts elicit information on sensitive issues and gives stakeholders confidence that their input will be treated fairly." },
{ id: "E-MIND-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "After go-live, the business analyst runs short sessions that help users understand the new business rules and checks that the information sticks. Which interaction skill is this?",
  options: ["Facilitation", "Teamwork", "Teaching", "Leadership and influencing"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Teaching helps business analysts communicate information, concepts and issues so stakeholders understand and retain them." },
{ id: "E-MIND-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "Stakeholders must pick one of three vendors. The business analyst helps them agree criteria, compare the options against them and understand the trade-offs. Which competency is the analyst using MOST?",
  options: ["Visual thinking", "Decision making", "Learning", "Creative thinking"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Decision making covers understanding the criteria involved in a decision and helping others make better decisions, including comparing alternatives and trade-offs." },
{ id: "E-MIND-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst keeps asking how a proposed change will feel for the end customer, not just for internal teams. Which shared value from The Business Analysis Standard does this reflect?",
  options: ["Courage", "Continuous learning", "Customer focus", "Improvement"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Customer focus means keeping real user needs at the center of the work. The Standard lists it among the shared values of an effective business analysis mindset." },

{ id: "E-IMPL-13", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.3 Who is a Business Analyst?",
  question: "Which of these job titles could belong to someone performing business analysis?",
  options: ["Business analyst, since that is the title the BABOK Guide is written for", "Product owner, process analyst, data analyst and many others", "Only “requirements engineer” or “systems analyst”", "Roles in the IT department, such as developer or architect"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Anyone who performs business analysis tasks is doing business analysis. BABOK lists many titles, including business architect, data analyst, process analyst, product manager, product owner and systems analyst." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "In an adaptive approach, which requirements documentation is typically mandatory?",
  options: ["A full, signed-off requirements specification before build", "Detailed use cases for every feature in the backlog", "A complete data model before any development starts", "Often just a prioritized requirements list"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Adaptive approaches favour defining requirements through team interaction and feedback. Mandatory representations are often limited to a prioritized list; further models are created at the team’s discretion." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.3 Foundational Techniques · BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops",
  question: "A business analyst brings eight stakeholders together for a structured half-day session to agree the steps of a new process. Which foundational technique is this?",
  options: ["Workshops", "Backlog management", "Data storytelling", "Customer journey mapping"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Workshops bring stakeholders together to collaborate on a defined goal, such as agreeing a process. The Standard lists workshops/interviews as a foundational technique." },

{ id: "E-CHG-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Which information is typically included in a change request to support the decision?",
  options: ["The test cases that will verify the change", "Benefits, risks, priority and possible courses of action", "A detailed design for the change", "The original approved requirements document"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Change requests usually include the benefits, risks, priority and one or more courses of action, so decision makers can choose the option that best serves the initiative." },
{ id: "E-CHG-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "After a proposed change has been assessed, what can the authorized stakeholders decide?",
  options: ["Approve it or send it back for more analysis", "Reprioritize it", "Approve, deny or defer it", "Send it back to the requester without a decision"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Depending on the approach, authorized stakeholders may approve, deny or defer a proposed change. The decision and its impacts are then documented and communicated." },
{ id: "E-CHG-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Why might a change strategy include transition states?",
  options: ["Because the future state can’t be reached in one step", "So each release can be approved under a separate business case", "Because every change must have at least three releases", "So that the current state doesn’t have to be analyzed"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "When the future state can’t be reached at once, because of constraints, dependencies or the need to deliver value early, the change strategy defines intermediate transition states." },
{ id: "E-CHG-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A business analyst proposes delivering the change strategy as three projects followed by a continuous improvement effort. Is that consistent with BABOK?",
  options: ["No; a change strategy is delivered as a single project, with one plan and one budget", "Yes; a change strategy can run as several projects or improvement efforts in turn", "No; continuous improvement falls outside business analysis", "Yes, provided each project has its own separate business case and sponsor"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A change strategy can be one initiative split into smaller changes, run as a series of projects or as continuous improvement, because one change may not meet the whole need." },
{ id: "E-CHG-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Besides its capacity to make a change, what else does an enterprise readiness assessment look at?",
  options: ["Mainly whether the budget for the change is approved", "Mainly whether IT has enough people to build the solution", "Mainly whether the sponsor and steering committee agree", "Ability to use and sustain it, cultural and operational readiness, and time to value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Readiness covers the capacity to make the change and to use and sustain the solution, cultural and operational readiness, the timeline to value, and available resources." },

{ id: "E-NEED-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A sales manager says she needs weekly margin data by region to decide where to focus her team. Where does this business need come from?",
  options: ["An external driver", "Middle management", "A strategic goal from the top", "A bottom-up process failure"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Needs come from middle management when a manager needs better information to make decisions, or has to take on extra work to meet objectives." },
{ id: "E-NEED-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Which output of analyzing the current state describes the problem or opportunity and why a change is needed?",
  options: ["Design options", "Solution requirements", "Business requirements", "Transition requirements"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Analyze Current State produces the current state description and business requirements, which describe the need and why change is required." },
{ id: "E-NEED-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis",
  question: "A team draws a diagram with the problem at the head and possible causes grouped on branches such as people, process and technology. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Mind mapping of the problem and its themes", "State modelling", "Fishbone diagram (root cause analysis)", "Data flow diagram"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram groups possible causes of a problem into categories. With the five whys, it is one of the root cause analysis techniques." },
{ id: "E-NEED-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "According to BABOK, what must a solution to a business need have the potential to do?",
  options: ["Generate benefits or avoid losses", "Use new technology that the enterprise doesn’t already have", "Be delivered within a single budget year", "Satisfy every stakeholder group equally"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A solution must have the potential to generate benefits or avoid losses. Neither technology nor equal satisfaction is required." },
{ id: "E-NEED-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases",
  question: "What is the purpose of the “need assessment” part of a business case?",
  options: ["To list every feature of the preferred solution", "To set out the project schedule", "To define the problem or opportunity the investment would address", "To record who approved the business case"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A business case starts by identifying the need: the problem or opportunity. Desired outcomes, alternatives and a recommended solution follow." },

{ id: "E-SOL-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "A design option for a new returns service describes the new refund policy, the steps staff follow, who does each step, and the software used. What are these?",
  options: ["Transition states for the service", "Risk responses for the option", "Design elements of the design option", "Stakeholder requirements for the service"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Design options are described by design elements, which may include policies and rules, business processes, people and roles, operational decisions, software and organizational structures." },
{ id: "E-SOL-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "When recommending a design option, which factors does BABOK say to consider besides value?",
  options: ["Available resources, constraints, and dependencies", "The sponsor’s preference and the vendor’s delivery date", "Stakeholder attitudes and communication needs", "The level of abstraction of each option’s models"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "When assessing design options, business analysts consider available resources, constraints on the solution, and dependencies between requirements." },
{ id: "E-SOL-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "After analyzing all design options, none of them delivers enough value for its cost. What may the business analyst recommend?",
  options: ["The cheapest option, to limit losses", "The option with the highest benefits", "A new combined option made of the best parts of all three", "Doing nothing, since BABOK notes this can be the best recommendation"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK notes that the best recommendation may be to do nothing. A recommendation should follow value, not a requirement to always pick something." },
{ id: "E-SOL-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "A solution isn’t delivering the expected value, and the business analyst looks for the underlying reasons within the solution itself. Which activity is this?",
  options: ["Defining the future state of the solution", "Investigating solution problems, e.g. root cause analysis", "Planning stakeholder engagement for the fix", "Defining the requirements architecture again"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Assess Solution Limitations includes investigating solution problems, often with root cause analysis, to find why a solution isn’t performing as expected." },
{ id: "E-SOL-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A new CRM includes features the company doesn’t need today but expects to use next year. What does BABOK call recognizing this?",
  options: ["Identifying additional capabilities", "Scope creep that should be removed", "Eliminating redundancy across systems", "A solution limitation to report"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Identifying additional capabilities means recognizing capabilities beyond the current requirements that may provide future value." },

{ id: "E-STK-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Which stakeholder role looks after a system day to day once it is running?",
  options: ["Supplier", "Sponsor", "Domain subject matter expert", "Operational support"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Operational support handles the daily running and upkeep of a system or product, for example the help desk or operations staff." },
{ id: "E-STK-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "An external company provides the cloud platform the new solution will run on. Which stakeholder role does it play?",
  options: ["Customer", "Regulator", "Supplier", "End user"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A supplier is an outside provider of products or services to the organization." },
{ id: "E-STK-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "When monitoring stakeholder engagement, which risk should the business analyst watch for?",
  options: ["Stakeholders preferring group workshops", "Stakeholders being diverted to other work", "Stakeholders asking to review models before sign-off", "Stakeholders agreeing too quickly to commitments"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Business analysts monitor participation and watch for risks such as stakeholders being diverted to other work, which can delay or weaken business analysis results." },
{ id: "E-STK-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "In a RACI matrix, how many people should be Accountable for a given task?",
  options: ["Exactly one", "At least two, for back-up", "Everyone who does the work", "As many as the sponsor chooses"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The Accountable person is the one who ultimately answers for the task and makes the decision. Only one person gets this role." },
{ id: "E-STK-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "On a stakeholder matrix, the change has a big impact on a group of call center agents, but they have little influence over it. What does BABOK suggest?",
  options: ["Work closely with them as key players in the change", "Make sure they remain satisfied with the change", "Keep them informed; they may feel a lack of control", "Monitor them occasionally, in case their interest or influence changes"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Low influence but high impact: keep them informed, as they are likely to be concerned and may feel a lack of control over the change." },

{ id: "E-VAL-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "When estimating the expected benefits of a design option, what should the business analyst include?",
  options: ["The financial benefits that appear in next year’s budget", "Only benefits that start in the first year", "Both tangible and intangible benefits", "The benefits the vendor has committed to in the contract"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Expected benefits include tangible and intangible benefits. Intangible ones, such as improved morale, still contribute to value even if they are harder to measure." },
{ id: "E-VAL-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Choosing design option A uses the team and budget that could otherwise have delivered option B. What is the value of option B in this decision called?",
  options: ["Sunk cost", "Opportunity cost", "Fixed cost", "Residual risk"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Opportunity cost is the value of the best option you didn’t choose. BABOK says it belongs in estimates of what a change really costs." },
{ id: "E-VAL-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "A change costs €50,000 and is expected to produce €80,000 in total benefits. Using BABOK’s ROI formula, what is the return on investment?",
  options: ["30%", "60%", "62.5%", "160%"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "ROI is the total benefits minus the investment, divided by the investment: (80,000 − 50,000) ÷ 50,000 = 60%." },
{ id: "E-VAL-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "Which measure looks at all the costs of a solution over its whole life, including running, support and maintenance, not just the purchase price?",
  options: ["Payback period", "Return on investment", "Net present value", "Total cost of ownership"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Total cost of ownership covers the cost to acquire, run and maintain a solution over its life, which often differs a lot from the initial price." },
{ id: "E-VAL-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.1 Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria",
  question: "Stakeholders list the qualities that make a solution valuable to them, such as response time, ease of use and the ability to produce certain reports. What are these called?",
  options: ["Transition requirements", "Value attributes", "Business rules", "Risk factors"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Value attributes are the qualities of a solution that decide, or strongly shape, how valuable it is to stakeholders. They feed acceptance and evaluation criteria." },

{ id: "E-CTX-14", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A company plans to enter a new industry where value is created in very different ways. Which external influencer is MOST relevant?",
  options: ["Internal assets", "Industry structure", "Organizational culture", "Business rules"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Industry structure describes how value is created within an industry. It matters especially when a change involves entering a new industry." },
{ id: "E-CTX-15", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Which of these is an external influencer on an organization?",
  options: ["Its internal approval policy for spending", "The skills and experience of its own staff", "Its company values statement", "Customers’ price sensitivity and buying power"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "External influencers include industry structure, competitors, customers, suppliers, the political and regulatory environment, technology and macroeconomic factors." },
{ id: "E-CTX-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "While analyzing the current state, the business analyst looks at how the organization’s capabilities, processes, information and structure fit together. Which element of the current state is this?",
  options: ["Business architecture", "External influencers", "Internal assets", "Technology and infrastructure"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business architecture describes how the elements of the enterprise relate and work together. Understanding it helps ensure a change fits the whole organization." },
{ id: "E-CTX-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "When defining the future state, the business analyst agrees with stakeholders which kinds of solutions are open for consideration and which are ruled out. What is being defined?",
  options: ["The solution scope", "The scope of the solution space", "The business architecture", "The change strategy"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Defining the scope of the solution space sets which kinds of options will be considered. The solution scope comes later, once a change strategy is chosen." },
{ id: "E-CTX-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model · BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Halfway through an initiative, the company is acquired, and the new owner has very different policies. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Continue as planned, since context was analyzed at the start", "Pause all work until the acquisition is fully complete and the new owner gives direction", "Reassess the need, solution and stakeholders for the new context", "Ask the sponsor to approve a revised schedule and budget"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Context can change during an initiative. Because the core concepts affect each other, a change in context means reassessing the need, stakeholders, solution and value." },

/* ===================== CCBA · batch 2 (BABOK-checked) ===================== */
{ id: "CC-BAPM-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "Which factor does BABOK list as increasing the complexity of an initiative when planning the business analysis approach?",
  options: ["How many business areas or systems the change touches", "The level of formality chosen for documentation", "The sponsor’s seniority", "Whether the approach is predictive or adaptive"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Complexity rises with the size of the change, the number of business areas or systems involved, geographic and cultural spread, technical difficulty, and risks that could get in the way." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-11", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "According to BABOK, which of these makes a business analysis effort riskier?",
  options: ["A large number of documented business rules to be analyzed", "Low analyst experience and limited domain knowledge", "Use of a predictive approach with formal sign-offs", "A short initiative timeline set by the sponsor"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Factors affecting the risk of a business analysis effort include the business analyst’s experience, their domain knowledge, and stakeholders’ attitudes and availability, among others." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Which requirements attribute gives each requirement a unique identifier that is never reused, even if the requirement is moved or deleted?",
  options: ["Author", "Ownership", "Status", "Absolute reference"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The absolute reference is a unique ID that stays with a requirement for good: it isn’t reused even if the requirement moves, changes or is deleted." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "A requirement turns out to be ambiguous months after it was written. Which attribute tells the team who to consult?",
  options: ["Priority", "Author", "Complexity", "Urgency"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The author attribute tells the team whom to ask if a requirement later turns out to be unclear, ambiguous or conflicting." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-14", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Which attribute identifies the person or group that needs a requirement, or that will own the business result after implementation?",
  options: ["Ownership", "Source", "Stability", "Absolute reference"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Ownership records who needs the requirement, or who will own the business result once the solution is live." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "When designing the change control process, which decision is part of “determine the process for requesting changes”?",
  options: ["Which vendor will build each change, and at what cost", "The test cases each change must pass before it is released", "The design of the change request form", "What it covers, who can propose changes and how requests are made"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Determining the request process covers which requirements and designs are in scope, whether it applies to all or only some changes, when and by whom changes can be proposed, and how requests are communicated." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Which of these could serve as a measure of business analysis performance?",
  options: ["The number of review cycles before work is accepted", "The project’s earned value at each milestone", "The solution’s availability after go-live", "The sponsor’s satisfaction with the project schedule"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK suggests measures such as meeting due dates, the frequency of changes to work products, the number of review cycles, task efficiency, and qualitative feedback from stakeholders and peers." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-17", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "A business analyst plans one collaboration approach for internal teams and a different one for an external partner. Is that appropriate?",
  options: ["No; one approach must be used for all stakeholders, for fairness", "No; external partners shouldn’t be part of collaboration planning", "Yes; approaches can differ by stakeholder group and activity", "Yes, provided the sponsor approves each approach in writing"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK allows different ways of collaborating with internal and external stakeholders, and for different activities, so each group gets what suits it." },

{ id: "CC-EC-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "The business analyst presents a requirements package to six stakeholders at once, so issues can be discussed immediately. Which communication platform is this?",
  options: ["Individual collaboration", "Group collaboration", "Email or other non-verbal communication", "Formal documentation"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Group collaboration communicates the package to several relevant stakeholders at the same time and allows immediate discussion of the information and related issues." },
{ id: "CC-EC-11", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "A senior stakeholder needs a one-to-one walkthrough of the requirements to understand how they affect her department. Which communication platform fits BEST?",
  options: ["Group collaboration", "Email with the document attached", "Individual collaboration", "A recorded presentation"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Individual collaboration communicates the package to one stakeholder at a time, helping them understand the information when group sessions aren’t practical or suitable." },
{ id: "CC-EC-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "Which of these is something a business analyst checks when monitoring stakeholder engagement?",
  options: ["That elicitation results are confirmed promptly and agreed commitments are kept", "That stakeholders’ communication preferences are recorded in the plan", "That every stakeholder attends every session", "That stakeholders approve requirements without changes"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Monitoring engagement means checking that the right SMEs take part effectively, that attitudes and interest hold steady or improve, that results get confirmed promptly, and that agreements are kept." },
{ id: "CC-EC-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.18 Document Analysis",
  question: "What are the three main steps of document analysis in BABOK?",
  options: ["Plan the review, read every document, and archive copies of the documents", "Interview the authors, survey users, then observe the process", "Prepare, distribute, document", "Preparation, document review and analysis, and recording findings"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Document analysis involves preparation, reviewing and analyzing the documents, and recording the findings." },
{ id: "CC-EC-14", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.21 Focus Groups",
  question: "Which roles should be assigned to run a focus group?",
  options: ["A sponsor and a project manager", "A moderator and a recorder", "A facilitator and a timekeeper only", "A product owner and a scribe"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A focus group needs a moderator, who guides the discussion, and a recorder, who captures the participants’ comments." },
{ id: "CC-EC-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.25 Interviews",
  question: "In an unstructured interview, how are the questions handled?",
  options: ["There is no set format or order; questions follow the answers", "The interviewer reads a fixed list of questions in a fixed order", "Questions are sent ahead for written replies", "Only yes/no questions are used, to keep answers short"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "In an unstructured interview the interviewer has no predetermined format or order of questions; questions vary based on the interviewee’s responses." },
{ id: "CC-EC-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.31 Observation",
  question: "Before observing claims handlers, what should the business analyst do as part of preparing for observation?",
  options: ["Decide who, when, and what the observation should achieve", "Draft the future process so the handlers can be compared with it", "Ask the handlers to write down their process instead", "Skip detailed preparation, so the observation stays as natural as possible"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Observation starts with clear objectives and preparation: who will be observed, when, and what information is needed. Unprepared observation risks missing key activities." },
{ id: "CC-EC-17", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops",
  question: "In a workshop, who documents decisions in the agreed format and keeps track of items deferred during the session?",
  options: ["The facilitator", "The sponsor", "The scribe", "The timekeeper"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The scribe documents decisions in the format agreed beforehand and keeps track of items or issues deferred during the session. The facilitator keeps the session focused." },

{ id: "CC-RLCM-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "How does prioritization usually change as requirements are refined?",
  options: ["It is done once at the highest level and then frozen for stability", "It starts high-level and becomes more granular, adding bases as needed", "It is done once requirements are fully detailed, so the ranking is final", "It is handed to developers once requirements are detailed"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Prioritization is continual. It starts at a high level of abstraction and becomes more granular as requirements are refined, possibly using different bases at different stages." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "Why should requirements attributes, such as status and priority, be maintained along with the requirements themselves?",
  options: ["So the requirements can be verified as atomic and unambiguous", "So each requirement can be reused without further review", "Because attributes are only needed during elicitation sessions", "They help manage trade-offs, affected stakeholders and changes"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Attributes support ongoing management: they help with trade-offs, show who is affected by changes, and help assess the effect of a proposed change. They must be kept current to be useful." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-11", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance · BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "A change request includes several possible courses of action, including the requester’s preferred option and alternatives from other stakeholders. Why?",
  options: ["So decision makers can choose the option that best serves the initiative", "To share accountability", "So the business analyst can recommend the cheapest option", "So the requester can choose the outcome"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Identifying alternative courses of action, with their cost, time, benefits, risks and priority, lets decision makers choose the option that best serves the initiative." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Why should the business analyst keep accurate records of the current approval status of requirements?",
  options: ["So the business analyst can show how much work was done", "So everyone can see what is approved and ready to build", "Because approval records replace the need for traceability", "Because approval status sets each requirement’s priority"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Accurate approval records let stakeholders see what is currently approved and ready for implementation. An audit history of changes may also be valuable." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Before seeking approval, the business analyst confirms who has authority to approve each set of requirements and who only needs to be consulted. Which element of Approve Requirements is this?",
  options: ["Gain consensus", "Track and communicate approval", "Understand stakeholder roles", "Conflict and issue management"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Understanding stakeholder roles means knowing who has approval authority and whose input matters, so approval is sought from the right people." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-14", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "Which statement about how decisions on changes are communicated is MOST accurate?",
  options: ["Only the requester needs to be told the outcome", "Decisions can stay informal if the change is small", "Only approved changes need to be documented and shared", "All impacts and resolutions are documented and communicated"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Whatever is decided, the impacts and the resolution of a change analysis should be written down and shared with all stakeholders." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "Which requirements are MOST suitable for reuse on future initiatives?",
  options: ["Requirements that apply beyond the current solution", "Requirements that describe a specific screen layout in detail", "Requirements with the highest priority in the current release", "Requirements that include vendor-specific configuration settings"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Requirements that apply beyond a single solution, such as regulatory or enterprise-wide rules, are good candidates for reuse; design-specific detail limits reuse." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Where is traceability information typically kept?",
  options: ["In the data dictionary, next to each data element", "In a traceability repository, often a tool", "In the change log, with each approved change", "In the project schedule, linked to tasks"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Traceability is recorded in a repository, often a requirements management tool, so relationships can be maintained and queried." },

{ id: "CC-SA-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "Stakeholders accept that a change carries risk but want to go ahead while spending effort to reduce the likelihood of key risks. Which BABOK recommendation category is this?",
  options: ["Pursue the benefits regardless of the risk", "Do not pursue the benefits of the change", "Seek ways to increase the benefits to outweigh the risk", "Pursue the benefits while investing in reducing risk"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK’s risk recommendations include pursuing benefits regardless of risk, pursuing them while investing to reduce risk, increasing benefits to outweigh risk, managing opportunities, or not pursuing the change." },
{ id: "CC-SA-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "During current-state analysis, the business analyst notes a sharp rise in interest rates that is reducing customers’ ability to borrow. Which external influencer is this?",
  options: ["Industry structure", "Macroeconomic factors", "Internal assets", "Technology"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Macroeconomic factors, such as interest rates, inflation and economic cycles, are external influencers on the organization and its customers." },
{ id: "CC-SA-11", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A new law requires all lenders to run affordability checks. Which external influencer is this?",
  options: ["Political and regulatory environment", "Competitors", "Suppliers", "Business architecture"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Laws and regulations fall under the political and regulatory environment, one of the external influencers considered in current-state analysis." },
{ id: "CC-SA-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "What does potential value represent in the future-state definition?",
  options: ["The value already realized by the current solution", "The budget approved for the change", "The value that could be realized by reaching the future state", "The cost of the cheapest solution option"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Potential value is the value the future state might deliver if it is achieved. It helps decide whether the change is worth pursuing." },
{ id: "CC-SA-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "What does a gap analysis in Define Change Strategy identify?",
  options: ["Defects found in testing that block the release", "The gap between the approved budget and actual spend", "Missing stakeholders in the RACI matrix for the change", "Capabilities the change must add to reach the future state"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Gap analysis compares current and future states to identify the capabilities the change needs to deliver." },
{ id: "CC-SA-14", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "During risk assessment, the business analyst notes that the future state depends on an assumption nobody has confirmed. How should this be treated?",
  options: ["Log it as an issue, not a risk", "Remove the assumption from the future-state description", "Treat it as a fact, since stakeholders agreed it", "Consider it as a source of risk, along with constraints and dependencies"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Assess Risks considers unknowns, constraints, assumptions and dependencies as sources of risk to the value of the change." },
{ id: "CC-SA-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Which element of Define Change Strategy decides which capabilities will be delivered in which release?",
  options: ["Gap analysis", "Transition states and release planning", "Solution scope", "Enterprise readiness assessment"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Transition states and release planning define the intermediate states and decide which parts of the solution are delivered in each release." },
{ id: "CC-SA-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "A future-state definition describes new processes and software but says nothing about who will own the processes or how roles change. What is MOST likely missing?",
  options: ["Organizational structure and culture", "External influencers", "Current-state metrics", "Technology and infrastructure"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Defining the future state covers organizational structure and culture, capabilities and processes, technology, policies, business architecture and internal assets, not just processes and software." },

{ id: "CC-RADD-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.30 Non-Functional Requirements Analysis",
  question: "“The app must work with the company’s existing printers and scanners without extra drivers.” Which non-functional category is this?",
  options: ["Portability", "Compatibility", "Availability", "Scalability"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Compatibility is about how well the solution works alongside the other components in its environment." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-11", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.30 Non-Functional Requirements Analysis",
  question: "“The system must be movable from the on-premise data center to any major cloud provider with minimal changes.” Which non-functional category is this?",
  options: ["Reliability", "Usability", "Portability", "Compatibility"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Portability is about how easily a solution or component can be moved from one environment to another." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.47 Use Cases and Scenarios",
  question: "Several use cases all need to verify the customer’s identity in the same way. Which use case relationship lets them reuse that behaviour?",
  options: ["Include", "Extend", "Generalize", "Associate"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Include lets a use case make use of functionality in another use case. It is often used to pull out common behaviour shared by several use cases." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.47 Use Cases and Scenarios",
  question: "An optional “apply voucher” behaviour is added to the “Check out” use case, which works fully without it. Which relationship is this?",
  options: ["Include", "Generalization", "Association", "Extend"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Extend inserts additional behaviour into a use case. The extended use case must be complete on its own and not depend on the extending one." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-14", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.32 Organizational Modelling",
  question: "A company groups staff by customer segment (retail, SME, corporate) rather than by skill. Which type of organizational model is this?",
  options: ["Functionally-oriented", "Matrix", "Market-oriented", "Hierarchical project"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Market-oriented organizations group staff to serve particular customer groups, regions, projects or processes, rather than by common skills." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.34 Process Analysis",
  question: "The business analyst wants a simple overview of who supplies inputs to a process, what the process produces and who receives the outputs. Which process analysis method fits?",
  options: ["SIPOC", "Value stream mapping", "Decision table", "Sequence diagram"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "SIPOC (Suppliers, Inputs, Process, Outputs, Customers) gives a simple overview of a process and who is involved at each end." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.34 Process Analysis",
  question: "A team maps every step from order to delivery and measures waiting time against actual processing time at each stage. Which method is this?",
  options: ["SIPOC", "Value stream mapping", "Root cause analysis", "State modelling"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Value stream mapping, from Lean, diagrams the flow from the front of the supply chain and measures wait time against processing time at each stage." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-17", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.14 Data Mining",
  question: "Analysts use data mining to estimate how likely each customer is to cancel next month. Which type of data mining is this?",
  options: ["Descriptive", "Diagnostic", "Predictive", "Prescriptive"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK describes descriptive (patterns, e.g. clustering), diagnostic (why a pattern exists) and predictive (how likely something is) data mining. Estimating cancellation likelihood is predictive." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-18", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "A team uses a standard list of quality questions (for example, “Is every term defined in the glossary?”) when reviewing requirements. What is this list, and which task does it support?",
  options: ["A checklist, used in Verify Requirements", "A traceability matrix, used in Trace Requirements", "Acceptance criteria, used in Validate Requirements", "A governance approach, used in Approve Requirements"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Checklists are used in quality control when verifying requirements and designs, to make sure important elements and quality characteristics are covered." },

{ id: "CC-SE-09", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "Which of these is one of the characteristics of a good indicator, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["Popular with senior managers and executives", "Economical: available at reasonable cost", "Timely: reported as soon as each period closes", "Comparable: the same in every business unit"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A good indicator is clear, relevant, economical, adequate, quantifiable, and trustworthy and credible." },
{ id: "CC-SE-10", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "An indicator can be independently checked by someone else using the same data. Which characteristic does this describe?",
  options: ["Relevant", "Adequate", "Clear", "Quantifiable"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Quantifiable means the indicator can be independently validated. Clear means precise and unambiguous; relevant means appropriate to the concern; adequate means a sufficient basis to assess performance." },
{ id: "CC-SE-11", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "What makes the results of performance measures accurate and reliable, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["They are reproducible and repeatable", "They are approved by the sponsor", "They reveal a clear trend", "They link to business goals"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business analysts test the data collected to check accuracy. To be accurate and reliable, results should be reproducible and repeatable." },
{ id: "CC-SE-12", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "After finding why a solution component underperforms, the business analyst assesses how much the problem affects the solution’s ability to deliver value. Which element is this?",
  options: ["Enterprise culture assessment", "Impact assessment", "Gap analysis", "Operational assessment"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Assess Solution Limitations includes an impact assessment of identified problems, to judge their effect on the solution’s ability to deliver value." },
{ id: "CC-SE-13", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A new solution has no existing performance measures. Where can measures come from?",
  options: ["From the vendor’s standard reports, since they have already been tested", "Only from industry benchmarks, so results are comparable", "Define them with stakeholders from goals, objectives and processes", "Use the project’s schedule and budget variance as the measures"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "If suitable measures don’t exist, business analysts work with stakeholders to define them, based on the business goals, objectives and processes the solution supports." },
{ id: "CC-SE-14", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.3 Balanced Scorecard",
  question: "In a balanced scorecard, which dimension covers staff skills, training and the organization’s ability to improve?",
  options: ["Financial", "Customer", "Business process", "Learning and growth"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The scorecard looks at four areas: financial, customer, business process, and learning and growth. Skills and the capacity to improve sit in learning and growth." },
{ id: "CC-SE-15", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A reporting system’s technology has reached the end of its supported life and replacement parts are no longer available. Which recommendation does BABOK describe for this situation?",
  options: ["Retire the solution and consider a replacement", "Reduce the complexity of interfaces", "Eliminate redundancy with other systems", "Identify additional capabilities to extend its life"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Retiring a solution or a component may be needed, for example when its technology is at the end of its life." },
{ id: "CC-SE-16", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "When assessing enterprise limitations, the business analyst checks whether staff and customers understand and accept why the solution exists. Which assessment is this?",
  options: ["Operational assessment", "Organizational structure changes", "Enterprise culture assessment", "Impact assessment"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An enterprise culture assessment looks at whether stakeholders understand and accept the reasons for the solution, and at beliefs and attitudes that affect its use." },

/* ===================== CBAP · batch 2 (BABOK-checked) ===================== */
{ id: "CP-BAPM-08", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "An insurer outsources development to a vendor in another country under a fixed-price contract. Staff turnover in the insurer’s own team is high. How should this shape the business analysis approach?",
  options: ["Use as little documentation as possible so the vendor can move fast", "Leave the approach to the vendor, since it owns delivery", "More formality and documentation, given the contract, outsourcing and turnover", "Use the same approach as the last internal project"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK lists contracts that require formality, outsourced resources and high staff turnover among the conditions that call for more formal documentation in the business analysis approach." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-09", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach · BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "The two domain experts essential for a claims initiative can give only two hours a week. How should the business analyst treat this when planning?",
  options: ["As a risk, adjusting the approach to their real availability", "As an assumption to confirm at the end of elicitation", "As a reason to rely on documents and the experts’ managers instead of the experts", "As the sponsor’s problem rather than something to plan for"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK notes that a domain expert can become a source of risk when their input is essential but they aren’t available. The approach should be planned around their real availability." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Requirements for a new HR system include salary bands and disciplinary rules that only HR and legal may see. Which part of planning business analysis information management addresses this?",
  options: ["Level of abstraction", "Plan for requirements reuse", "Requirements attributes", "Storage and access"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Storage and access planning decides where information is kept and who can access it, based on factors such as confidentiality and who needs it." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "When is it useful to record a requirement’s urgency separately from its priority?",
  options: ["Always, for every requirement", "When there is a deadline by which the requirement must be met", "Only for non-functional requirements", "Never; urgency and priority mean the same thing"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Urgency shows how soon a requirement is needed. BABOK notes it is usually only necessary to specify it separately from priority when a deadline exists." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "A department measured its business analysts by the number of requirements they wrote. Specifications grew longer, and quality and reuse dropped. What does this illustrate?",
  options: ["Business analysts shouldn’t be measured, since their work is qualitative", "A lack of business analysis standards and templates in the department", "Poorly chosen metrics can drive harmful behaviour", "The measure was fine; the analysts misunderstood what it was for"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK warns that every performance metric rewards some behaviours and discourages others, and a badly chosen one can push people toward behaviour that hurts the enterprise." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-13", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "When defining stakeholder communication needs, which set of considerations does the business analyst evaluate?",
  options: ["What, how, to whom, when, how often, where, detail and formality", "The stakeholders’ preferred meeting times and channels", "Only which documents need formal sign-off and by whom", "The communication budget and the tools available"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Stakeholder communication needs cover what to communicate, delivery method, audience, timing, frequency, location, level of detail and level of formality." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "BABOK says planning typically happens more than once on an initiative. Why?",
  options: ["Because the first plan is usually wrong", "Because adaptive work rules out long-range plans", "Because each knowledge area needs its own separate plan", "Because plans are updated as conditions change and issues arise"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Plans usually get revisited as business conditions change and new issues come up. The approach should say how plans will be updated." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-15", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "A change request process exists, but nobody has defined who performs impact analysis or who authorizes changes. Which BABOK task should have covered this?",
  options: ["Plan Business Analysis Information Management", "Plan Business Analysis Governance", "Assess Requirements Changes", "Manage Stakeholder Collaboration"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Plan Business Analysis Governance defines the change control process, including who performs impact analysis and who authorizes changes. Assess Requirements Changes then applies it." },

{ id: "CP-EC-08", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Which factors does BABOK say influence the choice of elicitation techniques?",
  options: ["Mainly the business analyst’s experience with each technique", "Mainly what the sponsor and project manager request", "Cost, time, information sources, culture, desired outcomes and stakeholder needs", "Mainly the number of requirements expected and the deadline"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Technique selection depends on cost and time constraints, information sources and access, organizational culture, desired outcomes, and the needs, availability and location of stakeholders." },
{ id: "CP-EC-09", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation",
  question: "Halfway through an elicitation workshop, participants start debating database designs instead of the business process the session was planned for. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Let the discussion continue, since all information is useful", "Guide the group back to the purpose and park the topic", "End the workshop early and reschedule", "Switch to a data modelling exercise while interest is high"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Guiding the elicitation activity includes keeping it focused on its purpose and agreed scope; off-topic items can be recorded for later." },
{ id: "CP-EC-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation · BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "In an unplanned hallway conversation, a warehouse supervisor explains an important exception in the returns process. What should the business analyst do with this information?",
  options: ["Capture it and confirm it later, like other elicitation results", "Record it as a risk in the initiative’s risk register", "Add it straight into the approved requirements", "Ask the supervisor to submit it as a formal change request"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Elicitation can be spontaneous. The outcome should still be captured and then confirmed against sources and other results before it is relied on." },
{ id: "CP-EC-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.10 Collaborative Games",
  question: "Participants write possible features on sticky notes, put them up on a wall and cluster similar ones to find themes. Which collaborative game is this?",
  options: ["Product box", "Fishbowl", "Affinity map", "Mind map"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "In an affinity map, participants group features on sticky notes by similarity to identify related themes. Product box and fishbowl are other collaborative games." },
{ id: "CP-EC-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "The business analyst emails a 90-page package to twelve stakeholders and asks for sign-off within two days, with no session to discuss it. What is the main weakness?",
  options: ["The package should be under 50 pages so it can be read in time", "Stakeholders get no real chance to review, ask and raise concerns", "Email isn’t a suitable channel for requirements packages of this size", "Sign-off should only be requested from the sponsor"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Communicating the business analysis package should give stakeholders the chance to review it, ask questions and raise concerns, using a suitable platform." },
{ id: "CP-EC-13", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "Stakeholders in a hierarchical organization seem afraid to raise concerns about requirements in front of their managers. How can the business analyst support collaboration?",
  options: ["Send the requirements package for formal review and sign-off", "Ask managers to speak for their teams, since they know the work", "Report the culture problem to HR and wait for a fix", "Offer safer channels, such as one-to-ones or anonymous input"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Managing stakeholder collaboration includes encouraging the free flow of information and ideas. Adjusting how input is gathered helps when the culture discourages open disagreement." },
{ id: "CP-EC-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.45 Survey or Questionnaire",
  question: "The business analyst needs results from 4,000 customers that can be analyzed statistically and compared by region. Which type of survey questions is MOST suitable?",
  options: ["Closed questions with defined answer options", "Open-ended questions that invite detailed comments", "Questions written by each regional manager", "Hypothetical questions"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Closed questions give answers that are easy to analyze statistically across large samples. Open-ended questions are richer but harder to analyze at scale." },
{ id: "CP-EC-15", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.36 Prototyping",
  question: "A team builds a prototype that is refined over several iterations and eventually becomes part of the delivered solution. What kind of prototype is this?",
  options: ["Throw-away prototype", "Proof of principle", "Storyboard prototype", "Evolutionary prototype"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Throw-away prototypes clarify requirements and are discarded. Evolutionary (functional) prototypes are refined until they become part of the working solution." },

{ id: "CP-RLCM-08", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "A tester finds a feature in the build that traces to no requirement at all. What does this MOST likely indicate?",
  options: ["Good initiative from the developers, worth keeping", "Possible unauthorized scope that should be investigated", "A defect to log against the nearest requirement", "That the feature can be released as a bonus"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Traceability helps manage scope by linking solution components to requirements. A component that traces to nothing may be unauthorized scope or a missing requirement; either way it needs investigation." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-09", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "Three teams each keep their own copy of the same identity-verification requirements, and the wording has started to differ. What should the business analyst recommend?",
  options: ["Maintain one authoritative version that all teams reuse", "Let each team keep its own version for flexibility", "Add a version attribute to each team’s copy", "Copy the newest version into each team’s backlog"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Maintaining requirements for reuse means keeping one accurate, current version, clearly named and stored where others can find it, instead of diverging copies." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "When estimating the cost of a proposed change, what should the business analyst include?",
  options: ["The developers’ estimate for the new work", "Only the license cost of any new software needed", "Cost of the change, related rework, and opportunity cost", "The costs that fall in the current budget year"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The cost of a change includes making it, any rework it causes, and opportunity cost, such as other features that have to be dropped or delayed." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "The team decides to build the requirements with the most technical uncertainty first, so that if they prove impossible, little else is wasted. Which basis for prioritization is this?",
  options: ["Benefit", "Penalty", "Stability", "Risk"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Risk is a basis for prioritization: requirements with a high risk of failure may be done early, so effort isn’t wasted if they turn out not to be feasible." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Two stakeholders insist their requirements are “all critical,” and the scoring criteria seem to conflict across different types of requirements. What does BABOK say is often needed?",
  options: ["Dropping the scoring criteria and ranking by stakeholder seniority", "Stakeholders making trade-offs between requirements", "Re-validating every requirement against the business case", "Letting the business analyst decide alone"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes that different kinds of requirements can react differently to the same criteria and seem to clash, so stakeholders may need to make trade-offs." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-13", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements · BABOK v3 7.3 Validate Requirements",
  question: "Tracing upward, the business analyst finds several solution requirements with no link to any business or stakeholder requirement. What should happen?",
  options: ["Approve them, since solution requirements are the most detailed", "Delete them immediately to keep scope clean", "Check they support a real need; add the link or raise them for removal", "Leave them, since traceability is optional for detail"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Requirements should derive from higher-level needs. Orphan requirements may not deliver value, so they need validating: find the real need or remove them." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Two senior stakeholders disagree about a requirement, and the deadline for approval is near. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Ask the project manager to choose a version", "Approve the version from the more senior stakeholder to save time", "Remove the requirement for now to avoid the conflict", "Facilitate resolution, and escalate through governance if needed"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Conflict and issue management in Approve Requirements means facilitating resolution between stakeholders and escalating through the agreed governance when needed." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-15", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A stakeholder says a feature is valuable only if it ships before a competitor’s launch in March. Which basis for prioritization is MOST relevant?",
  options: ["Stability", "Dependencies", "Time sensitivity", "Cost"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Time sensitivity is the “best before” date of a requirement, after which it loses significant value." },

{ id: "CP-SA-08", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Interviews show that staff deeply distrust automated decisions after a past system error caused public embarrassment. Which element of the current state does this belong to?",
  options: ["Organizational structure and culture", "Technology and infrastructure", "External influencers", "Business architecture"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Beliefs, values and attitudes, such as distrust from past experience, are part of organizational culture in the current state and strongly affect how a change will be received." },
{ id: "CP-SA-09", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A manufacturer plans a new service business built on its patented sensors and its trusted brand. Which current-state element is the business analyst drawing on?",
  options: ["External influencers", "Policies", "Internal assets", "Capabilities and processes"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Internal assets are the tangible and intangible resources the enterprise owns, such as patents, brands, data and expertise, that a change can build on." },
{ id: "CP-SA-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "Which business objective BEST supports the goal “become the region’s most convenient bank for small businesses”?",
  options: ["Be more convenient than competitors for small businesses", "Launch a new mobile app for small business customers in the next financial year", "Increase small business customer satisfaction significantly", "Cut SME account opening from 10 days to 2 by end of next year"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Objectives should be SMART and contribute to the goal. Only one option is specific, measurable and time-bound; one names a solution, and two are vague." },
{ id: "CP-SA-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "Group policy says customer data may not be stored outside the EU. How should this be treated when defining the future state?",
  options: ["As a constraint on the solution space", "As an assumption to test later", "As a business objective", "As a risk that can be accepted"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Constraints, such as policies, regulations, budgets and technical limits, restrict the options for the solution and are identified when defining the future state." },
{ id: "CP-SA-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "A small start-up is willing to accept high uncertainty for the chance of a much larger return. Which risk tolerance is this?",
  options: ["Risk-aversion", "Risk-seeking", "Neutrality", "Risk avoidance"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK describes risk-aversion, neutrality and risk-seeking. Risk-seeking organizations accept greater uncertainty in pursuit of higher potential value." },
{ id: "CP-SA-13", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "A change carries real risk, so the team looks for additional features that would raise the expected benefits enough to justify that risk. Which BABOK recommendation category is this?",
  options: ["Pursue the benefits while investing in reducing the likelihood of the risks", "Pursue the benefits regardless of the risk", "Seek ways to increase benefits to outweigh the risk", "Look for ways to manage and make the most of opportunities"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "One of BABOK’s recommendation categories is to look for ways to raise the benefits of a change until they outweigh the risk." },
{ id: "CP-SA-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "BABOK says a change strategy may be made up of several smaller changes. Why might more than one change be needed?",
  options: ["Because each change must stay within one business unit", "Because adaptive approaches require it", "Because each element of change might not fully address the need", "Because transition states are mandatory"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A change strategy can be a series of projects or continuous improvements, because any single change may not meet the whole need." },
{ id: "CP-SA-15", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.4 Benchmarking and Market Analysis",
  question: "To shape a new product, the business analyst studies target customer segments, competitors’ offers and buying trends. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Benchmarking", "Market analysis", "SWOT analysis", "Business capability analysis"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Market analysis studies customers, competitors and market trends. Benchmarking compares the organization’s performance or practices with peers or best practice." },

{ id: "CP-RADD-09", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "A process model uses a notation the team doesn’t recognize, and an entity in the data model never appears in the process model. Which task finds these issues?",
  options: ["Validate Requirements", "Verify Requirements", "Approve Requirements", "Define Change Strategy"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Verification activities include checking correct use of modelling notation, completeness within each model, and comparing models for elements missing or inconsistent between them." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.3 Validate Requirements",
  question: "A requirement for automated reminders is expected to cut missed appointments, but only if patients read text messages. What should the business analyst do during validation?",
  options: ["Identify and manage the assumption, e.g. test it in a pilot", "Record it as a design constraint for the solution", "Remove the requirement until the benefit is proven", "Approve the requirement, since it is well written and testable"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Validate Requirements includes identifying assumptions that the expected benefits depend on, so they can be managed and tested." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "Midway through design, the team learns that integrating one component costs far more than estimated. What does BABOK say may be necessary?",
  options: ["Carrying on with the original plan, since the design is agreed", "Re-evaluating how requirements are allocated to components", "Treating the extra cost as a solution limitation to assess", "Removing the integration requirements from scope"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes that how design elements are split between components may need to be revisited, for example once the real cost of building a component is clearer." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "How is the value of a design option determined, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["By the vendor’s price alone, since cost is easiest to compare", "By how many requirements each option covers, as a proxy for benefit", "By weighing expected benefits against expected costs, tangible and intangible", "By the sponsor’s view, since the sponsor owns the business case"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Determining value weighs expected benefits against expected costs, tangible and intangible, considering the perspectives of the stakeholders involved." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-13", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.11 Concept Modelling",
  question: "In a concept model, “Customer places Order” links two concepts. What is “places”?",
  options: ["A noun concept", "A verb concept", "An attribute", "A state"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Concept models use noun concepts (Customer, Order) and verb concepts that connect them (places)." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "The business analyst breaks a large “manage claims” requirement into smaller parts to see what it involves and to find gaps and overlaps. Which element of Specify and Model Requirements is this?",
  options: ["Represent requirements and attributes", "Model requirements", "Implement the appropriate levels of abstraction", "Analyze requirements"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Analyzing requirements means decomposing business analysis information into components to find what must change, what should stay the same, and any missing or unnecessary components." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-15", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "Which of these is a design element that may describe part of a design option?",
  options: ["Organizational structures and their interactions", "The approval status of each requirement", "The business need the option is meant to satisfy", "The evaluation criteria used to compare the options"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Design elements can describe policies and rules, business processes, people and roles, operational decisions, software applications and organizational structures." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-16", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.17 Decision Modelling",
  question: "A complex pricing rule involves a sequence of decisions, where each answer leads to a different next question. Which decision model represents this sequence MOST naturally?",
  options: ["A decision table", "A state diagram", "A data dictionary", "A decision tree"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Decision tables and decision trees both represent rules. In a tree, each level is a data element and each path to a leaf is one rule, which suits decisions taken in sequence; a table shows combinations of conditions in a grid." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.30 Non-Functional Requirements Analysis",
  question: "“The payment service must run without failure for at least 30 days under normal load.” Which non-functional category is this?",
  options: ["Usability", "Portability", "Reliability", "Compatibility"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Reliability is about a solution or component doing what it should, under given conditions, for a given length of time, measured for example as mean time to failure." },

{ id: "CP-SE-08", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A team measures a new booking system’s response time only at 7 a.m., when almost nobody uses it. What problem does this create?",
  options: ["No problem, since any consistent measurement is valid", "The timing can bias results so they don’t reflect real use", "Response time can’t count as a performance measure", "It measures an output rather than an outcome"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "When collecting performance measures, the frequency and timing of measurements can affect the outcome, so they should reflect real conditions such as peak load." },
{ id: "CP-SE-09", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "While analyzing performance measures, the business analyst notices that support costs are trending upward, which could wipe out the solution’s savings within two years. Which element of Analyze Performance Measures is this?",
  options: ["Accuracy", "Performance variances", "Trends and risks", "Solution scope"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Analyzing performance measures covers performance against desired value, risks, trends, accuracy and variances. A worsening trend that threatens value is both a trend and a risk." },
{ id: "CP-SE-10", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "A new solution’s results look excellent, but they come from only two weeks of data during a promotion. What should the business analyst consider before concluding?",
  options: ["Whether the period was long and representative enough", "Whether enterprise culture is ready for the new solution", "Only the cost of the promotion against the extra revenue", "Whether the sponsor is satisfied with the launch"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Trends need a sufficient and representative period. Short or unusual periods can make performance look better or worse than it really is." },
{ id: "CP-SE-11", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "Several defects are found in a newly released solution. How should the business analyst decide which ones matter most?",
  options: ["Fix them in the order they were reported by users", "Fix the easiest ones first to show quick progress", "Trace each defect back to the requirement it breaks", "Assess each problem’s impact on delivering value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Impact assessment in Assess Solution Limitations judges the effect of each problem on value, which guides what to address first." },
{ id: "CP-SE-12", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "For a new scheduling solution, the business analyst analyzes how each group’s functions change, where they are located, and what concerns they have. What is this?",
  options: ["Stakeholder impact analysis", "Interface analysis", "Organizational modelling", "Market analysis"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Stakeholder impact analysis, part of Assess Enterprise Limitations, looks at how a solution affects stakeholder groups: their functions, locations and concerns." },
{ id: "CP-SE-13", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Staff still print, sign, scan and re-upload forms the new system can already approve digitally. Which recommendation fits BEST?",
  options: ["Avoid waste by removing steps that add no value", "Retire the solution, since staff don’t use it properly", "Identify additional capabilities in the new system", "Do nothing, since the forms still get processed"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Avoiding waste means removing activities that don’t add value and minimizing those that don’t contribute directly to the outcome." },
{ id: "CP-SE-14", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 11.5 The Business Process Management Perspective",
  question: "In the business process management life cycle, which activity uses data from the actual running of processes, giving reliable, objective input for analyzing value?",
  options: ["Designing", "Modelling", "Optimizing", "Execution and monitoring"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Execution and monitoring provides the same kind of input as modelling, but from the actual execution of processes, so the data is reliable and objective for analyzing value." },
{ id: "CP-SE-15", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Fixing a problem in a solution would deliver little value compared with the effort, and the change carries significant risk. What does BABOK say is usually recommended?",
  options: ["Retire the solution", "Organizational change", "Do nothing", "Eliminate redundancy"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Doing nothing is the usual recommendation when a change would add little value for the effort, or when changing is riskier than staying as things are." },
/* ===================== ECBA · batch 3 (BABOK-checked) ===================== */
{ id: "E-UBA-31", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "In BABOK terms, what is an organization?",
  options: ["An autonomous group of people under one individual or board, working toward common goals", "Any legal entity registered as a company, whatever its size, structure or number of sites", "A system of several enterprises and the solutions they share to pursue common goals", "The departments that report to the same executive, excluding external partners"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "In BABOK, an organization is an independent group of people under one person or board, working toward shared goals. An enterprise is wider: one or more organizations plus the solutions they use." },
{ id: "E-UBA-32", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "Which type of requirement serves as a bridge between business requirements and solution requirements?",
  options: ["Transition requirements", "Stakeholder requirements", "Non-functional requirements, which describe the qualities of the solution", "Business rules, which constrain how the business operates"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Stakeholder requirements state what stakeholders need so that the business requirements can be met. They often link business requirements to solution requirements." },
{ id: "E-UBA-33", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "“The system shall calculate VAT on each invoice line.” What type of requirement is this?",
  options: ["Business requirement", "Non-functional requirement describing a quality", "Functional requirement", "Transition requirement needed only during migration"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Functional requirements describe what the solution must be able to do, in terms of its behaviour and the information it handles, such as calculating VAT." },
{ id: "E-UBA-34", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which knowledge area covers preparing for and conducting elicitation, confirming results and working with stakeholders?",
  options: ["Elicitation and Collaboration", "Business Analysis Planning and Monitoring, which plans all stakeholder work", "Strategy Analysis", "Requirements Life Cycle Management"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Elicitation and Collaboration covers preparing for and conducting elicitation, confirming results, communicating business analysis information and managing stakeholder collaboration." },
{ id: "E-UBA-35", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which knowledge area covers managing and maintaining requirements and designs from inception to retirement?",
  options: ["Requirements Analysis and Design Definition", "Solution Evaluation, which looks after solutions in operation", "Strategy Analysis", "Requirements Life Cycle Management"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Requirements Life Cycle Management covers tracing, maintaining, prioritizing, assessing changes to and approving requirements and designs throughout their life." },
{ id: "E-UBA-36", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which knowledge area organizes and coordinates the efforts of business analysts and stakeholders?",
  options: ["Elicitation and Collaboration, which coordinates stakeholder sessions", "Business Analysis Planning and Monitoring", "Strategy Analysis", "Solution Evaluation"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Business Analysis Planning and Monitoring covers planning the approach, stakeholder engagement, governance and information management, and improving business analysis performance." },
{ id: "E-UBA-37", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which knowledge area covers structuring requirements, specifying and modelling them, verifying and validating them, and identifying solution options?",
  options: ["Requirements Life Cycle Management, since it manages requirements", "Strategy Analysis", "Requirements Analysis and Design Definition", "Elicitation and Collaboration"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Requirements Analysis and Design Definition covers specifying, modelling, verifying and validating requirements, defining the requirements architecture and design options, and recommending a solution." },
{ id: "E-UBA-38", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "In the BACCM, which concept is the specific way a need is satisfied?",
  options: ["Change", "Value", "Context", "Solution"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The solution is the particular way a need is met in its context. Change is the transformation itself, and value is what it is worth to stakeholders." },
{ id: "E-UBA-39", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "Two teams both want the same budget. The business analyst lays out what each option would deliver and helps the decision makers compare them. Which contribution to value from The Business Analysis Standard is this?",
  options: ["Helping teams weigh trade-offs and prioritize value", "Translating needs into detailed designs for the developers", "Communicating desired outcomes", "Discovering and sharing business information"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The Standard lists helping teams weigh trade-offs and prioritize value among the ways business analysis contributes to value." },
{ id: "E-UBA-40", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.2 What is Business Analysis?",
  question: "According to BABOK, what does business analysis enable an enterprise to do?",
  options: ["Approve solutions on behalf of stakeholders once the requirements have been agreed and signed off", "Replace project management on change initiatives", "State its needs and the case for change, and design and describe solutions that bring value", "Document every current process in full before any change to the enterprise is allowed to go ahead"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK says business analysis helps an enterprise explain what it needs and why change is required, and then design and describe solutions that create value." },
{ id: "E-UBA-41", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "In the BABOK Guide, what is the role of the techniques chapter?",
  options: ["It lists mandatory steps that every initiative must follow, in a fixed order", "It describes different ways a task can be performed or its output shaped", "It defines the exam blueprint and weighting for each IIBA certification", "It replaces the knowledge areas for agile work"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Techniques provide additional information on ways a task may be performed or the form its output may take. BABOK describes 50 of them; none is mandatory." },

{ id: "E-MIND-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "A business analyst joins a new insurance team and quickly absorbs the product rules, adapting what she already knows. Which underlying competency is this?",
  options: ["Learning", "Conceptual thinking about the whole insurance product", "Decision making", "Visual thinking"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Learning is the ability to pick up new and different information quickly and adapt what you already know, so you can work well as things change." },
{ id: "E-MIND-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "A business analyst makes sure the team agrees what the problem actually is, checks that its cause is understood, and only then helps generate options. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Creative thinking", "Teamwork", "Problem solving", "Organization and time management, by sequencing the work"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Problem solving involves defining the problem, making sure the underlying cause is understood, and generating and evaluating options." },
{ id: "E-MIND-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "With three workshops, two reviews and a deadline in the same week, a business analyst ranks the tasks and plans her time to get the most important ones done. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Personal accountability for every loose end", "Ethics", "Adaptability", "Organization and time management"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Organization and time management means ranking tasks, doing them efficiently and using time well." },
{ id: "E-MIND-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.4 Communication Skills",
  question: "In interviews, a business analyst focuses fully on the speaker, asks clarifying questions and summarizes what she heard before moving on. Which communication skill is this?",
  options: ["Written communication", "Listening", "Verbal communication, since she is speaking", "Teaching"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Listening, a communication skill, means paying full attention to the speaker and checking understanding, for example by asking questions and summarizing." },
{ id: "E-MIND-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.4 Communication Skills",
  question: "A stakeholder says “that’s fine,” but folds his arms and looks away. The business analyst notices and asks a follow-up question. Which skill is the analyst using?",
  options: ["Written communication", "Teaching", "Non-verbal communication", "Negotiation and conflict resolution between the parties"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Non-verbal communication covers sending and reading signals such as body language and tone, which can reveal what words don’t." },
{ id: "E-MIND-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 9.4 Communication Skills",
  question: "Which communication skill matters MOST when producing requirements that people in other time zones will read without the analyst present?",
  options: ["Verbal communication", "Non-verbal communication, such as gestures", "Listening", "Written communication"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Written communication conveys information clearly in text, which is essential when readers can’t ask the author directly." },
{ id: "E-MIND-31", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "A business analyst works closely with developers, testers and the project manager, shares information openly, and helps resolve friction within the team. Which interaction skill is this?",
  options: ["Facilitation of formal workshops", "Teaching", "Leadership and influencing", "Teamwork"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Teamwork covers working effectively with other team members, building relationships and handling conflict within the team." },
{ id: "E-MIND-32", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.3 Business Knowledge",
  question: "A business analyst knows the current CRM’s features and limitations so well that she quickly spots which requests the existing solution can already meet. Which business knowledge area is this?",
  options: ["Industry knowledge of the sector’s trends", "Solution knowledge", "Methodology knowledge", "Business acumen"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Business knowledge includes business acumen, industry, organization, solution and methodology knowledge. Knowing existing solutions well is solution knowledge." },

{ id: "E-IMPL-16", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.3 Foundational Techniques",
  question: "A business analyst maps every step a customer takes to open an account, including how they feel and where they get frustrated. Which foundational technique is this?",
  options: ["Process analysis of the internal workflow", "Customer journey mapping", "Backlog management", "Data storytelling"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Customer journey mapping visually maps the end-to-end customer experience, including motivations and how customers interact with the solution." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-17", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "In adaptive approaches, when is more formal documentation often produced?",
  options: ["Before any development starts, as a gate for the first iteration", "Before the first iteration, as a baseline that the team then works from", "Never; adaptive teams don’t document", "After the solution is implemented, to support knowledge transfer"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "In adaptive approaches, more formal documentation is often written after the solution goes live, to help hand knowledge over." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-18", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.3 Foundational Techniques · BABOK v3 10.2 Backlog Management",
  question: "Which of these can be an item in a backlog?",
  options: ["User stories written by the product owner in the agreed template", "User stories, defects, change requests, non-functional requirements and more", "Features the sponsor approved at the start of the initiative, plus their estimates", "Items that have already been estimated by the team"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A backlog can contain any item with work associated with it, such as use cases, user stories, defects, change requests, functional and non-functional requirements, and planned rework." },

{ id: "E-CHG-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "When designing a change control process, which of these does the business analyst need to decide?",
  options: ["How changes will be prioritized against other competing work", "Which developer will code each change and in which sprint", "The elicitation technique for each change request", "Which design option each approved change must follow"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A change control process defines how changes are requested, what a request contains, how changes are prioritized, documented and communicated, who analyzes their impact, and who authorizes them." },
{ id: "E-CHG-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "While developing a change strategy, how much detail should the options have?",
  options: ["Full detail for every option, including designs and test plans", "Full technical designs for each option, so they can be costed exactly", "Enough detail to tell which options are feasible", "A name and a rough cost estimate for each option"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Several options are found, explored and described just enough to tell which are feasible, before one is recommended." },
{ id: "E-CHG-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "How does BABOK describe organizational change?",
  options: ["A new organization chart, produced by HR once the solution has been delivered", "Any change to the company’s legal structure", "A software upgrade rolled out to all staff, with training for each department that uses it", "A way of managing people’s attitudes to, perceptions of and involvement in the change"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK describes organizational change as managing how people feel about, see and take part in the change that comes with the solution." },
{ id: "E-CHG-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "When assessing a proposed change, what does the “impact” factor measure?",
  options: ["How many customers or business processes the change would affect", "How much the requester wants the change", "The amount of documentation that must be rewritten for the change", "The delay the change would add to current commitments"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "In impact analysis, the impact factor is how many customers or business processes would be affected if the change goes ahead." },
{ id: "E-CHG-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "A change is needed because a safety regulation takes effect next month. Which impact analysis factor captures this?",
  options: ["Benefit", "Cost, including any rework the change causes", "Urgency", "Schedule"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Urgency is the level of importance, including factors that drive necessity such as regulatory or safety issues." },

{ id: "E-NEED-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Invoices are often paid late because the approval step in the current process keeps stalling. Where does this business need come from?",
  options: ["Top-down, from a strategic goal", "External drivers such as customer demand", "Middle management needing more information", "Bottom-up, from a problem with a current process"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A bottom-up need starts from a problem in how a current process, function or system works." },
{ id: "E-NEED-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "The board sets a goal to expand into two new countries within three years. Where does the resulting business need come from?",
  options: ["Top-down, from a strategic goal", "Bottom-up, from a process problem", "From a single customer complaint", "From middle management needing more data for reports"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A top-down need starts from a strategic goal the organization wants to reach." },
{ id: "E-NEED-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.34 Process Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst studies the current claims process to find gaps and areas that could work better. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Process analysis", "Glossary", "Sequence diagrams of system messages", "Organizational modelling"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Process analysis examines processes to identify gaps and areas to improve, their root causes, and options for improvement." },
{ id: "E-NEED-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases",
  question: "In a business case, what follows naturally from the need assessment?",
  options: ["The final test plan for the chosen solution", "The desired outcomes the change should achieve", "The vendor contract and payment schedule", "The detailed user interface design"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A business case covers the need assessment, desired outcomes, assessment of alternatives and a recommended solution." },
{ id: "E-NEED-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.4 Benchmarking and Market Analysis",
  question: "Comparing its loan approval time with industry leaders shows the bank is three times slower. How does this help with understanding the need?",
  options: ["It proves the bank needs new loan software, since the leaders must have better tools", "It reveals why the bank is slower", "It identifies a performance gap that may point to a problem or opportunity", "It replaces the need for stakeholder input on what the problem is"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Benchmarking compares performance with peers or best practice, which can reveal gaps that point to needs, without prescribing a solution." },

{ id: "E-SOL-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "The team plans to modify the company’s existing in-house booking system rather than buy a new one. Which solution approach is this?",
  options: ["Purchase, since the system already exists", "Combination of purchase and outsourcing", "Create", "Retire"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The create approach covers components that are assembled, constructed or developed in response to requirements, and BABOK notes it includes modifying an existing solution." },
{ id: "E-SOL-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "The team selects a software-as-a-service product from several offerings that meet the requirements. Which solution approach is this?",
  options: ["Purchase", "Create, because it will be configured", "Combination", "Transition"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "With the purchase approach, the team chooses components from existing offerings that meet the requirements, usually owned by a third party." },
{ id: "E-SOL-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "A new solution works, but nobody in operations has been trained to support it. Which assessment would find this?",
  options: ["Gap analysis during strategy work", "Requirements verification", "Operational assessment", "Stakeholder list review"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An operational assessment, part of assessing enterprise limitations, checks whether the organization can operate and support the solution." },
{ id: "E-SOL-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.49 Vendor Assessment",
  question: "During a vendor assessment, the team compares per-user subscription fees with one-off perpetual licenses. Which element of vendor assessment is this?",
  options: ["Knowledge and expertise", "Vendor market position", "Experience, reputation and stability of the vendor", "Licensing and pricing models"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Vendor assessment covers knowledge and expertise, licensing and pricing models, market position, terms and conditions, and experience, reputation and stability." },
{ id: "E-SOL-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Which kinds of measures can be used to evaluate solution performance?",
  options: ["Financial measures such as cost savings", "Both quantitative and qualitative measures", "Only system logs and technical metrics", "The measures the vendor provides with the product"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Solution performance measures can be quantitative (such as time or cost) or qualitative (such as satisfaction or perception)." },

{ id: "E-STK-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Which stakeholder uses, or might use, the enterprise’s products or services and can hold contractual or moral rights?",
  options: ["Supplier", "Sponsor", "Customer", "Operational support staff who maintain the product"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Customers use or might use what the enterprise offers, and they can hold contractual or moral rights that the enterprise has to respect." },
{ id: "E-STK-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Which stakeholder role is responsible for defining and enforcing standards that the solution must meet?",
  options: ["Regulator", "Tester", "Domain subject matter expert who knows the rules", "Project manager"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Regulators define and enforce standards, which can be imposed through legislation, corporate governance, audit standards and similar means." },
{ id: "E-STK-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Which stakeholder role works out how to check that the solution meets the requirements, and then does the checking?",
  options: ["End user", "Implementation subject matter expert who builds it", "Sponsor", "Tester"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Testers decide how to check that the solution meets the requirements and then carry out those checks, with an eye on quality standards." },
{ id: "E-STK-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Why does a business analyst analyze stakeholders’ attitudes toward an initiative?",
  options: ["To determine each stakeholder’s level of decision-making authority over deliverables", "To rank stakeholders by seniority so the steering committee can be formed from the most senior", "To understand what may affect their actions and plan collaboration with them accordingly", "To decide which stakeholders are allowed to attend workshops and which are kept informed"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Knowing how a stakeholder perceives the initiative helps the analyst understand their behaviour and plan collaboration and engagement with them." },
{ id: "E-STK-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "In a RACI matrix, who is Responsible for a task?",
  options: ["The single decision maker for the task", "The persons who will perform the work", "Anyone who must be told the result", "Subject matter experts who give opinions before the task is done"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Responsible means the people who perform the work. Accountable is the single decision maker, Consulted gives input, and Informed is kept up to date." },

{ id: "E-VAL-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "What does the internal rate of return (IRR) represent?",
  options: ["The interest rate at which the investment exactly breaks even", "The total benefits of a change minus its costs, in today’s money", "The time needed to recover the cost of a change", "The yearly running cost of a solution"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "IRR is the rate at which the investment breaks even. Comparing it with a minimum acceptable rate shows whether the change is worth funding." },
{ id: "E-VAL-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "Option X has a net present value (NPV) of €40,000; option Y has an NPV of €15,000. Based on NPV alone, which is the better investment?",
  options: ["Option Y, because it is cheaper and lower risk", "They are equal, since both are positive", "Option X", "Neither, because NPV ignores costs"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "NPV takes the value of future benefits in today’s money and subtracts what the investment costs, so the higher NPV is the better investment." },
{ id: "E-VAL-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.16 Decision Analysis",
  question: "Criteria are weighted cost 50% and ease of use 50%, each scored 1–10. Tool X: cost 8, ease 4. Tool Y: cost 5, ease 9. Which tool scores higher?",
  options: ["Tool X, with 6.0", "Tool Y, with 7.0", "They tie at 6.5", "Tool X, with 7.0"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "X = 0.5 × 8 + 0.5 × 4 = 6.0. Y = 0.5 × 5 + 0.5 × 9 = 7.0. A weighted decision matrix favours tool Y." },
{ id: "E-VAL-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.1 Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria",
  question: "Which statement BEST describes evaluation criteria?",
  options: ["The minimum set of conditions a solution has to meet before stakeholders will accept and sign it off", "The measures used to assess requirements in order to choose between several solutions", "The test cases written by the testers to confirm the solution works as designed", "The criteria the regulator uses to approve a solution"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Evaluation criteria are measures used to choose between multiple solutions. Acceptance criteria are the conditions a solution must meet to be considered acceptable." },
{ id: "E-VAL-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Two design options have similar benefits. Option X needs half the ongoing support staff of option Y. How should this affect the recommendation?",
  options: ["It shouldn’t; the initial investment is what matters most when comparing two design options", "It should favour option X, since expected costs include ongoing costs and affect value", "It should favour option Y, since more support staff means a more reliable solution", "It should be treated as a sunk cost and ignored"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Value weighs expected benefits against expected costs, and expected costs include ongoing operating and support costs, not just the initial price." },

{ id: "E-CTX-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "In a SWOT analysis, which strategy uses an external opportunity to eliminate or reduce an internal weakness?",
  options: ["SO strategy", "ST strategy", "WT strategy", "WO strategy"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "WO strategies ask whether an opportunity can be used to eliminate or mitigate a weakness. SO uses strengths to exploit opportunities; ST uses strengths against threats." },
{ id: "E-CTX-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "While analyzing the current state, the business analyst documents the company’s servers, networks and the software it runs. Which element is this?",
  options: ["Technology and infrastructure", "Internal assets such as patents and brand", "Business architecture", "Policies"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Technology and infrastructure covers the information systems and supporting infrastructure the organization uses today." },
{ id: "E-CTX-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A rule says all purchases above €5,000 need two signatures. In current-state analysis, which element is this?",
  options: ["External influencers", "Technology and infrastructure", "Policies", "Capabilities and processes"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Policies define the scope of decision making and the rules the organization operates under; they are an element of the current state." },
{ id: "E-CTX-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "The business analyst documents what the organization is able to do today and the activities it performs to do it. Which element of the current state is this?",
  options: ["Internal assets", "Capabilities and processes", "Business needs", "Organizational structure and culture"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Capabilities and processes describe what the enterprise does and the activities it performs, which helps identify what must change." },
{ id: "E-CTX-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.32 Organizational Modelling",
  question: "The organization chart shows a flat team, but in practice one senior engineer informally shapes most decisions. Which part of organizational modelling captures this?",
  options: ["Influencers", "Roles defined in the job descriptions", "Interfaces", "Functional structure"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Organizational models can show influencers: people or relationships that affect decisions beyond the formal reporting lines." },

/* ===================== CCBA · batch 3 (BABOK-checked) ===================== */
{ id: "CC-BAPM-18", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "When planning the change control process, the business analyst decides where approved changes will be recorded and in what format. Which part of the process is this?",
  options: ["Determining how changes will be prioritized", "Determining how changes will be documented", "Determining who will perform impact analysis on every change", "Determining the elements of the change request"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The change control process covers requesting changes, the content of a request, prioritization, how changes are documented and communicated, who analyzes impact and who authorizes." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-19", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "When planning the prioritization approach, which decisions does the business analyst make?",
  options: ["Which developer works on each item first", "The formality of the process, who takes part, and the criteria to be used", "The budget for each requirement", "The requirements attributes to record, such as source, status and owner"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Planning prioritization covers the formality and rigour of the process, the participants and their roles, the process itself, and the criteria used." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-20", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Which requirements attribute indicates how difficult a requirement will be to implement?",
  options: ["Priority", "Stability", "Complexity", "Ownership of the business result"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The complexity attribute shows how hard a requirement will be to implement." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-21", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Which requirements attribute records where a requirement came from, so the team knows whom to consult about changes?",
  options: ["Source", "Absolute reference, which uniquely identifies it", "Urgency", "Status"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The source attribute identifies the origin of the requirement, often a stakeholder who must be consulted when changes are considered." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-22", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "During stakeholder analysis, the business analyst learns that the finance director doubts business analysis adds value at all. Which element of stakeholder analysis does this relate to?",
  options: ["Roles", "Decision-making authority", "Level of power or influence over the change", "Attitudes"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholder analysis covers roles, attitudes, decision-making authority and level of power or influence. Attitudes include views on the goals, the solution approach and business analysis itself." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-23", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "Which element of the business analysis approach identifies the activities the business analyst will perform and the deliverables they will produce?",
  options: ["Business analysis activities", "Acceptance", "Timing of business analysis work in relation to delivery", "Complexity and risk"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "This element sets out the kinds of activities the business analyst will carry out and the deliverables that will come out of them." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-24", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Stakeholders give feedback that the business analyst’s models are hard to follow, and review cycles are taking longer than planned. What is the business analyst doing when comparing this with expectations?",
  options: ["Validating requirements", "Defining the change strategy", "Performance analysis of business analysis work", "Assessing enterprise limitations of the solution"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements starts with performance analysis: comparing how the business analysis work is performing against agreed measures and expectations." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-25", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.19 Estimation",
  question: "Early in an initiative, with little information available, the sponsor asks for a quick estimate of the analysis effort, knowing it may be far off. What kind of estimate is this?",
  options: ["Bottom-up estimate built from detailed tasks", "Rough order of magnitude (ROM)", "Parametric estimate", "Delphi estimate"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A rough order of magnitude estimate is high level, based on limited information, and may have a very wide confidence interval." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-26", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Which question is part of planning for approvals?",
  options: ["Which design option is cheapest overall, so the sponsor can approve it", "Which requirements management tool will store the approved requirements", "How many elicitation workshops to hold and who should attend them", "Which types of business analysis information will need approval, when, and by whom"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Planning for approvals identifies what needs approval, the timing, the process and the stakeholders who approve, taking culture and regulation into account." },

{ id: "CC-EC-18", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops",
  question: "What should the business analyst do to prepare for a workshop?",
  options: ["Invite everyone in the department to make sure no one is missed", "Define the purpose and agenda, choose participants and arrange logistics", "Write the requirements in advance and ask participants to sign them", "Agree the approval process with the sponsor first"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Preparing a workshop includes defining its purpose and outcomes, identifying participants, setting the agenda and arranging logistics and materials." },
{ id: "CC-EC-19", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops",
  question: "Which is a responsibility of the facilitator in a workshop?",
  options: ["Approving the decisions made in the workshop on behalf of the sponsor", "Documenting decisions, outstanding items and actions in the agreed format for the group", "Enforcing structure and ground rules, and making sure everyone is heard", "Keeping track of time and alerting the group when an item runs over"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The facilitator sets the tone, introduces goals and agenda, enforces ground rules, keeps the session focused and ensures everyone has a chance to be heard." },
{ id: "CC-EC-20", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.25 Interviews",
  question: "How might a business analyst order interview questions?",
  options: ["In the order the sponsor approved when signing off the interview plan", "Only from the most sensitive questions to the least sensitive ones", "In the order the interviewee prefers on the day", "From general to specific, start to finish, or detailed to summary"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Interview questions are often organized by priority and significance, for example general to specific, start to finish, or detailed to summary." },
{ id: "CC-EC-21", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.21 Focus Groups",
  question: "How many participants does BABOK suggest for a focus group?",
  options: ["2 to 4", "6 to 12", "15 to 20", "As many as possible, to cover all views"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK suggests a focus group of about 6 to 12 people, inviting a few extra in case some don’t turn up." },
{ id: "CC-EC-22", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.31 Observation",
  question: "What should the business analyst do after an observation session?",
  options: ["Review the notes, follow up on open questions and gaps, and share the results", "Delete the notes once the requirements are written, to protect privacy", "Ask the sponsor to approve the notes as the agreed current state", "Set the observation objectives and choose active or passive mode"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "After observing, the analyst reviews notes and data, follows up with participants to fill gaps, and shares results to confirm understanding." },
{ id: "CC-EC-23", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.45 Survey or Questionnaire",
  question: "What is one of the first steps in preparing a survey?",
  options: ["Holding a focus group to agree the findings", "Piloting the survey with a small test group", "Defining a clear objective and the target group", "Analyzing the results"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Preparing a survey starts with a clear objective and an identified target group, which shape the questions and survey type." },
{ id: "CC-EC-24", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.5 Brainstorming",
  question: "What is the main purpose of brainstorming?",
  options: ["To reach a final decision quickly as a group", "To generate a wide and varied set of options", "To verify requirements against a checklist", "To document the current process in step-by-step detail"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Brainstorming aims for a wide, varied set of options on a topic, with no judging while ideas are being generated." },
{ id: "CC-EC-25", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.10 Collaborative Games",
  question: "Participants split into two groups. One group discusses a topic while the other listens and notes its observations. Which collaborative game is this, and what does it help reveal?",
  options: ["Product box; product features that sell", "Affinity map; themes in a set of ideas", "Brainstorming; a broad range of options", "Fishbowl; hidden assumptions or perspectives"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "In the fishbowl game, one group speaks while the other listens and documents observations. It helps identify hidden assumptions or perspectives." },

{ id: "CC-RLCM-17", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.26 Item Tracking",
  question: "A team logs open issues with an identifier, summary, category and owner, and follows them until they are resolved. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Item tracking", "Backlog management", "Lessons learned", "Root cause analysis"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Item tracking captures and assigns responsibility for issues and concerns, recording attributes such as identifier, summary, category and owner until they are resolved." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-18", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.2 Backlog Management",
  question: "A stakeholder adds a new user story to the backlog mid-iteration. How is it normally handled?",
  options: ["It goes straight to the top of the backlog, since it is the newest item", "It is ignored until the next release is planned", "It waits for a change control board decision", "It is prioritized relative to the other items in the backlog"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "In backlog management, new items are added and prioritized relative to the existing items, so the backlog stays ordered by value." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-19", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Why don’t teams usually trace every relationship between every requirement and artifact in full detail?",
  options: ["Because traceability is optional in BABOK and only used on large initiatives", "Because the effort grows with detail, so the level of formality should match the need", "Because requirements tools can’t store detailed links between artifacts", "Because traceability is mainly for regulators and auditors"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Tracing takes effort that grows with the number of relationships and detail, so the level of formality is chosen to fit the initiative’s complexity, risk and regulatory needs." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-20", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A requirement directly supports the organization’s top business objective. Which basis for prioritization does this reflect MOST directly?",
  options: ["Benefit", "Penalty", "Stability", "Dependencies"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Benefit is the advantage that results from implementing the requirement, such as its contribution to business goals and objectives." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-21", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Two requirements offer similar benefit, but one costs a quarter as much to implement. Which basis for prioritization helps decide between them?",
  options: ["Stability", "Time sensitivity", "Regulatory or policy compliance", "Cost"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Cost, the cost to implement a requirement, is a basis for prioritization and is often considered together with benefit." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-22", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Several stakeholders must approve a set of requirements, and they are close to agreement. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Escalate the remaining points to the sponsor for an immediate final decision", "Facilitate discussion to reach consensus among those with approval authority", "Verify the requirements again", "Wait until every single stakeholder in the organization agrees"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Gaining consensus means facilitating agreement among the stakeholders with approval authority on what is approved." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-23", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.39 Roles and Permissions Matrix",
  question: "A department head asks that her approval authority be passed to her deputy while she is on leave. What does BABOK call this in a roles and permissions matrix?",
  options: ["An inheritance", "A conflict of interest", "A delegation", "A segregation of duties"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Delegations identify which authorities can be passed from one individual to another, short term or permanently." },

{ id: "CC-SA-17", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.8 Business Model Canvas",
  question: "Which of these is one of the nine building blocks of the business model canvas?",
  options: ["Value proposition", "Stakeholder matrix", "Requirements attributes", "Release plan"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The nine blocks are key partnerships, key activities, key resources, value proposition, customer relationships, channels, customer segments, cost structure and revenue streams." },
{ id: "CC-SA-18", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "In a SWOT analysis, a company plans to use its strong distribution network to fend off a new low-cost competitor. Which strategy type is this?",
  options: ["SO", "WO", "WT", "ST"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "ST strategies use strengths to ward off threats. SO uses strengths to exploit opportunities; WO uses opportunities to address weaknesses." },
{ id: "CC-SA-19", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "The company only funds changes with an internal rate of return above 12%. Option X has an IRR of 15% and option Y of 9%. What does this suggest?",
  options: ["Option Y, since a lower rate of return means lower risk", "Neither, since IRR can’t be compared with a threshold", "Option X meets the threshold and option Y doesn’t", "Both; each IRR is positive"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "IRR is the discount rate at which an investment’s costs and benefits balance out exactly. Checking it against a minimum acceptable rate shows whether a change is worth investing in." },
{ id: "CC-SA-20", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "Two options cost the same today. Option X returns its benefits in year 1; option Y returns the same total in year 5. Which has the higher net present value?",
  options: ["Option Y, because the benefits will have grown over five years", "Option X, because earlier benefits are discounted less", "They are equal, since the totals are the same", "It can’t be known without first working out the payback period"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "NPV discounts future benefits to present value, so the same benefits received earlier are worth more today." },
{ id: "CC-SA-21", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "During current-state analysis, the business analyst records that the main ordering system is 15 years old and only runs on-premise. Which element is this?",
  options: ["Technology and infrastructure", "Organizational structure and culture", "External influencers", "Business needs"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Technology and infrastructure describes the information systems and infrastructure the organization relies on today." },
{ id: "CC-SA-22", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "The future state requires customers to sign contracts digitally, but current company rules require ink signatures. What should the future-state description include?",
  options: ["Nothing about policies, since policies are owned by HR", "The policy as a fixed constraint", "A note to revisit policies after go-live", "The policy changes needed to support the future state"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Defining the future state covers policies along with structure, capabilities, technology and assets. Policies that block the future state must change." },
{ id: "CC-SA-23", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "Nobody knows yet how customers will react to a new pricing model. When assessing risks, how does BABOK treat this?",
  options: ["As an unknown that creates uncertainty about the value of the change", "As a constraint that limits the options for the pricing model", "As a confirmed assumption, since customers usually accept change", "As irrelevant until launch, when real customer data becomes available"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Assess Risks considers unknowns, constraints, assumptions and dependencies as sources of uncertainty that may affect the value of the change." },
{ id: "CC-SA-24", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.6 Business Capability Analysis",
  question: "What is the main purpose of business capability analysis?",
  options: ["To design the user interface around the tasks each business unit performs", "To build a shared understanding of what the enterprise can do, as a basis for scoping and prioritizing", "To estimate the cost of each project in the change portfolio", "To list every system the company owns and map each one to the team that uses it daily"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Business capability analysis gives a framework for scoping and planning by creating shared understanding of outcomes, alignment with strategy, and a scope and prioritization filter." },
{ id: "CC-SA-25", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.38 Risk Analysis and Management",
  question: "Where are identified risks, their likelihood, impact and planned responses typically recorded?",
  options: ["In the glossary", "In the requirements traceability matrix", "In a risk register", "In the business model canvas"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A risk register lists each risk and supports analyzing them and planning responses." },

{ id: "CC-RADD-19", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.42 Sequence Diagrams",
  question: "In a sequence diagram, what does a lifeline represent?",
  options: ["How long an object exists during the scenario being modelled", "The time limit for completing the process in the scenario", "The data stored in a database during the interaction", "The status history of a requirement over the course of the project"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A lifeline represents an object’s lifespan during the scenario, drawn as a dashed line descending from the object box." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-20", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.13 Data Flow Diagrams",
  question: "In a data flow diagram, a bank that sends payment confirmations into the system but is outside it is shown as which element?",
  options: ["A process that transforms the incoming data", "A data store holding the confirmations", "A data flow", "An external entity (source or sink)"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Externals (entities, sources or sinks) are outside the system and send data to it or receive data from it." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-21", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.12 Data Dictionary",
  question: "Sales calls it “client number,” finance calls it “account ID.” Where in the data dictionary are these alternate names recorded?",
  options: ["Values/meanings", "Composite structure", "Aliases", "Description"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "For each data element, the data dictionary records a unique name, aliases used by different stakeholders, allowed values and meanings, and a description." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-22", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.47 Use Cases and Scenarios",
  question: "In a use case description, what is a trigger?",
  options: ["The final step of the main flow, which returns control to the actor", "An event that starts the flow of events, often an action by the primary actor", "A condition that has to hold before the use case can begin", "An error that ends the use case before the goal is reached"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A trigger is an event that starts the use case, most commonly an action by the primary actor, though a temporal event can also trigger it." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-23", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.48 User Stories",
  question: "Why is “conversation” an element of a user story?",
  options: ["Because the written story can’t capture everything; discussion builds shared understanding", "Because every story must be formally approved by the product owner in a meeting", "Because it records the story’s source and its priority", "Because conversations replace acceptance criteria for most user stories"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A user story doesn’t capture everything about a need. Conversation helps the team explore and understand the feature and the value it will deliver." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-24", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "A requirement is technically correct but written in jargon that business stakeholders can’t follow. Which quality characteristic is it missing?",
  options: ["Prioritized", "Consistent", "Feasible", "Understandable"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Understandable means represented using common terminology the audience can follow. A requirement can be correct and still fail this test." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-25", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.44 State Modelling",
  question: "Before drawing a state diagram, a business analyst lists states and allowed transitions in a two-dimensional grid. What is this?",
  options: ["A decision table", "A RACI matrix", "A state table", "A traceability matrix"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A state table sets out the states in a grid along with the moves allowed between them. It can be used instead of a state diagram, before one, or alongside it." },

{ id: "CC-SE-17", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "The difference between the expected and actual results of a solution is called what in BABOK?",
  options: ["A performance variance", "A solution limitation", "An enterprise limitation", "A dependency"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Performance variances are differences between expected and actual performance. Analyzing them and their causes leads to recommendations." },
{ id: "CC-SE-18", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Why should solution performance measures be validated with stakeholders before data is collected?",
  options: ["To make sure the vendor agrees with them before the contract is signed", "To make sure they align with business goals and help evaluate value", "To speed up data collection", "Because measures can’t be changed once data collection has started"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Validating performance measures confirms with stakeholders that they are aligned with business goals and objectives and will be useful for judging value." },
{ id: "CC-SE-19", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "A reporting module can’t deliver its value because it depends on data from another part of the solution that is often incomplete. Which element of Assess Solution Limitations covers this?",
  options: ["Stakeholder impact analysis of the report users", "Operational assessment of the reporting process", "Identify internal solution component dependencies", "Investigate solution problems in the reporting module"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Identifying internal solution component dependencies finds where one part of the solution limits the value another part can deliver." },
{ id: "CC-SE-20", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "Staff don’t see a new solution as beneficial and quietly work around it. Which assessment looks at this?",
  options: ["Gap analysis of the current and future state", "Interface analysis of the system hand-offs", "Requirements validation", "Enterprise culture assessment"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "An enterprise culture assessment checks whether stakeholders understand why the solution exists and see it as beneficial." },
{ id: "CC-SE-21", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A process step produces a monthly report nobody reads, and staff spend a day each month preparing it. Which recommendation fits BEST?",
  options: ["Avoid waste by removing the report", "Retire the whole solution", "Identify additional capabilities", "Reduce the complexity of interfaces"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Avoiding waste means removing activities that don’t add value and minimizing those that don’t contribute directly to the outcome." },
{ id: "CC-SE-22", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.27 Lessons Learned",
  question: "At the end of a release, the team meets to discuss what went well, what didn’t and what to change next time. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Item tracking", "Lessons learned", "Root cause analysis", "Benchmarking"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A lessons learned session gathers and records what went well, what could improve, what failed and what to recommend for future work." },
{ id: "CC-SE-23", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "In BABOK, what is reporting in the context of metrics and KPIs?",
  options: ["Sharing metrics or indicators with stakeholders in agreed formats and at agreed intervals", "Writing the business case that justifies the cost of collecting the measures", "Recording the defects found during testing and tracking how each one was resolved", "Documenting the elicitation results and sharing them with the stakeholders who took part"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Reporting is about getting the measures in front of stakeholders, in a format and on a schedule agreed in advance, as part of monitoring and evaluation." },
{ id: "CC-SE-24", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.3 Balanced Scorecard",
  question: "Why might an organization use a balanced scorecard rather than financial measures alone?",
  options: ["Because it converts customer and process results into financial terms", "Because regulators require it", "Because it balances financial measures with learning and growth, process and customer views", "Because it replaces the need for KPIs with a single summary score for the business"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The balanced scorecard measures performance across four dimensions, so financial results are balanced with customer, process and learning-and-growth measures." },
{ id: "CC-SE-25", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A team is rewarded for closing tickets quickly, so they close them before problems are truly fixed. Which recommendation addresses this?",
  options: ["Eliminate redundancy between the ticket queues used by each team", "Do nothing, since the agreed performance measure is being met by the team", "Retire the ticketing solution", "Adjust the solution performance measures so they reflect real value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Adjusting solution performance measures is appropriate when existing measures drive behaviour that doesn’t reflect the value the solution should deliver." },

/* --- Case study: Brightwater Clinics --- */
{ id: "K-CLN-1", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", case: "CASE-CLINIC", ref: "BABOK v3 10.31 Observation",
  question: "Receptionists say booking “just works” but can’t explain how they juggle phone calls, walk-ins and doctors’ schedule changes. Which technique should the business analyst use FIRST?",
  options: ["A survey of all patients about the booking experience", "Document analysis of the vendor’s product brochure", "A focus group with the doctors", "Observation of receptionists at the busiest clinic"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Observation suits work people find hard to describe and reveals the workarounds and exceptions that interviews or surveys miss." },
{ id: "K-CLN-2", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", case: "CASE-CLINIC", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "Which business objective BEST fits the case?",
  options: ["Implement an online booking app with reminders in all six clinics within six months", "Improve the patient experience in all clinics as quickly as possible", "Reduce missed appointments from 30% to 15% across all six clinics within 12 months", "Make receptionists’ work easier"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A business objective should be specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound. One option names a solution and two are vague." },
{ id: "K-CLN-3", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", case: "CASE-CLINIC", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "The practice manager wants to approve the reminder-message requirements herself, but the case says the data protection lead must approve anything involving patient contact data. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Let the practice manager approve them, since she sponsors the initiative", "Have the data protection lead approve the requirements that involve patient data", "Skip formal approval for reminder messages, since they are low risk and easy to change", "Have the receptionists approve them"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Approve Requirements starts with understanding stakeholder roles: who has authority to approve which requirements. Here the data protection lead holds that authority for patient data." },
{ id: "K-CLN-4", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", case: "CASE-CLINIC", ref: "BABOK v3 10.30 Non-Functional Requirements Analysis",
  question: "Many patients are over 70, and several have poor eyesight. Which non-functional category should the booking app’s requirements MOST clearly address?",
  options: ["Compatibility", "Portability across devices and operating systems", "Scalability", "Usability"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK treats usability as how easily users can learn to use the solution. For older patients with poor eyesight, that matters most." },

/* ===================== CBAP · batch 3 (BABOK-checked) ===================== */
{ id: "CP-BAPM-16", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Three business units will compete for the same development capacity. When planning the prioritization approach, what should the business analyst define?",
  options: ["Its formality, the participants and their roles, the process and the criteria", "Only which unit’s requests go first, ranked by the revenue each unit brings in", "The deadline for submitting requests to the team", "The tool used to record priorities and who can update it"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Planning the prioritization approach covers its formality and rigour, who takes part and in what role, the process itself, and the criteria used." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "The head of IT sees business analysis as overhead and pushes his team to skip reviews. How should this finding shape the business analyst’s planning?",
  options: ["Record it in the stakeholder list, then plan his engagement exactly as for any other stakeholder", "Place him in the low-interest quadrant of the stakeholder matrix and keep him informed by status reports", "Escalate immediately to the sponsor and stop planning until it is resolved", "Plan targeted collaboration with him that shows the value of the work, since his attitude can affect the team’s behaviour"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholder attitudes, including toward business analysis itself, affect behaviour. Knowing them lets the analyst plan collaboration and engagement specifically for that stakeholder." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "An enterprise program will produce thousands of requirements across eight products. Before work starts, the business analyst defines how this information will be structured and related. Which element is this?",
  options: ["Level of abstraction for each audience", "Requirements attributes", "Organization of business analysis information", "Plan for approvals"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The organization of business analysis information defines how information is structured and related, so it can be found and used effectively across a large initiative." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-19", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "A bank’s culture expects formal sign-off from every department head, while the regulator expects documented approval of risk-related requirements. What should the plan for approvals take into account?",
  options: ["The regulator’s expectations, since it has legal authority over the bank", "Culture, regulatory demands, the type of information, and who approves what and when", "The department heads’ expectations, since they own the processes", "Nothing in particular; approvals simply follow the organization’s default methodology"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Planning for approvals considers the types of information to approve, timing, process and approvers, shaped by organizational culture and regulatory needs." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-20", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Requirements reviews keep missing the same kind of omission. The business analyst introduces a review checklist so the omission is less likely to happen again. What type of improvement action is this?",
  options: ["Corrective, because it fixes the omissions already found", "Improvement, because it increases a positive outcome", "Preventive, because it reduces the probability of a negative event", "None; it is a verification activity, not an improvement"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK separates three kinds of action: preventive (make a negative event less likely), corrective (limit the harm once an event happens) and improvement (make positive events more likely or bigger)." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-21", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.19 Estimation",
  question: "The business analyst gives a detailed estimate for the next iteration and only rough estimates for later work, refining them as the initiative progresses. Which estimation method is this?",
  options: ["Delphi", "Rolling wave", "Parametric estimation from a calibrated model", "Bottom-up"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Rolling wave estimation repeats estimates throughout an initiative, with detailed estimates for near-term activities and coarser ones for later work." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-22", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.19 Estimation",
  question: "What is the main difference between top-down and bottom-up estimation?",
  options: ["Top-down estimates high-level components; bottom-up estimates the lowest-level elements and adds them up", "Top-down estimates are made by managers, while bottom-up estimates are made by the developers", "Top-down is always more accurate, because it uses data from similar past initiatives", "They differ in the units used for the estimate, such as hours or story points"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Top-down estimation examines components at a high level of a hierarchical breakdown; bottom-up estimates the lowest-level elements in detail and adds them up." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-23", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "The organization has a standard, repeatable business analysis process. How should the business analyst use it when planning a new initiative?",
  options: ["Let the project manager decide whether it applies", "Follow it exactly with no changes, whatever the initiative", "Use it for predictive initiatives and drop it for adaptive ones", "Use it as the basis and adapt the approach to the initiative’s needs"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A methodology or organizational standard can shape the approach and be reused across initiatives, while still being tailored to each one." },

{ id: "CP-EC-16", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.50 Workshops",
  question: "What does the facilitator do in the wrap-up after a workshop?",
  options: ["Approve the requirements agreed in the session on behalf of the sponsor and attendees", "Follow up open action items, complete the documentation and share it with those who need it", "Prioritize the requirements raised in the session together with the sponsor", "Confirm the agenda, ground rules and roles with attendees"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "After a workshop, the facilitator chases open actions, finishes the documentation and shares it with attendees and anyone else who needs to know." },
{ id: "CP-EC-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.25 Interviews",
  question: "Before scheduling interviews about a new pricing model, what should the business analyst define first?",
  options: ["The interview goal and the potential interviewees", "The final requirements to confirm with the interviewees", "Whether the interviews are structured", "The list of answers expected from each person"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Interview preparation starts with a clear goal and the right interviewees, then the questions, logistics and flow." },
{ id: "CP-EC-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.31 Observation",
  question: "The business analyst wants to measure how long each step of a claims process really takes, without the observer’s questions affecting the work. Which observation approach fits?",
  options: ["Active/noticeable observation", "Participatory observation, doing the work alongside staff", "Passive/unnoticeable observation", "A survey of claims handlers"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "In passive observation, the observer doesn’t interrupt the work and raises questions afterwards, which allows a natural flow and measurement of time and quality." },
{ id: "CP-EC-19", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.14 Data Mining",
  question: "In data mining, which activity comes before choosing a modelling technique?",
  options: ["Deployment of the model into operations", "Writing the final report", "Preparing the analytical dataset", "Measuring the model’s business impact"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK describes data mining as requirements elicitation, data preparation (the analytical dataset), data analysis, modelling techniques and deployment." },
{ id: "CP-EC-20", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "Why does the business analyst communicate a business analysis package to stakeholders?",
  options: ["To get formal sign-off from each stakeholder group before the solution design work begins", "To establish the requirements baseline that change control will apply to from now on", "To give stakeholders the right level of detail to understand the information and raise concerns", "To archive the requirements for future audits and reuse"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Communicating the package gives stakeholders an appropriate level of detail to understand the change, with the chance to review, ask questions and raise concerns." },
{ id: "CP-EC-21", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "While monitoring engagement, the business analyst sees a key stakeholder’s attitude toward the initiative getting steadily worse. What should happen?",
  options: ["Nothing, since a stakeholder’s attitude doesn’t affect the requirements themselves", "Change their RACI role from consulted to informed", "Wait until the stakeholder raises a formal complaint, then escalate it to the sponsor", "Act early to understand the cause and adjust collaboration, since engagement should hold or improve"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Monitoring stakeholder engagement checks that attitudes and interest stay constant or improve. A decline is a risk to address early." },
{ id: "CP-EC-22", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.10 Collaborative Games",
  question: "Product managers can’t agree which features would make customers want a new product. The business analyst asks teams to design its retail packaging. What is this game mainly used for?",
  options: ["Identifying features that drive interest in the marketplace", "Estimating the development effort for each proposed feature", "Finding hidden assumptions and differences between two groups", "Grouping existing requirements into themes"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Product box helps identify the features of a product that drive interest in the marketplace." },
{ id: "CP-EC-23", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.5 Brainstorming",
  question: "A brainstorming session generated 80 ideas. When should they be evaluated?",
  options: ["After each idea is written up as a requirement", "After idea generation, in a separate step", "During idea generation, so weak ideas are dropped early", "Only by the sponsor, without the group"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Brainstorming relies on a non-judgemental environment while ideas are generated; evaluation happens afterwards, in a separate step." },

{ id: "CP-RLCM-16", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.2 Backlog Management",
  question: "Why are items near the top of a backlog usually described in more detail than those near the bottom?",
  options: ["Because items at the bottom have already been rejected by the product owner", "Because items lower in the backlog have lower value and are less likely to be built at all", "Because detail is added just in time, to items that will be worked on soonest", "Because detailed items are more valuable, so they are placed higher"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Backlogs are refined continually. Items about to be worked on get detail and estimates; lower-priority items stay at a higher level until they move up." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.26 Item Tracking",
  question: "A team tracks open issues. Which use of item-tracking metrics is MOST valuable?",
  options: ["Tracking each item’s priority and owner in the issue log", "Seeing how long items stay open and spotting trends that need action", "Using the item count as a measure of requirements quality", "Measuring how many items each stakeholder raises, to see who is most engaged"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Item tracking metrics, such as items opened and closed and how long they stay open, show progress and trends that may need attention." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "A requirement is linked to the payment module that implements it and to the test case that checks it. Which two relationships are these?",
  options: ["Derive and depends", "Depends and validate", "Satisfy and validate", "Derive and satisfy"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Satisfy links a requirement to the implementation element that fulfils it; validate links it to a test or element that checks whether the solution meets it." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-19", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "What is the difference between the penalty and regulatory compliance bases for prioritization?",
  options: ["Penalty is the consequence of not implementing it; compliance covers what regulation or policy demands", "They are the same basis under different names, used in different methodologies", "Penalty applies only to vendor contracts; compliance applies only to internal staff policies", "Compliance is about the cost of a requirement; penalty is about the time it takes to deliver"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Penalty is the consequence of not implementing a requirement, such as fines or lost customers. Regulatory or policy compliance covers requirements that must be implemented to meet regulatory or policy demands." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-20", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "A proposed change would affect all 40,000 retail customers and four business processes, and would push a committed release back by a month. Which two impact analysis factors are these?",
  options: ["Benefit and cost", "Urgency and benefit", "Cost and urgency", "Impact and schedule"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Impact is how many customers or business processes are affected. Schedule is the effect on delivery dates already committed." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-21", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "A requirement is reworded after a change is approved, but its priority and owner are left as they were, even though both changed. What has been missed?",
  options: ["Maintaining the requirement’s attributes along with the requirement", "Tracing the requirement to its tests and the components that implement it", "Verifying the new wording against the organization’s quality checklist", "Approving the original requirement again"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Maintain Requirements covers both the requirements and their attributes. Out-of-date attributes mislead people who rely on them for decisions." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-22", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.39 Roles and Permissions Matrix",
  question: "A regional manager is given approval rights at her regional level, and stakeholders want these rights to apply to that region and its sub-units only. What does BABOK call this refinement in a roles and permissions matrix?",
  options: ["Delegation", "Segregation of duties", "Escalation", "Inheritance"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Inheritance means an authority assigned at one organizational level applies only to that level and its subsidiary units. Delegation passes authority from one person to another." },

{ id: "CP-SA-16", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.8 Business Model Canvas",
  question: "On a business model canvas for a new subscription service, where would the external logistics firm that delivers the boxes belong?",
  options: ["Customer segments", "Revenue streams", "Key partnerships", "Value proposition"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Key partnerships describe the network of suppliers and partners that help the business model work, such as an external delivery firm." },
{ id: "CP-SA-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "A company with an outdated product line faces a new regulation that would hit that line hardest. In SWOT terms, which strategy type addresses this combination?",
  options: ["SO", "ST", "WO", "WT"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "WT strategies address a weakness combined with a threat, typically by defending against the threat while reducing the weakness." },
{ id: "CP-SA-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "Option X has an internal rate of return of 18% and option Y of 11%. The company’s minimum acceptable rate is 12%. Both need the same investment. What does IRR suggest?",
  options: ["Both are acceptable, since they are positive", "Option X clears the minimum rate; option Y does not", "Option Y, since a lower IRR means lower risk", "IRR can’t be used to compare options"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "IRR is the discount rate at which an investment’s costs and benefits balance out exactly. Checking it against the minimum acceptable rate shows whether the investment is worth making." },
{ id: "CP-SA-19", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "A cheap software license turns out to need expensive integration, training and yearly support. Which financial measure would have shown this up front?",
  options: ["Payback period", "Return on investment for the first month", "Sensitivity analysis", "Total cost of ownership"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Total cost of ownership looks at all costs of acquiring, running and supporting a solution over its life, not just the purchase price." },
{ id: "CP-SA-20", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.16 Decision Analysis",
  question: "Which of these is a component of decision analysis in BABOK?",
  options: ["The decision maker, the alternatives and the decision criteria", "The project schedule and the resource plan for the initiative", "The conditions, outcomes and rules laid out in a decision table", "The stakeholders, their interests and their influence"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Decision analysis components include the decision to be made (or problem statement), the decision maker, the alternatives and the decision criteria." },
{ id: "CP-SA-21", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State · BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "What is the difference between the scope of the solution space and the solution scope?",
  options: ["They are the same thing", "The solution space is defined in Define Change Strategy, and solution scope in Define Future State", "Solution space: which kinds of options are considered. Solution scope: the boundaries of the chosen solution", "Solution scope is used only in agile approaches; the solution space is used in predictive ones"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Define Future State sets the scope of the solution space: the kinds of solutions that will be considered. Define Change Strategy then defines the solution scope: what the chosen solution will include." },
{ id: "CP-SA-22", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "The future state for a new wealth-advice service relies on the bank’s existing customer data and its trusted brand. Which future-state element does this concern?",
  options: ["Internal assets", "Policies", "External influencers", "Technology and infrastructure"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Defining the future state includes internal assets: the resources, tangible and intangible, that the future state will use or need." },
{ id: "CP-SA-23", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 11.4 The Business Architecture Perspective",
  question: "What does the business architecture perspective focus on?",
  options: ["Designing the user interfaces that staff use across every business unit, so they look and work the same way", "Modelling the enterprise to show how strategic concerns are met and to support transformation", "Managing software releases across the enterprise’s portfolio of systems", "Writing test cases for data warehouses and reporting platforms"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Business architecture models the enterprise to show how key stakeholders’ strategic concerns are addressed, and supports ongoing transformation." },

{ id: "CP-RADD-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.37 Reviews",
  question: "Two departments disagree on a proposed process. The business analyst runs a review of the draft with both, aiming to agree an approach. Which review objective is this?",
  options: ["To remove defects", "To reach agreement on an approach or a solution", "To ensure conformance to modelling standards", "To answer a single technical question"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Review objectives can include removing defects, ensuring conformance, checking completeness and correctness, establishing consensus, and answering questions or resolving issues." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-19", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.13 Data Flow Diagrams",
  question: "In a data flow diagram, a process has data flowing out of it but nothing flowing in. What does BABOK say about this?",
  options: ["It is fine, since processes can generate new data on their own", "It means the process is really an external entity acting as a source", "It should be redrawn as a data store, since it only produces data", "It breaks the rule that every process needs at least one flow in and one flow out"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK’s rule is that every process in a data flow diagram needs at least one flow in and at least one flow out." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-20", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.15 Data Modelling",
  question: "A data model records when each entity was created or changed, why, how it should be used and who uses it. What is this information called?",
  options: ["Metadata", "Cardinality", "Attributes of the entities", "Primitive data elements"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Metadata is data about the data: what an entity represents, when and why it was created or changed, and how, how often and by whom it is used." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-21", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.22 Functional Decomposition",
  question: "When using functional decomposition, how deep should the breakdown go?",
  options: ["To a fixed number of levels, so all decompositions look consistent", "Until each item can be traced to a single requirement", "Until the pieces are understood well enough for the objective", "One level below the top, to keep it readable"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The level of decomposition depends on the objective: break things down until the components are understood well enough to analyze, estimate or allocate them." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-22", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 11.2 The Business Intelligence Perspective",
  question: "What is the focus of the business intelligence perspective?",
  options: ["Automating business processes through workflow tools and robotic process automation", "Designing organizational structures that support data ownership", "Agile delivery of software features in short iterations", "Turning data into value-added information: sourcing, integrating, enhancing and delivering it"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The BI perspective is about turning data into useful information: where it comes from, how it is combined, and how it is improved and delivered for decisions." },

{ id: "CP-SE-16", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options · BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "When are solution performance measures first defined, according to BABOK’s guidance on design options?",
  options: ["After go-live, once the solution has produced enough data", "For each design option, while design options are being defined", "When performance problems appear in operation", "Only in the business case, when the investment is first approved"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes that performance measures are set for each design option, so they are ready when the chosen solution is evaluated later." },
{ id: "CP-SE-17", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "A solution meets its targets, but the business now expects more value from it than when the targets were set. What should the analysis consider?",
  options: ["The original targets, since those are what stakeholders agreed to", "Nothing more; targets met means the value has been delivered", "How performance compares with the value now desired, not just the original targets", "The cost of the solution compared with the original budget"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Analyzing performance compares solution performance with the desired value. If expectations have changed, the comparison should reflect that." },
{ id: "CP-SE-18", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "Users report that a new pricing engine sometimes gives wrong quotes. What should the business analyst do FIRST when investigating the solution?",
  options: ["Investigate the problem to find its underlying cause before recommending anything", "Recommend replacing the pricing engine with a better-tested product", "Ask users to check every quote by hand until the vendor fixes it", "Assess enterprise culture to see whether staff trust the pricing engine’s quotes"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Investigating solution problems, often with root cause analysis, finds the underlying cause so recommendations target it rather than the symptom." },
{ id: "CP-SE-19", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "When assessing enterprise culture for a new customer portal, whose perceptions should be considered?",
  options: ["The project team’s and the sponsor’s, since they understand the solution best", "Only senior management’s, since they set the culture for everyone", "The vendor’s, since it has seen many similar rollouts", "Both internal and external stakeholders’, such as staff and customers"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Cultural assessment looks at the beliefs and attitudes of the stakeholders affected, both internal and external, since both affect whether value is realized." },
{ id: "CP-SE-20", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Work passes between five people and three systems to approve a single discount, and each hand-off introduces errors. Which recommendation fits BEST?",
  options: ["Do nothing, since the discounts still get approved in the end", "Reduce interface complexity, such as the hand-offs between people and systems", "Train the five people involved so each hand-off is checked more carefully", "Identify additional capabilities the discount process could offer"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "An interface exists wherever work passes between systems or people. Simplifying these hand-offs makes the work easier to understand and cuts errors." },
{ id: "CP-SE-21", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "An indicator gives enough information to judge whether performance is good or bad. Which characteristic of a good indicator does this describe?",
  options: ["Clear", "Economical", "Adequate", "Trustworthy and credible"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An adequate indicator gives enough basis to judge whether performance is good or bad." },
{ id: "CP-SE-22", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "In BABOK, how do an indicator and a metric relate?",
  options: ["An indicator measures progress toward a goal; a metric is a quantified level of it", "A metric is always financial, while an indicator is always qualitative", "They are synonyms; BABOK uses both terms for any measure of performance", "An indicator is a KPI, while a metric is an operational measure"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "An indicator names a measurement of progress toward a goal. A metric is a level of that indicator expressed as a number. A KPI tracks progress toward a strategic goal." },
{ id: "CP-SE-23", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 11.5 The Business Process Management Perspective",
  question: "In the business process management life cycle, which activity follows monitoring and uses its data to improve the process?",
  options: ["Designing", "Modelling", "Retiring", "Optimizing"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK’s BPM life cycle covers designing, modelling, execution and monitoring, and optimizing. Optimizing repeats the earlier phases, using execution and monitoring results to remove inefficiencies and add value." },
{ id: "CP-SE-24", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 11.1 The Agile Perspective",
  question: "According to the agile perspective, how do business analysts work on agile initiatives?",
  options: ["They complete all analysis before the first iteration, then support the team", "They hand requirements to the team and step back", "They continually reassess, adapt and adjust as the initiative changes", "They focus on the item at the top of the product backlog"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Agile initiatives involve constant change, so business analysts continually reassess, adapt and adjust their work." },

/* --- Case study: Quillmoor Bank --- */
{ id: "K-BNK-1", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", case: "CASE-BANK", ref: "BABOK v3 10.34 Process Analysis",
  question: "The COO wants a new loan system to cut approval time from 21 to 5 days. What analysis should the business analyst do FIRST to see whether a new system would achieve that?",
  options: ["Analyze the current process, e.g. value stream mapping, to separate waiting and work time", "Issue a request for proposal to loan origination software vendors straight away", "Write the requirements for the new loan system based on the COO’s goal", "Benchmark the bank’s IT spending on lending against its main competitors"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "With a weekly credit committee and several hand-offs, much of the 21 days may be waiting time. Value stream mapping separates waiting from processing time and shows whether new software would help." },
{ id: "K-BNK-2", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", case: "CASE-BANK", ref: "BABOK v3 10.9 Business Rules Analysis · BABOK v3 10.17 Decision Modelling",
  question: "Credit rules live in analysts’ spreadsheets, and two analysts apply them differently. What should the business analyst recommend for these rules?",
  options: ["Train the credit analysts on the credit policy so each applies the rules the same way", "Hard-code the rules into the new system’s screens so analysts can’t change them", "Document the rules in the process model only, next to the steps that use them", "Define the rules explicitly and manage them separately, e.g. in a decision model, for consistency"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Business rules should be stated explicitly and managed separately from processes and systems. Decision modelling makes complex rules consistent and easier to change." },
{ id: "K-BNK-3", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", case: "CASE-BANK", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Changes to credit rules must be approved by the credit risk department. Where should this be defined?",
  options: ["In the stakeholder register, as a note against the credit risk department’s entry", "In the governance approach, including who approves changes to credit rules", "In the elicitation plan for the credit risk interviews", "In the information management approach for the rules"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The governance approach defines decision making, the change control process and who approves what, which here includes credit risk approval of rule changes." },
{ id: "K-BNK-4", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", case: "CASE-BANK", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements · BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "The regulator can ask the bank to show which credit rules implement which capital requirements. What level of traceability is appropriate?",
  options: ["No traceability, since the rules can be read directly in the system", "Informal links, kept in the analysts’ notes and spreadsheets and shared on request", "Formal, maintained traceability from regulatory requirements to credit rules and tests", "Traceability for the rules that change most often, which keeps the effort proportionate to the risk"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The level of traceability depends on complexity, risk and regulation. Regulatory scrutiny calls for formal, maintained traceability that can be shown on request." },
{ id: "K-BNK-5", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", case: "CASE-BANK", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance · BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "After go-live, approval time falls to 6 days. Which additional measure is MOST important before calling the change a success?",
  options: ["The default rate on newly approved loans, to check speed hasn’t increased risk", "The number of re-keying steps removed across the three systems", "The number of training hours delivered to relationship managers", "The adoption rate of the new system among relationship managers"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Performance measures should reflect the full value and the risks involved. Faster approval is only valuable if loan quality holds, so the default rate must be monitored alongside speed." },
/* ===================== CCBA · batch 4 (BABOK-checked) ===================== */
{ id: "CC-BAPM-27", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "Development will be outsourced to a team in another country, and staff turnover in the business unit is high. How should this affect the business analysis approach?",
  options: ["Less documentation, since outsourced teams prefer working software over documents", "More formality and documentation, since both factors call for it", "No effect; formality depends only on the methodology the organization uses", "More workshops, so knowledge passes on verbally before staff leave"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK lists outsourced resources and high staff turnover among the factors that call for more formal documentation and representations." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-28", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "When is a predictive planning approach MOST likely to be preferred?",
  options: ["When the team wants to explore options to find the best solution", "When the goal is incremental improvement of an existing solution", "When getting the implementation wrong would be unacceptably risky", "When stakeholders want frequent feedback on a working solution from the start"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Predictive approaches tend to suit work where requirements can be set before building, where a wrong implementation would be too risky, or where stakeholders are hard to engage." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-29", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "The business analyst builds the plan by taking a similar past initiative as an outline and adding the tasks unique to the new one. Which element of the business analysis approach is this?",
  options: ["Timing of business analysis work", "Complexity and risk", "Acceptance of the approach", "Business analysis activities"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Defining business analysis activities can mean breaking deliverables into tasks, working iteration by iteration, or using a similar previous initiative as an outline." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-30", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "Which factor does BABOK list as raising the RISK level of business analysis work, rather than its complexity?",
  options: ["How many business areas or systems the change touches", "Technological complexities in the solution and its integrations", "How experienced stakeholders are at communicating their needs", "Geographic and cultural considerations"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Risk factors include the analyst’s experience and domain knowledge, stakeholders’ experience communicating needs, their attitudes and the time they can give. The other three are listed as complexity factors." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-31", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "How often is business analysis planning usually done on an initiative?",
  options: ["Once at the very start, after which the approved plan is followed without change", "Typically more than once, as plans are updated for changing conditions and new issues", "At the end of each phase, when the sponsor reviews what was delivered", "Never formally; plans emerge from the team’s daily work"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes planning usually happens more than once, as plans are revised for changing business conditions and new issues." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-32", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Where can a business analyst look to find stakeholders OUTSIDE the organization?",
  options: ["Existing contracts, likely vendors, and regulatory and governing bodies", "The organizational chart and the internal phone directory, since they list every party", "The sponsor’s list of department heads and their direct reports", "The help desk’s ticket history, since it shows who complains"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "External stakeholders can be found through existing contracts, anticipated vendors, and regulatory and governing bodies. Shareholders, customers and suppliers are also considered." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-33", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "A change needs the support of three departments, but the sponsor has real influence over only one of them. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Proceed anyway, since the sponsor’s approval is enough", "Note it as an assumption that support will follow, and revisit it later", "Plan less collaboration with the two departments, since the sponsor can’t influence them", "Develop strategies and risk responses to secure the support the change needs"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "When the influence a change needs doesn’t match the influence key stakeholders have, BABOK says to develop risk plans, responses and other strategies to get the support required." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-34", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Which of these is a consideration when planning stakeholder collaboration?",
  options: ["The sponsor’s preferred meeting times and the size of the meeting rooms available", "The number of requirements each stakeholder has raised in earlier sessions", "Timing, frequency, location, tools, delivery method and stakeholder preferences", "Seniority, since senior stakeholders need less collaboration than junior ones"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Planning collaboration considers timing and frequency, location, available tools, in-person or virtual delivery, and the preferences of the stakeholders." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-35", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Field engineers are rarely at a desk and read messages only on their phones. Which stakeholder communication need does this MOST affect?",
  options: ["Who the appropriate audience is", "The appropriate delivery method", "What needs to be communicated", "How formal the requirements must be"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Communication needs include what to communicate, the delivery method, the audience, timing and frequency. Here the delivery method is the main concern." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-36", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Stakeholders are deadlocked on a requirement. Which part of governance planning should already define what happens next?",
  options: ["The decision-making process, including escalation paths and who has final authority", "The requirements attributes, including priority, stability and ownership", "The elicitation plan, including which techniques to use and who attends", "The traceability approach, including which links to keep and how often to review them"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The decision-making process says what happens when people can’t agree, including how to escalate and who makes the final call." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-37", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "When deciding where to store business analysis information, what should the business analyst consider?",
  options: ["Which tool is cheapest, and whether the vendor offers a discount for a three-year contract", "Where the previous initiative kept its documents, to stay consistent across projects", "Who needs access, how often and under what conditions, plus standards and available tools", "Only the developers’ preferred repository, since they use the information most often"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Where information lives depends on who needs it, how often and under what conditions, as well as on organizational standards and the tools available." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-38", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Which requirements attribute indicates how mature a requirement is?",
  options: ["Status", "Priority", "Complexity", "Stability"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stability indicates the maturity of a requirement. Status shows its state, such as proposed, accepted or implemented." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-39", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "When does BABOK suggest recording a requirement’s urgency separately from its priority?",
  options: ["Usually only when a deadline exists for implementation", "Always, for every requirement in the initiative", "Never, because urgency and priority mean the same thing", "Only when the sponsor asks for it, as an exception"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Urgency says how soon a requirement is needed. It usually only needs recording separately from priority when there is a deadline." },
{ id: "CC-BAPM-40", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Stakeholders say the business analyst’s models couldn’t be used without a long walkthrough. Which business analysis performance measure does this concern?",
  options: ["Timeliness", "Knowledge", "Effectiveness", "Significance"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Effectiveness looks at whether work products could be used on their own or needed a lot of explaining." },

{ id: "CC-EC-26", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Why should the business analyst understand the scope of an elicitation activity before it starts?",
  options: ["To decide who will approve the results", "To respond if the activity strays and to recognize when it is complete", "To decide which requirements attributes will be recorded later", "So the activity can cover every topic stakeholders raise, whether in scope or not"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Understanding the scope of elicitation lets the analyst respond if the activity strays, notice when people or materials aren’t available, and recognize when the activity is complete." },
{ id: "CC-EC-27", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "For a remote session, the business analyst plans to use an online whiteboard that stakeholders have never seen. What does BABOK suggest?",
  options: ["Run the session as planned and gather feedback on the tool afterwards", "Use a tool stakeholders already know instead, even if it can’t do what is needed", "Confirm the tool is listed in the information management approach", "Plan extra preparation and show stakeholders how it works beforehand"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Novel tools may need additional planning, and preparing stakeholders can include educating them on how a technique or tool works." },
{ id: "CC-EC-28", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Which activity is part of securing supporting material for elicitation?",
  options: ["Gathering documents such as business rules, policies, regulations and contracts", "Booking the meeting room, the equipment and the catering for the session", "Explaining the session’s purpose to stakeholders and how the technique works", "Choosing which stakeholders to invite and booking the room and equipment for them"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Supporting material can include people, systems, historical data and documents such as business rules, policies, regulations, contracts and draft models. Booking rooms is logistics." },
{ id: "CC-EC-29", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation",
  question: "During an interview, the stakeholder starts discussing a topic outside the scope of the change. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Let it continue as long as needed, since information outside scope may still turn out useful", "Reschedule the rest of the interview", "Recognize it, then acknowledge it and continue, or steer the conversation back", "Add the topic to the scope of the change so the stakeholder feels heard"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "While guiding elicitation, the analyst spots off-scope discussion as it happens and decides whether to note it and carry on, or steer back." },
{ id: "CC-EC-30", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation",
  question: "A useful requirement comes up in an unplanned conversation by the coffee machine. What should happen to it?",
  options: ["Ignore it, since only planned elicitation activities count", "Raise it as a change request, since it came up outside a planned session", "Wait for the next workshop to see if it comes up again", "Capture it and integrate it into the planned elicitation outcomes"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Results from unplanned elicitation are still captured and folded into the planned outcomes they relate to." },
{ id: "CC-EC-31", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "Interviews and document analysis give different accounts of how refunds are approved. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Keep the interview version, since staff know the real process", "Identify the variation and resolve it with stakeholders", "Keep the documented version, since it is written down", "Log both as assumptions to confirm during testing"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "When comparing results from several elicitation activities, the analyst identifies variations and resolves them in collaboration with stakeholders." },
{ id: "CC-EC-32", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "Besides checking with stakeholders, what else can help confirm recent elicitation results?",
  options: ["Comparing them with historical data", "A MoSCoW ranking of the results by stakeholders", "The stakeholder matrix, to weigh results by influence", "Approval of the results by the solution vendor"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Elicitation results can be compared with source information, with other elicitation results, and with historical data." },
{ id: "CC-EC-33", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "The business analyst needs a stable, long-term record of requirements that follows the organization’s template. Which form of package fits?",
  options: ["Informal documentation", "A presentation", "Formal documentation", "Photos of the workshop whiteboards"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Formal documentation usually follows the organization’s template and gives a stable, easy-to-use record that lasts." },
{ id: "CC-EC-34", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "A requirements package is mature and needs little or no explanation. Which communication platform does BABOK suggest?",
  options: ["Group collaboration with the whole team", "Individual collaboration", "A facilitated workshop with all reviewers", "E-mail or other written channels"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A mature package that needs little or no talking through can go out by e-mail or another written channel." },
{ id: "CC-EC-35", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "A key subject matter expert keeps cancelling sessions because her manager hasn’t freed up her time. What does the business analyst MOST need?",
  options: ["A RACI matrix that marks her as accountable for the requirements", "Agreement on her time commitment, with her and her manager", "To continue without her input and note the gap", "To switch to a survey so she can answer in her own time"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Managing stakeholder collaboration includes gaining agreement on the time and resources stakeholders commit to business analysis activities." },
{ id: "CC-EC-36", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.21 Focus Groups",
  question: "What does a focus group discussion guide give the moderator?",
  options: ["The screening criteria and invitee list for choosing participants", "A seating plan, the room layout and a list of the refreshments to provide", "A prepared script of questions and topics, and the structure to follow", "The recorder’s note template and the outline of the final report"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A discussion guide gives the moderator a prepared script of questions and topics that meet the objective, along with the structure of the session." },
{ id: "CC-EC-37", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.18 Document Analysis",
  question: "For each document reviewed, the business analyst notes the topic, type and source, quotes the key details, adds a short critique and lists follow-up actions. What is the business analyst using?",
  options: ["A traceability matrix", "A document analysis chart", "A data dictionary", "A stakeholder register"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK describes a document analysis chart for this: each document’s topic, type and source, the exact details, the analyst’s own critique, and any follow-ups." },

{ id: "CC-RLCM-24", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Online card payments only make sense if the online checkout is also built. Which traceability relationship is this?",
  options: ["Derive", "Depends, of the effort type", "Satisfy", "Depends, of the necessity type"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A necessity link means one requirement is pointless without the other. An effort link means it is easier to build alongside the other." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-25", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Which three purposes does BABOK give for tracing requirements?",
  options: ["Budgeting, scheduling and staffing", "Estimation, prioritization and approval", "Impact analysis, coverage and allocation", "Testing, deployment and user training"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Tracing connects requirements to each other and to designs, solution components and other outputs, so the team can see the impact of a change, check nothing is missed, and allocate requirements." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-26", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "A team wants to reuse a set of requirements from an earlier initiative. What must happen before they are accepted into the new change?",
  options: ["Stakeholders confirm the requirements are still valid for reuse", "Nothing further; requirements approved once can be reused as they are", "The original author rewrites them in full for the new context", "The developers decide whether they still apply"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Requirements meant for reuse should reflect how things work today, and stakeholders validate them before they are taken into a new change." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-27", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Stakeholders prioritize by benefit. Developers then reorder for technical sequence, and stakeholders reprioritize once costs are known. What does this illustrate?",
  options: ["A conflict that should be escalated through the decision process", "Scope creep that the change control process should have blocked earlier", "Gold plating by the implementation team, which the change board should control", "Continual prioritization, as the basis changes when information emerges"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK describes continual prioritization: priorities shift as context and information change, and the basis may differ at different stages, exactly as in this example." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-28", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Different types of requirements respond differently to the same prioritization criteria, so they appear to conflict. What may stakeholders need to do?",
  options: ["Give every requirement the same priority", "Make trade-offs", "Switch to a time-boxing approach", "Escalate the conflict to the change control board"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes that different requirement types may not respond to criteria in the same way, so stakeholders may need to make trade-offs." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-29", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Two requirements change the same screen and would be cheaper to build together, but one has lower value. Which prioritization basis might raise its priority?",
  options: ["Dependencies", "Stability", "Penalty", "Regulatory or policy compliance"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Under dependencies, BABOK notes that building related requirements together can sometimes save effort." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-30", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "What determines how formally a proposed requirements change is assessed?",
  options: ["The seniority of the person who proposed it and how urgently they asked", "The number of pages it affects in the specification", "The information available, the change’s apparent importance and the governance process", "Whether the change came from inside or outside the organization, and from whom"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "How formal the assessment is depends on what is known, how important the change looks and the governance process. Predictive approaches usually assess more formally." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-31", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "A change has been analyzed and a decision made. What does impact resolution require?",
  options: ["The sponsor is told the outcome, and the change log is updated by the business analyst", "The change is reprioritized in the backlog", "Only the developers need the details, since they will implement the change", "All impacts and resolutions are documented and shared with all stakeholders"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Authorized stakeholders may approve, deny or defer a change. All impacts and resolutions are documented and communicated to all stakeholders." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-32", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Only two people can approve the requirements, but several others can influence the decision. How should the business analyst treat those influential stakeholders?",
  options: ["Ignore them, since they have no formal authority", "Consult or inform them as appropriate", "Log their concerns as project assumptions", "Ask them to approve the requirements informally"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Only a few people may have authority to approve, but many can sway the decision. BABOK says to think about who should be consulted or kept informed." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-33", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Two departments interpret the same requirement differently and value it differently. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Pick the interpretation of the larger department, since more people use it", "Ask the sponsor to choose one interpretation, so the team can move on quickly", "Prioritize the requirement lower until the departments agree", "Facilitate communication so each group better understands the other’s needs"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "In conflict and issue management, the analyst facilitates communication between stakeholders so each group better appreciates the needs of the others." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-34", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Why might it be valuable to keep an audit history of requirements changes and approvals?",
  options: ["It shows what changed, who changed it, why and when", "It replaces the need for traceability on later changes", "It lets developers skip impact analysis for small changes", "It removes the need for approval in future"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Tracking approval keeps an accurate record of what is approved. An audit history records what was changed, who changed it, the reason and when." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-35", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.26 Item Tracking",
  question: "An issue’s record names who must resolve it and how it is escalated if not resolved by its due date. Which technique uses records like this?",
  options: ["Backlog management", "Lessons learned", "Item tracking", "Risk analysis and management"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Item tracking records attributes such as owner, resolver, resolution date, status, agreed strategy and an escalation matrix for each item." },
{ id: "CC-RLCM-36", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.33 Prioritization",
  question: "Finance and HR disagree on which payroll requirements matter most, so the business analyst brings them together to reach a consensus on what comes first. Which prioritization technique is this?",
  options: ["Grouping", "Ranking", "Budgeting", "Negotiation"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Negotiation means getting stakeholders to agree among themselves which requirements take priority." },

{ id: "CC-SA-26", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "During current-state analysis, the business analyst notes that loan decisions must follow a credit policy approved by the board. Which current-state element is this?",
  options: ["Internal assets", "Policies", "Business architecture", "External influencers"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Policies set the limits of decision making at different levels, and they are one of the things examined in the current state." },
{ id: "CC-SA-27", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Only two companies make a component the business depends on, and both can set prices more or less as they like. Which external influencer is this?",
  options: ["Industry structure", "Competitors", "Suppliers", "Political and regulatory environment"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "External influencers include suppliers: the variety and diversity of suppliers, and the power they have over their customers, can influence the enterprise." },
{ id: "CC-SA-28", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "Why should assumptions be identified when defining the future state?",
  options: ["So appropriate decisions can be made if an assumption later proves invalid", "So they can be treated as confirmed facts for the rest of the initiative", "So the sponsor can approve them as requirements", "So they can be listed in the business case as facts the sponsor has accepted"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "It is often impossible to prove a new capability will meet a need. Assumptions are identified and understood so decisions can be made if they prove invalid." },
{ id: "CC-SA-29", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "The sponsor says the future state must reuse the existing CRM and be live by the third quarter. What are these, in BABOK terms?",
  options: ["Assumptions", "Business objectives", "Potential value", "Constraints"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Constraints are things the solution can’t change or must include, such as fixed technology or time limits." },
{ id: "CC-SA-30", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "The change strategy assumes a vendor will deliver an interface on time. How can the business analyst handle this when assessing risks?",
  options: ["Ignore it, since assumptions are agreed facts that don’t need analysis", "Turn it into a contractual requirement for the vendor and move on", "Restate it as a risk: the event that could occur and its consequences", "Record it as a constraint on the strategy"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Assumptions, constraints and dependencies can be examined for risk, and turned into risks by naming what could happen and what would follow." },
{ id: "CC-SA-31", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "How does BABOK describe a neutral attitude toward risk?",
  options: ["Accepting some risk, as long as the action won’t cause a loss even if the risks happen", "An unwillingness to accept much uncertainty, even if reducing it means accepting lower value", "A willingness to take on more risk in exchange for greater value", "Indifference to whether or not risks occur"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK describes three attitudes: risk-averse, neutral and risk-seeking. Neutral means some risk is fine, as long as the action won’t end in a loss if the risks do happen." },
{ id: "CC-SA-32", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "After assessing each risk’s likelihood and impact, what does the business analyst estimate from the full set of risks?",
  options: ["The number of risk owners needed to manage all of the risks", "The sponsor’s risk tolerance for the change", "The date by which each risk is expected to occur, if it occurs", "A total risk level showing the overall potential impact"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "From the full set of risks, the analyst estimates an overall risk level. Sometimes it can be expressed in money, time or effort." },
{ id: "CC-SA-33", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Why does BABOK recommend describing the current and future states with the same techniques?",
  options: ["It is required by most methodologies and by the IIBA standards", "It simplifies the comparison when doing gap analysis", "It lets the team reuse the models in design", "It makes the future state easier to approve by the steering committee"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Describing both states the same way makes them easier to compare, which helps gap analysis." },
{ id: "CC-SA-34", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Gap analysis shows the enterprise can meet the need with its current capabilities. What does this suggest?",
  options: ["The change is likely to be small, or no change may be needed", "Transition states are needed to bring the capabilities into place", "The current state analysis must have been wrong", "The solution scope should be widened to deliver more value"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "If today’s capabilities already meet the need, the change will probably be small, or may not be needed at all." },
{ id: "CC-SA-35", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Two change strategies deliver the same total value, but one lets the company start realizing benefits six months sooner. Which selection factor does this concern?",
  options: ["Organizational readiness", "Opportunity cost", "Timelines for value realization", "Alignment to the business objectives"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Choosing a change strategy weighs readiness, major costs, time to make the change, fit with objectives, when value starts to arrive, and opportunity cost." },
{ id: "CC-SA-36", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Why should the change strategy options that were rejected be kept as part of the final strategy?",
  options: ["To show the sponsor how much analysis work was done on the strategy", "They are needed to calculate net present value", "So the team can switch to one of them if the chosen option is cancelled later", "They show stakeholders the pros and cons of the approaches considered"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Recording the options that were rejected helps stakeholders see the pros and cons of each approach considered." },
{ id: "CC-SA-37", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A future state promises high net benefits, but the investment it needs is more than the company can afford. What may happen?",
  options: ["The company proceeds anyway, since the net benefits are positive", "It may decide not to go ahead, and put the money into something else", "The future state is approved, and funding is found later in the year", "Its risk level is rated lower, since high net benefits offset the investment"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes that even large net benefits may not be enough if the investment is beyond what the enterprise can bear, so it may invest elsewhere." },
{ id: "CC-SA-38", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis",
  question: "A team has built a fishbone diagram listing possible causes of late deliveries. What should happen before they brainstorm solutions?",
  options: ["Implement fixes for every cause listed", "Vote on the most likely cause and move on", "Confirm the real cause, preferably with data", "Ask the sponsor to choose which cause to address"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A fishbone diagram only shows possible causes. More analysis, preferably backed by data, should confirm the real cause before anyone brainstorms fixes." },
{ id: "CC-SA-39", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.4 Benchmarking and Market Analysis",
  question: "The business analyst identifies customers and their preferences, investigates competitors, looks for market trends and estimates potential profitability. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Market analysis", "SWOT analysis", "Business model canvas", "Focus groups"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Market analysis involves identifying customers and preferences, investigating competitors, looking for trends, anticipating growth and estimating potential profitability." },

{ id: "CC-RADD-26", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "Which model category represents the “why” of a change?",
  options: ["Activity flow", "Rationale", "Capability", "People and roles"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Rationale models explain why a change is needed. Examples include root cause analysis, decision modelling, scope modelling, business rules analysis and the business model canvas." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-27", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "Which set of techniques does BABOK list for capability models?",
  options: ["Use cases and scenarios, user stories and process modelling for each feature", "Data dictionary, glossary, data flow diagrams, data modelling and state modelling", "Organizational modelling, roles and permissions matrix, and personas", "Business capability analysis, functional decomposition and prototyping"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Capability models focus on features or functions, using business capability analysis, functional decomposition and prototyping. The other sets belong to other categories." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-28", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "When analyzing requirements, what does the business analyst examine the decomposed information for?",
  options: ["What must change to meet the business need, plus anything the sponsor has asked to be added later", "Only missing components, since all the others are already known", "What must change or stay the same, missing or unnecessary parts, and constraints or assumptions", "Only the components that developers have flagged as risky to build or test"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Analyze Requirements decomposes information to look for what must change, what should stay the same, missing and unnecessary components, and constraints or assumptions." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-29", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "What does the required level of decomposition and detail in requirements depend on?",
  options: ["Stakeholders’ understanding, risk of miscommunication, standards, and contractual or regulatory duties", "Mostly the number of developers on the team, how experienced they are and how soon they start", "Mainly the size of the budget for the initiative and how much has already been spent", "Only the requirements tool the team is using and the templates it provides"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The level of decomposition and detail varies with stakeholders’ knowledge, the potential for misunderstanding, organizational standards, and contractual or regulatory obligations." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-30", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.3 Validate Requirements",
  question: "A product has no precedent, so stakeholders assume customers will respond well to it. What should the business analyst do when validating requirements?",
  options: ["Treat the assumption as a confirmed requirement for the solution", "Record it as a constraint on the design", "Wait until launch, when real customer data is available, before validating", "Identify and define the assumption so its risks can be managed"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Validate Requirements includes identifying assumptions, such as customer response to an unprecedented product, so that the associated risks can be managed." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-31", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.3 Validate Requirements",
  question: "When validating requirements, the business analyst sets baseline metrics from the current state and target metrics for the business objectives. Why?",
  options: ["To estimate how much effort it will take to build and test each of the solution components", "To define measurable criteria for judging the change’s success after implementation", "To rank the requirements for the first release by how much they move each metric", "To decide which vendor to contract, based on which one can reach the target metrics"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Define Measurable Evaluation Criteria sets how success will be evaluated after implementation, often with baseline and target metrics." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-32", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.3 Validate Requirements",
  question: "A requirement would benefit a stakeholder but doesn’t align with the solution scope. What are the options?",
  options: ["Keep it in scope, since any requirement that benefits a stakeholder is worth delivering", "Verify it against the quality checklist before deciding on its scope", "Re-evaluate the future state and change the scope, or remove the requirement", "Mark it as approved but low priority, so it can be built if time allows"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A requirement can benefit a stakeholder and still not be desirable. When requirements don’t align, the future state is re-evaluated and scope changed, or the requirement is removed." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-33", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "In requirements architecture, what is the difference between a viewpoint and a view?",
  options: ["A viewpoint sets the conventions for representing requirements; a view is what is produced from it", "A view is the template for representing requirements; a viewpoint is the finished document", "They mean the same thing", "A viewpoint belongs to the sponsor and a view belongs to the developers who build it"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Viewpoints tell analysts what information to provide for each stakeholder group. Views are the actual requirements and designs produced for a solution from a chosen viewpoint." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-34", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "Why shouldn’t a business analyst put the conventions of many viewpoints into one single viewpoint?",
  options: ["Because BABOK allows only one model type and one notation per viewpoint", "Because stakeholders are shown one model at a time, so combined views can’t be reviewed", "Because most tools can’t store combined viewpoints, link them to each other or report on them", "Because it becomes overwhelming and holds information irrelevant to some stakeholder groups"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Putting too much into one viewpoint makes it too complex and filled with information irrelevant to particular stakeholder groups." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-35", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "While verifying requirements relationships, the business analyst finds one that links two elements in two different and conflicting ways. Which quality criterion does it fail?",
  options: ["Necessary", "Unambiguous", "Correct", "Consistent"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Relationships should be necessary, correct, unambiguous and consistent. A link that ties two elements together in two conflicting ways fails the unambiguous test." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-36", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "A design option changes staff responsibilities and shares one process between two teams to simplify the work. Which improvement opportunity is this?",
  options: ["Improve access to information", "Identify additional capabilities", "Reduce complexity of interfaces", "Increase efficiencies"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Increasing efficiencies means automating or simplifying work, for example by redesigning or sharing processes, changing responsibilities or outsourcing." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-37", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "When does requirements allocation usually begin, and how long does it continue?",
  options: ["It begins at the start of elicitation and ends as soon as the requirements are approved by stakeholders", "It begins once a solution approach is determined and continues through design and implementation", "It happens once, after testing, when the team knows what actually works", "It begins at go-live and continues through the support period"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Allocation usually starts once the solution approach is chosen and carries on through design and implementation." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-38", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "A design option would tie up two senior subject matter experts for six months. Should this count as an expected cost?",
  options: ["Yes; effort and human resources are expected costs", "No; only money paid to vendors counts as cost", "No; staff time is already paid for in salaries", "Yes, but only if the experts are contractors"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Expected costs include timeline, effort, operating and maintenance costs, physical and information resources, purchase and implementation costs, and human resources." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-39", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "A design option increases value for the call center but slightly reduces it for the back office. Overall value for the enterprise goes up. What does BABOK say?",
  options: ["The option is rejected, since one group would lose value", "The back office must approve it before any other group can benefit from it", "Value can’t be compared across stakeholder groups", "An overall increase in value for the enterprise justifies proceeding"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Value to the enterprise is weighted more heavily than value to any single group. An overall positive increase for the enterprise justifies proceeding." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-40", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.15 Data Modelling",
  question: "A data model talks about “relationships” and “cardinality”. Which terms does a class model use for the same ideas?",
  options: ["Link and count", "Dependency and frequency", "Association and multiplicity", "Inheritance and range of values"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Class models say association where data models say relationship, and multiplicity where data models say cardinality." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-41", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.12 Data Dictionary",
  question: "A data dictionary entry reads “Customer Name = First Name + Middle Name + Surname”. What does this describe?",
  options: ["A business rule for validating customer names", "A composite data element built as a sequence of primitive elements", "An alias used for the name by different stakeholders", "A one-to-many relationship between the Customer entity and its Name attributes"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Composite data elements combine primitive elements. A plus sign shows a sequence, where one element is followed by another." },
{ id: "CC-RADD-42", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.23 Glossary",
  question: "When should a term be added to the glossary?",
  options: ["When it is unique to the domain, has several meanings, is used unusually, or may be misunderstood", "When the sponsor, as owner of the glossary, asks for it to be added", "Only when it appears in the requirements more than ten times, so the glossary stays short and focused", "When it is an acronym that stakeholders outside IT might not know"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A term is included when it is unique to a domain, has multiple definitions, is used outside its common meaning, or has a reasonable chance of being misunderstood." },

{ id: "CC-SE-26", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Which statement about solution performance measures is correct?",
  options: ["They are financial, so that they can be compared across different initiatives", "They can be numeric, qualitative or a mix, depending on the value being measured", "They must be set by the vendor, since the vendor knows the solution best", "They are collected after a full year of operation, to avoid seasonal bias"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Measures can be numeric, qualitative or both. Qualitative ones capture attitudes and perceptions of how well the solution meets the need." },
{ id: "CC-SE-27", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Who usually decides which measures will be used to evaluate solution performance?",
  options: ["The business analyst alone, since she defined the measures", "The development team, since it built the solution being measured", "The solution vendor, since it is responsible for performance", "The sponsor, though any stakeholder with decision authority may"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The sponsor often decides which measures to use, but anyone with decision-making authority can." },
{ id: "CC-SE-28", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "The team has performance data from two years ago and from last month. All else being equal, which is likely to be more representative?",
  options: ["Last month’s, since more recent measurements tend to be more representative", "The older data, since it covers a longer period and so has more detail", "Neither, since only data collected after the next release will count", "Both equally, since the age of performance data doesn’t matter"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Currency matters when collecting measures: newer data usually reflects current performance better than old data." },
{ id: "CC-SE-29", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "When assessing the impact of a problem found in a solution, what does the business analyst determine?",
  options: ["The cost of fixing it, who will pay for the work and how long the fix will take", "Who caused it and when, so the right team can be held accountable for putting it right", "Its severity, chance of recurring, effect on operations and the capacity to absorb it", "Whether the vendor is responsible under the support contract and what the penalty is"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Impact assessment looks at how severe the problem is, how likely it is to happen again, how it affects operations and how well the business can absorb it." },
{ id: "CC-SE-30", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "After assessing the impact of solution problems, what can the business analyst decide for each one?",
  options: ["Whether it must be resolved, can be mitigated, or can be accepted", "Whether it must be fixed before the next release, or after it goes live", "Which requirements attribute to update, such as status or stability", "Whether to retire the whole solution and replace it straight away"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "For each problem, the analyst decides whether it has to be fixed, can be softened through other means, or can be lived with." },
{ id: "CC-SE-31", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "Field staff in remote areas have poor connectivity and struggle to use a new cloud-based solution. Which stakeholder impact analysis consideration is this?",
  options: ["Functions", "Concerns", "Culture", "Locations"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholder impact analysis considers functions, locations and concerns. Disparate locations may affect use of the solution and value realization." },
{ id: "CC-SE-32", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "An informal alliance between two managers is blocking adoption of a new process. Which element of Assess Enterprise Limitations considers this?",
  options: ["Organizational structure changes", "Operational assessment", "Solution component dependencies", "Stakeholder impact analysis of the users"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Organizational structure changes assess formal and informal relationships, such as alliances, friendships or matrix reporting, that can enable or block a solution." },
{ id: "CC-SE-33", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "Which of these does an operational assessment consider?",
  options: ["The vendor’s market share and its plans for future releases", "The sponsor’s personal goals, how the solution supports them and how it affects the budget", "Policies and procedures, skills and training, and the tools supporting the solution", "The project’s original budget and how much of it was spent"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "An operational assessment looks at policies and procedures, enabling capabilities and processes, skills and training, HR practices, risk tolerance, and tools and technology." },
{ id: "CC-SE-34", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "An old system costs more to run every year. A replacement costs more upfront but much less to maintain. Which factor in a retirement decision is this?",
  options: ["Necessity of replacement", "Ongoing cost versus initial investment", "Opportunity cost of waiting", "Sunk cost of the old system to date"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Ongoing cost versus initial investment compares an existing solution’s rising costs with alternatives that cost more upfront but less to maintain." },
{ id: "CC-SE-35", exam: "CCBA", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A component still works today, but its technology is reaching the end of its life. Which retirement factor applies?",
  options: ["Sunk cost", "Opportunity cost", "Ongoing cost versus initial investment", "Necessity"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Necessity reflects that most components have a limited lifespan. After a point it becomes impractical or impossible to maintain them." },

/* --- Case study: Lakeside College --- */
{ id: "K-COL-1", exam: "CCBA", area: "EC", level: "mid", case: "CASE-COLLEGE", ref: "BABOK v3 10.18 Document Analysis · BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "Course prerequisites in the faculty handbooks don’t always match what the student records system enforces. What should the business analyst do FIRST?",
  options: ["Use the system’s rules, since that is what students actually experience when enrolling", "Compare the handbooks with the system rules and confirm the differences with faculty heads", "Use the handbooks, since the faculty heads wrote them and own the prerequisites", "Ask the system vendor which version is correct, since the system enforces the rules"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Document analysis can compare existing sources, and elicitation results are confirmed against source information. The faculty heads set the prerequisites, so they resolve the differences." },
{ id: "K-COL-2", exam: "CCBA", area: "RADD", level: "hard", case: "CASE-COLLEGE", ref: "BABOK v3 10.9 Business Rules Analysis",
  question: "How should the business analyst specify the course prerequisites for the new online enrolment?",
  options: ["As screen validations described inside the user interface design for each course", "As notes in the process model, next to the enrolment step where each prerequisite is checked", "As business rules in agreed business terms, kept separate from how the system enforces them", "As a list in each faculty handbook, updated by each faculty"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Business rules should be written in agreed business terms and kept separate from the technology that enforces them, so business people can read and manage them." },
{ id: "K-COL-3", exam: "CCBA", area: "SA", level: "mid", case: "CASE-COLLEGE", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Online enrolment must work for the August peak, ten months away, but not every feature can be ready. What should the business analyst help stakeholders do?",
  options: ["Plan releases so the core enrolment is live before the peak, and schedule other features later", "Delay go-live until every feature is ready and tested, even if that means missing August", "Launch every feature in one big-bang release just before the August peak", "Let IT decide what goes live and when, since they have four people to do the work"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Release planning considers deadlines, resources and the business’s ability to absorb change, and times implementation to cause minimal disruption." },
{ id: "K-COL-4", exam: "CCBA", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", case: "CASE-COLLEGE", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "The student union has asked to be involved, and most students will enrol from their phones. How should the business analyst plan stakeholder engagement?",
  options: ["Leave students out of the analysis, since they are not employees of the college", "Involve only the registrar, who sponsors the initiative and represents students", "Wait until go-live, then survey students to find out what they think of it", "Include student representatives as stakeholders and plan how to collaborate with them"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholder analysis covers everyone directly or indirectly affected. Students are key users, so the analyst plans collaboration that suits them." },
{ id: "K-COL-5", exam: "CCBA", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", case: "CASE-COLLEGE", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "Two months in, Finance asks for fee payment to be taken during online enrolment. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Add it to the scope straight away, since Finance is an important stakeholder", "Reject it outright, since the requirements were already agreed two months ago", "Assess its benefit, cost, impact, schedule and urgency, then use change control", "Treat it as a new business need and restart the current-state analysis for Finance"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Proposed changes are assessed through impact analysis, considering benefit, cost, impact, schedule and urgency, and decided through the agreed governance process." },

/* ===================== CBAP · batch 4 (BABOK-checked) ===================== */
{ id: "CP-BAPM-24", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "Midway through an initiative, a merger changes the business priorities and several planned deliverables are no longer needed. What should the business analysis approach already have described?",
  options: ["Nothing; the approach is fixed once it is approved", "How plans will be altered when changes are required", "A list of the deliverables, which are produced as originally planned", "The stakeholders who must be consulted on each deliverable"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Plans are usually revisited during an initiative, so the approach should explain how they will be adjusted when things change." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-25", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach",
  question: "After scope expands, the number of stakeholder groups grows from 4 to 25. How might the business analysis approach change?",
  options: ["It shouldn’t; the number of stakeholders doesn’t affect the approach", "It should switch to an adaptive approach automatically", "It may add process steps so the larger effort stays manageable", "It should reduce the number of reviews, so the larger group doesn’t slow the work down"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "With more stakeholders, the approach may need extra process steps to keep the business analysis work under control." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-26", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Three months in, a reorganization creates a new compliance unit that will be affected by the change. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Revisit the stakeholder analysis, since it is repeated as the work continues", "Wait for the compliance unit to ask to be involved, then add it to the list", "Leave the analysis as it is, since the sponsor approved it at the start", "Ask the sponsor whether the new unit should be kept out of scope until the next phase starts"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Stakeholder analysis is repeated as the work goes on, so new or changed stakeholders are picked up." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-27", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "A stakeholder group discovered during testing raises critical needs nobody had captured. What is the likely result?",
  options: ["No real impact, since finding missed needs is what testing exists to do", "A defect log entry for each need, fixed within the current test cycle", "A need to restart the initiative with a new plan, budget and set of requirements", "Rework of tasks in progress or done, higher costs and lower satisfaction"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Stakeholder needs uncovered late often require revising tasks in progress or completed, which can increase costs and decrease stakeholder satisfaction." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-28", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Minor wording fixes are clogging the weekly change board, delaying decisions on important changes. What should the governance approach define?",
  options: ["That every change still goes to the board, but small ones are batched and reviewed once a month", "That change control applies only to changes above a set size, cost or effort", "That nobody may change any requirement once it has been approved", "That developers decide which changes need the board and which don’t"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "When defining how changes are requested, the analyst can decide whether the process covers every change or only those above a certain size, cost or effort." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-29", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Which set of components might be included in a change request, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["Cost and time estimates, benefits, risks, priority and courses of action", "The requester’s job title, department, manager and contact details", "The test scripts the change will need and who will run each of them", "The original requirement and the requester’s reason, so the board can make a quick decision"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Possible change request components include cost and time estimates, benefits, risks, priority and the course or courses of action, often with alternatives." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-30", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "Change requests pile up because nobody knows who should analyze their impact or who can approve them. What has the governance plan missed?",
  options: ["The elicitation plan, including the techniques and participants for each session", "The requirements attributes, such as owner, status and priority", "Who will perform impact analysis and who will authorize changes", "The stakeholder communication schedule and the delivery methods"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The change control process includes determining who will perform impact analysis and who will authorize changes." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-31", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "Which requirements are usually represented in the GREATEST detail?",
  options: ["Those requested by the most senior stakeholders", "Those already approved and baselined", "Those that will be delivered last", "Those of significant importance or high risk"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Topics that matter most or carry the most risk usually get the most detail." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-32", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
  question: "A small internal tool has low risk, few stakeholders and no regulatory requirements. What traceability approach fits BEST?",
  options: ["Full formal traceability of every requirement to code and tests", "A level of tracing that adds value without excessive overhead", "No formal traceability, since the tool is small and low risk", "The same approach used on the bank’s regulatory program"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The traceability approach depends on complexity, views, risks, standards, regulation, and costs and benefits. It should add value without excessive overhead." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-33", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "Who assesses the performance of business analysis work?",
  options: ["The business analyst, through a self-assessment at each milestone", "Only the sponsor, at the end of the initiative, based on whether the outcome was achieved", "It varies: the people receiving the work, a line manager, or a Centre of Excellence", "An external auditor, to keep the assessment independent"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Performance may be judged by the recipients of the work, or by a personnel manager or Centre of Excellence. Organizations differ in who sets the targets." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-34", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "A business analyst’s satisfaction ratings fell after she raised risks that stakeholders didn’t want to hear. What should be kept in mind when interpreting this measure?",
  options: ["Qualitative measures are subjective and heavily influenced by attitudes and perceptions", "Ratings are objective, so her performance has clearly declined and needs action", "Satisfaction ratings should never be used to assess business analysis work", "Quantitative measures are what BABOK recommends, so this one should be ignored"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Qualitative measures are subjective, so stakeholders’ attitudes and perceptions can strongly colour them." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-35", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
  question: "A 200-page specification took three months to write, but the team only ever used its data model. Which performance measure does this MOST concern?",
  options: ["Timeliness", "Knowledge", "Organizational support", "Significance"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Significance considers the benefit obtained from work products and whether the cost, time and resources spent were justified by the value delivered." },
{ id: "CP-BAPM-36", exam: "CBAP", area: "BAPM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "What should personas be based on?",
  options: ["The business analyst’s own experience of what typical users are like and what they want", "Research about real users, using techniques such as interviews and surveys", "The sponsor’s description of the ideal customer the product should attract", "The stakeholders’ positions on the influence and interest grid"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A persona is a fictional archetype of a typical user, built from research and knowledge rather than opinion, often using interviews and surveys." },

{ id: "CP-EC-24", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Halfway through elicitation, interviews aren’t surfacing the tacit steps staff take to handle exceptions. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Run more interviews with the same questions until the steps come out", "Accept the gap, since the plan has been approved", "Adjust the techniques, for example by adding observation", "Proceed to confirm the elicitation results gathered so far"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Due to changing situations, the analyst may need to adjust the initial selection by including more appropriate techniques." },
{ id: "CP-EC-25", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.1 Prepare for Elicitation",
  question: "Two key stakeholders openly dislike each other, and joint sessions turn into arguments. What should the business analyst consider when preparing for elicitation?",
  options: ["The group dynamics, which may call for separate sessions or another technique", "Nothing in particular; professionals are expected to work together whatever they feel", "Their levels of influence, so the more senior one leads each session", "Asking the sponsor to attend and referee each session between them"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Understanding the scope of elicitation includes considering the stakeholders involved and their group dynamics, which affect technique choice." },
{ id: "CP-EC-26", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.2 Conduct Elicitation",
  question: "When should an elicitation activity be stopped?",
  options: ["When the scheduled time runs out, whatever has been covered", "When every stakeholder in the room has spoken and had their say at least once", "When the sponsor says so", "When there has been sufficient elicitation to meet its goal"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The analyst uses the activity’s goal, scope and expected outputs to judge when enough has been gathered, and then stops." },
{ id: "CP-EC-27", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results",
  question: "While drafting a process model after a round of interviews, the business analyst finds two accounts of a step that contradict each other. What does this show?",
  options: ["That modelling should always wait until all elicitation is finished", "A common way inconsistencies surface, to be confirmed and resolved with stakeholders", "A stakeholder conflict to escalate to the sponsor for a ruling", "That the interviews were badly run and should be repeated from the start with new questions"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Contradictions often surface when results are turned into models or specifications. They are then resolved with the stakeholders." },
{ id: "CP-EC-28", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.4 Communicate Business Analysis Information",
  question: "A stakeholder has strong concerns but won’t speak openly in group meetings. Which communication platform suits sharing the package with her?",
  options: ["Group collaboration", "E-mail only", "Individual collaboration", "A presentation to the steering committee"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Individual collaboration is used when a group setting isn’t feasible, most productive or likely to yield the best results." },
{ id: "CP-EC-29", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "Departments keep sending junior delegates who can’t make decisions, and approvals are slipping. What is this, in terms of managing stakeholder collaboration?",
  options: ["A normal situation that needs no action, since delegates can take notes", "A risk to monitor and address, since the right stakeholders aren’t participating", "An elicitation issue, best handled by switching to surveys instead of meetings", "A sign that the departments don’t support the initiative, which should go to the steering committee"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Monitoring engagement checks the right SMEs participate effectively and watches for risks such as diversion to other work and delayed approvals." },
{ id: "CP-EC-30", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "According to BABOK, what makes stakeholders more likely to support a change?",
  options: ["Working together in a way that lets information, ideas and innovation flow freely", "Formal sign-off on every document, so that their agreement is on record for audit", "Limiting communication to monthly status reports, to avoid overloading them", "Keeping them away from early drafts so they see finished work"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "People are more likely to back a change when the analyst works with them openly and keeps information and ideas flowing both ways." },
{ id: "CP-EC-31", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.45 Survey or Questionnaire",
  question: "A survey of 8,000 staff will use mostly open-ended questions. What does BABOK suggest about the sample?",
  options: ["Always survey everyone in the population, whatever the question type", "Survey managers instead, since they can answer for their teams", "Sample the volunteers who reply first, since they are most engaged", "A representative subset may be needed, chosen by statistical sampling"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "If the population is large and the questions are open-ended, a subset may be needed. Statistical sampling helps ensure results can be generalized." },
{ id: "CP-EC-32", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.21 Focus Groups",
  question: "Which is a limitation of focus groups?",
  options: ["They can’t be run online, so participants must travel", "What people say may not match how they actually behave", "They can’t reveal people’s attitudes, experiences or desires", "They need no moderator, since participants lead themselves"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Focus group limitations include a mismatch between what people say and do, trust concerns, homogeneous groups, and one vocal participant swaying results." },
{ id: "CP-EC-33", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.10 Collaborative Games",
  question: "Two teams keep talking past each other. One team discusses the topic while the other listens and records observations. Which collaborative game is this?",
  options: ["Product box", "Affinity map", "Fishbowl", "Brainstorming"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "In a fishbowl, one group talks about the topic while the other listens and notes what it observes. It helps bring hidden assumptions or points of view to the surface." },
{ id: "CP-EC-34", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "During a workshop the business analyst is facilitating, the sponsor asks her to choose between two options the group is debating. What should she do?",
  options: ["Choose the option she prefers, since she knows the analysis best", "Pick whichever option the sponsor seems to favour, since the sponsor is funding the initiative", "Stay neutral and help the group decide, since a facilitator isn’t the decision maker", "End the workshop and make the decision herself afterwards"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Effective facilitation makes it clear the facilitator is a third party, not a decision maker or topic owner, and remains neutral while helping the group." },

{ id: "CP-RLCM-23", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "A reporting dashboard will be much easier to build if the data warehouse requirement is implemented first. Which relationship is this?",
  options: ["Derive", "Depends, of the necessity type", "Validate", "Depends, of the effort type"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "An effort link means one requirement is easier to build when a related one is built too. A necessity link means it is pointless without the other." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-24", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "A team keeps a reusable password-reset requirement that names the identity system the company is about to retire. What should happen?",
  options: ["Rewrite it in general terms, since naming specific applications limits reuse", "Keep it exactly as it is, since it was approved and is already in the library", "Delete it, since a requirement tied to a retiring system can’t be reused", "Add the new identity system’s name alongside the old one"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Requirements written without ties to a particular tool or structure are more reusable. Specific references to applications or departments limit reuse." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-25", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.2 Maintain Requirements",
  question: "Why are requirements written at a high level of abstraction less likely to need revision during a change?",
  options: ["They have usually been through more review and approval cycles", "They have fewer stakeholders interested", "They are usually written by more senior people", "They have limited reference to specific solutions"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "High-level requirements with limited reference to specific solutions are more reusable and less subject to revision. More detail ties them to a particular solution." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-26", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Stakeholders still disagree on one requirement, and it needs more analysis. Using the stability basis, how might it be prioritized?",
  options: ["Higher, to force stakeholders to reach a decision quickly", "Lower, so less work is wasted if the requirement changes", "At the top, since it is the most debated item in the backlog", "It must be removed from the backlog until everyone agrees"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Stability is how likely a requirement is to change. An unstable requirement may be ranked lower so the team doesn’t waste effort or redo work later." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-27", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "There is a real chance the solution can’t be built at all. Using the risk basis, which requirement might be prioritized first?",
  options: ["The cheapest requirement", "The requirement the sponsor likes most", "The requirement that is hardest to implement", "The requirement with the fewest dependencies on the others"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "If the solution may not be technically feasible, the most difficult requirement may be prioritized first, so the team learns early whether it can be done." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-28", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Customers rank a feature highly, then lower it after hearing it will take four months. Which prioritization basis has come into play?",
  options: ["Cost", "Penalty", "Stability", "Time sensitivity"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Cost is the effort and resources needed. Customers may change a requirement’s priority after learning its cost, often as part of a cost-benefit analysis." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-29", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A requirement can’t be fulfilled unless another team delivers an interface first. Which prioritization basis does this concern?",
  options: ["Penalty", "Dependencies", "Time sensitivity", "Benefit"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Dependencies are relationships where one requirement can’t be fulfilled unless another is. They may be external, such as other teams’ decisions or funding." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-30", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "An inspector identifies a safety issue that a proposed change would fix. Which impact analysis factor does this MOST affect?",
  options: ["Benefit", "Cost", "Schedule", "Urgency"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Urgency reflects how important and pressing a change is, including drivers such as regulators or safety." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-31", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "Must every proposed requirements change go through formal approval?",
  options: ["Yes, every proposed change must be formally approved before anything else", "Changes raised by the sponsor do, since the sponsor owns the budget", "No; many are withdrawn or declined before formal approval is needed", "Changes in predictive initiatives do; adaptive ones skip approval"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK points out that many proposed changes are withdrawn or turned down before they ever reach formal approval." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-32", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.5 Approve Requirements",
  question: "Beyond agreeing with the requirements themselves, what may stakeholder approval confirm?",
  options: ["That stakeholders believe enough value will be created to justify the investment", "That the requirements are traced to the design elements that will satisfy them", "That the change strategy’s transition states are agreed and scheduled", "That the requirements are now baselined, so further changes are out of scope for this initiative"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Approval can also signal that stakeholders believe the solution will create enough value to be worth the investment." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-33", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.2 Backlog Management",
  question: "Who can add new items to a backlog?",
  options: ["The developers, since they do the work and know what fits in each sprint", "Anyone, at any time, with no rules about what is added or when", "Only the sponsor, since the sponsor funds the work in the backlog", "It varies: one person, a committee, or a delegate such as the analyst"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "One person may have authority to add items, or a committee by consensus, or it may be delegated to the analyst. Policies may dictate what is added and when." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-34", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.39 Roles and Permissions Matrix",
  question: "When identifying roles for a roles and permissions matrix, what should the business analyst keep in mind about job titles?",
  options: ["Each job title maps to exactly one role, so the organization chart can be used to list the roles", "People with the same title may have different roles, and different titles may share one", "Job titles should be ignored entirely, since only system access matters", "Managers have roles; other staff inherit their manager’s permissions"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Through review and discussion, the analyst considers that people with the same title may have different roles, and people with different titles the same role." },
{ id: "CP-RLCM-35", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.39 Roles and Permissions Matrix",
  question: "A team needs to show which IT system roles can create, read, update and delete each type of record. Which form of matrix fits?",
  options: ["A RACI matrix", "A traceability matrix", "A CRUD matrix", "A stakeholder matrix"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Initiative-level responsibilities may be shown in a RACI matrix. Specific IT system roles and permissions may be shown in a CRUD (create, read, update, delete) matrix." },

{ id: "CP-SA-24", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "The business analyst wants to find innovative solutions that combine existing capabilities into a new outcome. Which view of the enterprise does BABOK suggest?",
  options: ["A capability-centric view", "A process-centric view", "An organizational chart view", "A financial view"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A capability-centric view suits looking for innovative solutions that combine capabilities. A process-centric view suits improving the performance of current activities." },
{ id: "CP-SA-25", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "A new regulation forces a change that will cost more than it returns. Can the future state still be justified?",
  options: ["No; a change has to increase value for every stakeholder group to be approved", "No; a change needs a positive financial return to be approved", "Yes, if the payback period stays under the policy limit", "Yes; some changes are needed to keep operating, even if they reduce value"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK notes some changes, such as meeting new regulations, are needed to stay in business even if the future state captures less value overall." },
{ id: "CP-SA-26", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "What is the potential value of the future state, in BABOK terms?",
  options: ["The total revenue the solution will generate over its lifetime, before any costs", "The net benefit of the solution once its operating costs are taken off", "The purchase price of the solution, plus its implementation cost", "The number of business objectives the solution is expected to meet"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Potential value is the solution’s net benefit after operating costs. Hitting objectives isn’t enough on its own; the value has to justify the change." },
{ id: "CP-SA-27", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "A change offers high benefits but carries significant risk. Stakeholders want to proceed, but only if the likelihood and impact of the main risks come down. Which recommendation category fits?",
  options: ["Pursue the benefits regardless of the risk", "Do not pursue the benefits of the change until the risks have gone", "Pursue the benefits while investing in reducing risk", "Seek ways to increase benefits to outweigh the risk"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK’s recommendation categories include pursuing benefits while investing in reducing the likelihood or impact of risks, which matches this situation." },
{ id: "CP-SA-28", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "The change goes ahead with known risks. What should be in place?",
  options: ["Stakeholders identified to monitor the risks and their consequences", "A formal agreement that the risks are accepted, so they no longer need to be reviewed", "A risk tolerance rating agreed for the enterprise as a whole", "A gap analysis between current and future state"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "If a change goes ahead with known risks, someone should be named to watch them, and a plan may be prepared in case a risk happens." },
{ id: "CP-SA-29", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "The new system can be ready in six months, but the call center needs nine months to retrain staff and cover peak season. Which element of Define Change Strategy addresses this?",
  options: ["Gap analysis of capabilities", "Solution scope definition", "Enterprise readiness assessment", "Business case approval"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The readiness assessment considers the capacity to make, use and sustain the change, including operational readiness and the time from implementation to value." },
{ id: "CP-SA-30", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Why might a solution scope also list components that are out of scope?",
  options: ["To provide clarity about what the solution won’t include", "To record the assumptions and constraints that limit the solution", "To reduce the solution’s cost", "To list the gaps found in the current state"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Listing what is out of scope helps make the boundaries of the solution clear." },
{ id: "CP-SA-31", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases",
  question: "A draft business case defines success as “the new CRM is live in all regions.” What is wrong with this desired outcome?",
  options: ["Nothing; go-live in every region is the clearest and most measurable sign of success", "It is too detailed for a business case, which should stay high level", "It needs a risk rating, since rollouts across regions often slip", "Outcomes should be measurable and independent of the recommended solution"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Desired outcomes describe the state that should result if the need is met. They should be measurable and independent of the recommended solution." },
{ id: "CP-SA-32", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases",
  question: "In a business case, besides its financial value, how should each alternative be assessed?",
  options: ["On its purchase price and the cost of implementing it", "On how quickly it can start delivering to customers", "On feasibility, assumptions, risks and constraints, and non-financial benefits", "Only on how popular it is with the stakeholders who will use it, measured through a survey"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Each alternative is assessed on organizational and technical feasibility, its assumptions, risks and constraints, and financial and non-financial value." },
{ id: "CP-SA-33", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.6 Business Capability Analysis",
  question: "Both the retail and business divisions perform customer onboarding. How many times should “customer onboarding” appear on the capability map?",
  options: ["Once for each division that performs it", "Once, even though several units have it", "Once for each business process that includes it", "Once for each system that supports it"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A capability appears once on the map, however many business units have it." },
{ id: "CP-SA-34", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.6 Business Capability Analysis",
  question: "In business capability analysis, where do risks lie?",
  options: ["In the capability itself, regardless of how it performs", "In the technology that supports the capability, since that is what can fail", "In how well the capability performs, or fails to perform", "Only in the people who perform it"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A capability carries no risk by itself. The risk lies in how well it performs, or fails to: business, technology, organizational and market risk." },
{ id: "CP-SA-35", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis",
  question: "When is the five whys technique especially simple and effective?",
  options: ["When problems involve complex statistics", "When the cause has already been proven with data from several sources", "When there is no problem statement", "When the problem involves how people interact"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK sees the five whys as a very simple facilitation tool, and it works well where people’s interactions are part of the problem. It can be used alone or with a fishbone diagram." },

{ id: "CP-RADD-23", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "Hundreds of product codes all share the same set of attributes, such as price band, tax class and warehouse. Which model format fits BEST?",
  options: ["A matrix", "A narrative description", "A mind map", "A sequence diagram"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A matrix is used for requirements with a complex but uniform structure that can be broken into elements that apply to every entry." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-24", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "The business analyst writes an executive summary and a detailed specification of the same requirements. What must she take special care with?",
  options: ["Using exactly the same level of detail in both versions, so nothing is lost", "Keeping the meaning and intent of the requirements the same in both", "Getting each version signed off separately by its audience", "Leaving the assumptions and constraints out of the summary"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Different stakeholders may need the same need presented in different ways. The analyst has to make sure every version keeps the same meaning and intent." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-25", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.1 Specify and Model Requirements",
  question: "Why does BABOK suggest categorizing requirements as they are specified?",
  options: ["To decide which developer or team should build each requirement and in which release it goes", "To reduce the total number of requirements that need to be written, reviewed and approved", "To make sure they are understood, each set is complete, and tracing between types is right", "To meet the naming rules of the requirements management tool in use"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Grouping requirements by type helps make sure each one is understood, each set is complete, and the links between types are right." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-26", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "A requirement includes three paragraphs about the history of the department. Which quality characteristic does it MOST clearly lack?",
  options: ["Feasible", "Testable", "Prioritized", "Concise"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Concise means the requirement contains no extraneous and unnecessary content." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-27", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.2 Verify Requirements",
  question: "Which activity is part of verifying requirements?",
  options: ["Checking each model against related models for missing or inconsistent elements", "Confirming that the solution delivers its expected business value after go-live", "Negotiating the budget for each requirement with the sponsor", "Choosing between design options based on cost and benefit"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Verification includes checking standards, notation and completeness, and cross-checking models for elements that are missing in one or described differently in another." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-28", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "A team models only the business processes for a new claims solution. What risk does BABOK point to?",
  options: ["None; business processes cover everything that matters, since data and screens follow from them", "The model will be at the wrong level of abstraction for most stakeholders", "The solution would likely fail, since viewpoints such as data, user interaction and security are missed", "The process model can’t be traced to requirements or tests"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Each viewpoint is strong for some aspects and weak for others. The solution would likely not succeed if the analyst looked only at the business process viewpoint." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-29", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.4 Define Requirements Architecture",
  question: "What does a complete requirements architecture take into account, beyond having no missing or contradictory requirements?",
  options: ["The order in which the requirements were elicited and written down", "Dependencies between requirements that could stop the objectives being met", "The template used for each view, so that every part of the architecture follows one organizational standard", "The tool each stakeholder group uses to read and comment on requirements"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A complete architecture also considers dependencies between requirements that could stop the objectives being met." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-30", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "The team plans to modify the company’s existing in-house system to meet the new requirements. Which solution approach is this?",
  options: ["Purchase", "Combination of create and purchase", "Retire", "Create"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "In BABOK, the create approach includes modifying an existing solution. Purchase means selecting third-party offerings." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-31", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "A purchased product includes features the organization doesn’t need now but expects to use in future. Which improvement opportunity is this?",
  options: ["Identify additional capabilities", "Increase efficiencies", "Improve access to information", "Eliminate redundancy"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Identifying additional capabilities highlights features that may provide future value, even if they aren’t of immediate value to the organization." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-32", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "Can a change to staff job responsibilities be part of a design option?",
  options: ["No; design options describe the software and technical components of a solution", "Only if HR writes the requirement and approves the new job descriptions", "Yes; design elements can describe people and their job responsibilities", "In adaptive approaches, where teams organize their own work"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Design elements may describe policies and rules, processes, people and their responsibilities, decisions, software, and organizational structures." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-33", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Three design options have potential values of €2.0m (X), €1.5m (Y) and €1.2m (Z). If option X is chosen, what is its opportunity cost?",
  options: ["€0.5m", "€1.5m", "€2.7m", "€0.8m"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The opportunity cost of an option is the value of the best option you didn’t pick. Here that is option Y, at €1.5m." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-34", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "A regulation requires a particular check to be done by a person, not automated. Which factor in assessing design options is this?",
  options: ["Available resources", "Dependencies between requirements", "Corporate culture", "Constraints on the solution"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Constraints on the solution include regulatory requirements or business decisions that require certain requirements to be handled manually or automatically." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-35", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "How is the total expected benefit of a design option determined?",
  options: ["As the combined net benefit of every requirement the option addresses", "As the benefit of its single most valuable requirement", "As the vendor’s estimate of the savings the option will bring", "As the benefit of the requirements with the highest priority that the option covers"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Expected benefits can be calculated per requirement or set. The total is the net benefit of all requirements a design option addresses." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-36", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.11 Concept Modelling",
  question: "In a concept model, “an Engine is part of a Car” is which kind of connection?",
  options: ["A verb concept", "A categorization of concepts", "A classification", "A partitive connection"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Besides verb concepts, concept models use other kinds of connection: categorizations, classifications, whole-part (partitive) links and roles." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-37", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.24 Interface Analysis",
  question: "While identifying interfaces for a new payroll system, which kind of interface is MOST likely to be overlooked?",
  options: ["The HR system feed that supplies employee master data", "The bank file used to pay salaries each month, since it moves money between organizations", "Less obvious ones, such as those for auditing or regulatory reporting", "The login screen and password reset for employees"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK notes some interfaces are less obvious or less frequent, such as those for regulatory functions, auditing or employee training." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-38", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.36 Prototyping",
  question: "The team wants to check that a proposed design will technically work, without modelling its appearance or workflows. Which form of prototype fits?",
  options: ["Form study prototype", "Proof of principle or proof of concept", "Storyboard", "Evolutionary prototype of the full interface"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A proof of concept (or proof of principle) checks that a system’s design works, without modelling how it looks, what it is made of or its workflows." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-39", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.37 Reviews",
  question: "A draft is shared in one common copy, and several reviewers add written comments in turn. Which review technique is this?",
  options: ["Inspection", "Desk check", "Single issue review", "Pass around"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A pass around is informal: multiple reviewers give feedback on a common copy or one passed from person to person. A desk check uses one uninvolved reviewer." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-40", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.30 Non-Functional Requirements Analysis",
  question: "A customer app must support French and Arabic, local currencies and right-to-left text. Which non-functional category is this?",
  options: ["Localization", "Portability", "Extensibility", "Compatibility"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Localization covers local languages, laws, currencies, cultures and spelling conventions." },
{ id: "CP-RADD-41", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.47 Use Cases and Scenarios",
  question: "In a use case, a post-condition must be true when?",
  options: ["Only when the main success flow completes without errors", "Before the use case starts, as the condition that must be true for the actor to trigger it", "For all possible flows, primary and alternative, when the use case completes", "Only when an error occurs and the use case ends early"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A post-condition has to hold once the use case ends, whichever flow was followed. Success and failure can have separate post-conditions." },

{ id: "CP-SE-25", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "There is no hard data on how much time a new knowledge base saves. How can the business analyst estimate the value realized?",
  options: ["Assume no value was realized, since time saved can’t be counted", "Ask the vendor to provide a value figure based on what its other customers have reported saving", "Facilitate discussion so knowledgeable stakeholders agree an estimate based on facts and assumptions", "Wait until enough hard data has been collected, then calculate the value from the measured time savings"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "With qualitative measures, analysts can facilitate discussions in which knowledgeable stakeholders reach a consensus on value based on facts and reasonable assumptions." },
{ id: "CP-SE-26", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A regulator requires a bank to report fraud detection rates for a new system. How does this affect the solution performance measures?",
  options: ["It doesn’t; only internally chosen measures count for evaluation", "Measures may be imposed by third parties such as regulators, so it must be included", "It should be handled as a non-functional requirement for the vendor rather than a performance measure", "It replaces all other measures, since the regulator’s view comes first"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Outside parties such as vendors, government bodies and regulators can shape or impose performance measures." },
{ id: "CP-SE-27", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "A team measures call handling time for a new tool, but the company’s strategic scorecard tracks first-contact resolution. What should the business analyst check?",
  options: ["That the solution’s measures align with the higher-level measures already in place", "Nothing; each team is free to measure whatever it finds most useful", "That the strategic scorecard is changed to match the team’s own measure of call handling time", "That call handling time is dropped and replaced with customer surveys"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "When validating measures, the analyst checks that they line up with any higher-level measures already in use." },
{ id: "CP-SE-28", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "The measures collected aren’t enough for stakeholders to judge whether a solution delivers value. What does BABOK suggest?",
  options: ["Recommend actions to increase solution value straight away", "Retire the solution, since its value can’t be shown", "Investigate the root cause of the solution’s poor performance", "Collect more measures, or treat the lack of them as a solution risk"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "If the measures can’t show whether the solution delivers value, the analyst either gathers more or treats the gap itself as a risk." },
{ id: "CP-SE-29", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "Performance data for a new ordering system was collected only in the week before a public holiday. What is the main concern?",
  options: ["The time period may produce anomalies and skewed trends", "The data is too recent to be useful for any decisions", "There is no concern, since a single week is always enough", "The measures may not be aligned with the business goals"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The period the data covers matters: too short or unusual a window can create misleading trends. Enough data over enough time gives a fair picture." },
{ id: "CP-SE-30", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "Two analysts calculate the same KPI from the same data and get different results. Which aspect of analyzing performance measures is in question?",
  options: ["Trends", "Performance variances", "Accuracy", "Desired value"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Measures are only trustworthy if the same data gives the same result each time, whoever calculates it." },
{ id: "CP-SE-31", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "A new web front end is fast, but each page waits on a slow legacy back end, so the whole solution feels slow. What should the business analyst examine?",
  options: ["The enterprise culture around the new tool", "Internal solution component dependencies", "The vendor’s market position and roadmap", "Staff training records for the front end"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Internal dependencies can limit the performance of the whole solution to that of its least effective component." },
{ id: "CP-SE-32", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "A solution works, but the savings promised in the change strategy haven’t appeared. Is this a solution problem to investigate?",
  options: ["No; it is a benefits realization issue for the finance team, not a solution problem", "No; it is an enterprise limitation, since the solution itself works", "Only if users complain", "Yes; failing to realize a projected benefit can indicate a problem"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Problems may show as an inability to meet a goal, objective or requirement, or a failure to realize a benefit projected in the change strategy." },
{ id: "CP-SE-33", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "A new claims system is live, but adjusters’ bonuses still reward closing claims the old way. Which assessment would MOST likely surface this?",
  options: ["An operational assessment, which considers HR practices", "A gap analysis of the new system’s features and screens", "Interface analysis between the claims and payroll systems", "A review of the stakeholder list and RACI matrix"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "An operational assessment considers policies and procedures, enabling capabilities, skills and training, human resources practices, risk tolerance and tools." },
{ id: "CP-SE-34", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "Staff say they don’t know why a new system was introduced and see no benefit in it. Which assessment helps, and what may follow?",
  options: ["A financial analysis, followed by cuts to the system’s budget", "An enterprise culture assessment, then communication to build understanding", "A vendor assessment, followed by renegotiating the vendor’s contract", "A data model review, followed by new reports for the staff"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Cultural assessment checks whether stakeholders understand why the solution exists and see it as beneficial, and what communication is needed." },
{ id: "CP-SE-35", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Sales and service both need a customer contact history, and each is planning its own tool. Which recommendation fits?",
  options: ["Avoid waste", "Retire the solution", "Eliminate redundancy", "Do nothing"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Eliminating redundancy means serving several groups’ shared need with one solution, which lowers the cost." },
{ id: "CP-SE-36", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A solution has a small performance gap. Closing it would take a large effort, and the value gained would be low. Which recommendation is MOST likely?",
  options: ["Retire the solution", "Organizational change", "Identify additional capabilities", "Do nothing"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Doing nothing is usually the advice when the gain is small compared with the effort, or when changing is riskier than leaving things alone." },

/* --- Case study: Varrowby Energy --- */
{ id: "K-ENG-1", exam: "CBAP", area: "SE", level: "hard", case: "CASE-ENERGY", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Extending the current billing system looks cheaper than buying the cloud product. What must the recommendation also weigh?",
  options: ["That support ends in 18 months, so keeping it becomes impractical, and ongoing versus upfront costs", "The lower upfront cost of extending the system, since budgets are tight and the CFO wants quick savings", "Only the money already spent on the current system, so that two decades of investment aren’t wasted", "Which option the CFO prefers, since the CFO is the sponsor and holds the budget"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Necessity (components have a limited lifespan) and ongoing cost versus initial investment both affect retirement decisions. Money already spent is a sunk cost." },
{ id: "K-ENG-2", exam: "CBAP", area: "RADD", level: "hard", case: "CASE-ENERGY", ref: "BABOK v3 10.9 Business Rules Analysis · BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "The cloud product would need heavy customization to support all 300 tariffs. What should the business analyst do?",
  options: ["Require the vendor to support all 300 tariffs exactly as they work today, at no extra cost", "Rule out the cloud product immediately, since it can’t meet every requirement", "Challenge whether every tariff is still needed, including those with few customers", "Ask IT to choose the option based on their technical preference and skills"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Old business rules are worth questioning to check they still serve the business goals, particularly when a new kind of solution comes along." },
{ id: "K-ENG-3", exam: "CBAP", area: "RLCM", level: "mid", case: "CASE-ENERGY", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Stakeholders want every current feature moved to the new platform in the first release. Which bases should MOST drive what goes first?",
  options: ["The features that the CFO and the finance team use most often in their monthly reporting cycle", "Stability, so the features least likely to change go first", "Whatever is cheapest and quickest to migrate first", "Regulatory compliance and time sensitivity, given the regulator and support deadline"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Regulatory or policy compliance and time sensitivity are prioritization bases. Here the regulator and the support deadline make them decisive." },
{ id: "K-ENG-4", exam: "CBAP", area: "SA", level: "hard", case: "CASE-ENERGY", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "The plan is to finish migrating customers in December. The head of customer operations is worried about call volumes. What should the business analyst help stakeholders do?",
  options: ["Keep the December date, since the plan has already been approved by the CFO", "Plan the transition to avoid the winter peak and assess operational readiness", "Keep December, and add temporary call center staff for the busiest migration weeks", "Leave the timing to the vendor, since it has done many migrations before"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Release planning considers the business’s ability to absorb change and aims for minimal disruption. Readiness covers the operational capacity to make the change." },
{ id: "K-ENG-5", exam: "CBAP", area: "EC", level: "mid", case: "CASE-ENERGY", ref: "BABOK v3 10.14 Data Mining",
  question: "The business analyst needs to know how many customers are on each legacy tariff and how often billing errors occur per tariff. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Data mining of billing and complaint records", "A focus group with a sample of customers", "A brainstorming session with the CFO and finance", "Interviews with the vendor"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Data mining analyzes large amounts of data to find patterns and relationships, such as customer counts and error rates by tariff." },
/* ===================== ECBA · batch 5, part 1 (BABOK / BA Standard checked) ===================== */
{ id: "E-UBA-42", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.2 What is Business Analysis?",
  question: "At which levels can business analysis initiatives take place, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["Strategic, tactical or operational", "The strategic level, where the major decisions are made", "Only inside IT projects that build software", "The operational level, once systems are live"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK says business analysis takes place on many kinds of initiative, whether strategic, tactical or operational." },
{ id: "E-UBA-43", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.2 What is Business Analysis?",
  question: "Which set of activities can business analysis be used for?",
  options: ["Writing up the requirements once a solution has already been chosen and funded by the sponsor", "Testing a solution before it goes live to customers", "Understanding the current state, defining the future state and planning how to get there", "Managing the budget and schedule of a project"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Business analysis helps understand where things are now, define where they should be, and work out how to get there." },
{ id: "E-UBA-44", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.2 What is Business Analysis?",
  question: "Which list names the five perspectives described in the BABOK Guide?",
  options: ["Strategy, tactics, operations, delivery, support and continuous improvement", "Planning, elicitation, analysis, design and evaluation, one for each stage of a project", "Business, stakeholder, solution and transition requirements, plus the design perspective", "Agile, business intelligence, IT, business architecture and business process management"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK covers five perspectives: agile, business intelligence, IT, business architecture and business process management, and says these don’t cover every context." },
{ id: "E-UBA-45", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.3 Who is a Business Analyst?",
  question: "According to BABOK, what is the business analyst responsible for eliciting?",
  options: ["Exactly what each stakeholder asks for, recorded word for word so nothing is lost", "The actual needs of stakeholders, not only their expressed desires", "Only the requirements the sponsor has already approved", "Only the technical constraints that developers raise"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The business analyst elicits the actual needs of stakeholders, which often means investigating and clarifying their expressed desires to find underlying issues and causes." },
{ id: "E-UBA-46", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.3 Who is a Business Analyst?",
  question: "From which sources does a business analyst discover and analyze information?",
  options: ["Interviews with senior managers, who see the big picture", "Existing documentation and system manuals, since they are the most reliable source of information", "Tools, processes, documentation and stakeholders across the enterprise", "The requirements tool used by the team"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Business analysts discover, synthesize and analyze information from a variety of sources, including tools, processes, documentation and stakeholders." },
{ id: "E-UBA-47", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.3 Who is a Business Analyst?",
  question: "Which of these is one of the activities BABOK lists for business analysts?",
  options: ["Devising strategies", "Approving the annual budget", "Writing production code for the solution", "Running staff performance reviews"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK describes business analysts as people who work out problems and goals, analyze needs and solutions, devise strategies, drive change and help stakeholders work together." },
{ id: "E-UBA-48", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "How are the tasks within a BABOK knowledge area related to each other?",
  options: ["They are done in the order they are listed in the guide", "They are logically, but not sequentially, related", "They map to the phases of a project life cycle", "Each task is finished before the next one starts"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A knowledge area groups tasks that belong together by subject. They aren’t steps in a fixed order." },
{ id: "E-UBA-49", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "BABOK says an initiative may start with any task. Which two tasks does it name as likely starting points?",
  options: ["Plan Business Analysis Approach and Approve Requirements", "Trace Requirements and Verify Requirements", "Define Design Options and Prioritize Requirements", "Analyze Current State and Measure Solution Performance"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Tasks can happen in any order once their inputs exist. BABOK names Analyze Current State and Measure Solution Performance as likely places to start." },
{ id: "E-UBA-50", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which section of a BABOK task lists the resources needed to transform the inputs into outputs?",
  options: ["Elements", "Purpose and description", "Guidelines and tools", "Stakeholders and outputs"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Guidelines and tools lists the resources required to transform inputs into outputs. A guideline explains why or how; a tool is used to undertake the task." },
{ id: "E-UBA-51", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Must an input be complete and final before a BABOK task can begin?",
  options: ["No; it only needs to be complete enough for the work to begin", "Yes; every input must be complete, reviewed and formally signed off first", "Yes, but only for tasks in Strategy Analysis", "No; inputs can be skipped in adaptive approaches"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK says there is no assumption that an input is complete or final. It only needs to be sufficiently complete for successive work to begin." },
{ id: "E-UBA-52", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "How does BABOK mark an input that is produced outside business analysis work?",
  options: ["With an asterisk next to its name", "It adds “(external)” after the input’s name", "It is shown in a different colour", "It isn’t shown, since only business analysis inputs are listed"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Inputs that come from outside business analysis work are tagged “(external)” in the list of inputs." },
{ id: "E-UBA-53", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Are the elements described in a BABOK task mandatory?",
  options: ["Yes; each element is performed each time the task is carried out", "Yes, but only in predictive approaches", "The first element is required; the rest are optional", "No; their use may depend on the business analysis approach"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Elements describe key concepts needed to perform a task. They are not mandatory, and their usage may depend on the business analysis approach." },
{ id: "E-UBA-54", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "A task’s stakeholder list includes a tester and a regulator. Must these roles be filled on every initiative?",
  options: ["Yes, or the task can’t be performed and must wait until both are found", "No; BABOK doesn’t mandate that these roles be filled", "The first role listed must be filled; the others are optional", "Yes, but one person can’t hold both roles"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The stakeholders section is a generic list of roles likely to take part in or be affected by a task. BABOK doesn’t mandate that they be filled for any given initiative." },
{ id: "E-UBA-55", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "How many knowledge areas does the BABOK Guide define?",
  options: ["Four", "Five", "Six", "Nine"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The six are Strategy Analysis; Planning and Monitoring; Elicitation and Collaboration; Requirements Analysis and Design Definition; Requirements Life Cycle Management; and Solution Evaluation." },
{ id: "E-UBA-56", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "What structure does BABOK use to describe each underlying competency?",
  options: ["Purpose, definition and effectiveness measures", "Inputs, elements and outputs", "Purpose, description, inputs, elements and outputs", "Strengths and limitations only"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Underlying competencies are described by purpose, definition and effectiveness measures. Tasks use a different, longer format." },
{ id: "E-UBA-57", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which statement about underlying competencies is correct?",
  options: ["They are unique to business analysis and used in no other profession", "They only apply to senior business analysts with many years in the role", "They replace the need for techniques once a business analyst is experienced", "They aren’t unique to business analysis, but tasks often depend on them"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK points out that other professions share these competencies too, yet how well an analyst performs the tasks and techniques often rests on them." },
{ id: "E-UBA-58", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "A business analyst’s employer also asks her to run the office’s fire drills. How does BABOK view activities like this?",
  options: ["As part of the Solution Evaluation knowledge area, since it concerns safety", "As a business analysis technique she should document", "As activities she may do, but not part of the business analysis profession", "As an underlying competency"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Organizations can give business analysts other work as well, but BABOK doesn’t count that work as part of the business analysis profession." },
{ id: "E-UBA-59", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "In BABOK terms, what is a plan?",
  options: ["A proposal for doing something: its events, sequence, schedule, resources and stakeholders", "A signed contract between the sponsor and the vendor that fixes the scope, price and dates", "The list of requirements approved for the next release, ranked by priority and assigned to the team", "A diagram of the future state of the organization, approved by the sponsor and the board"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A plan is a proposal for doing something. It sets out the events and how they depend on each other, their order and timing, the expected results, the resources and the people involved." },
{ id: "E-UBA-60", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "Two companies in a joint venture share processes and tools to pursue common goals. For business analysis, can they be treated as one enterprise?",
  options: ["No; an enterprise is a single legal entity, so each company is analyzed separately", "No; an enterprise is limited to one organizational unit", "Yes, but only if one of the companies owns the other outright", "Yes; the enterprise’s boundaries can be drawn around the change"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "For business analysis, the enterprise can be drawn around the change, so it isn’t limited to one legal entity or organization and can include several." },
{ id: "E-UBA-61", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "How does an organization differ from a project team, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["An organization usually operates continuously; a project team may be disbanded when done", "A project team is larger than the organization it works for, since it spans departments", "An organization has no clearly defined boundary, while a project team has a clear, fixed membership", "There is no difference between them in BABOK"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Organizations usually have clear boundaries and keep running, while a project team may be broken up once its work is done." },
{ id: "E-UBA-62", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "Why does BABOK talk about “business analysis information” rather than just “requirements”?",
  options: ["Because requirements are no longer used in business analysis and have been replaced", "So all inputs and outputs, such as elicitation results and change strategy, are covered", "Because information is easier to store, search and version in requirements tools", "Because the word “requirements” is reserved for the developers’ technical specifications"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Expanding the object from requirements to information ensures all inputs and outputs of business analysis, such as elicitation results and change strategy, fall under the tasks." },
{ id: "E-UBA-63", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "Another role completes the detailed designs for a solution. What does the business analyst often do?",
  options: ["Hand over and step back, since detailed design is the other role’s responsibility", "Update the requirements so they match the details in the final designs", "Approve the designs on behalf of the sponsor", "Review the final designs to make sure they align with the requirements"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Whoever finishes the designs, the business analyst often reviews them at the end to check they still match the requirements." },
{ id: "E-UBA-64", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "According to BABOK, what question should a business analyst keep asking, whatever the stakeholder brings?",
  options: ["“Why?” — why is this requirement or design needed to deliver value?", "“How much will it cost?” — before anything else is discussed", "“When is it due?” — so the schedule can be set and agreed with everyone", "“Who needs it?” — so the stakeholder list stays complete"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK says the importance of the business analyst’s role lies in continuously asking “why?”, for example why a requirement or design is needed to provide value." },
{ id: "E-UBA-65", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "The need is to reduce the time it takes to pick and pack a customer order. Which of these is a design for that need?",
  options: ["The goal of cutting picking time", "A process model of the new picking steps", "The complaint that orders ship late", "The warehouse manager’s budget for new staff"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK’s example pairs the requirement “reduce time to pick and pack an order” with a process model as the design." },
{ id: "E-UBA-66", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "How does The Business Analysis Standard say desired outcomes are often expressed?",
  options: ["As one large project with a single go-live date for the whole organization", "As a list of software features to build, ordered by the sponsor", "As a set of future states, each reached through individual pieces of work", "As the sponsor’s personal objectives for the year"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The Standard notes desired outcomes are often expressed as a set of future states, each achieved through individual pieces of work." },

{ id: "E-MIND-33", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "Which of these is one of the shared values listed in The Business Analysis Standard?",
  options: ["Respect", "Seniority", "Adaptability", "Accountability for outcomes"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The Standard lists nine shared values: respect, courage, collaboration, ethics, curiosity, continuous learning, improvement, customer focus and value maximization." },
{ id: "E-MIND-34", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst takes a course in data analysis because her initiatives increasingly depend on data. Which shared value does this MOST reflect?",
  options: ["Courage", "Ethics", "Continuous learning", "Value maximization"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Continuous learning is one of the Standard’s shared values: keeping knowledge and skills growing as the work changes." },
{ id: "E-MIND-35", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "In a workshop, a business analyst gives a junior clerk’s input the same careful attention as the director’s. Which shared value does this MOST reflect?",
  options: ["Value maximization", "Respect", "Improvement", "Curiosity"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Respect is one of the shared values in the Standard. Treating every stakeholder’s contribution seriously reflects it." },
{ id: "E-MIND-36", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst uses real usage data from the first release to decide what the team should change next. Which part of the mindset does this show?",
  options: ["Influencing strategy at board and executive level", "Leveraging technology to automate the work", "Defining the full scope before the next release", "Driving feedback and learning based on evidence"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The Standard says a flexible, adaptable mindset supports driving feedback and learning based on evidence, among other things." },
{ id: "E-MIND-37", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "How does The Business Analysis Standard describe business analysis?",
  options: ["As a human-centered, creative process aimed at desired outcomes", "As a strictly technical discipline focused on software", "As a documentation activity done at the start of a project", "As a technical discipline that is mainly about writing documents"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The Standard describes business analysis as a human-centered, creative process aimed at achieving desired outcomes and driving change." },
{ id: "E-MIND-38", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "Before anyone discusses features, a business analyst asks why the change is needed and how it fits the wider business context. Which principle is this?",
  options: ["Avoid waste", "Get real using examples", "See the whole", "Understand what is doable"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "See the whole means analyzing needs in the big-picture context and identifying why a change is necessary." },
{ id: "E-MIND-39", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "Besides building shared understanding of a need, what can concrete examples inform, according to the principle “Get real using examples”?",
  options: ["The project budget and staffing plan", "Acceptance criteria, design and testing", "The organization chart", "The vendor contract and the delivery schedule"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Concrete examples build shared understanding and can also inform acceptance criteria, design and testing." },
{ id: "E-MIND-40", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.4 Foundational Competencies",
  question: "Which list matches the five foundational competencies in The Business Analysis Standard?",
  options: ["Coding, testing, designing, deploying and supporting the solution in the months after go-live", "Planning, budgeting, staffing, reporting and closing projects on time and on budget", "Listening, writing, speaking, reading, presenting and negotiating with stakeholders", "Adaptability, facilitation, leadership and influencing, problem solving, systems thinking"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The Standard’s five foundational competencies are adaptability, facilitation, leadership and influencing, problem solving, and systems thinking." },
{ id: "E-MIND-41", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.3 Business Knowledge",
  question: "A business analyst new to a logistics company quickly applies what she knows about finance, HR and supply chains from earlier jobs. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Business acumen", "Industry knowledge", "Methodology knowledge", "Teaching"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business acumen means using experience from other settings to understand business needs, because organizations share many common practices." },
{ id: "E-MIND-42", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.3 Business Knowledge",
  question: "A business analyst follows market trends, customer segments, regulations and competitors in the insurance sector. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Organization knowledge", "Solution knowledge", "Industry knowledge", "Business acumen"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Industry knowledge covers current trends, market forces and drivers, customer segments, suppliers, practices, regulations and how a company is positioned in its industry." },
{ id: "E-MIND-43", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.3 Business Knowledge",
  question: "A business analyst knows who really influences decisions, which informal channels people use, and the internal politics. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Industry knowledge", "Organization knowledge", "Methodology knowledge", "Trustworthiness"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Organization knowledge includes the management structure, how the enterprise makes money, key stakeholders, formal and informal channels and internal politics." },
{ id: "E-MIND-44", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.3 Business Knowledge",
  question: "Before planning her work, a business analyst learns how the organization usually runs change: in big steps or small increments, and how much risk it accepts. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Business acumen", "Solution knowledge", "Adaptability to change", "Methodology knowledge"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Methodology knowledge covers the methodologies an organization uses, which determine timing, approach, roles and the accepted risk level." },
{ id: "E-MIND-45", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.4 Communication Skills",
  question: "A business analyst carefully chooses her words and tone when explaining a sensitive change to a team. Which communication skill is this?",
  options: ["Written communication", "Listening", "Verbal communication", "Teaching"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Verbal communication is about the spoken word: the speaker’s choice of words and tone of voice." },
{ id: "E-MIND-46", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "Two managers disagree on a policy. The business analyst mediates so they recognize their different views, resolve them and keep a good working relationship. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Negotiation and conflict resolution", "Teamwork and collaboration", "Leadership and influencing across departments", "Decision making"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "This competency is about mediating so people can settle their differences while keeping, and even strengthening, their working relationship." },
{ id: "E-MIND-47", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 9.6 Tools and Technology",
  question: "Which category of tools do word processors, spreadsheets and presentation software belong to?",
  options: ["Business analysis tools and technology", "Communication tools and technology", "Data modelling and diagramming software tools", "Office productivity tools and technology"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Office productivity tools and technology help business analysts document, track, organize and communicate information." },
{ id: "E-MIND-48", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.6 Tools and Technology",
  question: "A team uses a specialized tool to model processes, link requirements to each other and track their status. Which category does it belong to?",
  options: ["Office productivity tools and technology", "Business analysis tools and technology", "Communication tools and technology", "Personal productivity apps"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Business analysis tools provide specialized capabilities such as modelling, diagramming, analyzing and mapping requirements, identifying relationships and tracking artifacts." },
{ id: "E-MIND-49", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 9.6 Tools and Technology",
  question: "A dispersed team relies on video calls, chat and shared online whiteboards to work together. Which category of tools is this?",
  options: ["Communication tools and technology", "Business analysis tools and technology", "Office productivity tools and technology", "Data analytics tools"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Communication tools support conversational and collaborative interactions and let business analysts work with virtual and co-located teams." },

{ id: "E-IMPL-19", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.2 Approaches to Your Work",
  question: "An initiative uses fixed, upfront planning for its regulatory reporting and short iterations for its customer app. Which approach is this?",
  options: ["Predictive", "Adaptive", "Hybrid", "Waterfall only"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The Standard describes a hybrid approach as combining predictive and adaptive methods, across initiatives or within parts of the same initiative." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-20", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.2 Approaches to Your Work",
  question: "When does The Business Analysis Standard suggest an adaptive approach is MOST suitable?",
  options: ["When the work is well defined, straightforward and heavily regulated", "When the work is well understood and can be done in a fixed sequence that is known upfront", "When the problem is simple and has been solved many times before by the team", "For complex needs, poorly defined problems, high uncertainty or emerging needs"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Adaptive suits complex business needs, poorly defined problems, high uncertainty or needs that emerge during construction. Predictive suits well-defined, regulated or sequential work." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-21", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.2 Approaches to Your Work",
  question: "A business analyst prefers adaptive work, but the organization requires a predictive approach for her initiative. What does The Business Analysis Standard suggest?",
  options: ["Learn to create value with whichever approach is used, since some may be imposed", "Propose a hybrid approach, since the Standard favours mixing methods", "Treat the approach as a constraint and limit her work to writing requirements", "Use adaptive practices quietly, then report the results in the predictive format required"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The Standard advises learning to create value with any approach, noting that some approaches may be imposed on the team." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-22", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.6 Managing the Delivery of Business Analysis",
  question: "What is the difference between a business analysis Centre of Excellence and a Community of Practice?",
  options: ["They are the same thing under two names", "A Centre of Excellence is outsourced to a provider; a Community of Practice is always in-house", "A CoE is a unit dedicated to raising quality; a CoP is where practitioners share experience", "A Community of Practice sets mandatory standards; a Centre of Excellence is an informal network"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The Standard describes a Centre of Excellence as a specialized unit improving business analysis quality, and a Community of Practice as a space to share experience and practices." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-23", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.6 Managing the Delivery of Business Analysis",
  question: "A company has no business analysts of its own and contracts a specialist firm to provide business analysis. Which delivery model is this?",
  options: ["Community of Practice", "Business analysis as a service", "Dispersed across business units", "Centre of Excellence"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Business analysis as a service means outsourcing business analysis functions to specialized providers." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.4 Understanding Requirements and Designs · BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "Besides showing where requirements came from, what can a traceability model reveal?",
  options: ["Whether each requirement is testable, unambiguous and written at the right level", "Which developer wrote which line of code, when it was committed and who reviewed it", "The effort and cost needed to build each solution component", "Missing requirements or designs, and components no requirement supports"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A traceability model tracks links in both directions and reveals missing requirements or designs and solution components not supported by any requirement." },

/* ===================== ECBA · batch 5, part 2 (BACCM domains) ===================== */
{ id: "E-CHG-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "According to the BACCM, how are the improvements brought by a change made?",
  options: ["Mainly through training, so that staff adopt new ways of working on their own", "Deliberately, and controlled through business analysis activities", "By the IT department, once it has budget approval", "Only when a regulator requires them"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Change aims to improve how the enterprise performs, and those improvements are planned and steered through business analysis." },
{ id: "E-CHG-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "A new online channel launches, and the old phone-ordering system suddenly delivers much less value. What does this show about changes and needs?",
  options: ["That the change was poorly scoped, since its effect on other solutions was missed", "The phone system now has a solution limitation that must be fixed within it", "A change can create new needs by eroding or enhancing existing solutions’ value", "Needs only come from customer complaints"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "In the BACCM, needs can lead to changes, and changes can create new needs by raising or lowering the value of solutions already in place." },
{ id: "E-CHG-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Stakeholders can’t decide between three possible change strategies. What may the business analyst develop to support the decision?",
  options: ["A business case for each of the candidate strategies", "A detailed test plan for each strategy", "A new organization chart and job descriptions for each option", "A RACI matrix for each candidate strategy"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK notes a business case can be written for each candidate change strategy to help decision makers choose." },
{ id: "E-CHG-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Where can ideas for alternative change strategies come from?",
  options: ["The sponsor’s original idea, refined with the help of the solution vendor’s experts", "Only from the solution vendor, which knows what its product can do", "The most senior manager involved, since they set the direction", "Brainstorming, SMEs, past changes, other markets’ strategies and competitors"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Ideas can come from brainstorming and subject matter experts, past ideas and changes, strategies used in other markets and what competitors do." },
{ id: "E-CHG-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "When deciding which requirements go into each release, which factor should the business analyst help stakeholders consider?",
  options: ["The order in which the requirements were elicited and written down", "Budget, deadlines, resources, training schedules and how much change the business can absorb", "The seniority of the stakeholder who raised each requirement, so senior requests go first", "The vendor’s preferred release dates and maintenance windows"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Release planning considers budget, deadlines, resource constraints, training schedules and the business’s ability to absorb change, aiming for minimal disruption." },
{ id: "E-CHG-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes · BABOK v3 5.1 Trace Requirements",
  question: "A stakeholder wants to change an approved requirement. What helps MOST in seeing which other requirements and components the change will affect?",
  options: ["Traceability between the requirements and solution components", "The project budget and spending to date", "The stakeholder list and their contact details", "The meeting minutes from the project kick-off and the original business case"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Traceability helps with impact analysis: when one requirement changes, you can follow its links to see which other requirements and components are affected." },
{ id: "E-CHG-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "How does assessing proposed changes usually differ in an adaptive approach compared with a predictive one?",
  options: ["It is more formal, since changes happen more often", "It is skipped completely, since adaptive teams welcome every change", "It may need less formality, as iterative delivery reduces each change’s impact", "The sponsor assesses changes, without involving the team"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Predictive approaches may call for more formal assessment. Adaptive approaches may require less, since iterative and incremental delivery reduces the impact of each change." },
{ id: "E-CHG-31", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A first change has been delivered, but it doesn’t fully address the need. What does BABOK say about this?",
  options: ["The change has failed and should be reversed before anything else is done", "The need was wrong and should be removed from the change strategy", "The shortfall is accepted as residual risk and the need is marked as met", "Each element of change may not fully address the need, so several changes may be needed"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK notes one change may not fully meet the need, so several changes may be required." },
{ id: "E-CHG-32", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "A change would bring large net benefits, but the investment needed is more than the company can afford. What may happen?",
  options: ["The company must proceed anyway, since large benefits always justify the investment", "It may decide not to go ahead, and invest in something else instead", "It is recorded as a solution limitation and reassessed after the next release", "The change is split into two projects funded in separate years"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes that if the investment is unbearable, the enterprise may pass on the opportunity even if the net benefits are high, and invest elsewhere." },
{ id: "E-CHG-33", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Which of these can be compared in a gap analysis between the current and future states?",
  options: ["Processes, staff competencies, training, locations, data and technology", "The software systems in use and the licenses they need", "Only the organization chart and reporting lines", "The budget for the change and how it will be spent"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Capabilities compared in gap analysis include processes, staff competencies, functions, training, organizational structures, facilities, locations, systems, data and infrastructure." },
{ id: "E-CHG-34", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "When estimating the cost of a proposed change, what should be included besides the effort to make it?",
  options: ["Nothing else; the direct effort is the cost, and rework is part of normal development", "Only the cost of new hardware and software licenses it needs", "The cost of rework, and opportunity cost such as features that must be deferred", "Only the sponsor’s time spent reviewing and approving the change request"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Cost in impact analysis includes the cost to make the change, associated rework and opportunity costs, such as other features sacrificed or deferred." },

{ id: "E-NEED-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "In the BACCM, how can a need lead to a change?",
  options: ["It can’t; changes only come from management", "By motivating stakeholders to act", "By being written into a contract", "After a solution has been bought"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Needs can trigger change by prompting stakeholders to act." },
{ id: "E-NEED-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "From whose perspective should a business need be expressed?",
  options: ["The enterprise’s, not any single stakeholder’s", "The sponsor’s, since they fund the work", "The business analyst’s, since they write it", "The end users’, since they will use the solution every day"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A business need is framed around what the enterprise as a whole needs, not what one stakeholder wants." },
{ id: "E-NEED-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Why is defining the business need often called the most critical step in business analysis?",
  options: ["Because it is the longest step and takes the most effort to complete", "Because it is the only step the sponsor sees and signs off personally", "Because once it is defined, no further elicitation is needed", "Because it determines which solutions, stakeholders and approaches will be considered"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "How the need is framed decides which solutions get considered, which stakeholders are consulted and which approaches are evaluated." },
{ id: "E-NEED-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "A manager says: “We need a chatbot to cut call volumes.” What should the business analyst do with the assumptions in this statement?",
  options: ["Accept them, since the manager knows the business and owns the budget", "Start building the chatbot now to save time, and refine it later", "Question the assumptions, so the right problem is solved and more options considered", "Run a vendor assessment of chatbot products to confirm the best fit"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Needs are often expressed with a presumed solution. The analyst questions buried assumptions and constraints so the correct problem is solved and more alternatives are considered." },
{ id: "E-NEED-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "When analyzing a business need, which factor does BABOK suggest considering?",
  options: ["The problem’s adverse impacts, expected benefits, how fast it could be solved, the cost of doing nothing and its source", "Only the price of the most likely solution, whether the budget covers it, and who will pay for it", "Only who first raised the problem and how senior that person is", "Only how many people have complained about it in the last month"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK suggests looking at the harm the problem causes, the benefits a fix would bring, how fast it could be solved, the cost of doing nothing, and the root of the problem." },
{ id: "E-NEED-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis",
  question: "On a fishbone diagram, which categories of potential causes does BABOK give as examples?",
  options: ["Budget, schedule and scope", "People, processes, tools and policies", "Strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats", "Must, should, could and won’t"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The fishbone diagram’s diagonal lines represent categories of potential causes, which may include people, processes, tools and policies." },
{ id: "E-NEED-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "How do business goals differ from business objectives?",
  options: ["Goals are short-term and specific; objectives are long-term and vague", "They mean the same thing; BABOK uses the two terms interchangeably for any target", "Objectives are set by the sponsor; goals by staff", "Goals are longer-term and qualitative; objectives are specific and measurable"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Goals are longer-term, ongoing, qualitative statements. As they are analyzed, they are turned into more specific objectives linked to measures." },
{ id: "E-NEED-31", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "In the SMART test for objectives, what does the R stand for?",
  options: ["Realistic, meaning cheap enough to achieve within the current budget", "Reviewed, meaning approved by the sponsor and the steering group before work starts", "Relevant, meaning it fits the enterprise’s vision, mission and goals", "Repeatable, meaning the objective can be achieved again in later years"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK’s SMART test: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant (fits the vision, mission and goals) and time-bounded." },
{ id: "E-NEED-32", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "While studying the current state, the business analyst finds that the reported problem is caused by a deeper issue nobody had mentioned. What does BABOK say about this?",
  options: ["The deeper cause often becomes an additional business need", "It should be ignored, to keep the scope of the analysis small", "It means the original need was invalid and should be dropped", "It must be passed to another team"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Looking at the current state often turns up root causes that sit beneath the original problem or opportunity, and these can become business needs in their own right." },
{ id: "E-NEED-33", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.14 Data Mining",
  question: "Customer data shows a spike in cancellations, and the team wants to understand why it happens. Which type of data mining fits?",
  options: ["Descriptive", "Predictive", "Prescriptive", "Diagnostic"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Descriptive mining shows patterns, diagnostic mining shows why a pattern exists, and predictive mining shows how likely something is in future." },
{ id: "E-NEED-34", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.45 Survey or Questionnaire",
  question: "The business analyst wants to know how widespread a delivery problem is among 2,000 customers, quickly and cheaply. Which technique fits BEST?",
  options: ["Interviews with every customer", "A survey or questionnaire", "Observation of each delivery", "A prototype of the delivery app"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A survey or questionnaire gathers structured answers from many people in a fairly short time, and close-ended answers are easier to analyze." },

{ id: "E-SOL-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "How does a solution satisfy a need, according to the BACCM?",
  options: ["By resolving a problem or helping stakeholders take advantage of an opportunity", "By being delivered on time, on budget and to the agreed scope, as signed off by the sponsor", "By using the newest technology available on the market", "By meeting every stakeholder request"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A solution meets a need by fixing a problem stakeholders face or by helping them seize an opportunity." },
{ id: "E-SOL-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "In BABOK, the solutions an enterprise uses are also called organizational capabilities. Which of these can they be?",
  options: ["Software systems", "Only physical equipment", "Processes, tools or information", "Staff and their skills"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK sees an enterprise as a system made up of organizations and the solutions they rely on (their organizational capabilities), whether those are processes, tools or information." },
{ id: "E-SOL-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "The business analyst assigns each requirement to a solution component and a release. What is this called?",
  options: ["Requirements tracing", "Requirements verification", "Requirements prioritization", "Requirements allocation"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Allocation is deciding which solution component and which release each requirement goes into, to best meet the objectives." },
{ id: "E-SOL-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "A design option gives branch staff direct access to customer history, so they no longer need to call a specialist for each query. Which improvement opportunity is this?",
  options: ["Identify additional capabilities", "Improve access to information", "Increase efficiencies through outsourcing", "Retire the solution"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Improving access to information gives more information to staff who deal with customers, reducing the need for specialists." },
{ id: "E-SOL-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "Why might automating a manual approval step also improve consistency?",
  options: ["Automation can stop different people performing the same function in different ways", "Automation removes the approval step completely, so there is no longer anything left that can vary", "Automation logs each approval, which gives a full audit trail of who approved what", "It doesn’t; automation affects speed, not consistency"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Under increasing efficiencies, BABOK notes automation may increase consistency of behaviour, reducing the chance that stakeholders perform the same function differently." },
{ id: "E-SOL-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Which of these is an expected cost of a design option?",
  options: ["The price paid to buy it from the vendor", "Money already spent on earlier analysis of the need", "The training budget for the staff who will use it", "Purchase, implementation, operating and maintenance costs over time"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Expected costs include acquiring the solution, negative effects on stakeholders and maintaining it over time: timeline, effort, operating, maintenance, purchase and resource costs." },
{ id: "E-SOL-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Where can a business analyst commonly find measures for evaluating a solution’s performance?",
  options: ["In the business analysis performance metrics agreed during planning", "In the original project schedule and budget", "In the business goals, objectives and business processes", "Only from the developers’ test results before go-live"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Business goals, objectives and processes are the usual places to find measures of solution performance." },
{ id: "E-SOL-31", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 8.2 Analyze Performance Measures",
  question: "A new transaction system is fast and reliable, but it is contributing less value than expected. What does this show?",
  options: ["That the performance figures must be wrong, since high performance means high value", "A solution can perform well yet still deliver lower value than expected", "That the value must have been measured wrongly and should be recalculated", "The solution should be retired immediately"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes a solution might be high performing but contribute lower value than expected, while a low-performing but valuable one can be enhanced." },
{ id: "E-SOL-32", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.3 Assess Solution Limitations",
  question: "After assessing the impact of problems found in a solution, what can the business analyst decide about each one?",
  options: ["Whether it must be resolved, can be mitigated, or can be accepted", "Whether it is a solution or an enterprise limitation", "Only whether to cancel the solution and start again", "Which stakeholder must approve a change request for it"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "For each problem, the analyst decides whether it must be fixed, can be eased in other ways, or can be accepted." },
{ id: "E-SOL-33", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.4 Understanding Requirements and Designs · BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "The requirement is that information must be available in English and French. Which of these is a design for it?",
  options: ["The business goal of entering the Canadian market", "The complaint from French-speaking customers", "The decision to hire a translator", "A prototype showing the English and French text"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The Standard’s example pairs the requirement “information in English and French” with a prototype showing the English and French text as its design." },
{ id: "E-SOL-34", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.36 Prototyping",
  question: "A team sketches screens on a whiteboard to clarify requirements, knowing the sketches won’t become part of the final system. Which prototyping approach is this?",
  options: ["Evolutionary prototyping", "Functional prototyping", "Throw-away prototyping", "Proof of concept"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Throw-away prototypes use simple tools to uncover and clarify requirements and don’t become workable code. Evolutionary prototypes grow into a working solution." },

{ id: "E-STK-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "A claims process owner with deep knowledge of the claims rules joins the initiative. Which stakeholder role does she MOST likely fill?",
  options: ["Implementation subject matter expert", "Domain subject matter expert", "Operational support", "Regulator"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A domain SME knows a lot about a topic that matters to the need or the solution. Process owners, managers and legal staff are typical examples." },
{ id: "E-STK-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Which stakeholders directly interact with the solution?",
  options: ["End users", "Sponsors", "Regulators", "Suppliers"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "End users work with the solution directly, for example anyone taking part in the business process or using the product." },
{ id: "E-STK-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Which stakeholder role balances scope, budget, schedule, resources, quality and risk to deliver the solution?",
  options: ["Sponsor", "Business analyst", "Tester", "Project manager"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The project manager runs the work of delivering a solution that meets the need, trading off scope, budget, schedule, resources, quality and risk." },
{ id: "E-STK-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "A trainer will teach staff how to use the new system. Which generic stakeholder role does BABOK place trainers under?",
  options: ["End user of the system", "Domain subject matter expert on the new system", "Implementation subject matter expert", "Customer of the enterprise"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK lists trainer among common implementation SME roles, along with solution architect, developer, database administrator and change manager." },
{ id: "E-STK-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "The help desk and the release manager will look after the new app day to day once it is live. Which stakeholder role is this?",
  options: ["Implementation subject matter expert", "Project manager", "Sponsor", "Operational support"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Operational support looks after the day-to-day running and upkeep. Typical roles include operations and product analysts, the help desk and the release manager." },
{ id: "E-STK-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "An internal audit team sets standards that the new finance system must meet. Which stakeholder role does it fill?",
  options: ["Regulator", "Tester", "Domain subject matter expert", "Sponsor"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Regulators define and enforce standards, including audit standards. BABOK lists government, regulatory bodies and auditor as alternate roles." },
{ id: "E-STK-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "On a small initiative, the department manager is both the sponsor and a domain subject matter expert. Is this allowed in BABOK terms?",
  options: ["No; each person may hold only one role", "Yes; one person can hold more than one role", "Yes, once a RACI matrix records both of the roles", "In adaptive approaches, where roles are more flexible"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes each category can contain several roles, and one person can hold more than one of them." },
{ id: "E-STK-31", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Where can a business analyst start when identifying internal stakeholders?",
  options: ["The list of IT systems that the change will touch", "The list of the company’s suppliers and their contracts, plus the vendor’s client list", "The organizational chart and business processes, plus the sponsor’s suggestions", "The previous year’s budget and spending by department"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The organization chart and business processes are a good starting point for internal stakeholders, and the sponsor can point out others." },
{ id: "E-STK-32", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "On a stakeholder matrix, a group has low influence and the change has little impact on them. How should they be engaged?",
  options: ["Work closely with them on every decision", "Ask them to approve each requirement that affects them", "Leave them out entirely", "Keep them informed using general communications"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Low influence and low impact stakeholders can be kept informed with general communications. More engagement may turn them into goodwill ambassadors." },
{ id: "E-STK-33", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "In BABOK’s onion diagram, which layer holds end users, the help desk and others whose day-to-day work will change?",
  options: ["Affected organizational unit", "Solution delivery", "Organization or enterprise", "Affected external stakeholders"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The onion diagram shows the project team in solution delivery, end users and help desk in the affected organizational unit, then the wider organization, then external stakeholders." },
{ id: "E-STK-34", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "Why should a stakeholder list be as complete as possible?",
  options: ["Because a complete list removes the need to revisit stakeholders later on", "Because an overlooked stakeholder may mean requirements are missed later", "Because every stakeholder must approve every requirement", "Because each stakeholder needs a separate interview"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "An exhaustive list helps make sure no important stakeholder or group is overlooked, which would risk missing requirements later." },

{ id: "E-VAL-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "Which of these is an example of tangible value?",
  options: ["A better company reputation with customers", "Higher employee morale", "€200,000 a year saved in license fees", "A stronger sense of team spirit across departments"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Tangible value is directly measurable and often has a significant monetary component. Reputation and morale are intangible." },
{ id: "E-VAL-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "Which statement about value in the BACCM is correct?",
  options: ["Value exists once a solution has been live for a year", "Value can only increase as a result of change, never decrease", "Value is measured in money, so it belongs in the business case", "Value can be potential or realized, and can decrease through losses, risks and costs"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Value can be potential or actually realized, as returns, gains or improvements. It can also go down, through losses, risks and costs." },
{ id: "E-VAL-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "A design option increases value for one team but reduces it slightly for another. Value for the enterprise as a whole goes up. How is this usually judged?",
  options: ["The overall increase for the enterprise justifies proceeding", "The team that loses value can veto the option, since its view comes first", "Each team’s value counts equally", "Value can’t be compared across teams"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Value to the enterprise is almost always weighted more heavily than value to any single group. An overall increase for the enterprise justifies proceeding." },
{ id: "E-VAL-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Why should estimates of a change’s value take uncertainty into account?",
  options: ["Because costs are hard to know in advance, while benefits are fixed", "Because potential value is uncertain, and events could raise or lower the actual value", "Because a discount rate turns uncertain benefits into guaranteed present value", "Because benefits that are uncertain shouldn’t be counted in the estimate"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Potential value is uncertain value. Events or conditions could increase or decrease the actual value, so estimates must consider the degree of uncertainty." },
{ id: "E-VAL-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "What does net present value (NPV) represent?",
  options: ["The total of all future benefits added together, without any discounting or adjustment", "The time it takes to recover the investment", "The benefits valued in today’s money, minus the original investment", "The interest rate at which the investment exactly breaks even"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK describes NPV as the benefits’ value in today’s money less the original investment. The higher it is, the better the investment." },
{ id: "E-VAL-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "Why does financial analysis express future benefits as present value?",
  options: ["Because future benefits are less certain, so they are reduced to make the case look safer", "Because the payback period can’t be worked out without it", "Because discounting removes the risk from the estimate", "So benefits arriving at different times and rates can be compared in today’s terms"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Solutions may realize benefits at different rates and times. Benefits are calculated in present-day value, reduced by a discount rate, so they can be compared objectively." },
{ id: "E-VAL-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Customer satisfaction ratings collected after go-live are which kind of solution performance measure?",
  options: ["Qualitative", "Quantitative only", "Financial", "Technical"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Qualitative measures are subjective and include attitudes and perceptions. Quantitative measures are numerical amounts, quantities or rates." },
{ id: "E-VAL-31", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A team ranks requirements by the advantage each one gives to stakeholders. Which basis for prioritization is this?",
  options: ["Penalty", "Benefit", "Stability", "Dependencies"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Benefit is the advantage that accrues to stakeholders from implementing a requirement. Penalty is the consequence of not implementing it." },
{ id: "E-VAL-32", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Failing to implement a requirement would lead to a regulator’s fine. Which basis for prioritization does this MOST concern?",
  options: ["Benefit", "Stability", "Penalty", "Cost"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Penalty is the consequence of not implementing a requirement, such as fines or lost customers." },
{ id: "E-VAL-33", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.3 Balanced Scorecard",
  question: "Which four dimensions make up a balanced scorecard?",
  options: ["Strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats", "People, process, technology and data, weighted equally", "Plan, do, check and act, repeated for each business unit", "Financial, customer, business process, and learning and growth"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A balanced scorecard tracks performance from four angles: learning and growth, business processes, customers and finances." },
{ id: "E-VAL-34", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "What is a key performance indicator (KPI)?",
  options: ["An indicator that measures progress toward a strategic goal or objective", "Any number that appears in a monthly management report to the board", "A requirement that has been given the highest priority", "A target set by the vendor in the service contract, with penalties for missing it"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A KPI measures progress toward a strategic goal or objective. An indicator identifies a measurement; a metric is a quantified level of an indicator." },

{ id: "E-CTX-24", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "Can things like the season or the weather be part of the context of a change?",
  options: ["No; context means the IT systems and infrastructure that the change will affect", "Yes; context covers anything in the environment that bears on the change", "Yes, if they are written into the business case and approved", "No; context covers the organization chart and its reporting lines"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK’s examples of context range from culture, competitors and demographics to languages, seasons, technology and the weather." },
{ id: "E-CTX-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Staff distrust any new system because of two failed rollouts in the past. Which current-state element does this concern?",
  options: ["Technology and infrastructure", "Internal assets", "External influencers on the enterprise", "Organizational structure and culture"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Culture is the shared beliefs, values and norms of the people in an organization, and it drives how they act." },
{ id: "E-CTX-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "New AI tools are changing how quickly competitors can process claims. Which external influencer is this?",
  options: ["Technology", "Industry structure", "Macroeconomic factors", "Suppliers"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The technology influencer is the boost to productivity that new and upcoming technology can bring, along with any barriers to it." },
{ id: "E-CTX-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Only one company can supply a part the business depends on, so it can dictate prices. Which external influencer is this?",
  options: ["Competitors", "Customers", "Suppliers", "Political and regulatory environment"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The variety and diversity of suppliers, and the power they have over their customers, can be an external influence." },
{ id: "E-CTX-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "In a SWOT analysis, a new government tax incentive for energy-saving equipment that the company could use is a(n):",
  options: ["Strength", "Weakness", "Opportunity", "Threat to the business"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Opportunities are external factors the organization could take advantage of. Strengths and weaknesses are internal." },
{ id: "E-CTX-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "In a SWOT analysis, outdated internal systems that slow down order processing are a(n):",
  options: ["Weakness", "Threat", "Opportunity", "Strength"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A weakness is something the group does badly or doesn’t do at all, which is internal. Threats come from outside." },
{ id: "E-CTX-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.32 Organizational Modelling",
  question: "Employees report both to a functional manager and to a product manager. Which type of organizational model is this?",
  options: ["Functionally-oriented", "Market-oriented", "Flat", "Matrix"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "In the matrix model, there are separate managers for each functional area and each product, service or customer group, and employees report to both." },
{ id: "E-CTX-31", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.32 Organizational Modelling",
  question: "Which problem are functionally-oriented organizations prone to, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["Duplicate work and inconsistent practices in every market or region they serve", "Poor communication and coordination across functions, often called silos", "Too many layers of managers for each employee in a department", "Inconsistent ways of working within each function and between its teams"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Organizing by function can keep costs down and cut duplication, but it tends to create communication and coordination gaps between functions, known as silos." },
{ id: "E-CTX-32", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 11.3 The Information Technology Perspective",
  question: "A vendor announces it will stop supporting software the company relies on. Which kind of IT change does BABOK say this typically triggers?",
  options: ["Creating a new organizational capability", "Repairing a broken system", "Maintaining an existing IT system", "Retiring the business process"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "In the IT perspective, maintaining an existing system includes changes forced by technology, such as a vendor ending support." },
{ id: "E-CTX-33", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "What are core capabilities or processes, in BABOK terms?",
  options: ["Functions every firm in the industry must perform", "The processes that are performed most often each day by the most people", "The capabilities with the highest operating cost", "The essential functions that differentiate the enterprise from others"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Core capabilities or processes are the essential functions that set the enterprise apart from others, tracked through performance indicators." },
{ id: "E-CTX-34", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "When is a process-centric view of the enterprise MOST useful?",
  options: ["When looking for ways to make current activities perform better", "When designing a brand-new product line from scratch for a new market", "When combining existing capabilities into an innovative new product or outcome", "When choosing which software vendor to buy a new solution from"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A process-centric view suits improving current activities, since processes run end to end. A capability-centric view suits combining capabilities into new outcomes." },
/* ===================== ECBA · batch 6, part 1 (BABOK / BA Standard checked) ===================== */
{ id: "E-UBA-67", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "At what level can business requirements apply?",
  options: ["The whole enterprise, one business area or a single initiative", "A single system feature, described for the development team", "One stakeholder group’s day-to-day tasks and the screens they use", "The test cases that prove a solution works as designed"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business requirements set out the goals, objectives and outcomes that explain why a change was started. They can cover the whole enterprise, one business area or a single initiative." },
{ id: "E-UBA-68", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "What makes transition requirements different from the other types of requirements?",
  options: ["They are written by the vendor rather than the business analyst", "They are temporary: needed to move to the future state, but not once the change is complete", "They describe the long-term goals that explain why the change was started", "They describe how the solution behaves and what information it manages day to day"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Transition requirements cover what the solution needs only while moving from the current to the future state. Once the move is complete, they no longer apply." },
{ id: "E-UBA-69", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.3 Requirements Classification Schema",
  question: "What is another name BABOK gives for non-functional requirements?",
  options: ["Transition requirements", "Business rules", "Quality of service requirements", "Stakeholder requirements"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Non-functional, or quality of service, requirements describe the qualities a solution must have or the conditions it must keep working under, rather than what it does." },
{ id: "E-UBA-70", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "When applying the BACCM, which question helps a business analyst check the context concept?",
  options: ["What sorts of change are we making?", "Who are the stakeholders involved in the change?", "What do the stakeholders see as valuable?", "In what circumstances do we and the solution operate?"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK offers a guiding question for each core concept. For context, it asks about the circumstances that the team and the solution are in." },
{ id: "E-UBA-71", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "When applying the BACCM, which question helps a business analyst check the value concept?",
  options: ["What do the stakeholders see as valuable?", "Which needs are we trying to meet?", "Which solutions are we building or changing?", "What sorts of change are we making to the enterprise?"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "For the value concept, BABOK suggests asking what stakeholders consider to be of value. The other questions belong to need, solution and change." },
{ id: "E-UBA-72", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which topics does the Business Analysis Key Concepts chapter of the BABOK Guide cover?",
  options: ["The 50 techniques and their strengths and limitations", "The BACCM, key terms, requirements classification, stakeholders, and requirements and designs", "The six knowledge areas and the 30 tasks they contain", "The five perspectives and how each one affects the tasks"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The Key Concepts chapter covers the BACCM, key terms, the requirements classification schema, stakeholders, and requirements and designs." },
{ id: "E-UBA-73", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which sections does BABOK use to describe each technique?",
  options: ["Purpose, definition and effectiveness measures", "Inputs, elements, guidelines and tools, and outputs", "Purpose, description, elements and usage considerations", "Change scope, business analysis scope and impact on knowledge areas"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Techniques are described by purpose, description, elements and usage considerations. Usage considerations explain when a technique is more or less effective." },
{ id: "E-UBA-74", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Is the list of techniques in the BABOK Guide complete?",
  options: ["Yes; business analysts should use only the techniques listed", "Yes, once the perspectives’ techniques are included", "No; it lists the techniques for agile work, while predictive techniques are elsewhere", "No; business analysts are encouraged to adapt techniques or create new ones"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK says its techniques aren’t a complete list, and encourages business analysts to adapt them or invent new ones." },
{ id: "E-UBA-75", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Can a single initiative use more than one BABOK perspective, such as agile and business intelligence?",
  options: ["Yes; perspectives are not mutually exclusive", "No; each initiative must choose exactly one", "Yes, if one is designated as the primary perspective", "No; perspectives describe roles, not initiatives"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Perspectives can overlap, so one initiative can use several of them." },
{ id: "E-UBA-76", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which knowledge area produces outputs that serve as key inputs and guidelines for tasks in the other knowledge areas?",
  options: ["Solution Evaluation", "Business Analysis Planning and Monitoring", "Requirements Life Cycle Management", "Elicitation and Collaboration"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Planning and Monitoring organizes the work of business analysts and stakeholders, and what it produces guides and feeds the tasks in the other knowledge areas." },
{ id: "E-UBA-77", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which knowledge area covers the work from the initial concept and exploration of a need through to a recommended solution?",
  options: ["Strategy Analysis, with support from Solution Evaluation", "Solution Evaluation", "Requirements Analysis and Design Definition", "Business Analysis Planning and Monitoring"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "RADD takes a need from first concept and exploration, step by step, to a recommended solution." },
{ id: "E-UBA-78", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Besides identifying the business need, what does the Strategy Analysis knowledge area do with the strategy for the change?",
  options: ["Defines the detailed design options for the solution", "Hands it to the project manager to schedule", "Gets it signed off by every stakeholder group", "Aligns it with higher- and lower-level strategies"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Strategy Analysis finds a strategic or tactical need, helps the enterprise respond to it, and makes sure the change strategy fits with strategies above and below it." },
{ id: "E-UBA-79", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Besides assessing the value a solution delivers, what does the Solution Evaluation knowledge area recommend?",
  options: ["Removing whatever stops the solution from delivering its full value", "Defining the change strategy for moving to the future state", "Improving how the business analysis work itself is performed", "Writing new requirements for the next solution to be built"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Solution Evaluation looks at how a solution in use is performing and what value it gives, then recommends removing whatever blocks it from delivering all of that value." },
{ id: "E-UBA-80", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.5 Outcomes and Value Creation",
  question: "A business analyst gathers data from several reports, explains what it means for the business and shares the findings with the leadership team. Which contribution of business analysis is this?",
  options: ["Turning business needs into requirements and designs", "Discovering, interpreting and sharing business information", "Supporting the building of high-value solutions for the business", "Helping teams weigh trade-offs and prioritize value"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The Standard lists discovering, interpreting and sharing business information among the ways business analysis contributes to value." },
{ id: "E-UBA-81", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.1 Defining Business Analysis",
  question: "Do the fundamentals of business analysis change when someone works as a product owner or a data analyst?",
  options: ["Yes; each role has its own separate set of fundamentals", "Yes; the fundamentals were written for traditional projects and need adapting for these roles", "No; they stay consistent across roles, industries and methodologies", "No, but they apply to product owners only in large organizations"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The Standard says the fundamentals stay consistent across organizational structures, industries and methodologies, including adaptive work, data analytics, cybersecurity and product ownership." },
{ id: "E-UBA-82", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 2.2 A Model for Effective Analysis (BACCM)",
  question: "A team analyzes the need, solution and value carefully, but ignores the context. What does The Business Analysis Standard say about this?",
  options: ["It is fine, since context matters mainly for strategy work", "It is fine, as long as the stakeholders have signed off", "Context can be added later, once the solution is built", "The concepts work as one system, so ignoring one weakens the whole model"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The Standard describes the six core concepts as an interconnected system. Ignoring any concept, or the connections between them, weakens the model." },
{ id: "E-UBA-83", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.5 Requirements and Designs",
  question: "How much design work is a business analyst responsible for?",
  options: ["It varies with the perspective the business analyst is working in", "None; design is the job of architects and developers", "All of the detailed design, on every initiative", "The screen layouts, but not processes or data"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Business analysts are responsible for design at some level, and the level of responsibility varies with the perspective they are working in." },
{ id: "E-UBA-84", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.3 Who is a Business Analyst?",
  question: "According to BABOK, what role does the business analyst play once solutions are being designed and delivered?",
  options: ["Approving the budget for each release", "Keeping the solutions being built in line with stakeholders’ needs", "Writing and running the system test scripts for the solution", "Managing the delivery team’s day-to-day tasks and timesheets"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK says business analysts help keep the solutions being designed and delivered in line with what stakeholders need." },
{ id: "E-UBA-85", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.2 What is Business Analysis?",
  question: "Do the five perspectives in the BABOK Guide cover every context in which business analysis is practised?",
  options: ["Yes; any initiative fits one of the five", "Yes, once they are combined with the knowledge areas", "No; they don’t cover every context or business analysis discipline", "No; they cover IT initiatives and none of the others"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK notes its perspectives don’t cover every context or every discipline in business analysis." },
{ id: "E-UBA-86", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Can a BABOK task be performed informally?",
  options: ["No; tasks are formal activities with signed deliverables", "Only in adaptive approaches, where documentation is lighter", "Only when the sponsor agrees to skip the formal version", "Yes; a task may be performed formally or informally"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK describes a task as a distinct piece of work that can be done formally or informally." },
{ id: "E-UBA-87", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Does the definition of a BABOK task depend on the type of initiative?",
  options: ["No; task definitions apply universally, whatever the initiative", "Yes; tasks are defined differently for IT and business initiatives", "Yes; each industry has its own version of the tasks", "No, but tasks apply to projects and not to ongoing operations"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK says each task is defined the same way whatever kind of initiative it is used on." },
{ id: "E-UBA-88", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "What does the form of a task’s output depend on?",
  options: ["The template set out in the BABOK Guide for that output", "The initiative type, the organization’s standards and the business analyst’s judgment", "The tool that the development team happens to use", "The sponsor’s preferred document format and length"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "An output’s form depends on the initiative type, the standards the organization has adopted, and the analyst’s judgment of how best to meet stakeholders’ information needs." },
{ id: "E-UBA-89", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "Which section of a BABOK task explains why a business analyst performs it and the value it creates?",
  options: ["Description", "Elements", "Purpose", "Outputs"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The Purpose section gives a short description of the reason for performing the task and the value created by performing it." },
{ id: "E-UBA-90", exam: "ECBA", area: "UBA", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 1.1 Purpose of the BABOK Guide",
  question: "What is the primary purpose of the BABOK Guide?",
  options: ["To give step-by-step instructions for running a project from start to finish", "To certify business analysts against a defined set of skills", "To prescribe one methodology and one set of templates that all organizations should adopt", "To define the business analysis profession and set out practices that are widely accepted"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK sets out to describe what the business analysis profession is and to capture the practices that are generally accepted in it. It isn’t a methodology, a project manual or a source of exam questions." },

{ id: "E-MIND-50", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 1.4 Structure of the BABOK Guide · BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "Which of these is one of the six categories of underlying competencies in the BABOK Guide?",
  options: ["Project scheduling", "Business knowledge", "Software engineering", "Financial reporting"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The six categories are analytical thinking and problem solving, behavioural characteristics, business knowledge, communication skills, interaction skills, and tools and technology." },
{ id: "E-MIND-51", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.3 Business Analysis Principles",
  question: "Where do the seven business analysis principles in The Business Analysis Standard come from?",
  options: ["The BABOK Guide’s underlying competencies chapter", "The Agile Extension to the BABOK Guide", "The IIBA Code of Ethical Conduct", "The CBAP exam blueprint"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The Standard presents the principles as those detailed in the Agile Extension, and treats them as key ingredients of an effective mindset." },
{ id: "E-MIND-52", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst invites developers, testers and front-line staff to shape the solution together instead of working alone. Which shared value does this MOST reflect?",
  options: ["Courage", "Curiosity", "Collaboration", "Ethics"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Collaboration is one of the Standard’s nine shared values: respect, courage, collaboration, ethics, curiosity, continuous learning, improvement, customer focus and value maximization." },
{ id: "E-MIND-53", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "After each release, a business analyst looks for ways to make the team’s own review process faster and clearer. Which shared value does this MOST reflect?",
  options: ["Respect", "Ethics", "Courage", "Improvement"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Improvement is one of the Standard’s shared values: continually looking for better ways of working and better outcomes." },
{ id: "E-MIND-54", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "A business analyst brings suppliers and a logistics partner into discussions about a new returns process. What does this reflect in the Standard’s description of the mindset?",
  options: ["Engaging stakeholders within the business ecosystem", "Leveraging technology to implement the change", "Influencing strategy at the executive level", "Empathizing with customers to understand their experience"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The Standard says a flexible mindset supports influencing strategy, empathizing with customers, changing processes, engaging stakeholders within the business ecosystem, evidence-based learning and leveraging technology." },
{ id: "E-MIND-55", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "Which of these is an effectiveness measure of ethical behaviour, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["Delivering every requirement before its deadline", "Promptly and fully disclosing any possible conflict of interest", "Stakeholders bringing issues and concerns to the business analyst", "Using the organization’s templates correctly"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Measures of ethics include prompt resolution of ethical dilemmas, decisions seen as transparent and fair, and full and prompt disclosure of conflicts of interest." },
{ id: "E-MIND-56", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "Stakeholders willingly discuss difficult topics with a business analyst and don’t blame her when problems occur. Which competency do these signs show?",
  options: ["Organization and time management", "Adaptability", "Trustworthiness", "Business acumen"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Measures of trustworthiness include stakeholders bringing issues to the analyst, discussing difficult or controversial topics, and not blaming the analyst when problems occur." },
{ id: "E-MIND-57", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "Stakeholders feel their time in meetings is well spent, and the business analyst arrives fully prepared for each workshop. Which competency does this show?",
  options: ["Personal accountability", "Negotiation and conflict resolution", "Systems thinking", "Organization and time management"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Measures of organization and time management include stakeholders feeling their time is well spent and complete preparation for meetings, interviews and workshops." },
{ id: "E-MIND-58", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.2 Behavioural Characteristics",
  question: "A business analyst plans her work clearly, keeps the status of planned and unplanned work known, and acts on risks and issues as they appear. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Personal accountability", "Trustworthiness", "Teaching", "Conceptual thinking"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Measures of personal accountability include planned and easily explained work, known status of planned and unplanned work, and risks and issues acted on appropriately." },
{ id: "E-MIND-59", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "Which of these is an effectiveness measure of teamwork, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["Producing the most detailed documentation in the team", "Helping the whole team feel shared ownership of its goals", "Making the final decision whenever the team disagrees", "Adjusting their approach quickly when the team’s priorities change"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Measures of teamwork include a collaborative environment, resolving conflict, building trust, shared high standards and a shared sense of ownership of team goals." },
{ id: "E-MIND-60", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.4 Communication Skills",
  question: "Readers of a business analyst’s requirements can explain them back accurately in their own words. Which competency does this show?",
  options: ["Listening", "Visual thinking", "Written communication", "Verbal communication"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A measure of effective written communication is the reader’s ability to paraphrase and describe its content, along with style suited to the audience." },
{ id: "E-MIND-61", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.4 Communication Skills",
  question: "In an interview, the business analyst gives the speaker undivided attention, defers judgment and checks understanding before moving on. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Facilitation", "Teaching", "Verbal communication", "Listening"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Measures of listening include undivided attention, encouraging the speaker, giving feedback to check understanding, and active listening that defers judgment." },
{ id: "E-MIND-62", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.5 Interaction Skills",
  question: "After training staff on a new process, the business analyst asks them to walk through it to show they understood. Which competency does this check reflect?",
  options: ["Teaching", "Facilitation", "Leadership and influencing", "Teamwork"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Measures of teaching include using different methods, validating that the audience understands the key messages, and verifying they can demonstrate the new knowledge." },
{ id: "E-MIND-63", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "A business analyst quickly absorbs new facts about a domain and applies them to areas she hasn’t worked in before. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Industry knowledge", "Learning", "Decision making", "Business acumen"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Measures of learning include rapidly absorbing new facts and ideas and applying concepts to new areas or relationships." },
{ id: "E-MIND-64", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 9.1 Analytical Thinking and Problem Solving",
  question: "A business analyst combines incomplete and uncertain information into an abstract idea, drawing on past experience, and checks that stakeholders understand it. Which competency is this?",
  options: ["Visual thinking", "Systems thinking", "Conceptual thinking", "Creative thinking"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Conceptual thinking shows up as linking unrelated pieces of information, shaping abstract ideas out of incomplete and uncertain information, and using past experience." },
{ id: "E-MIND-65", exam: "ECBA", area: "MIND", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 3.2 The Way to Think About Business Analysis",
  question: "Which of these does The Business Analysis Standard say business analysis requires, besides suitable techniques, processes and tools?",
  options: ["A formal project charter and a fixed budget", "A senior job title and certification", "A dedicated business analysis department", "An open mind and the right mix of competencies across the team"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The Standard describes business analysis as needing an open mind, appropriate techniques, processes and tools, and competency coverage across the team." },

{ id: "E-IMPL-25", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.2 Approaches to Your Work",
  question: "Which set of documents is typical of a predictive approach, according to The Business Analysis Standard?",
  options: ["Scope statement, requirements and design documents, schedule and cost baselines, and test plans", "A prioritized backlog refined just in time before each short iteration", "A lean business case updated after each round of feedback", "Release notes written after each feature goes live"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "In a predictive approach, work is planned upfront and documents and models such as a scope statement, requirements, designs, baselines and test plans are produced." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-26", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.2 Approaches to Your Work",
  question: "In an adaptive approach, which iterations are planned first?",
  options: ["All iterations, in full detail, before any work starts", "The upcoming iterations, with high-value outcomes prioritized", "The final iteration, so the end date is fixed early", "None; adaptive teams don’t plan their work"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The Standard describes adaptive work as prioritizing high-value outcomes, planning upcoming iterations first, and sharing results to gather feedback." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-27", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.6 Managing the Delivery of Business Analysis",
  question: "Each department in a company has its own business analysts, who report to that department’s manager. Which delivery model is this?",
  options: ["Centre of Excellence for the company", "Business analysis as a service", "Dispersed across business units", "Community of Practice"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "In the dispersed model, business analysis functions sit within individual departments or business units." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-28", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "easy", ref: "BA Standard 4.3 Foundational Techniques",
  question: "A business analyst holds one-on-one conversations with stakeholders to gather and check key information. Which foundational technique in the Standard covers this?",
  options: ["Process analysis", "Data storytelling", "Customer journey mapping", "Workshops/interviews"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The Standard’s foundational technique “workshops/interviews” covers engaging stakeholders in group sessions or one-on-one conversations to gather and check information." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-29", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.4 Understanding Requirements and Designs",
  question: "Which of these does traceability help manage, according to The Business Analysis Standard?",
  options: ["Scope, impact, change, risk, time, cost and communication", "Stakeholder interest and influence levels", "The vendor’s internal development process", "Solution performance against its KPIs"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The Standard says traceability helps confirm conformance and supports managing scope, impact, change, risk, time, cost and communication." },
{ id: "E-IMPL-30", exam: "ECBA", area: "IMPL", level: "mid", ref: "BA Standard 4.4 Understanding Requirements and Designs · BABOK v3 2.2 Key Terms",
  question: "What form can requirements and designs take?",
  options: ["A formal document that has been signed by the sponsor", "An artifact, a set of documents or a set of models", "A user story written on a card and nothing else", "A diagram drawn in an approved modelling tool"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "The Standard says requirements and designs can be an artifact, a document set or a set of models. BABOK adds that the representation can vary widely with the circumstances." },

/* ===================== ECBA · batch 6, part 2 (BACCM domains) ===================== */
{ id: "E-CHG-35", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Once the solution scope has been agreed, can it change during the initiative?",
  options: ["Yes; it may evolve as more information is discovered", "No; it is fixed once the sponsor has approved it", "No; later discoveries are logged as risks, not scope", "No; changing scope means starting a new initiative"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK notes the solution scope can evolve over the initiative as more is learned." },
{ id: "E-CHG-36", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Why might a solution scope also describe components that are out of scope?",
  options: ["To serve as the list of constraints on the design", "To provide clarity about what the solution won’t include", "Because every solution scope has to list exclusions first", "To mark which parts are left for the transition state"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Saying what is out of scope can be part of the solution scope too, because it makes the boundaries clearer." },
{ id: "E-CHG-37", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "A proposed change has been assessed and a decision made. Who should be told about the impacts and the resolution?",
  options: ["The sponsor, who made the decision", "The developers who will make the change", "All stakeholders, with the impacts and resolution documented", "The person who requested the change, by email"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Every impact and resolution from the change analysis should be written down and shared with all stakeholders." },
{ id: "E-CHG-38", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.4 Assess Requirements Changes",
  question: "Does every proposed change to requirements end up going through formal approval?",
  options: ["Yes; each proposal goes to the change board for a decision", "Yes, but adaptive teams approve them in the daily stand-up", "In predictive approaches it does; in adaptive approaches, the team decides informally", "No; many are withdrawn or declined before formal approval is needed"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK points out that many proposed changes are withdrawn or turned down before formal approval is ever needed." },
{ id: "E-CHG-39", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.27 Lessons Learned",
  question: "At the end of a release, the team reviews what went well, what didn’t, variances from expected results and their root causes. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Lessons learned", "Root cause analysis", "Item tracking", "Process analysis"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "A lessons learned session, also called a retrospective, reviews activities, the solution, performance and variances, root causes, and recommendations." },
{ id: "E-CHG-40", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Which of these should be considered when selecting a preferred change strategy?",
  options: ["How fully the current state has been documented", "Organizational readiness, costs, timelines, alignment with objectives and opportunity cost", "The detailed design specifications for each option", "Which option the most senior stakeholder proposed"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Choosing a strategy weighs readiness, major costs, how long the change takes, fit with objectives, when value starts to flow, and opportunity cost." },
{ id: "E-CHG-41", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "A new solution automates much of the work in a team, so their jobs will change a lot. What may the business analyst help develop?",
  options: ["A decommissioning plan for the old system", "A technical design for the automated steps", "Recommendations on changes to the organization’s structure or staffing", "A business case for the automation"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "As job functions may change significantly when work is automated, the business analyst may help develop recommendations for changes to organizational structure or personnel." },
{ id: "E-CHG-42", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance",
  question: "When defining the process for requesting changes, what does the business analyst specify?",
  options: ["The test cases each change has to pass", "The developers who will build each change", "The elicitation techniques for each change", "How a change is proposed, when it can be proposed and who may propose it"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The request process sets out how a change is proposed, when, by whom, and how requests are communicated." },
{ id: "E-CHG-43", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Improving a solution would take a large effort for a small gain, and the risks of changing it outweigh the risks of leaving it. What is MOST likely to be recommended?",
  options: ["Do nothing", "Retire the solution", "Identify additional capabilities", "Organizational change"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Doing nothing is usually the advice when the gain is small for the effort, or when changing is clearly riskier than leaving things as they are." },
{ id: "E-CHG-44", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "A new process is being blocked by an informal alliance between two team leaders. Which part of assessing enterprise limitations looks at this?",
  options: ["Operational assessment", "Organizational structure changes", "Solution component dependencies", "Analysis of performance variances"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Assessing organizational structure changes includes formal and informal relationships, such as alliances or friendships, that can enable or block a solution." },
{ id: "E-CHG-45", exam: "ECBA", area: "CHANGE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.4 Define Change Strategy",
  question: "Why should the change strategy options that were considered and rejected be kept as part of the final strategy?",
  options: ["To feed each option’s risks into the risk register", "So stakeholders understand the pros and cons of the approaches considered", "Because the rejected options become the backup plan automatically", "So the rejected options can be reused as design options when requirements are defined"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK sees the rejected options as part of the final strategy, because they show stakeholders the pros and cons of each route considered." },

{ id: "E-NEED-35", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Which of these typically prompts a business need to be evaluated?",
  options: ["A customer complaint, falling revenue or a new market opportunity", "The approval of next year’s project budget", "A request from the vendor for a contract renewal", "The completion of the business analysis approach"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Something like a customer complaint, falling revenue or a new market opening usually prompts a look at the business need." },
{ id: "E-NEED-36", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Must every aspect of the current state be fully detailed before the change strategy is developed?",
  options: ["Yes; the change strategy can’t start until the whole current state is documented", "No; it isn’t necessary, though exploring it often reveals deeper causes", "Yes, but only for predictive approaches with fixed budgets", "No; the current state is never analyzed in adaptive approaches"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK says it isn’t necessary to fully detail the current state before developing the change strategy, although exploring it often uncovers deeper causes." },
{ id: "E-NEED-37", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "Can a business goal be about keeping a current condition the same, rather than changing something?",
  options: ["No; goals describe changes the organization wants", "No; conditions to maintain are written as constraints", "Yes; goals can describe changes or current conditions to maintain", "Yes, but only if the regulator requires it"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Goals and objectives can be about changes the organization wants, or about conditions it wants to keep." },
{ id: "E-NEED-38", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "In the SMART test for objectives, what does “specific” mean?",
  options: ["Set within a defined time frame, with a clear end date", "Limited to a single department of the organization", "Tracked and measured against a target", "Describing something with an observable outcome"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "In BABOK’s SMART test, specific means describing something that has an observable outcome." },
{ id: "E-NEED-39", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "In the SMART test for objectives, what does “achievable” check?",
  options: ["The feasibility of the effort", "That the sponsor has approved the objective", "That the objective aligns with the enterprise’s mission", "That every stakeholder agrees with the objective"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "In BABOK’s SMART test, achievable means testing the feasibility of the effort." },
{ id: "E-NEED-40", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis",
  question: "Why might a team build a fishbone diagram before discussing solutions?",
  options: ["It shows the sequence of activities in the process", "It keeps the focus on the causes of the problem rather than on solutions", "It replaces the need to collect data about the problem", "It ranks each cause by how often it occurs in the data"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A fishbone diagram keeps attention on what causes the problem rather than on fixes, and organizes ideas for further digging." },
{ id: "E-NEED-41", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.40 Root Cause Analysis",
  question: "Can the five whys be used together with a fishbone diagram?",
  options: ["No; the two techniques give conflicting results", "No; the five whys replaces the fishbone diagram", "Yes; it can be used on its own or within a fishbone diagram", "Yes, but only when the problem is technical"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK says the five whys works on its own or as part of building a fishbone diagram." },
{ id: "E-NEED-42", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.4 Benchmarking and Market Analysis",
  question: "Which activity is part of benchmarking?",
  options: ["Writing user stories for the new solution", "Running a workshop to agree acceptance criteria", "Drawing the current organization chart", "Identifying gaps between current practices and best practices"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Benchmarking includes identifying leaders in the sector, studying their practices and determining gaps between current and best practices." },
{ id: "E-NEED-43", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "hard", ref: "BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases",
  question: "In a business case, what should the desired outcomes be independent of?",
  options: ["The recommended solution", "The business need", "The business objectives", "The stakeholders affected"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Desired outcomes describe the state that should result if the need is met. They should be measurable and independent of the recommended solution." },
{ id: "E-NEED-44", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.7 Business Cases",
  question: "Should a business case consider the option of doing nothing?",
  options: ["No; a business case is about justifying a new solution", "Yes; the do-nothing option should be assessed as well", "Only if the sponsor asks for it to be included", "No; doing nothing is never a valid recommendation"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK says the option of doing nothing should be assessed and considered alongside the others." },
{ id: "E-NEED-45", exam: "ECBA", area: "NEED", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.18 Document Analysis",
  question: "Before meeting stakeholders, a business analyst reads existing policies and reports to understand the background of a business need. Which technique is this?",
  options: ["Observation", "Brainstorming", "Document analysis", "Prototyping"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Document analysis can give background on the context of a business need before talking to stakeholders." },

{ id: "E-SOL-35", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "How is a design option made up, in BABOK terms?",
  options: ["Of one software product, together with its license and support terms", "Of the requirements that the option satisfies, ranked by priority", "Of a single process model that the sponsor has approved", "Of several design components, each one set out by a design element"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "A design option usually combines several design components. Each component is described by a design element, for example a policy, a process, a role, a decision or a piece of software." },
{ id: "E-SOL-36", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "A team plans to buy a CRM product and build its own integration to the billing system. Which solution approach is this?",
  options: ["A combination of create and purchase", "Create", "Purchase", "Retire"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Design options may combine creating and purchasing components. Here part is bought and part is built." },
{ id: "E-SOL-37", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.5 Define Design Options",
  question: "When describing a design option, what else should be considered besides whether components are built or bought?",
  options: ["The acceptance criteria each component must pass", "How the components will be integrated", "The sunk cost of the existing components", "How many vendors offered a similar product"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Whatever the approach, how the components will fit together is part of the design option." },
{ id: "E-SOL-38", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "When are the benefits of a solution usually realized?",
  options: ["On the day the solution goes live", "As soon as the transition requirements are met", "Over a period of time", "At the end of the payback period"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK notes benefits usually arrive gradually, over time." },
{ id: "E-SOL-39", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.1 Measure Solution Performance",
  question: "Can solution performance measures come from outside the organization?",
  options: ["No; the organization defines its own measures", "No; outside measures can’t be trusted", "Yes, as benchmarks but not as targets", "Yes; vendors, government bodies or regulators may influence or impose them"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Outside parties such as vendors, government bodies and regulators can shape or impose performance measures." },
{ id: "E-SOL-40", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations",
  question: "When analyzing how a solution affects a stakeholder group, the business analyst looks at the inputs they provide, how they use the solution and the outputs they receive. Which consideration is this?",
  options: ["Functions", "Locations", "Concerns", "Culture"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Stakeholder impact analysis considers functions (inputs, use and outputs), locations and concerns." },
{ id: "E-SOL-41", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Work passes between four people and three systems before a refund is approved, and each hand-off causes confusion. Which recommendation fits BEST?",
  options: ["Identify additional capabilities", "Reduce the complexity of interfaces", "Retire the solution", "Do nothing, since refunds are still approved"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Any time work moves between people or systems there is an interface. Simplifying those interfaces makes the work easier to understand." },
{ id: "E-SOL-42", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
  question: "Staff still print, sign and scan a form that the new system can approve on screen. Which recommendation to increase solution value fits?",
  options: ["Eliminate redundancy", "Identify additional capabilities", "Avoid waste", "Retire the solution"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Avoiding waste means removing activities that don’t add value and minimizing those that don’t contribute directly to the final product." },
{ id: "E-SOL-43", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.1 Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria",
  question: "How are acceptance criteria usually checked?",
  options: ["By ranking each option against weighted scores", "By tracing each one back to a business objective", "By comparing the solution with competitors’ products", "As statements verified as true or false, often in user acceptance testing"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Acceptance criteria are written so they can be tested: each is a statement that turns out true or false, often checked in user acceptance testing." },
{ id: "E-SOL-44", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.49 Vendor Assessment",
  question: "During a vendor assessment, the team checks whether the license terms would make it hard to move to another supplier later. Which part of vendor assessment is this?",
  options: ["Terms and conditions", "Vendor market position", "Knowledge and expertise", "Licensing and pricing models"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Terms and conditions cover whether licensing terms, intellectual property rights and infrastructure could become challenges if the organization later changes supplier." },
{ id: "E-SOL-45", exam: "ECBA", area: "SOLUTION", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.36 Prototyping",
  question: "A team builds a quick model to check that a proposed design will work technically, without worrying about how it looks. Which kind of prototype is this?",
  options: ["Form study prototype", "Proof of concept", "Storyboard", "Visual mock-up"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A proof of concept (or proof of principle) checks that a system’s design works, without modelling how it looks, what it is made of or its workflows." },

{ id: "E-STK-35", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Besides requirements, what can a stakeholder be a source of?",
  options: ["Funding for the initiative", "Test cases and test data", "Assumptions and constraints", "The project schedule"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "BABOK notes stakeholders can be a source of assumptions and constraints as well as requirements." },
{ id: "E-STK-36", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "Is the list of generic stakeholder roles in BABOK a complete list of every possible stakeholder?",
  options: ["Yes; every stakeholder fits one of the listed roles", "Yes, for predictive initiatives", "No; it lists internal roles, and external ones are covered elsewhere", "No; it isn’t intended to be exhaustive"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "BABOK says its stakeholder roles aren’t meant to cover every possible kind of stakeholder." },
{ id: "E-STK-37", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.4 Stakeholders",
  question: "How does BABOK describe the business analyst as a stakeholder?",
  options: ["As a stakeholder in every business analysis activity by default", "As an observer who doesn’t take part in decisions", "As a stakeholder only when the sponsor names them", "As the owner of the solution after it goes live"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The business analyst is automatically a stakeholder in all business analysis work, and is assumed to be responsible and accountable for doing it." },
{ id: "E-STK-38", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "How often is stakeholder analysis performed during an initiative?",
  options: ["Once, at the very start", "Repeatedly, as business analysis activities continue", "In planning, then passed to the project manager", "When the sponsor changes"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Stakeholder analysis is repeated as the work goes on, so new or changed stakeholders are picked up." },
{ id: "E-STK-39", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Why should a business analyst understand stakeholders’ decision-making authority early on?",
  options: ["To set the priority of each one’s requirements", "To set each stakeholder’s workload", "So the right people are involved when decisions and approvals are needed", "To choose the elicitation technique for each one"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Understanding authority levels upfront avoids confusion and ensures the analyst works with the right stakeholders for decisions and approvals." },
{ id: "E-STK-40", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "A subject matter expert is needed for six workshops. What should the business analyst do first?",
  options: ["Send her the workshop agenda and pre-reading", "Add her to the RACI matrix as consulted", "Replace her with someone who has more free time", "Gain her agreement on the time she will commit"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Managing stakeholder collaboration includes gaining agreement on the time and resources stakeholders commit to business analysis activities." },
{ id: "E-STK-41", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 4.5 Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
  question: "What does genuine stakeholder engagement require, according to BABOK?",
  options: ["Stakeholders feel listened to, their views count and their input is recognized", "Stakeholders sign off each requirement they contributed", "Stakeholders attend every meeting in person", "Stakeholders agree with every recommendation"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Genuine engagement requires that stakeholders feel heard, that their opinions matter and that their contributions are recognized." },
{ id: "E-STK-42", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "On a stakeholder matrix, a group has high influence and the change has a high impact on them. How should they be engaged?",
  options: ["Keep them informed through newsletters", "Treat them as key players: focus on them and engage them regularly", "Monitor them occasionally in case things change", "Make sure they remain satisfied, with little involvement"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "High influence and high impact stakeholders are key players in the change. The business analyst should focus efforts on them and engage them regularly." },
{ id: "E-STK-43", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.43 Stakeholder List, Map, or Personas",
  question: "In BABOK’s onion diagram, which group sits in the outermost layer?",
  options: ["The project team", "End users and the help desk", "Customers, suppliers, regulators and other external stakeholders", "Sponsors and executives"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The outer layer holds affected external stakeholders such as customers, suppliers and regulators. The project team is at the center." },
{ id: "E-STK-44", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Which of these attitudes does BABOK suggest the business analyst analyze?",
  options: ["Their views on the company’s competitors", "Their level of technical expertise", "Their reporting line and job grade", "Their attitude toward business analysis in general and toward the sponsor"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The analyst looks at attitudes to the goals and solutions, to business analysis itself, to the change, the sponsor, the team and to working collaboratively." },
{ id: "E-STK-45", exam: "ECBA", area: "STAKEHOLDER", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 3.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
  question: "Why does a business analyst analyze how much influence stakeholders have?",
  options: ["To build relationships and plan how to get buy-in and support", "To decide whose requirements get the highest priority", "To assign each one a RACI responsibility", "To choose which stakeholders can skip reviews"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Knowing who has influence, and how they feel, helps build trust and plan how to win support and cooperation." },

{ id: "E-VAL-35", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 2.1 The Business Analysis Core Concept Model",
  question: "How is intangible value measured, according to the BACCM?",
  options: ["Directly, in money", "It can’t be measured at all", "Indirectly", "By the sponsor’s opinion"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Tangible value is directly measurable. Intangible value, such as reputation or morale, is measured indirectly." },
{ id: "E-VAL-36", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "Can the potential value of a solution be negative?",
  options: ["No; value is positive by definition", "No; a solution that costs money still delivers value", "Yes, but only for intangible benefits", "Yes; if the costs exceed the benefits"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Value can be positive if benefits exceed costs, or negative if costs exceed benefits." },
{ id: "E-VAL-37", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "How far into the future should a cost-benefit analysis look?",
  options: ["Far enough that the solution is fully in use and delivering its planned value", "One year, the standard budget period", "Until the project closes and the team is released", "Until the vendor’s contract ends"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "The analysis should look far enough ahead to see the solution fully in use and its planned value being delivered." },
{ id: "E-VAL-38", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "In a cost-benefit analysis, the cumulative net benefits are negative for the first two years. Does that mean the change isn’t worthwhile?",
  options: ["Yes; any negative year means the change should stop", "Not necessarily; benefits often come in later years after early costs", "Yes, unless the sponsor approves an exception for the first two years", "No; the discount rate will turn those years positive"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "BABOK notes some benefits aren’t realized until future years, so cumulative net benefits can be negative for some years before turning positive." },
{ id: "E-VAL-39", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
  question: "How can the expected benefit of a single requirement be estimated?",
  options: ["By the number of stakeholders who asked for it in workshops", "By the effort the developers estimate for it", "By how much it contributes to an overall business objective", "By the seniority of the person who asked for it"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "A requirement’s expected benefit can be estimated from how much it adds toward an overall business objective once met." },
{ id: "E-VAL-40", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "A requirement has to be implemented to meet a new data-protection law. Which basis for prioritization does this concern?",
  options: ["Stability", "Benefit", "Dependencies", "Regulatory or policy compliance"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Compliance as a prioritization basis applies to requirements that have to be delivered because a regulator or a policy demands it." },
{ id: "E-VAL-41", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 5.3 Prioritize Requirements",
  question: "Stakeholders haven’t agreed on a requirement yet, and it is likely to change. What does the stability basis suggest about its priority?",
  options: ["It may be given a lower priority to reduce rework", "It should go first, to force a decision", "Its priority can’t be set until all stakeholders agree", "It should be given to the sponsor to rank"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "When a requirement isn’t stable, ranking it lower can save the team from redoing work or wasting effort if it changes." },
{ id: "E-VAL-42", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "A proposed indicator would cost more to collect than the decisions it supports are worth. Which characteristic of a good indicator does it fail?",
  options: ["Clear", "Economical", "Relevant", "Adequate"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "A good indicator should be economical: available at a reasonable cost." },
{ id: "E-VAL-43", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.3 Balanced Scorecard",
  question: "In a balanced scorecard, which dimension covers customer satisfaction and the delivery of value to customers?",
  options: ["Financial", "Business process", "Customer", "Learning and growth"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The customer dimension tracks things like customer focus, satisfaction and the value delivered to customers." },
{ id: "E-VAL-44", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.20 Financial Analysis",
  question: "Which statement about the payback period is correct?",
  options: ["It is the rate at which an investment exactly breaks even", "It is the present value of benefits minus the investment", "It already includes the discount rate", "It is how long it takes to earn back the cost of the change, ignoring the discount rate"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The payback period estimates how long it takes for benefits to cover the cost of the change, without applying a discount rate." },
{ id: "E-VAL-45", exam: "ECBA", area: "VALUE", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.28 Metrics and KPIs",
  question: "What is “reporting” in the context of metrics and KPIs?",
  options: ["Sharing measures with stakeholders in an agreed format, on an agreed schedule", "Writing the business case that justifies collecting the measures", "Recording the defects found in testing and how each was resolved", "Listing every requirement in the backlog together with its status"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "Reporting means getting the metrics or indicators to stakeholders in a format and on a schedule agreed beforehand." },

{ id: "E-CTX-35", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "Why does a business analyst assess organizational culture during current-state analysis?",
  options: ["To map the formal reporting lines", "To see whether cultural changes are needed and how stakeholders view the current state", "To decide which departments to close", "To list the internal assets in use"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Cultural assessment identifies if cultural changes are needed, whether stakeholders understand the current state, and whether they see it as satisfactory." },
{ id: "E-CTX-36", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "How can an organization’s reporting structure affect a proposed change?",
  options: ["It has no effect, since communication isn’t limited to it", "It sets the boundaries of the solution scope", "It may help or limit the change", "It only matters for changes to HR systems"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The formal structure shapes how people communicate, and the reporting lines can help or hold back a change." },
{ id: "E-CTX-37", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State",
  question: "The government is preparing new data-privacy legislation that will affect the industry. Which external influencer is this?",
  options: ["Industry structure", "Macroeconomic factors", "Technology", "Political and regulatory environment"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "The political and regulatory environment covers how current and coming laws and regulations affect the industry." },
{ id: "E-CTX-38", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.1 Analyze Current State · BABOK v3 6.2 Define Future State",
  question: "Which elements does BABOK describe in both the current state and the future state?",
  options: ["Structure and culture, capabilities, technology, policies and internal assets", "The software systems in use and the interfaces between them", "The project budget, the timeline and the planned milestones", "The stakeholder list, the RACI matrix and the communication plan"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "BABOK uses the same set of elements to describe both states: policies, internal assets, business architecture, technology and infrastructure, capabilities and processes, and structure and culture. Budgets, systems lists and RACI charts are only parts of the picture." },
{ id: "E-CTX-39", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "A board is unwilling to accept much uncertainty and will invest more to reduce risk, even if it means lower potential value. Which attitude toward risk is this?",
  options: ["Risk-seeking", "Risk-aversion", "Neutrality", "Risk transfer"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Risk-aversion is an unwillingness to accept much uncertainty, with a preference to avoid risky actions or invest more to reduce risk." },
{ id: "E-CTX-40", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 6.3 Assess Risks",
  question: "When assessing risks of a change with many unknowns, what can help the business analyst estimate impact and likelihood?",
  options: ["The organization’s risk tolerance", "Transferring the riskiest items to a third party first", "What was learned from earlier changes, and the judgment of experienced stakeholders", "Leaving uncertain risks out of the assessment"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "Experience from earlier, similar changes and the judgment of experienced stakeholders help estimate how likely and how serious each risk is." },
{ id: "E-CTX-41", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "easy", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "In a SWOT analysis, a large new competitor entering the market with lower prices is a(n):",
  options: ["Strength", "Weakness", "Opportunity", "Threat"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Threats are external factors that could harm the organization. Strengths and weaknesses are internal." },
{ id: "E-CTX-42", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.46 SWOT Analysis",
  question: "In a SWOT analysis, which kind of strategy looks at how the group can use its strengths to fend off threats?",
  options: ["ST", "SO", "WO", "WT"],
  answer: "A",
  why: "ST strategies use strengths to ward off threats. SO strategies use strengths to exploit opportunities." },
{ id: "E-CTX-43", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 10.32 Organizational Modelling",
  question: "A company organizes its staff around customer groups and regions rather than by skills. Which type of organizational model is this?",
  options: ["Functionally-oriented", "Market-oriented", "Matrix", "Hierarchical"],
  answer: "B",
  why: "Market-oriented models serve customer groups, geographical areas, projects or processes. They meet customer needs but can develop inconsistent or duplicate work." },
{ id: "E-CTX-44", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 11.2 The Business Intelligence Perspective",
  question: "An initiative focuses on turning data from many systems into reliable information for decision making. Which BABOK perspective fits it BEST?",
  options: ["Agile", "Business process management", "Business intelligence", "Business architecture"],
  answer: "C",
  why: "The business intelligence perspective focuses on transforming data into value-added information to support business decision making." },
{ id: "E-CTX-45", exam: "ECBA", area: "CONTEXT", level: "mid", ref: "BABOK v3 11.3 The Information Technology Perspective",
  question: "A payroll system starts calculating overtime incorrectly. In the IT perspective, which kind of change does this trigger?",
  options: ["Creating a new organizational capability", "Enhancing an existing capability to meet an objective", "Maintaining an existing IT system", "Repairing a broken IT system"],
  answer: "D",
  why: "Repairing a broken system corrects an IT system that isn’t performing as expected. Its urgency depends on the level of disruption." },

  ]
};
